{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011thfRLbuj1bheRHk5EqZwf","type":"tool_result","content":"     1\t% Appendix E: the first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of sparse random\n     2\t% graphs, in plain language (owner's request, 2026-10-31). Source:\n     3\t% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n     4\t% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n     5\t% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n     6\t\\section{The pathwidth of sparse random graphs}\n     7\t\\label{app:random}\n     8\t\n     9\tThe equivalences of Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} make every statement about the\n    10\tpathwidth of a graph a statement about the open stacks of an instance. This\n    11\tappendix proves one such statement for random graphs, in plain language, and\n    12\trecords what of it is checked in Lean. The result is that a sparse random\n    13\tgraph has pathwidth proportional to its number of vertices, with an explicit\n    14\tconstant. The argument is a first moment, an old tool; what is new here is the\n    15\texact form of its deterministic step, the constant it yields, and the\n    16\tmachine check. We found no prior statement of the constant.\n    17\t\n    18\t\\subsection{The statement}\n    19\t\n    20\t$G(n,p)$ is the random graph on $n$ labelled vertices in which each of the\n    21\t$\\binom n2$ possible edges is present with probability $p$, independently. We\n    22\ttake $p=c/n$, so that a vertex has about $c$ neighbours. Write\n    23\t$\\mathrm{pw}$ for pathwidth, $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm, and\n    24\t\\[\n    25\t  \\phi(x) = -x\\ln x \\quad(\\phi(0)=0),\\qquad\n    26\t  H(x,y,z) = \\phi(x)+\\phi(y)+\\phi(z)\n    27\t\\]\n    28\tfor the entropy of a probability vector with three entries.\n    29\t\n    30\t\\begin{theorem}[first moment, finite form]\n    31\t\\label{thm:firstmoment}\n    32\tLet $c>0$ and $0<\\beta<1$, and put $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ and\n    33\t\\[\n    34\t  \\delta(\\beta,c) \\;=\\; c\\,a^{2} \\;-\\; H(a,a,1-2a).\n    35\t\\]\n    36\tFor every integer $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$,\n    37\t\\[\n    38\t  \\Pr\\bigl[\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\le\\beta n\\bigr]\n    39\t  \\;\\le\\; \\exp\\bigl(-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n+3\\ln n+c\\,a\\bigr)\n    40\t  \\;\\le\\; e^{c/2}\\,n^{3}\\,e^{-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n}.\n    41\t\\]\n    42\t\\end{theorem}\n    43\t\n    44\tSo whenever $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$, the probability that the pathwidth is as small\n    45\tas $\\beta n$ is exponentially small, and the pathwidth is at least $\\beta n$\n    46\twith high probability. The next statement says for which $\\beta$ that happens.\n    47\t\n    48\t\\begin{theorem}[the admissible region]\n    49\t\\label{thm:betastar}\n    50\tIf $c\\le4\\ln2$ there is no $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ with $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. If\n    51\t$c>4\\ln2$ there is a number $\\beta^{*}(c)\\in(0,1)$ such that\n    52\t$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$; it is the root in\n    53\t$(0,1)$ of\n    54\t\\[\n    55\t  -(1-\\beta)\\ln\\tfrac{1-\\beta}{2}-\\beta\\ln\\beta \\;=\\; \\tfrac{c}{4}(1-\\beta)^{2}.\n    56\t\\]\n    57\tHence $\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\ge\\beta n$ with probability $1-O(n^{3}e^{-\\delta n})$\n    58\tfor every $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$, and as $c\\to\\infty$,\n    59\t$1-\\beta^{*}(c)=\\bigl(4\\ln c-4\\ln\\ln c+O(1)\\bigr)/c$.\n    60\t\\end{theorem}\n    61\t\n    62\tTable~\\ref{tab:betastar} gives $\\beta^{*}(c)$ at a few values of $c$, beside the\n    63\tonly published explicit lower bound we know for a width parameter of\n    64\t$G(n,c/n)$, the treewidth bound $1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ of\n    65\t\\citet{PerarnauSerra2014}, which is a lower bound on pathwidth too since\n    66\ttreewidth never exceeds pathwidth.\n    67\t\n    68\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n    69\t\\centering\n    70\t\\caption{The constant $\\beta^{*}(c)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, to four\n    71\tdecimals, and the treewidth bound of \\citet{PerarnauSerra2014} at the same\n    72\t$c$ (blank where that bound is not positive). Two independent programs agree\n    73\ton every digit of $\\beta^{*}$.}\n    74\t\\label{tab:betastar}\n    75\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}\n    76\t\\toprule\n    77\t$c$ & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 \\\\\n    78\t\\midrule\n    79\t$\\beta^{*}(c)$ & 0.0087 & 0.0613 & 0.1706 & 0.2628 & 0.3371 & 0.5535 & 0.7606 & 0.8574 \\\\\n    80\t$1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ & & & 0.0918 & 0.2136 & 0.2966 & 0.5027 & 0.6855 & 0.7776 \\\\\n    81\t\\bottomrule\n    82\t\\end{tabular}\n    83\t\\end{table}\n    84\t\n    85\tThe threshold $4\\ln2\\approx2.77$ in Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar} belongs to the\n    86\tmethod, not to the graphs: it is the point at which an edge-free split of the\n    87\tvertices into two equal halves stops being likely, and below it the first\n    88\tmoment simply says nothing. The pathwidth of $G(n,c/n)$ is in fact linear in\n    89\t$n$ for every $c>1$ \\citep{LeeLeeOum2012}, with a constant that no one has\n    90\tmade explicit.\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{The proof, in four steps}\n    93\t\n    94\t\\paragraph{Step 1: a narrow layout leaves an empty pair.}\n    95\tPut the vertices in a line and look at any position $i$. The first $i+1$\n    96\tvertices are the prefix; the rest are the suffix. A suffix vertex is\n    97\t\\emph{active} if it has a neighbour in the prefix. The vertex separation of\n    98\tthe line is the largest number of active vertices at any position, and by\n    99\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} the smallest vertex separation over all lines is the\n   100\tpathwidth. Now take a line of vertex separation $k$ and any position $i$. The\n   101\tsuffix has $n-i-1$ vertices, at most $k$ of them active, so at least $n-i-1-k$\n   102\tsuffix vertices have no neighbour at all in the prefix. Call that set $R$ and\n   103\tthe prefix $A$. Then $A$ and $R$ are disjoint, $|A|=i+1$, $|R|\\ge n-i-1-k$, and\n   104\tthere is no edge between them: an \\emph{empty pair}. This holds at every\n   105\tposition of every line, so a graph of pathwidth $k$ has an empty pair with\n   106\t$|A|=t$ and $|R|\\ge n-t-k$ for every $t\\le n$. With $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$\n   107\tboth parts have about $(n-k)/2$ vertices~\\leanref{emptypair}.\n   108\t\n   109\t\\paragraph{Step 2: count the graphs that have one.}\n   110\tFix a pair of disjoint sets $A$ and $R$ with $|A|=t$ and $|R|=t-1$. In\n   111\t$G(n,p)$ the $t(t-1)$ possible edges between them are all absent with\n   112\tprobability $(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$. There are $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}$ such\n   113\tpairs. A graph of pathwidth at most $k$ has at least one of them empty, so by\n   114\tthe union bound\n   115\t\\[\n   116\t  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\,(1-p)^{t(t-1)},\n   117\t  \\qquad t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil .\n   118\t\\]\n   119\tThis is the whole probabilistic content of the proof, and it is a Lean\n   120\ttheorem over the finite sum of the weights $p^{|E|}(1-p)^{\\binom n2-|E|}$ of\n   121\tall graphs on $n$ vertices, with no probability model needed; the count of\n   122\tgraphs with a given empty pair is exact there, $2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$ of\n   123\tthem~\\leanref{firstmoment}.\n   124\t\n   125\t\\paragraph{Step 3: estimate the count.}\n   126\tTwo standard inequalities turn the right-hand side into an exponential in\n   127\t$n$. The number of ways to choose the pair is the trinomial coefficient\n   128\t$n!/\\bigl(t!\\,(t-1)!\\,(n-2t+1)!\\bigr)$, and a trinomial coefficient is at most\n   129\t$\\exp(nH)$ with $H$ the entropy of the three proportions $t/n$, $(t-1)/n$ and\n   130\t$(n-2t+1)/n$ \\citep[Theorem~11.1.3]{CoverThomas2006}: expand\n   131\t$(x+y+z)^{n}=1$ at $x=t/n$, $y=(t-1)/n$, $z=(n-2t+1)/n$ and keep one term of\n   132\tthe sum. And $(1-p)^{m}\\le e^{-pm}$ because $1-p\\le e^{-p}$. With $p=c/n$ the\n   133\tsecond factor is at most $\\exp(-c\\,t(t-1)/n)$.\n   134\t\n   135\t\\paragraph{Step 4: the bookkeeping.}\n   136\tThe three proportions are not exactly $a$, $a$ and $1-2a$: with\n   137\t$k=\\lfloor\\beta n\\rfloor$, each is within $1/n$ of its target. A shift of $d$\n   138\tin one argument of $\\phi$ changes $\\phi$ by at most $\\phi(d)$ when $d\\le1/e$,\n   139\tand $\\phi(1/n)=(\\ln n)/n$; three shifts cost $3\\ln n$ in the exponent, which\n   140\tis the $3\\ln n$ of the theorem and the reason for $n\\ge3$. Likewise\n   141\t$t(t-1)\\ge a^{2}n^{2}-an$, and the $-an$ costs the term $c\\,a$. Multiplying\n   142\tthe two estimates of Step~3 into the bound of Step~2 gives\n   143\t$\\exp(nH(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}n+3\\ln n+ca)$, which is Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment};\n   144\t$a\\le1/2$ gives its second form. Nothing is hidden in an error term: the\n   145\tinequalities of Step~3 are exact bounds on the two factors, and the two\n   146\tcorrections are the ones just named~\\leanref{firstmomentA}.\n   147\t\n   148\t\\paragraph{The admissible region.}\n   149\tOn $(0,\\tfrac12]$ the function $g(a)=H(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}$ is strictly concave,\n   150\ttends to $0$ as $a\\to0$, and equals $\\ln2-c/4$ at $a=\\tfrac12$. If\n   151\t$c\\le4\\ln2$ it is nonnegative throughout, so $\\delta=-g$ is never positive.\n   152\tIf $c>4\\ln2$ it has exactly one zero $a^{*}$ in $(0,\\tfrac12)$, is positive\n   153\tbefore it and negative after it, so $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when\n   154\t$a=(1-\\beta)/2>a^{*}$, that is when $\\beta<1-2a^{*}=:\\beta^{*}(c)$.\n   155\tSubstituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\n   156\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\n   157\ttwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\n   158\tproved on paper only.\n   159\t\n   160\t\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n   161\t\\label{app:random-cs}\n   162\t\n   163\tThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\n   164\tgenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\n   165\tindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\n   166\taverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\n   167\tfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\n   168\tat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\n   169\trules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\n   170\tclasses. The MOSP graph of such an instance is the union of one clique per\n   171\tpattern. Steps 1 and 2 of the proof survive this change unaltered: a graph of\n   172\tpathwidth $k$ still has an empty pair, and a pair is empty exactly when no\n   173\tpattern contains a customer of each side. If the patterns are independent\n   174\trandom customer sets of sizes $s_1,\\dots,s_m$, the probability that a given\n   175\tpair $(A,R)$ with $|A|=t$, $|R|=t-1$ is empty factorises over the patterns,\n   176\tand the union bound gives\n   177\t\\[\n   178\t  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n   179\t  \\frac{\\binom{n-t}{s_j}+\\binom{n-t+1}{s_j}-\\binom{n-2t+1}{s_j}}{\\binom{n}{s_j}},\n   180\t\\]\n   181\tthe fraction being the share of $s_j$-sets that miss $A$ or miss $R$. This is\n   182\ta Lean theorem in the paper's own vocabulary, with the instance built from the\n   183\tpatterns and the event stated as $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le k+1$~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\n   184\tIt is computable from the pattern sizes alone, so it predicts the benchmark\n   185\tfrom the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\n   186\tthe forty classes, the mean certified optimum of its five instances beside\n   187\tthe floor the inequality gives at confidence $0.01$, that is, the largest\n   188\tvalue it does not rule out at that level plus one. None of the 200 certified\n   189\toptima lies below its floor. At $d=10$ the floor is within $5\\%$ of the\n   190\toptimum, at $d=4$ within $20\\%$; at $d=2$ it is a third short, and\n   191\tasymptotically it says nothing there: with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of $d$\n   192\tcustomers, the exponent of the expected number of balanced empty pairs is\n   193\t$\\ln2\\,\\bigl(1+\\mu(1-d)\\bigr)$, so the method gives a positive constant\n   194\texactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$. That quantity is the mean number of patterns per\n   195\tcustomer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of\n   196\tpatterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of\n   197\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\n   198\tsparsest ones ($d=2$), have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the\n   199\tregion where this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers\n   200\tthe cost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2$ to $2.4$, between the\n   201\t$d=2$ classes and the $d=4$ classes (excess $2.9$ to $3.4$ at 125 customers).\n   202\tSection~\\ref{app:excess} places every instance on that axis and tests, and\n   203\trejects, the hypothesis that the peak is where this bound is loosest.\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   206\t\\centering\n   207\t\\caption{The Chu \\& Stuckey classes: mean certified optimum of the five\n   208\tinstances, and the floor from the first-moment inequality at confidence\n   209\t$0.01$, computed from the pattern sizes of each file. No certified optimum\n   210\tlies below its floor.}\n   211\t\\label{tab:cs-floor}\n   212\t\\small\n   213\t\\input{tables/cs_firstmoment}\n   214\t\\end{table}\n   215\t\n   216\tThe inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\n   217\tpadding and the rejection break. What is proved is the inequality with the\n   218\tsizes the table has \\emph{before} the repairs. On the Bernoulli table a given\n   219\tpair is empty with probability exactly\n   220\t$[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction\n   221\tabove; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\n   222\tempty pair of the original~\\leanref{coupling} and the bound with Bernoulli\n   223\tsizes holds for the padded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the\n   224\trows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\max(s,2)$ with $s$\n   225\tbinomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an\n   226\tincreasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,\n   227\tdisconnect the instance and lower its pathwidth, so Harris's inequality does\n   228\tnot apply to it, and conditioning on acceptance costs at most the factor\n   229\t$1/\\Pr[\\text{accepted}]$, which is measured: within $1\\%$ of $1$ at\n   230\t$d\\ge6$ and on the square classes at $d=4$, $0.77$ at the $100\\times50$,\n   231\t$d=4$ class, $0.53$ to $0.87$ at $d=2$, and $0.005$ at the $100\\times50$,\n   232\t$d=2$ class. A Chernoff bound on the pattern sizes gives a quenched form of\n   233\tthe same floor, two to four stacks higher on the largest classes. These\n   234\tproved floors lie below the floors of Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor}, by zero to\n   235\tfive stacks at $d\\ge8$, by two to twelve at $d=4$ and $6$ on the square\n   236\tclasses and by more on the $100\\times50$ classes; at $d=2$ they are trivial,\n   237\tbecause $40\\%$ of the Bernoulli patterns have at most one customer and the\n   238\tpads that make them count are exactly what the proof cannot use. What\n   239\tremains between the proved floors and the table is, at $d\\ge4$, the\n   240\tdifference between a bound on the average over size vectors and the typical\n   241\tsize vector, and at $d=2$ the pads. With the sizes the files actually have\n   242\tthe statement is measured, not proved: under the fitted generator the repairs and the\n   243\trejection make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a factor\n   244\tthat falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, and on 7{,}200 fresh\n   245\tinstances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly none has an optimum below its\n   246\t$0.01$ floor.\n   247\t\n   248\tThe same first moment appears in \\citet{Gao2012} for random intersection\n   249\tgraphs, with treewidth in place of pathwidth. His proof drops the factor\n   250\tcorresponding to the product above between two lines, which is harmless when\n   251\tthe number of patterns is sublinear in $n$ and changes the constant when it is\n   252\tlinear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's\n   253\tartefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$.\n   254\t\n   255\t% The excess subsection (loop0010 item 12, 2026-11-01). Source:\n   256\t% reports/excess_phase_diagram.md (the opening and sections 1-11); Lean:\n   257\t% ForestCover.lean, TreeBound.lean, IndependenceBound.lean. The figure is\n   258\t% paper1/figures/excess_phase_diagram.pdf from\n   259\t% paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py (its CSV is committed under\n   260\t% data/); the table is tables/excess_beta.tex from make_tables.py.\n   261\t\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\n   262\t\\label{app:excess}\n   263\t\n   264\tThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.\n   265\tFor an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,\n   266\tthe \\emph{excess} is $e=(N-m)/n$, the mean number of patterns per customer\n   267\tminus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$ the mean customers per pattern,\n   268\t$e=r(c-1)$; and if the customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node per\n   269\tcustomer and per pattern and an edge per one in the table, has $\\kappa$\n   270\tcomponents, it has $n(e-1)+\\kappa$ independent cycles, so $B$ can be a forest\n   271\tonly when $e<1$. We read $e$ from the table, never from a generator's nominal\n   272\tparameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values of $e$ carry a meaning.\n   273\tThe constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that\n   274\tis when $e>1$ (on paper, from the exponent of~\\leanref{clique-cover}). On\n   275\trandom covers of 15 to 75 customers at every ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$,\n   276\tthe nodes our search spends to refute $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak at $e=2$ to\n   277\t$2.4$, falling by two orders of magnitude on either side (measured). And at\n   278\t$e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, $0.62$ for $m=n$, the branching factor of the\n   279\trandom incidence graph is one: on generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the\n   280\tshare of instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below\n   281\t$e_g(r)$ to $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above\n   282\t(measured). Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of\n   283\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} against the three. Below $e_g(r)$ the open stacks\n   284\tare governed by the largest pattern (Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}); between\n   285\t$e_g(r)$ and $1$ the first moment says nothing and the deterministic bounds\n   286\tare all there is; between $1$ and the ridge the certified optimum per\n   287\tcustomer sits $0.02$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$ and the cost of certification\n   288\tclimbs, and this is where the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results}\n   289\t($e=1.66$--$1.78$) and 22 of the 24 industrial instances sit; above the ridge\n   290\tthe cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of the certified\n   291\tslope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers), the $d=4$ classes\n   292\t($e=2.9$--$3.7$) being the dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we hold has\n   293\t$e<e_g(r)$, and 12 of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$.\n   294\t\n   295\t\\paragraph{The forest regime.}\n   296\tWrite $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern.\n   297\t\\begin{theorem}[blow-up]\n   298\t\\label{thm:blowup}\n   299\tFor every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,\n   300\t$\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\bigl(\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\rfloor+1\\bigr)$.\n   301\t\\end{theorem}\n   302\tReplace each pattern node in each bag of a path decomposition of $B$ by the\n   303\tpattern's customers: a customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so its bags stay\n   304\tan interval, and no bag grows by more than a factor $\\omega$. Nothing about\n   305\t$B$ is used; the forest enters only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$\n   306\tvertices, at most $\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, which is sharp. Both are\n   307\tLean theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose: on 7,004\n   308\tgraphs with exact pathwidth (clique trees to 936 vertices, random block trees,\n   309\t3,448 certified random covers whose $B$ is a forest, and 2,000 small random\n   310\tinstances) it is never violated and never exact, 2 to 5.5 times the truth on\n   311\tthe block-graph families; on the 3,448 random forests the pathwidth is\n   312\t$\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$, so below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is, in practice, the\n   313\tlargest pattern (measured). The exact theory on block graphs is polynomial\n   314\t\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}; on clique trees the exact values grow by about\n   315\t$(\\omega-2)/2$ per level of depth, no formula linear in $\\omega$ and the depth\n   316\tfits them, and we conjecture no closed form.\n   317\t\n   318\t\\begin{lemma}[pendant cliques]\n   319\t\\label{lem:pendant}\n   320\tIf a clique $R$ has $q\\ge1$ cliques of size at least $t$ pendant at distinct\n   321\tvertices of $R$, each meeting $R$ in its attachment vertex only, then\n   322\t$\\pw\\ge t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-2$.\n   323\t\\end{lemma}\n   324\tEvery clique lies inside one bag; if a pendant's bag is the bag of $R$ the two\n   325\tshare a bag of size $|R|+t-1$, and otherwise at least $\\lceil q/2\\rceil$\n   326\tpendants have their bags on one side of $R$'s, and the one nearest $R$ holds\n   327\tits own clique together with the attachment vertices of the others, by the\n   328\tinterval property. It is a Lean theorem~\\leanref{pendant}, and with\n   329\t$t=q=|R|$ it gives the exact value $\\lceil 3s/2\\rceil-2$ of a clique $K_s$\n   330\twith a pendant $K_s$ at every vertex, the depth-one clique tree; with the\n   331\tmatching decomposition (on paper), $K_s$ with a pendant $K_t$ at $q$ of its\n   332\tvertices has pathwidth exactly $\\max(s-1,\\,t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-2)$. It is a\n   333\tpathwidth argument that is not degree-based, and on the certified instances\n   334\tit is worth little: evaluated over the maximal cliques of the MOSP graph it is\n   335\tabove the clique floor $\\omega-1$ on 554 of the $6{,}376$ and tight on\n   336\t$44\\%$, but above the solver's bound on 5, and above $\\max(\\text{solver\n   337\tbound},\\,\\mathrm{tw}+1)$ on 3 of the 338 instances where that bound misses by\n   338\ttwo or more, by one stack each (measured, 9--134 customers).\n   339\t\n   340\t\\paragraph{Constructions from above.}\n   341\t\\begin{lemma}[layers]\n   342\t\\label{lem:layer}\n   343\tIf $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\n   344\tgraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\n   345\tindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n   346\t\\end{lemma}\n   347\tThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in\n   348\tI$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$\n   349\tpatterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion\n   350\tand the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns\n   351\tfollows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant\n   352\t$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\n   353\tcite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$\n   354\tand the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at\n   355\t30--100 customers. The sandwich\n   356\t$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\n   357\twidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\n   358\tleast where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use for\n   359\tupper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per\n   360\tcustomer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve).\n   361\t\n   362\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   363\t\\centering\n   364\t\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\n   365\tfirst-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the\n   366\tslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100\n   367\tcustomers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of\n   368\tthe analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up\n   369\tto concentration).}\n   370\t\\label{tab:excess}\n   371\t\\small\n   372\t\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n   373\t\\end{table}\n   374\t\n   375\t\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose.}\n   376\tA layout of width $k$ forces $\\ell$ nested empty pairs, one per prefix size,\n   377\tbut counting them gives a constant \\emph{below} $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell (by\n   378\t$0.051$ at $\\ell=2$, $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and more for larger $\\ell$, at every\n   379\tspacing): naming $\\varepsilon n$ more vertices costs\n   380\t$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy and buys\n   381\t$\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\n   382\tempty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n   383\t$\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\n   384\tcondition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to\n   385\tthree stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a\n   386\tcount over one representative per cluster is the route not taken.\n   387\t\n   388\t\\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}\n   389\tThe excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\n   390\t$e=2$ the certified slope is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$, $0.167$ for\n   391\t$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$ (standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$), and 13 of 16 pairs of\n   392\tseries matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their error;\n   393\t$\\beta^{*}$ has the same dependence ($0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$, $0.123$), and\n   394\tover 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$ with residual $0.024$. Nor is the\n   395\tridge where the first moment is loosest: on 25 series at 50--75 customers\n   396\tthe gap between the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$,\n   397\ton the dense side, with the ridge on a plateau within one stack of the\n   398\tmaximum. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search can\n   399\treach, not the slack of the bound.\n   400\t\n   401\t\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}\n   402\tThe 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018} have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$,\n   403\tmedian $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, none has a forest $B$, ten decompose. Every\n   404\trefutation on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the greedy\n   405\tclosing rule is exact on 12 and at most $0.061n$ above the optimum. One, with\n   406\t60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two stacks below the floor a\n   407\trandom cover with its pattern sizes has at confidence $0.01$: no\n   408\tcontradiction, the floor being a random-model statement, but a measure of\n   409\tdistance from random, on which the designed Challenge instances sit up to 39\n   410\tstacks below. These are 24 instances from one source, read with pieces as\n   411\tcustomers (the orientation of the files' format line, the opposite of their\n   412\tdescription's prose); we say nothing about industry beyond them.\n   413\t\n   414\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   415\t\\centering\n   416\t\\includegraphics[width=0.96\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}\n   417\t\\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of\n   418\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey\n   419\tclasses with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median\n   420\tand quartiles of the other collections. Dashed, $e_g(1)=0.62$; solid, $e=1$;\n   421\tthe band, the ridge $e=2$--$2.4$. Below, random covers with $m=n$ patterns of\n   422\t$d=2$ to $10$ customers: $\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the certified slope $b$ at\n   423\t30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and $\\beta^{+}$\n   424\tof the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and $m=2n$, which differ from\n   425\tit at equal excess. Regenerate:\n   426\t\\texttt{paper1/latex/figures/excess\\_phase\\_diagram.py}.}\n   427\t\\label{fig:excess}\n   428\t\\end{figure}\n   429\t\n   430\t\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n   431\t\n   432\tSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\n   433\tare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\n   434\tconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n   435\t$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in\n   436\tMathlib and is proved there from the binomial theorem. Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}\n   437\tis proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\n   438\tby two programs written independently.\n   439\t\n   440\tThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\n   441\tsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\n   442\tprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n   443\t$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\n   444\tstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\n   445\tused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\n   446\tChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n   447\t$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\n   448\tthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\n   449\t\n   450\tOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n   451\t133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\n   452\texactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\n   453\tfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\n   454\tprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n   455\t$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\n   456\tis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a count over one representative per cluster of empty\n   457\tpairs (Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}), not a sharper estimate of this one.","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"f4f0a0cb-191d-41e9-a317-1bb84326fa29","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:58:56.980Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"     1\t% Appendix E: the first-moment lower bound on the pathwidth of sparse random\n     2\t% graphs, in plain language (owner's request, 2026-10-31). Source:\n     3\t% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n     4\t% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n     5\t% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n     6\t\\section{The pathwidth of sparse random graphs}\n     7\t\\label{app:random}\n     8\t\n     9\tThe equivalences of Section~\\ref{sec:equiv} make every statement about the\n    10\tpathwidth of a graph a statement about the open stacks of an instance. This\n    11\tappendix proves one such statement for random graphs, in plain language, and\n    12\trecords what of it is checked in Lean. The result is that a sparse random\n    13\tgraph has pathwidth proportional to its number of vertices, with an explicit\n    14\tconstant. The argument is a first moment, an old tool; what is new here is the\n    15\texact form of its deterministic step, the constant it yields, and the\n    16\tmachine check. We found no prior statement of the constant.\n    17\t\n    18\t\\subsection{The statement}\n    19\t\n    20\t$G(n,p)$ is the random graph on $n$ labelled vertices in which each of the\n    21\t$\\binom n2$ possible edges is present with probability $p$, independently. We\n    22\ttake $p=c/n$, so that a vertex has about $c$ neighbours. Write\n    23\t$\\mathrm{pw}$ for pathwidth, $\\ln$ for the natural logarithm, and\n    24\t\\[\n    25\t  \\phi(x) = -x\\ln x \\quad(\\phi(0)=0),\\qquad\n    26\t  H(x,y,z) = \\phi(x)+\\phi(y)+\\phi(z)\n    27\t\\]\n    28\tfor the entropy of a probability vector with three entries.\n    29\t\n    30\t\\begin{theorem}[first moment, finite form]\n    31\t\\label{thm:firstmoment}\n    32\tLet $c>0$ and $0<\\beta<1$, and put $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ and\n    33\t\\[\n    34\t  \\delta(\\beta,c) \\;=\\; c\\,a^{2} \\;-\\; H(a,a,1-2a).\n    35\t\\]\n    36\tFor every integer $n\\ge\\max(3,c)$,\n    37\t\\[\n    38\t  \\Pr\\bigl[\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\le\\beta n\\bigr]\n    39\t  \\;\\le\\; \\exp\\bigl(-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n+3\\ln n+c\\,a\\bigr)\n    40\t  \\;\\le\\; e^{c/2}\\,n^{3}\\,e^{-\\delta(\\beta,c)\\,n}.\n    41\t\\]\n    42\t\\end{theorem}\n    43\t\n    44\tSo whenever $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$, the probability that the pathwidth is as small\n    45\tas $\\beta n$ is exponentially small, and the pathwidth is at least $\\beta n$\n    46\twith high probability. The next statement says for which $\\beta$ that happens.\n    47\t\n    48\t\\begin{theorem}[the admissible region]\n    49\t\\label{thm:betastar}\n    50\tIf $c\\le4\\ln2$ there is no $\\beta\\in(0,1)$ with $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. If\n    51\t$c>4\\ln2$ there is a number $\\beta^{*}(c)\\in(0,1)$ such that\n    52\t$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$; it is the root in\n    53\t$(0,1)$ of\n    54\t\\[\n    55\t  -(1-\\beta)\\ln\\tfrac{1-\\beta}{2}-\\beta\\ln\\beta \\;=\\; \\tfrac{c}{4}(1-\\beta)^{2}.\n    56\t\\]\n    57\tHence $\\mathrm{pw}(G(n,c/n))\\ge\\beta n$ with probability $1-O(n^{3}e^{-\\delta n})$\n    58\tfor every $\\beta<\\beta^{*}(c)$, and as $c\\to\\infty$,\n    59\t$1-\\beta^{*}(c)=\\bigl(4\\ln c-4\\ln\\ln c+O(1)\\bigr)/c$.\n    60\t\\end{theorem}\n    61\t\n    62\tTable~\\ref{tab:betastar} gives $\\beta^{*}(c)$ at a few values of $c$, beside the\n    63\tonly published explicit lower bound we know for a width parameter of\n    64\t$G(n,c/n)$, the treewidth bound $1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ of\n    65\t\\citet{PerarnauSerra2014}, which is a lower bound on pathwidth too since\n    66\ttreewidth never exceeds pathwidth.\n    67\t\n    68\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n    69\t\\centering\n    70\t\\caption{The constant $\\beta^{*}(c)$ of Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, to four\n    71\tdecimals, and the treewidth bound of \\citet{PerarnauSerra2014} at the same\n    72\t$c$ (blank where that bound is not positive). Two independent programs agree\n    73\ton every digit of $\\beta^{*}$.}\n    74\t\\label{tab:betastar}\n    75\t\\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrrrr@{}}\n    76\t\\toprule\n    77\t$c$ & 3 & 4 & 6 & 8 & 10 & 20 & 50 & 100 \\\\\n    78\t\\midrule\n    79\t$\\beta^{*}(c)$ & 0.0087 & 0.0613 & 0.1706 & 0.2628 & 0.3371 & 0.5535 & 0.7606 & 0.8574 \\\\\n    80\t$1-\\sqrt{9\\ln3/(2c)}$ & & & 0.0918 & 0.2136 & 0.2966 & 0.5027 & 0.6855 & 0.7776 \\\\\n    81\t\\bottomrule\n    82\t\\end{tabular}\n    83\t\\end{table}\n    84\t\n    85\tThe threshold $4\\ln2\\approx2.77$ in Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar} belongs to the\n    86\tmethod, not to the graphs: it is the point at which an edge-free split of the\n    87\tvertices into two equal halves stops being likely, and below it the first\n    88\tmoment simply says nothing. The pathwidth of $G(n,c/n)$ is in fact linear in\n    89\t$n$ for every $c>1$ \\citep{LeeLeeOum2012}, with a constant that no one has\n    90\tmade explicit.\n    91\t\n    92\t\\subsection{The proof, in four steps}\n    93\t\n    94\t\\paragraph{Step 1: a narrow layout leaves an empty pair.}\n    95\tPut the vertices in a line and look at any position $i$. The first $i+1$\n    96\tvertices are the prefix; the rest are the suffix. A suffix vertex is\n    97\t\\emph{active} if it has a neighbour in the prefix. The vertex separation of\n    98\tthe line is the largest number of active vertices at any position, and by\n    99\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:vs} the smallest vertex separation over all lines is the\n   100\tpathwidth. Now take a line of vertex separation $k$ and any position $i$. The\n   101\tsuffix has $n-i-1$ vertices, at most $k$ of them active, so at least $n-i-1-k$\n   102\tsuffix vertices have no neighbour at all in the prefix. Call that set $R$ and\n   103\tthe prefix $A$. Then $A$ and $R$ are disjoint, $|A|=i+1$, $|R|\\ge n-i-1-k$, and\n   104\tthere is no edge between them: an \\emph{empty pair}. This holds at every\n   105\tposition of every line, so a graph of pathwidth $k$ has an empty pair with\n   106\t$|A|=t$ and $|R|\\ge n-t-k$ for every $t\\le n$. With $t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil$\n   107\tboth parts have about $(n-k)/2$ vertices~\\leanref{emptypair}.\n   108\t\n   109\t\\paragraph{Step 2: count the graphs that have one.}\n   110\tFix a pair of disjoint sets $A$ and $R$ with $|A|=t$ and $|R|=t-1$. In\n   111\t$G(n,p)$ the $t(t-1)$ possible edges between them are all absent with\n   112\tprobability $(1-p)^{t(t-1)}$. There are $\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}$ such\n   113\tpairs. A graph of pathwidth at most $k$ has at least one of them empty, so by\n   114\tthe union bound\n   115\t\\[\n   116\t  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\,(1-p)^{t(t-1)},\n   117\t  \\qquad t=\\lceil(n-k)/2\\rceil .\n   118\t\\]\n   119\tThis is the whole probabilistic content of the proof, and it is a Lean\n   120\ttheorem over the finite sum of the weights $p^{|E|}(1-p)^{\\binom n2-|E|}$ of\n   121\tall graphs on $n$ vertices, with no probability model needed; the count of\n   122\tgraphs with a given empty pair is exact there, $2^{\\binom n2-t(t-1)}$ of\n   123\tthem~\\leanref{firstmoment}.\n   124\t\n   125\t\\paragraph{Step 3: estimate the count.}\n   126\tTwo standard inequalities turn the right-hand side into an exponential in\n   127\t$n$. The number of ways to choose the pair is the trinomial coefficient\n   128\t$n!/\\bigl(t!\\,(t-1)!\\,(n-2t+1)!\\bigr)$, and a trinomial coefficient is at most\n   129\t$\\exp(nH)$ with $H$ the entropy of the three proportions $t/n$, $(t-1)/n$ and\n   130\t$(n-2t+1)/n$ \\citep[Theorem~11.1.3]{CoverThomas2006}: expand\n   131\t$(x+y+z)^{n}=1$ at $x=t/n$, $y=(t-1)/n$, $z=(n-2t+1)/n$ and keep one term of\n   132\tthe sum. And $(1-p)^{m}\\le e^{-pm}$ because $1-p\\le e^{-p}$. With $p=c/n$ the\n   133\tsecond factor is at most $\\exp(-c\\,t(t-1)/n)$.\n   134\t\n   135\t\\paragraph{Step 4: the bookkeeping.}\n   136\tThe three proportions are not exactly $a$, $a$ and $1-2a$: with\n   137\t$k=\\lfloor\\beta n\\rfloor$, each is within $1/n$ of its target. A shift of $d$\n   138\tin one argument of $\\phi$ changes $\\phi$ by at most $\\phi(d)$ when $d\\le1/e$,\n   139\tand $\\phi(1/n)=(\\ln n)/n$; three shifts cost $3\\ln n$ in the exponent, which\n   140\tis the $3\\ln n$ of the theorem and the reason for $n\\ge3$. Likewise\n   141\t$t(t-1)\\ge a^{2}n^{2}-an$, and the $-an$ costs the term $c\\,a$. Multiplying\n   142\tthe two estimates of Step~3 into the bound of Step~2 gives\n   143\t$\\exp(nH(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}n+3\\ln n+ca)$, which is Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment};\n   144\t$a\\le1/2$ gives its second form. Nothing is hidden in an error term: the\n   145\tinequalities of Step~3 are exact bounds on the two factors, and the two\n   146\tcorrections are the ones just named~\\leanref{firstmomentA}.\n   147\t\n   148\t\\paragraph{The admissible region.}\n   149\tOn $(0,\\tfrac12]$ the function $g(a)=H(a,a,1-2a)-ca^{2}$ is strictly concave,\n   150\ttends to $0$ as $a\\to0$, and equals $\\ln2-c/4$ at $a=\\tfrac12$. If\n   151\t$c\\le4\\ln2$ it is nonnegative throughout, so $\\delta=-g$ is never positive.\n   152\tIf $c>4\\ln2$ it has exactly one zero $a^{*}$ in $(0,\\tfrac12)$, is positive\n   153\tbefore it and negative after it, so $\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$ exactly when\n   154\t$a=(1-\\beta)/2>a^{*}$, that is when $\\beta<1-2a^{*}=:\\beta^{*}(c)$.\n   155\tSubstituting $a=(1-\\beta)/2$ into $g(a)=0$ gives the equation of\n   156\tTheorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}, and the asymptotic form follows from the\n   157\ttwo-sided estimate $2\\ln(1/a)-ca\\le g(a)/a\\le2\\ln(1/a)+2-ca$. This part is\n   158\tproved on paper only.\n   159\t\n   160\t\\subsection{The benchmark generator}\n   161\t\\label{app:random-cs}\n   162\t\n   163\tThe random instances of Section~\\ref{sec:results} are not $G(n,p)$. The\n   164\tgenerator of \\citet{ChuStuckey2009} fills a customer-by-pattern table with\n   165\tindependent entries at a density chosen to give $d$ customers per pattern on\n   166\taverage, and discards any instance whose MOSP graph is disconnected; the\n   167\tfiles show two further rules the paper does not state, every customer given\n   168\tat least one pattern and every pattern at least two customers, and with those\n   169\trules a simulated generator reproduces the size statistics of all forty\n   170\tclasses. The MOSP graph of such an instance is the union of one clique per\n   171\tpattern. Steps 1 and 2 of the proof survive this change unaltered: a graph of\n   172\tpathwidth $k$ still has an empty pair, and a pair is empty exactly when no\n   173\tpattern contains a customer of each side. If the patterns are independent\n   174\trandom customer sets of sizes $s_1,\\dots,s_m$, the probability that a given\n   175\tpair $(A,R)$ with $|A|=t$, $|R|=t-1$ is empty factorises over the patterns,\n   176\tand the union bound gives\n   177\t\\[\n   178\t  \\Pr[\\mathrm{pw}\\le k]\\;\\le\\;\\binom nt\\binom{n-t}{t-1}\\prod_{j=1}^{m}\n   179\t  \\frac{\\binom{n-t}{s_j}+\\binom{n-t+1}{s_j}-\\binom{n-2t+1}{s_j}}{\\binom{n}{s_j}},\n   180\t\\]\n   181\tthe fraction being the share of $s_j$-sets that miss $A$ or miss $R$. This is\n   182\ta Lean theorem in the paper's own vocabulary, with the instance built from the\n   183\tpatterns and the event stated as $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le k+1$~\\leanref{clique-cover}.\n   184\tIt is computable from the pattern sizes alone, so it predicts the benchmark\n   185\tfrom the generator's parameters. Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor} gives, for each of\n   186\tthe forty classes, the mean certified optimum of its five instances beside\n   187\tthe floor the inequality gives at confidence $0.01$, that is, the largest\n   188\tvalue it does not rule out at that level plus one. None of the 200 certified\n   189\toptima lies below its floor. At $d=10$ the floor is within $5\\%$ of the\n   190\toptimum, at $d=4$ within $20\\%$; at $d=2$ it is a third short, and\n   191\tasymptotically it says nothing there: with $m=\\mu n$ patterns of $d$\n   192\tcustomers, the exponent of the expected number of balanced empty pairs is\n   193\t$\\ln2\\,\\bigl(1+\\mu(1-d)\\bigr)$, so the method gives a positive constant\n   194\texactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$. That quantity is the mean number of patterns per\n   195\tcustomer minus one, the number of ones in the table minus the number of\n   196\tpatterns, per customer; call it the excess. The classes of\n   197\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} that took thousands of core-hours to certify, the\n   198\tsparsest ones ($d=2$), have excess between $1.66$ and $1.78$, just inside the\n   199\tregion where this argument proves linear pathwidth; on generated random covers\n   200\tthe cost of the exact search peaks at excess about $2$ to $2.4$, between the\n   201\t$d=2$ classes and the $d=4$ classes (excess $2.9$ to $3.4$ at 125 customers).\n   202\tSection~\\ref{app:excess} places every instance on that axis and tests, and\n   203\trejects, the hypothesis that the peak is where this bound is loosest.\n   204\t\n   205\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   206\t\\centering\n   207\t\\caption{The Chu \\& Stuckey classes: mean certified optimum of the five\n   208\tinstances, and the floor from the first-moment inequality at confidence\n   209\t$0.01$, computed from the pattern sizes of each file. No certified optimum\n   210\tlies below its floor.}\n   211\t\\label{tab:cs-floor}\n   212\t\\small\n   213\t\\input{tables/cs_firstmoment}\n   214\t\\end{table}\n   215\t\n   216\tThe inequality assumes the patterns are uniform given their sizes, which the\n   217\tpadding and the rejection break. What is proved is the inequality with the\n   218\tsizes the table has \\emph{before} the repairs. On the Bernoulli table a given\n   219\tpair is empty with probability exactly\n   220\t$[(1-p)^t+(1-p)^{t-1}-(1-p)^{2t-1}]^m$, the binomial average of the fraction\n   221\tabove; the repairs only add ones, so an empty pair of the padded table is an\n   222\tempty pair of the original~\\leanref{coupling} and the bound with Bernoulli\n   223\tsizes holds for the padded instance; and if the patterns are padded before the customers, the\n   224\trows after that pad are independent uniform sets of size $\\max(s,2)$ with $s$\n   225\tbinomial, and the bound holds with those sizes. The rejection is not an\n   226\tincreasing event of the table entries: adding an entry can make a pad vanish,\n   227\tdisconnect the instance and lower its pathwidth, so Harris's inequality does\n   228\tnot apply to it, and conditioning on acceptance costs at most the factor\n   229\t$1/\\Pr[\\text{accepted}]$, which is measured: within $1\\%$ of $1$ at\n   230\t$d\\ge6$ and on the square classes at $d=4$, $0.77$ at the $100\\times50$,\n   231\t$d=4$ class, $0.53$ to $0.87$ at $d=2$, and $0.005$ at the $100\\times50$,\n   232\t$d=2$ class. A Chernoff bound on the pattern sizes gives a quenched form of\n   233\tthe same floor, two to four stacks higher on the largest classes. These\n   234\tproved floors lie below the floors of Table~\\ref{tab:cs-floor}, by zero to\n   235\tfive stacks at $d\\ge8$, by two to twelve at $d=4$ and $6$ on the square\n   236\tclasses and by more on the $100\\times50$ classes; at $d=2$ they are trivial,\n   237\tbecause $40\\%$ of the Bernoulli patterns have at most one customer and the\n   238\tpads that make them count are exactly what the proof cannot use. What\n   239\tremains between the proved floors and the table is, at $d\\ge4$, the\n   240\tdifference between a bound on the average over size vectors and the typical\n   241\tsize vector, and at $d=2$ the pads. With the sizes the files actually have\n   242\tthe statement is measured, not proved: under the fitted generator the repairs and the\n   243\trejection make empty pairs rarer than the uniform model says, by a factor\n   244\tthat falls from $1.0$ to $0.6$ as the sets grow, and on 7{,}200 fresh\n   245\tinstances at 30 and 40 customers solved exactly none has an optimum below its\n   246\t$0.01$ floor.\n   247\t\n   248\tThe same first moment appears in \\citet{Gao2012} for random intersection\n   249\tgraphs, with treewidth in place of pathwidth. His proof drops the factor\n   250\tcorresponding to the product above between two lines, which is harmless when\n   251\tthe number of patterns is sublinear in $n$ and changes the constant when it is\n   252\tlinear; his threshold of two patterns per customer is the dropped factor's\n   253\tartefact, and with it restored the condition at $m=n$ is about $2.46$.\n   254\t\n   255\t% The excess subsection (loop0010 item 12, 2026-11-01). Source:\n   256\t% reports/excess_phase_diagram.md (the opening and sections 1-11); Lean:\n   257\t% ForestCover.lean, TreeBound.lean, IndependenceBound.lean. The figure is\n   258\t% paper1/figures/excess_phase_diagram.pdf from\n   259\t% paper1/latex/figures/excess_phase_diagram.py (its CSV is committed under\n   260\t% data/); the table is tables/excess_beta.tex from make_tables.py.\n   261\t\\subsection{The excess, and a phase diagram}\n   262\t\\label{app:excess}\n   263\t\n   264\tThe quantity that ends the previous subsection deserves a name and an axis.\n   265\tFor an instance with $n$ customers, $m$ patterns and $N$ ones in the table,\n   266\tthe \\emph{excess} is $e=(N-m)/n$, the mean number of patterns per customer\n   267\tminus one. With $r=m/n$ and $c=N/m$ the mean customers per pattern,\n   268\t$e=r(c-1)$; and if the customer--pattern incidence graph $B$, with a node per\n   269\tcustomer and per pattern and an edge per one in the table, has $\\kappa$\n   270\tcomponents, it has $n(e-1)+\\kappa$ independent cycles, so $B$ can be a forest\n   271\tonly when $e<1$. We read $e$ from the table, never from a generator's nominal\n   272\tparameters, whose repairs inflate it. Three values of $e$ carry a meaning.\n   273\tThe constant $\\beta^{*}(d,\\mu)$ is positive exactly when $\\mu(d-1)>1$, that\n   274\tis when $e>1$ (on paper, from the exponent of~\\leanref{clique-cover}). On\n   275\trandom covers of 15 to 75 customers at every ratio $m/n$ from $1/8$ to $2$,\n   276\tthe nodes our search spends to refute $\\mathrm{optimum}-1$ peak at $e=2$ to\n   277\t$2.4$, falling by two orders of magnitude on either side (measured). And at\n   278\t$e_g(r)=(\\sqrt{r^{2}+4r}-r)/2$, $0.62$ for $m=n$, the branching factor of the\n   279\trandom incidence graph is one: on generated covers of 10 to 75 customers the\n   280\tshare of instances whose $B$ is a forest falls from $0.74$--$0.92$ below\n   281\t$e_g(r)$ to $0.23$--$0.40$ between $e_g(r)$ and $1$, and is zero above\n   282\t(measured). Figure~\\ref{fig:excess} places every instance of\n   283\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} against the three. Below $e_g(r)$ the open stacks\n   284\tare governed by the largest pattern (Theorem~\\ref{thm:blowup}); between\n   285\t$e_g(r)$ and $1$ the first moment says nothing and the deterministic bounds\n   286\tare all there is; between $1$ and the ridge the certified optimum per\n   287\tcustomer sits $0.02$--$0.10$ above $\\beta^{*}$ and the cost of certification\n   288\tclimbs, and this is where the $d=2$ classes of Section~\\ref{sec:results}\n   289\t($e=1.66$--$1.78$) and 22 of the 24 industrial instances sit; above the ridge\n   290\tthe cost falls and $\\beta^{*}$ comes within $0.04$--$0.06$ of the certified\n   291\tslope at $e\\ge6$ ($m=n$, 30--100 customers), the $d=4$ classes\n   292\t($e=2.9$--$3.7$) being the dense shoulder. No benchmark instance we hold has\n   293\t$e<e_g(r)$, and 12 of $6{,}376$ have $e<1$.\n   294\t\n   295\t\\paragraph{The forest regime.}\n   296\tWrite $\\omega$ for the size of the largest pattern.\n   297\t\\begin{theorem}[blow-up]\n   298\t\\label{thm:blowup}\n   299\tFor every instance, $\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\,(\\pw(B)+1)$. If $B$ is a forest,\n   300\t$\\mathrm{MOSP}\\le\\omega\\bigl(\\lfloor\\log_{3}\\tfrac{6(n+m)+3}{5}\\rfloor+1\\bigr)$.\n   301\t\\end{theorem}\n   302\tReplace each pattern node in each bag of a path decomposition of $B$ by the\n   303\tpattern's customers: a customer's nodes in $B$ form a star, so its bags stay\n   304\tan interval, and no bag grows by more than a factor $\\omega$. Nothing about\n   305\t$B$ is used; the forest enters only through the pathwidth of a forest on $N$\n   306\tvertices, at most $\\lfloor\\log_{3}((6N+3)/5)\\rfloor$, which is sharp. Both are\n   307\tLean theorems~\\leanref{blowup}~\\leanref{forest}. The bound is loose: on 7,004\n   308\tgraphs with exact pathwidth (clique trees to 936 vertices, random block trees,\n   309\t3,448 certified random covers whose $B$ is a forest, and 2,000 small random\n   310\tinstances) it is never violated and never exact, 2 to 5.5 times the truth on\n   311\tthe block-graph families; on the 3,448 random forests the pathwidth is\n   312\t$\\omega-1$ on $99.8\\%$, so below $e_g(r)$ the optimum is, in practice, the\n   313\tlargest pattern (measured). The exact theory on block graphs is polynomial\n   314\t\\citep{ChouKoHoChen2008}; on clique trees the exact values grow by about\n   315\t$(\\omega-2)/2$ per level of depth, no formula linear in $\\omega$ and the depth\n   316\tfits them, and we conjecture no closed form.\n   317\t\n   318\t\\begin{lemma}[pendant cliques]\n   319\t\\label{lem:pendant}\n   320\tIf a clique $R$ has $q\\ge1$ cliques of size at least $t$ pendant at distinct\n   321\tvertices of $R$, each meeting $R$ in its attachment vertex only, then\n   322\t$\\pw\\ge t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-2$.\n   323\t\\end{lemma}\n   324\tEvery clique lies inside one bag; if a pendant's bag is the bag of $R$ the two\n   325\tshare a bag of size $|R|+t-1$, and otherwise at least $\\lceil q/2\\rceil$\n   326\tpendants have their bags on one side of $R$'s, and the one nearest $R$ holds\n   327\tits own clique together with the attachment vertices of the others, by the\n   328\tinterval property. It is a Lean theorem~\\leanref{pendant}, and with\n   329\t$t=q=|R|$ it gives the exact value $\\lceil 3s/2\\rceil-2$ of a clique $K_s$\n   330\twith a pendant $K_s$ at every vertex, the depth-one clique tree; with the\n   331\tmatching decomposition (on paper), $K_s$ with a pendant $K_t$ at $q$ of its\n   332\tvertices has pathwidth exactly $\\max(s-1,\\,t+\\lceil q/2\\rceil-2)$. It is a\n   333\tpathwidth argument that is not degree-based, and on the certified instances\n   334\tit is worth little: evaluated over the maximal cliques of the MOSP graph it is\n   335\tabove the clique floor $\\omega-1$ on 554 of the $6{,}376$ and tight on\n   336\t$44\\%$, but above the solver's bound on 5, and above $\\max(\\text{solver\n   337\tbound},\\,\\mathrm{tw}+1)$ on 3 of the 338 instances where that bound misses by\n   338\ttwo or more, by one stack each (measured, 9--134 customers).\n   339\t\n   340\t\\paragraph{Constructions from above.}\n   341\t\\begin{lemma}[layers]\n   342\t\\label{lem:layer}\n   343\tIf $L_0,L_1,\\dots$ are the distance layers from any vertex of a connected\n   344\tgraph, $\\pw\\le\\max_i(|L_i|+|L_{i+1}|)-1$; and $\\pw\\le n-\\alpha$ for any\n   345\tindependent set of $\\alpha$ vertices.\n   346\t\\end{lemma}\n   347\tThe bags are $L_i\\cup L_{i+1}$, and $(V\\setminus I)\\cup\\{v\\}$ for $v\\in\n   348\tI$~\\leanref{layer}~\\leanref{indep}. On the uniform cover model with $m=\\mu n$\n   349\tpatterns of size $d$ the layer sizes follow a two-type exploration recursion\n   350\tand the greedy independent set that takes the customer with fewest patterns\n   351\tfollows a differential equation; the smaller limit is a constant\n   352\t$\\beta^{+}(d,\\mu)$, derived on paper up to the concentration step, which we\n   353\tcite and do not re-prove. Table~\\ref{tab:excess} gives it beside $\\beta^{*}$\n   354\tand the slope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on the certified instances at\n   355\t30--100 customers. The sandwich\n   356\t$\\beta^{*}\\le\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n\\le\\beta^{+}$ has\n   357\twidth below $0.12$ at $e\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\le e\\le3.5$: it says\n   358\tleast where the search works hardest. The greedy closing rule we use for\n   359\tupper bounds cannot be analysed this way; at 5,000 customers its width per\n   360\tcustomer is $0.06$--$0.36$ below $\\beta^{+}$ (measured, no exact solve).\n   361\t\n   362\t\\begin{table}[htbp]\n   363\t\\centering\n   364\t\\caption{Random covers with $m=n$ patterns of $d$ customers: the excess, the\n   365\tfirst-moment constant (a proved lower bound on $\\liminf\\mathrm{opt}/n$), the\n   366\tslope $b$ of $\\mathrm{opt}=a+bn$ on 600--625 certified instances at 30--100\n   367\tcustomers (measured; one standard error in parentheses), and the constant of\n   368\tthe analysed layouts (an upper bound on $\\limsup\\mathrm{opt}/n$, on paper up\n   369\tto concentration).}\n   370\t\\label{tab:excess}\n   371\t\\small\n   372\t\\input{tables/excess_beta}\n   373\t\\end{table}\n   374\t\n   375\t\\paragraph{Why the first moment is loose.}\n   376\tA layout of width $k$ forces $\\ell$ nested empty pairs, one per prefix size,\n   377\tbut counting them gives a constant \\emph{below} $\\beta^{*}$ on every cell (by\n   378\t$0.051$ at $\\ell=2$, $d=6$, $\\mu=1$, and more for larger $\\ell$, at every\n   379\tspacing): naming $\\varepsilon n$ more vertices costs\n   380\t$\\Theta(\\varepsilon\\log(1/\\varepsilon))$ of entropy and buys\n   381\t$\\Theta(\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\n   382\tempty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n   383\t$\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\n   384\tcondition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to\n   385\tthree stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a\n   386\tcount over one representative per cluster is the route not taken.\n   387\t\n   388\t\\paragraph{One parameter, or two.}\n   389\tThe excess sets the threshold, the ridge and the scale, not the value. At\n   390\t$e=2$ the certified slope is $0.282$, $0.232$, $0.200$, $0.167$ for\n   391\t$\\mu=1/4,1/2,1,2$ (standard errors $0.002$--$0.006$), and 13 of 16 pairs of\n   392\tseries matched in $e$ to $\\pm0.05$ differ by more than twice their error;\n   393\t$\\beta^{*}$ has the same dependence ($0.180$, $0.169$, $0.147$, $0.123$), and\n   394\tover 41 series $b=0.057+0.984\\,\\beta^{*}$ with residual $0.024$. Nor is the\n   395\tridge where the first moment is loosest: on 25 series at 50--75 customers\n   396\tthe gap between the certified optimum and the floor peaks at $e=2.2$--$5.3$,\n   397\ton the dense side, with the ridge on a plateau within one stack of the\n   398\tmaximum. What peaks on the ridge is the number of closed sets the search can\n   399\treach, not the slack of the bound.\n   400\t\n   401\t\\paragraph{Twenty-four industrial instances.}\n   402\tThe 24 SCOOP instances \\citep{Frinhani2018} have excess $0.97$ to $2.50$,\n   403\tmedian $1.35$; 19 lie in $[1,2)$, none has a forest $B$, ten decompose. Every\n   404\trefutation on record for them took at most $22{,}433$ nodes, and the greedy\n   405\tclosing rule is exact on 12 and at most $0.061n$ above the optimum. One, with\n   406\t60 customers, 49 patterns and optimum 7, sits two stacks below the floor a\n   407\trandom cover with its pattern sizes has at confidence $0.01$: no\n   408\tcontradiction, the floor being a random-model statement, but a measure of\n   409\tdistance from random, on which the designed Challenge instances sit up to 39\n   410\tstacks below. These are 24 instances from one source, read with pieces as\n   411\tcustomers (the orientation of the files' format line, the opposite of their\n   412\tdescription's prose); we say nothing about industry beyond them.\n   413\t\n   414\t\\begin{figure}[htbp]\n   415\t\\centering\n   416\t\\includegraphics[width=0.96\\linewidth]{excess_phase_diagram.pdf}\n   417\t\\caption{The excess phase diagram. Above, where the instances of\n   418\tSection~\\ref{sec:results} sit: the 24 SCOOP instances; the Chu \\& Stuckey\n   419\tclasses with $m=n$ as the median of each density over all sizes; the median\n   420\tand quartiles of the other collections. Dashed, $e_g(1)=0.62$; solid, $e=1$;\n   421\tthe band, the ridge $e=2$--$2.4$. Below, random covers with $m=n$ patterns of\n   422\t$d=2$ to $10$ customers: $\\beta^{*}$ (a theorem), the certified slope $b$ at\n   423\t30--100 customers with its two-standard-error bar (measured), and $\\beta^{+}$\n   424\tof the analysed layouts; in grey, $b$ at $m=n/2$ and $m=2n$, which differ from\n   425\tit at equal excess. Regenerate:\n   426\t\\texttt{paper1/latex/figures/excess\\_phase\\_diagram.py}.}\n   427\t\\label{fig:excess}\n   428\t\\end{figure}\n   429\t\n   430\t\\subsection{What is checked, and what is known}\n   431\t\n   432\tSteps 1, 2, 3 and 4 and the two inequalities of Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}\n   433\tare Lean theorems with the statement exactly as printed, including the\n   434\tconstant~3; so is the consequence that the probability tends to zero whenever\n   435\t$\\delta(\\beta,c)>0$. The entropy bound on trinomial coefficients is not in\n   436\tMathlib and is proved there from the binomial theorem. Theorem~\\ref{thm:betastar}\n   437\tis proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\n   438\tby two programs written independently.\n   439\t\n   440\tThe argument is published twice in cruder form. \\citet{Turner1986} used the\n   441\tsame empty pair, with the count $(en/k)^{2k}$ in place of the entropy, to\n   442\tprove that almost every $G(n,p)$ with $p$ fixed has bandwidth at least\n   443\t$n-4\\log_{1/(1-p)}n$; since bandwidth is at least pathwidth, our dense\n   444\tstatement implies his and not conversely. \\citet{DiazPetitSernaTrevisan2001}\n   445\tused it for vertex separation on mixing graphs, with a $3^{n}$ count and\n   446\tChernoff slack at a single position, which gives no positive constant below\n   447\t$c\\approx227$. Neither states the entropy form, a value of $\\beta^{*}(c)$, the\n   448\tthreshold $4\\ln2$ or the asymptotics, and we found no later work that does.\n   449\t\n   450\tOne computation says how far the finite inequality is from the truth. On\n   451\t133 random graphs with 60 vertices and $c=10$ whose pathwidth we computed\n   452\texactly, the smallest pathwidth is 25, while Theorem~\\ref{thm:firstmoment}'s\n   453\tfirst inequality, evaluated exactly rather than through Step~3, puts the\n   454\tprobability of pathwidth at most 23 below $0.35$ and of at most 21 below\n   455\t$10^{-3}$. The gap between $\\beta^{*}(c)$ and the slopes fitted to such data\n   456\tis of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a count over one representative per cluster of empty\n   457\tpairs (Appendix~\\ref{app:excess}), not a sharper estimate of this one.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011thfRLbuj1bheRHk5EqZwf","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
