e maximiser is binomial) | improved `i_E(Δ)` for `Δ ≥ 4`, Table 1 (§1.4 here); Thm 2: Bollobás's `√(ln 2)` is not the right coefficient | pp. 3–8 |\n61\t| Lichev–Mitsche 2023 | bisection width of random 3-regular; configuration model | minimum bisections with a structural characterisation (2-cores, forbidden winning sets, two types), then **graphs** containing such a bisection, divided by an overcounting factor (Lemma 5.6; \"we may divide by the corresponding overcounting factor\", p. 4) | over skeletons and labellings | configuration-model exact | `0.103295n ≤ bw ≤ 0.139822n` (Thm 1.1); type-one bisections `≤ 0.1069n` excluded by the plain structural count (§4), the overcount division is what the binding type-two case needs (§5) | pp. 1–4, 24–38 |\n62\t| Díaz–Serna–Wormald 2003/2007; Díaz–Diner–Serna–Serra 2022/2024 | bisection width; vertex bisection width; random `d`-regular | **not first moments**: priority-greedy and ball-growing algorithms analysed by the differential-equation method | — | — | upper bounds; bisection `b/n = 0.5028, 0.6674, 0.8502, 1.0386, 1.2317, 1.4278` at `d = 5, …, 10` (DSW Table 1); vertex bisection `α` (fraction of the red half with a neighbour across) `0.581, 0.610, 0.657, 0.656, 0.720, 0.881, 0.838` by the DEM and `0.466, 0.559, 0.626, 0.679, 0.721, 0.754, 0.778` experimentally at `d = 4, …, 10`, against KW's `0.290, 0.409, 0.502, 0.575, 0.632, 0.677, 0.714` (DDSS Table 2, p. 16) | DSW pp. 7–9; DDSS pp. 15–17 |\n63\t| Coja-Oghlan–Cooley–Kang–Skubch 2017 | bisection width; planted bisection `G(n, p₊, p₋)`, `d₊ − d₋ ≥ c√(d₊ ln d₊)` | **not a first moment**: `n^{−1} bis(G) → φ(p*)`, the skewed fixed point of Warning Propagation on a two-type Galton–Watson tree (Thm 1.1) | — | — | the exact constant, as a fixed point | pp. 3–5 |\n64\t| Dembo–Montanari–Sen 2017 | min/max bisection, max-cut; `G(n, [γn])` and `γ`-regular, `γ → ∞` | **not a first moment**: interpolation to the Sherrington–Kirkpatrick model | — | — | `mcut/n = γ/2 − P*√(γ/2) + o_γ(√γ)` (Thm 1.2; `γ/4 − P*√(γ/4)` regular, Thm 1.5), `P* = 0.76321 ± 0.00003` by Parisi's formula | pp. 1–4 |\n65\t| Bezrukov et al. 2004 | bisection width; deterministic (level structure of a bisection, Laplacian `λ₂`) | — | — | — | `bw ≥ 0.082n` for 3-regular and `0.176n` for 4-regular Ramanujan graphs; no random-graph statement (it misquotes Kostochka–Melnikov's `i_E(3) ≥ 1/4.95` as a bisection width `0.202n`, p. 158; Lichev–Mitsche give the bisection form `0.10101n`) | pp. 156–158 |\n66\t| Feige–Hajiaghayi–Lee 2008 | vocabulary only | a vertex separator is a triple `(A, B, S)`, balanced if `|A|, |B| ≤ 2|V|/3`; its sparsity `π(S)/(min(π(A), π(B)) + π(S))` | — | — | `O(√log n)` approximation; treewidth `O(k√log k)` | pp. 1–3 |\n67\t\n68\tTwo counts that *are* over separators, for the record: Do–Erde–Kang's,\n69\tabove, and the deterministic reduction every treewidth count rests on —\n70\tRobertson & Seymour's `tw ≥ sep_{½}(G) − 1`, where a `(k, ½)`-separator is\n71\ta `k`-set `S` with **every component of `G − S` of size at most half of\n72\t`|V ∖ S|`** (DEK Lemma 2.1, with Lemma 2.2 turning it into a `⅓–⅔`\n73\tbalanced partition). That is exactly this loop's relaxed event `E_relax`,\n74\tin the treewidth literature since 1986 as the thing a separator must\n75\tsatisfy; what is counted there is still the partition.\n76\t\n77\t### 1.3 Table B — the rates\n78\t\n79\t| statement | rate function, hypotheses | where | the corollary this loop needs |\n80\t|---|---|---|---|\n81\t| **The largest component of `G(n, c_n/n)`, `c_n → c > 0`, satisfies an LDP at scale `n`** on `[0, 1]`: `P(β^n/n ≈ u) = e^{−n I_c^β(u)(1+o(1))}`, with `I_c^β` a good rate function. In the form O'Connell gives it (Puhalskii's remark after Cor. 2.5): `I_c^β(u) = k K_c(u) + L_c(ku)` for `u ∈ [x_k, x_{k−1}]`, `x₀ = 1`, `x_k` the root of `x/(1 − e^{−cx}) = 1 − kx`, `K_ρ(u) = u log(ρu/(1 − e^{−ρu})) − ρu²/2`, `L_c(u) = (1 − u) log(1 − u) + (c − log c)u − cu²/2` (his (2.13)–(2.14)); `I_c^β(0) = L_c((1 − 1/c)⁺)`. Not convex. The most probable configuration with largest component `nu`: one giant if `u ≥ 1 − 1/c`, otherwise `⌊(1 − 1/c)/u⌋` components of size `≈ nu` plus one more. | Puhalskii 2005, Cor. 2.4(3) (p. 8 of the arXiv version), attributed to O'Connell 1998 Thm 3.1 \"which has a misprint\"; Thm 2.1 is the joint LDP for the number of components, all giant sizes and their excess edges; Cor. 2.2 the LDP for the sequence of giant sizes `U^n/n` in `S₁` with `I_c^U(u) = Σ K_c(u_i) + L_c(1 − (1/c) ∨ Σ u_i)` | **Yes, with the exact statement**: `[0, ½]` is closed, so `lim sup (1/N) log P(largest component of G(N, c′/N) ≤ N/2) ≤ −inf_{u ≤ ½} I_{c′}^β(u)`. Checked numerically on the quoted formula (`rate_check.log`): `I` vanishes at the law-of-large-numbers point `u* = 1 − ρ` to `10⁻¹⁶` at every `c` tried, and `inf_{u ≤ ½} I = I(½) > 0` **iff `c′ > 2 ln 2`** (`u* = ½` exactly at `c′ = 2 ln 2 = 1.3863`). Values of `I_{c′}^β(½)`: `0.036, 0.144, 0.183, 0.380, 0.595, 0.832, 1.316, 1.810` at `c′ = 2, 2.772, 3, 4, 4.98, 6, 8, 10`. So **the relaxed separator count has threshold `2 ln 2`**, as the plan predicted conditionally on this result — now a cited one, not a conjecture, up to the LDP being asymptotic |\n82\t| **All cluster sizes**: the pair (microscopic empirical measure `Mi^{(N)}`, macroscopic point measure `Ma^{(N)}`) of `G(N, t_N/N)`, `t_N → t`, satisfies an LDP at speed `N` with rate `I(Λ, α; t) = I_Mi(Λ; t) + I_Ma(α; t) + (1 − c_Λ − c_α)(t/2 − log t)`, `I_Mi(Λ; t) = Σ_k λ_k log(k! t λ_k/(e k^{k−2})) + c_Λ(1 + t/2 − log t)`, `I_Ma(α; t) = ∫ [x log(x/(1 − e^{−tx})) + (t/2)x(1 − x)] α(dx)` (Thm 1.1); contractions: the microscopic part (Cor. 1.2; always optimal to put the rest in one giant), the macroscopic part (Cor. 1.3), the mesoscopic total mass `J_Me(c; t) = (1 − c) log((1 − c)t) − (1 − c)t/2 + t/2 − log t` (Cor. 1.4), the total-mass phase transition with `J_Mi(c; t)` explicit and `J_Ma(c; t) = J_Mi(1 − c; t)` (Thm 1.5) | Andreis–König–Patterson 2021, §1.2–1.3 (pp. 4–8 of the preprint); §1.4 says O'Connell's two LDPs are contractions of theirs and that Puhalskii covers the macroscopic part by a different route, \"it would require some work to analytically check that it is identical to ours\" | The event `E_bip` is a variational problem over `(Λ, α)` under the constraint that the atoms of `α` plus the microscopic mass can be grouped into sides of sizes `t` and `N − t`; with microscopic components of sizes 1 and 2 at positive density under the tilt (the minimiser's `Λ*` is Borel, (1.13)) the exact-size grouping should cost nothing at the exponential scale once no atom exceeds `½` — **a plausible reading, not a theorem, and item 06's job**. If it holds, the rate of `E_bip` equals that of `E_relax` |\n83\t| Random intersection graphs: with `m = βn` auxiliary vertices and incidence probability `γ/n` (`α = 1`), mean degree `μ = βγ²`; `μ > 1` gives a unique giant of size `(1 − ρ + o_p(1))n`, `ρ` the least root of `ρ = g(ρ)`, `g(s) = exp(βγ(e^{γ(s−1)} − 1))` (compound Poisson offspring), second largest `O(log n)` (Thm 2); with `m = n^α`, `α ≠ 1`, `c = p²mn`: giant `(1 − ρ)n` for `α > 1`, `(1 − ρ)√(cmn)` for `α < 1`, `ρ = e^{c(ρ−1)}` (Behrisch Thm 1) | Lagerås–Lindholm 2008 pp. 2–3; Behrisch 2007 pp. 2–3 | **Laws of large numbers only; no LDP for the giant of a random intersection graph or of a random clique cover was found.** Item 07 has the giant's typical size (the onset line) from these, and must derive or compute the lower tail itself |\n84\t| Bisection width of `G(n, c/n)` is `0` whp for `c < ln 4` and `Ω(n)` for `c > ln 4`: the giant exceeds `n/2` iff `c > 2 ln 2` | Luczak–McDiarmid 2001 (not held), as restated by Lichev–Mitsche p. 2, Coja-Oghlan et al. p. 4, Dembo–Montanari–Sen p. 1 | the same threshold as the relaxed separator count's, for the same reason; their technique (a spanning tree bounds the cuts per separator) is the one Do–Erde–Kang reuse |\n85\t\n86\t### 1.4 Table C — representative and locally optimal counts, and what they bought\n87\t\n88\t| source | what is merged / restricted | parameter, model | constant before → after | in the units of the gap to the truth or to the best upper bound |\n89\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n90\t| Gao 2012 | `d`-rigid balanced partitions: partitions from which no small component of the larger side can be moved (Def. 3.2, Lemma 3.1); the rigidity probability by Hoeffding–Azuma on a weighted tree-component count (Thm 4) | treewidth threshold, `G(n, m)` | `m/n`: 1.18 (Kloks) → 1.083 (the same count, refined analysis) → **1.073**; in `c = 2m/n`: 2.166 → 2.146 | the truth is `c = 1` (Lee–Lee–Oum): the rigidity restriction moved the threshold by 0.020 of a gap of 1.166, **1.7%** |\n91\t| Lampis 2012 | locally optimal sets: every vertex has at most `Δ/2` neighbours across, so the out-degree profiles are truncated (§4) | edge expansion `i_E(Δ)`, random `Δ`-regular | Bollobás → Lampis (Table 1): `Δ = 4`: 0.440 → 0.444; 5: 0.730 → 0.7475; 6: 1.041 → 1.056; 7: 1.372 → 1.4035; 8: 1.716 → 1.740; 9: 2.0655 → 2.1105; 10: 2.430 → 2.465; 20: 6.320 → 6.4; 30: 10.47 → 10.59; 40: 14.74 → 14.9 — \"modest (of the order of 10⁻² for Δ ≤ 10 and 10⁻¹ for somewhat larger Δ)\" and growing with `Δ` (p. 8); Thm 2: the asymptotic coefficient of `√Δ` is strictly below Bollobás's `√(ln 2) = 0.833`, not computed | against the best upper bounds `i_E ≤ 2b(G)` from DDSW 2003 / DSW 2007 (`2/3, 1.0056, 1.3348, 1.7004` at `Δ = 4, 5, 6, 7`, KW p. 5): **1.8%, 6.3%, 5.1%, 9.6%** of the gap; asymptotically the truth is `Δ/2 − P*√Δ` with `P* = 0.7632` (Dembo–Montanari–Sen) against Bollobás's 0.833, and how much of that 0.07 Lampis's `α < 2 ln 2` recovers is not stated |\n92\t| Lichev–Mitsche 2023 | structure of a minimum bisection (2-cores, no winning sets, two types) and, for the binding type, counting *graphs* rather than bisections by dividing by the number of bisections of the same size one auxiliary graph yields (Lemma 5.6) | bisection width of random 3-regular | plain first moment (Bollobás) `n/11 = 0.0909n` → Kostochka–Melnikov `0.10101n` (edge weighting, not a first moment) → **`0.103295n`**; the type-one structural count alone reaches `0.1069n` on its own case, the type-two case with the division is what sets the constant | against their upper bound `0.139822n`: **25%** of the gap over the plain first moment, **6%** over Kostochka–Melnikov; the truth is unknown (the Zdeborová–Boettcher conjecture would put it at the max-cut complement, which \"our paper does not shed light on\", p. 2) |\n93\t| Do–Erde–Kang 2024 | separators `S` of a spanning tree's vertex set, `≤ 2^{Δ(T)|S|}` balanced separations each | treewidth threshold, `G(n, c/n)` | 2.146 (Gao) → **every `c > 1`**, i.e. the whole of the threshold gap, with the constant `Ω(ε³/log(1/ε))` | the only count in this table that reached the true threshold; it buys the threshold and not a constant |\n94\t| Kolesnik–Wormald 2014 | nothing merged: the direct first moment with the exact inner count; they note it is best possible for a direct application (p. 10) | vertex isoperimetric number, random `d`-regular | — | the gap it leaves at `d = 4`: lower 0.290 against upper 0.466 (experimental) / 0.581 (DEM); DDSS p. 17: \"the union bound involved in the computation is likely to weaken the strength of the lower bounds, which we believe have room for improvement\" |\n95\t| Gao 2006 | \"a new combinatorial construct\" | treewidth threshold | 2.36 → 2.162 | the construct is not in the held two pages; not read |\n96\t\n97\tSummary of the column that matters: where a representative or restricted\n98\tcount was applied to a first-moment cut constant, it moved the constant by\n99\t**1.7% of the gap** (rigidity, treewidth threshold), **2–10%** (local\n100\toptimality, edge expansion at `Δ = 4–7`, growing with `Δ`), and **6–25%**\n101\t(a full structural characterisation plus an overcount division, cubic\n102\tbisection, against a loosely known truth). The one count that closed a gap\n103\tentirely — Do–Erde–Kang's separator count — closed a *threshold*, by a\n104\tdeterministic bound on the bipartitions per separator, and gave up the\n105\tconstant to do it.\n106\t\n107\t### 1.5 The brief's question, in the brief's terms\n108\t\n109\t*\"The new idea is a better estimate of the inner probability, not a new\n110\tobject\" — is that so, or does someone already have the exact inner\n111\tprobability for a cut-type first moment?* Both halves need qualifying.\n112\tThe **object** — sum over separators `S`, with the bipartitions of\n113\t`G − S` counted inside — is in Do, Erde & Kang (2024), following Luczak &\n114\tMcDiarmid, with the inner count bounded by `2^{Δ(T)|S|}` and the inner\n115\tprobability by sprinkling; it is not new, and it is in print as a device\n116\tfor thresholds. The **exact inner probability** for a cut-type first\n117\tmoment is in Kolesnik & Wormald (2014) for a single vertex cut in the\n118\tconfiguration model (the set, its vertex boundary and its edge boundary\n119\tcounted exactly up to polynomial factors), and in Bollobás (1988) for\n120\tedge cuts; neither is a probability about the *rest* of the graph, and\n121\tneither is on `G(n, c/n)`. The inner probability of this loop's *relaxed*\n122\tevent, `P(G(N, c′/N) has no component above N/2)`, is known exactly at the\n123\texponential scale as a corollary of the LDP for the largest component\n124\t(O'Connell 1998; Puhalskii 2005 Cor. 2.4(3); Andreis–König–Patterson 2021\n125\tCor. 1.3), and we found no width or cut first moment that uses it. The\n126\tinner probability of the bipartition event `E_bip`, the closed-pair count\n127\tand the weighted count, for pathwidth or any width, we found no statement\n128\tof. So: the object is known with a crude inner bound; the relaxed inner\n129\trate is known in a different literature; their combination, and the finer\n130\tevents, are what the loop computes. \"We found no prior statement\" is the\n131\twording, and it is the strongest allowed.\n132\t\n133\t### 1.6 What items 05–08 take from this\n134\t\n135\t1. **Compare, do not re-derive, the relaxed count's rate.** Item 05's\n136\t   `O(N²)` DP for `E_relax` to `N = 400` has an exact asymptotic answer to\n137\t   check against: `I_{c′}^β(½)` from the quoted formula, zero iff\n138\t   `c′ ≤ 2 ln 2`. If the DP's fitted rate disagrees with the LDP's by more\n139\t   than the finite-size correction the DP itself shows, one of the two is\n140\t   wrong, and the quoted formula carries a known misprint risk\n141\t   (Puhalskii says O'Connell's Theorem 3.1 has one; the form used here is\n142\t   Puhalskii's restatement, checked only by its zero at the\n143\t   law-of-large-numbers point, `rate_check.log`).\n144\t2. **The planner's two routes were both the wrong route** (plan §0,\n145\t   correction 3). At `c = 6`, `c′ = 4.98`, the plan bracketed the rate of\n146\t   `E_relax` by the fixed-bipartition route `c′/4 = 1.245` and the global\n147\t   thinning route `1.19`; the LDP says the rate is **0.595**, half of\n148\t   either, and above the pair count's own `c′/4 − ln 2 = 0.552` as it must\n149\t   be. The cheapest way for `G(N, 4.98/N)` to have no component above\n150\t   `N/2` is neither a planted bipartition nor uniform thinning but the\n151\t   large-deviation tilt the rate function describes (Puhalskii: for\n152\t   `u < 1 − 1/c` the most probable configuration has several giants of\n153\t   size `≈ nu`). Items 05–06 should not fit a rate to those two routes.\n154\t3. **A preview of `β_relax(c)`, from the quotation alone, to be replaced\n155\t   by item 06** (`rate_preview.log`; the relaxed separator count\n156\t   `P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ C(n, βn) e^{−(1−β) I_{c(1−β)}^β(½) n + o(n)}`, root of\n157\t   `H(β) − (1 − β) I_{c(1−β)}^β(½)`): `β_relax = 0.0056, 0.0185, 0.0279,\n158\t   0.0368, 0.0827, 0.1328, 0.1817, 0.2690, 0.3409, 0.3998, 0.5542` at\n159\t   `c = 2, 2.5, 2.772, 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10, 12, 20`, against\n160\t   `β* = 0, 0, 0, 0.0087, 0.0613, 0.1175, 0.1706, 0.2628, 0.3371, 0.3973,\n161\t   0.5535`: gains of **+0.028 at `c = 3`, +0.021 at 4, +0.011 at 6, +0.004\n162\t   at 10, +0.0007 at 20**, and a positive constant for every `c > 2 ln 2`.\n163\t   This is an asymptotic statement resting on a cited LDP's upper bound\n164\t   and on the relaxed event; it is below the brief's kill of 0.02 at\n165\t   `c = 6` and `10` and above it at `c ≤ 4`; it sits inside the planner's\n166\t   bracket `[0.009, 0.056]` at `c = 3` and at the top of the planner's\n167\t   `+0.004–0.010` at `c = 6–10`. `E_bip` can only be rarer than `E_relax`,\n168\t   so `β_sep ≥ β_relax` if the LDP is used as an upper bound on the inner\n169\t   probability; whether it is strictly larger is item 06's question.\n170\t4. **For covers (item 07) there is no LDP to quote.** Behrisch and\n171\t   Lagerås–Lindholm give the giant's typical size for Bernoulli-incidence\n172\t   random intersection graphs (the onset line, in their coordinates); the\n173\t   lower tail of the giant of a random clique cover with fixed pattern\n174\t   sizes has to be computed (the isolated-customer generating function the\n175\t   plan proposes) or derived from the AKP machinery, which is for `G(N,p)`.\n176\t5. **What representative counts have bought elsewhere** is 2–25% of a\n177\t   gap (§1.4). The brief's kill of `0.02` in `β` is, at `c = 6`, 0.02 of a\n178\t   gap of `0.268 − 0.171 = 0.097` to the fitted slope, i.e. 21%; at\n179\t   `c = 10`, 26% of `0.078`. By the literature's track record that is at\n180\t   the top of what a representative count has ever delivered, and item 09\n181\t   (the weighted count) should know it is aiming above the record.\n182\t6. **The exact inner count of Kolesnik–Wormald is the model for item 07's\n183\t   inclusion–exclusion**: the vertex boundary and the edge boundary of the\n184\t   counted set enter the first moment as separate variables with an exact\n185\t   count per profile, and the union bound runs over `O(n²)` profiles at no\n186\t   exponential cost. The closed-pair count (`S = N(A) ∩ N(R)`) is a\n187\t   vertex-boundary condition of the same kind on `G(n, c/n)`.\n188\t\n189\t### 1.7 Where the record corrects the item\n190\t\n191\t- Kolesnik & Wormald's arXiv number is **1311.6555** (the held file says\n192\t  so); the item's 1109.0689 is a different paper. The item's Puhalskii\n193\t  venue is wrong (*Ann. Probab.*, not *Stochastic Processes and their\n194\t  Applications*).\n195\t- \"**Díaz–Serna–Wormald's locally optimal bisections**\" do not exist: the\n196\t  2003/2007 paper is a priority-greedy *upper* bound by the\n197\t  differential-equation method, with no first moment and no local\n198\t  optimality. The locally optimal count the item has in mind is\n199\t  **Lampis 2012**, cited by Kolesnik–Wormald as the improvement on\n200\t  Bollobás \"by investigating subsets which are, in a certain sense,\n201\t  locally optimal\" (p. 5); fetched and tabulated above.\n202\t- The held **Kloks 1994 preview is chapter 12** (cocomparability graphs)\n203\t  and contains no random-graph statement; the item's instruction \"do not\n204\t  assert his inner probability from memory\" is followed, and the chapter\n205\t  is now on the missing list by name.\n206\t- Díaz–Diner–Serna–Serra 2022 is published (JCSS 2024, DOI above); cite\n207\t  the journal version.\n208\t- Bezrukov et al. 2004 is deterministic and spectral; it belongs in no\n209\t  column of Table A but the vocabulary one, and its one random-graph\n210\t  sentence misquotes Kostochka–Melnikov by a factor of two (§1.2).\n211\t\n212\t### 1.8 Kill verdict, and what this does not claim\n213\t\n214\t**Kill verdict: none** (the item has no kill criterion); the stop rule\n215\tdid not fire (no prior separator first moment with an exact inner\n216\tprobability for pathwidth or vertex separation of `G(n, c/n)`), so items\n217\t05–06 derive, and compare against the three nearest results named at the\n218\ttop of this section.\n219\t\n220\tNothing here is a theorem of ours, and no number here is a result of the\n221\tloop:\n222\t\n223\t- The threshold `2 ln 2` for the relaxed separator count, and the values\n224\t  of `β_relax(c)`, rest on a quoted rate function that has a documented\n225\t  misprint history, checked only by its zero at the typical point and\n226\t  by its sign; they are asymptotic (an LDP upper bound for a closed set),\n227\t  not finite-`n` inequalities, and they concern `E_relax`, not `E_bip`.\n228\t  Item 06 owns them.\n229\t- That the rate of `E_bip` equals that of `E_relax` is a reading of the\n230\t  AKP minimiser, not a proof.\n231\t- Priority is claimed for nothing. The strongest statement made is that\n232\t  (a) and (b) of the item were done and the combination was not found.\n233\t- Bollobás 1988, O'Connell 1998, Luczak–McDiarmid 2001 and the journal\n234\t  Díaz–Serna–Wormald 2007 were not read; every statement attributed to\n235\t  them is from the restatements named beside it.\n236\t- Lichev–Mitsche's §6 (the upper bound) and the middle of their §§3–5\n237\t  were skimmed, not read line by line; the numbers quoted are from their\n238\t  Theorem 1.1, §1.2 and the ends of §§4–5.\n239\t- The fractions of a gap in §1.4 use the best *upper bounds* where the\n240\t  truth is unknown (edge expansion at small `Δ`, cubic bisection), so they\n241\t  are lower bounds on the fraction of the true gap.\n242\t\n243\t---\n244\t\n245\t## 2. The deterministic lemmas: balanced separator, its relaxation, the closed pair (item 02, 2026-11-01)\n246\t\n247\t**Question (A of the brief).** Exactly which structures does\n248\t`pw(G) ≤ k < n` force, in a form a first moment can count once per\n249\tseparator? Three, each one line from `exists_empty_pair_of_layout`\n250\t(`EmptyPair.lean`), and each checked by brute force before being called a\n251\tlemma: on every graph on 1–9 vertices from `nauty-geng` (288,266 graphs),\n252\ton 20,000 random `G(n, p)` graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` and on 2,000 random\n253\tclique covers at 4–10 customers (§2.4). **Zero counterexamples.** The\n254\tkill criterion (a counterexample to L1, L1′ or L2) is **not met**.\n255\t\n256\tNotation, for the whole section: `G` is a graph on the vertex set `V`,\n257\t`|V| = n`; `N[X] = X ∪ N(X)` is the closed neighbourhood and\n258\t`N(X) = N[X] ∖ X` the *outer* boundary of `X`; a pair `(A, R)` of disjoint\n259\tsets is **empty** when no edge joins `A` to `R`, equivalently\n260\t`R ∩ N[A] = ∅`; `k` is an integer with `pw(G) ≤ k < n`,\n261\t`N = n − k`, `t = ⌈N/2⌉`, so that `t ≥ N − t ≥ 0` and `t ≥ 1`. Nothing in\n262\tthis section is probabilistic.\n263\t\n264\t### 2.1 L1 — the balanced separator at exact sizes\n265\t\n266\t**Lemma L1.** If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, there are pairwise disjoint `A, R, S`\n267\twith `A ∪ R ∪ S = V`, `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, `|S| = k` and no `A–R`\n268\tedge.\n269\t\n270\t*Proof.* (Layout step) By `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth` there is a\n271\tlayout `σ` with `vs(σ) = pw(G) ≤ k`. (Prefix step) Apply\n272\t`exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at position `i = t − 1`, which is `< n`\n273\tbecause `t ≤ N ≤ n`: the prefix `A` of size `t` and the inactive suffix\n274\t`R₀` are disjoint, no edge joins them, and\n275\t`n ≤ |A| + |R₀| + vs(σ) ≤ t + |R₀| + k`, i.e. `|R₀| ≥ N − t`\n276\t(`card_inactiveSuffix`). (Shrink step) Take `R ⊆ R₀` with `|R| = N − t`;\n277\tthe edge condition is universally quantified over `R₀`, so it holds on\n278\t`R`, and `R ⊆ R₀` is disjoint from `A`. (Complement step)\n279\t`S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` has `n − t − (N − t) = k` vertices and is disjoint from\n280\tboth. ∎\n281\t\n282\t*Remarks.* (i) The sizes are exact: `|R| = N − t = ⌊N/2⌋`, which is the\n283\titem's `n − t − k`. The Lean corollary `exists_empty_pair` rounds it to\n284\t`|R| ≥ t − 1`, which coincides with `N − t` for `N` odd and is one less\n285\tfor `N` even; the count on record (`FirstMoment.lean`,\n286\t`card_emptyPairIndices`) uses `|R| = t − 1`, and plan §0 correction 2 is\n287\tabout this rounding. L1 is the sharper form and is what §2.4 checks.\n288\t(ii) `S` is the active suffix at position `t − 1` padded to exactly `k`\n289\tvertices with vertices of `R₀`; which vertices are moved is immaterial,\n290\tand the lemma says nothing about `S` beyond its size — that is what L2\n291\tadds.\n292\t\n293\t### 2.2 L1′ — the separator events `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n294\t\n295\tFor a `k`-set `S ⊆ V` write `G − S` for the subgraph induced on\n296\t`V ∖ S` (which has `N` vertices) and define two events:\n297\t\n298\t    E_bip(S):   the components of G − S can be grouped into two classes\n299\t                whose vertex counts are t and N − t;\n300\t    E_relax(S): no component of G − S has more than t vertices.\n301\t\n302\t**Lemma L1′.** (a) If `pw(G) ≤ k < n`, some `k`-set `S` satisfies\n303\t`E_bip(S)`. (b) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` implies `E_relax(S)`.\n304\t(c) For every `k`-set `S`, `E_bip(S)` holds iff `G` has an exact-size\n305\tempty pair `(A, R)` (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge) with\n306\t`V ∖ (A ∪ R) = S`. Hence *\"some `k`-set is in `E_bip`\"* and *\"an\n307\texact-size empty pair exists\"* are the same event; the two counts `X₂`\n308\t(separators) and `X₁` (pairs) count it differently. (d) The inclusion in\n309\t(b) is strict: on the graph `3K₂` (six vertices, edges `03, 14, 25`) with\n310\t`k = 0`, `S = ∅` satisfies `E_relax` (components of sizes `2, 2, 2 ≤ t = 3`)\n311\tand not `E_bip` (no sub-family of `{2, 2, 2}` sums to `3`). This is the\n312\tfirst graph on six vertices in `nauty-geng` order with a `k`-set in\n313\t`E_relax ∖ E_bip` (`check.log`, first line); its pathwidth is `1`, so at\n314\t`k = pw` the two events agree on it.\n315\t\n316\t*Proof.* (a) Take `A, R, S` from L1. (Connectivity step) A component `C`\n317\tof `G − S` is connected and lies in `A ∪ R`; if it met both `A` and `R`,\n318\tsome edge of `C` — a path inside `C` from a vertex of `A` to a vertex of\n319\t`R` has a first `A–R` edge — would join `A` to `R`, contradicting\n320\temptiness. So `C ⊆ A` or `C ⊆ R`. (Grouping step) The components inside\n321\t`A` partition `A` and sum to `t`; those inside `R` sum to `N − t`.\n322\t(b) Each component lies in a class of size `t` or `N − t ≤ t`.\n323\t(c, ⇒) Let `A` be the union of the class of size `t` and `R` the union\n324\tof the other; they partition `V ∖ S`, have the right sizes, and an edge\n325\tof `G − S` joins two vertices of one component, hence of one class, so\n326\tno edge joins `A` to `R` (an `A–R` edge of `G` would have both ends\n327\toutside `S`, so it would be an edge of `G − S`). (c, ⇐) is (a)'s\n328\targument applied to the given pair. (d) is checked directly. ∎\n329\t\n330\t*Remark.* `E_relax` is the Lean-cheap and LDP-shaped form (plan §0,\n331\tcorrection 3; §1.6): it is a statement about component sizes only, and\n332\t`P_N(E_relax)` on `G(N, p)` is the lower tail of the largest component.\n333\t`E_bip` is the event the pair count actually witnesses, and items 05–06\n334\tcompute `P_N(E_bip)` from the full component-size partition, not from\n335\tthe largest component alone. On the graphs checked the two differ at\n336\t`k = pw` on `0.3%` of graphs at 7 vertices and `2.2%` at 9 (§2.4,\n337\tcolumn \"`E_relax ⊋ E_bip`\"), so at these sizes the relaxation is nearly\n338\tfree; nothing here says what it costs at the scale of the rate.\n339\t\n340\t### 2.3 L2 — the closed pair, by the alternating closure\n341\t\n342\t**Lemma L2.** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair with `|A| ≥ t`,\n343\t`|R| ≥ N − t` and `|V ∖ (A ∪ R)| ≤ k`. Put\n344\t\n345\t    R* = V ∖ N[A],      A* = V ∖ N[R*],      S* = V ∖ (A* ∪ R*).\n346\t\n347\tThen: (i) `A ⊆ A*` and `R ⊆ R*` (monotone); (ii) `(A*, R*)` is an empty\n348\tpair; (iii) `R* = V ∖ N[A*]` and `A* = V ∖ N[R*]` (a **closed pair**), so\n349\tthe alternating closure `R₁ = V ∖ N[A]`, `A₁ = V ∖ N[R₁]`, `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁]`,\n350\t`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂]`, … is constant from `(A₁, R₁) = (A*, R*)` on — it stops\n351\tafter **one round**, not after an unbounded iteration; (iv)\n352\t`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`; (v) `|A*| ≥ t`, `|R*| ≥ N − t`, `|S*| ≤ k`.\n353\tConversely, every closed pair `(A, R)` (one with `A = V ∖ N[R]` and\n354\t`R = V ∖ N[A]`) is empty with `V ∖ (A ∪ R) = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, and is\n355\tdetermined by either side.\n356\t\n357\t*Proof.* (Galois step) For any `X, Y ⊆ V`: `Y ⊆ V ∖ N[X]` iff `Y ∩ X = ∅`\n358\tand no edge joins `X` to `Y`, iff `X ⊆ V ∖ N[Y]`, by the symmetry of\n359\tadjacency; and `X ⊆ X′` implies `N[X] ⊆ N[X′]` (monotone step).\n360\t(i) `(A, R)` is empty, so `R ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*` by the Galois step.\n361\t`R* ∩ N[A] = ∅` by definition, so `(A, R*)` is empty and the Galois step\n362\tgives `A ⊆ V ∖ N[R*] = A*`. (ii) `A* ∩ N[R*] = ∅` by definition.\n363\t(iii) From (ii) and the Galois step, `R* ⊆ V ∖ N[A*]`; from `A ⊆ A*` and\n364\tthe monotone step, `V ∖ N[A*] ⊆ V ∖ N[A] = R*`. So `R* = V ∖ N[A*]`, and\n365\t`A* = V ∖ N[R*]` is the definition. Hence `R₂ = V ∖ N[A₁] = R₁` and\n366\t`A₂ = V ∖ N[R₂] = A₁`, and by induction every later term equals these.\n367\t(iv) `v ∈ S*` iff `v ∉ A*` and `v ∉ R*`; `v ∉ A* = V ∖ N[R*]` means\n368\t`v ∈ N[R*]`, which with `v ∉ R*` is `v ∈ N(R*)`; symmetrically\n369\t`v ∈ N(A*)`. Conversely a vertex of `N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` is outside `A*` and\n370\toutside `R*` by the definition of the outer boundary. (v) From (i),\n371\t`|A*| ≥ |A| ≥ t` and `|R*| ≥ |R| ≥ N − t`; then\n372\t`|S*| = n − |A*| − |R*| ≤ n − t − (N − t) = k`. The converse is (ii)–(iv)\n373\tread with `A* = A`, `R* = R`; \"determined by either side\" is\n374\t`R = V ∖ N[A]`. ∎\n375\t\n376\t*Remarks.* (i) **The fixpoint is a representative, not the\n377\trepresentative.** Closing `R` first gives `(V ∖ N[R], V ∖ N[V ∖ N[R]])`,\n378\twhich can differ from `(A*, R*)`: on the graphs checked the two maps\n379\tdisagree on `7–10%` of exact-size pairs at 7–9 vertices (§2.4, column\n380\t\"closures differ\"). A union bound over closed pairs needs only *a*\n381\tmap from exact-size pairs to closed pairs of the forced sizes, and L2\n382\tgives one (`A` first; the mirror is another); it does not need the map\n383\tto be canonical, and this document never says it is. (ii) The item\n384\texpected an iteration `A ⊆ A₁ ⊆ A₂ ⊆ …`; (iii) says the chain is\n385\t`A ⊆ A₁ = A₂ = …`, `R ⊆ R₁ = R₂ = …`. Monotonicity holds as asserted\n386\t(with equality from the second step), which is what the check verifies\n387\tat every step — the item's expectation is corrected, not contradicted.\n388\t(iii) Because every closed pair is determined by `A`, the closed pairs\n389\tof the forced sizes (`X₃` below) are enumerated exactly by running over\n390\tall `A ⊆ V`, setting `R = V ∖ N[A]` and testing `A = V ∖ N[R]` and the\n391\tsizes; no pair is reached twice. (iv) The image of the exact-size pairs\n392\tunder the `A`-first closure (`X₃′`) can be a proper subset of `X₃`: a\n393\tclosed pair of the forced sizes contains exact-size sub-pairs, but\n394\tclosing a sub-pair may land on a *larger* closed pair (its `V ∖ N[A′]`\n395\tcontains `V ∖ N[A]`). Both counts are valid first moments; item 03\n396\tmeasures both.\n397\t\n398\t### 2.4 The check, before any of the above was called a lemma\n399\t\n400\t`reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py` (log beside it),\n401\ton the functions of `learning/separator_moment.py` (tested in\n402\t`tests/test_separator_moment.py`, which also re-enumerates every\n403\tlabelled graph to 6 vertices and pins the subset DP to\n404\t`learning.first_moment.vertex_separation_brute` and\n405\t`fixed_parameter_algorithm.pathwidth.compute_pathwidth`). For each graph,\n406\t`k = pw(G)` by the subset DP; then\n407\t\n408\t* **L1**: `X₁ > 0`, where `X₁` is the number of ordered exact-size empty\n409\t  pairs (`|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`; for `N` even `(A, R)` and `(R, A)` are\n410\t  both counted);\n411\t* **L1′**: `X₂ > 0`, `X₂ ≤ X₂′`, and `X₁ > 0 ⟺ X₂ > 0`, where `X₂`, `X₂′`\n412\t  are the numbers of `k`-sets `S` in `E_bip` and `E_relax`, computed from\n413\t  the components of `G − S` and a subset-sum over their sizes;\n414\t* **L2**: from every exact-size pair (all of them when `X₁ ≤ 200`, the\n415\t  first 200 otherwise), the closure sequence is monotone at every step,\n416\t  the second round repeats the first, the fixpoint is closed, empty, of\n417\t  the forced sizes, with `S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)`, and lies in `X₃` (all\n418\t  closed pairs of the forced sizes, by the enumeration of remark (iii));\n419\t  the share of pairs whose `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ;\n420\t* **L3** (covers only): a pair is empty iff no pattern meets both sides\n421\t  (on every exact-size pair and on 20 random disjoint pairs per\n422\t  instance), and every customer of `S*` shares a pattern with `A*` and\n423\t  with `R*`;\n424\t* at `k = pw − 1`: whether an exact-size pair (equivalently a `k`-set in\n425\t  `E_bip`) and whether a `k`-set in `E_relax` still exist — the necessary\n426\t  condition's slack at these sizes.\n427\t\n428\t**Results** (`check.log`, `summary.csv`; 2026-11-01, 4 workers at nice 19, 113 s wall; the\n429\trows for 1–3 vertices — 7 graphs, zero violations — are omitted). Medians and\n430\tmaxima are over the graphs of the row at `k = pw`; `X₃′` is computed only on\n431\tgraphs with `X₁ ≤ 200`, so its maximum at `n ≥ 10` is over that subset;\n432\t\"`E_relax ⊋ E_bip`\" is the share of graphs with `X₂′ > X₂`; \"closures differ\"\n433\tis the share of closed pairs whose `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ,\n434\tover the pairs closed; the last two columns are the shares of graphs with\n435\t`pw ≥ 1` on which the structure still exists at `k = pw − 1`.\n436\t\n437\t| graphs | count | viol. | `X₁` med / max | `X₂` med / max | `X₂′` med / max | `X₃` med / max | `X₃′` med / max | `E_relax ⊋ E_bip` | pairs closed | closures differ | L1 at `pw − 1` | `E_relax` at `pw − 1` |\n438\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n439\t| geng n=4 | 11 | 0 | 4 / 8 | 2 / 4 | 2 / 4 | 2 / 6 | 2 / 6 | 0.0% | 48 | 4.2% | 40.0% | 40.0% |\n440\t| geng n=5 | 34 | 0 | 6 / 18 | 3 / 9 | 3 / 9 | 3 / 10 | 3 / 10 | 0.0% | 218 | 8.7% | 42.4% | 42.4% |\n441\t| geng n=6 | 156 | 0 | 6.5 / 36 | 4 / 16 | 4 / 16 | 4 / 20 | 4 / 20 | 0.0% | 1,318 | 10.1% | 43.9% | 44.5% |\n442\t| geng n=7 | 1,044 | 0 | 8 / 84 | 4 / 25 | 4 / 25 | 4 / 35 | 4 / 35 | 0.3% | 10,530 | 9.9% | 45.6% | 46.1% |\n443\t| geng n=8 | 12,346 | 0 | 8 / 200 | 5 / 52 | 5 / 52 | 5 / 70 | 5 / 70 | 1.0% | 137,152 | 8.4% | 43.1% | 43.6% |\n444\t| geng n=9 | 274,668 | 0 | 9 / 450 | 6 / 105 | 6 / 105 | 6 / 126 | 6 / 126 | 2.2% | 3,243,462 | 6.7% | 38.5% | 39.0% |\n445\t| random n=10 | 6,667 | 0 | 13 / 1050 | 7 / 92 | 7 / 92 | 8 / 252 | 8 / 127 | 4.4% | 187,528 | 14.4% | 44.1% | 44.6% |\n446\t| random n=11 | 6,667 | 0 | 14 / 2310 | 8 / 145 | 8 / 145 | 9 / 420 | 8 / 122 | 5.9% | 209,294 | 11.6% | 43.2% | 43.7% |\n447\t| random n=12 | 6,666 | 0 | 16 / 3192 | 9 / 161 | 9 / 161 | 10 / 582 | 10 / 141 | 7.9% | 224,689 | 9.9% | 42.8% | 43.2% |\n448\t| covers n<=10 | 2,000 | 0 | 5 / 74 | 4 / 30 | 4 / 30 | 2 / 30 | 2 / 30 | 2.2% | 13,042 | 3.1% | 27.0% | 27.2% |\n449\t\n450\t**Total: 310,266 graphs, 4,027,298 closures checked, zero violations of any\n451\tkind** (the script's fourteen violation flags all stayed at zero; `grep -c\n452\tVIOLATION check.log` is `0`). Read across: `X₁ ≥ X₂` always (several pairs per\n453\tseparator), `X₂ = X₂′` in every maximum and median at these sizes (the\n454\trelaxation adds separators on 0.3–8% of graphs, growing with `n` and with\n455\tthe random graphs' density), `X₃′ < X₃` on 2.8% of graphs at 7 vertices, 1.8% at 8\n456\tand 0.9% of the random graphs at 10–12 where computed (closed pairs that\n457\tthe closure of no exact-size pair reaches, remark (iv) of §2.3;\n458\t`x3_vs_x3prime.log`), and the covers behave like sparse graphs (fewer closed pairs than\n459\tseparators, the closure orders rarely differing).\n460\t\n461\t### 2.5 L3 — the MOSP forms\n462\t\n463\tLet the instance have customers `C` (`|C| = n`) and patterns\n464\t`S_1, …, S_m ⊆ C`; its MOSP graph `mospGraph` (`MOSPGraph.lean`;\n465\t`coverGraph` in `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`) joins two distinct customers\n466\tiff some pattern contains both, and `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`\n467\t(`MOSPGraph.lean`, both directions). With `k = mospValue − 1 < n`:\n468\t\n469\t**Lemma L3.** (a) For disjoint `A, R ⊆ C`, `(A, R)` is an empty pair of\n470\t`mospGraph` iff no pattern meets both `A` and `R`\n471\t(`isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff`). (b) L1, L1′ and L2 hold for `mospGraph`\n472\twith `S` a set of `k` customers; in particular every instance with\n473\t`mospValue ≤ k + 1` has `k` customers whose removal leaves customer\n474\tclasses of sizes `t` and `N − t` such that no pattern meets both classes.\n475\t(c) In a closed pair `(A*, R*)` of `mospGraph`, a customer `c` lies in\n476\t`S* = N(A*) ∩ N(R*)` iff `c ∉ A* ∪ R*`, some pattern contains `c` and a\n477\tcustomer of `A*`, and some pattern contains `c` and a customer of `R*`.\n478\t\n479\t*Proof.* (a) is the cited lemma. (b) is L1, L1′, L2 applied to the graph\n480\t`mospGraph`, with `k ≥ pw` given by the equality theorem. (c) `c ∈ N(X)`\n481\tfor `mospGraph` means `c ∉ X` and `c` is adjacent to some `x ∈ X`, i.e.\n482\tsome pattern contains both `c` and `x`; apply L2 (iv). ∎\n483\t\n484\tThe check of §2.4 runs L1, L1′, L2 on the MOSP graphs of 2,000 random\n485\tcovers and tests (a) and (c) directly against the matrix.\n486\t\n487\t### 2.6 Lean-ready statements for item 10\n488\t\n489\tAll over `{V : Type*} [Fintype V] [DecidableEq V] (G : SimpleGraph V)\n490\t[DecidableRel G.Adj]`, in the vocabulary of `EmptyPair.lean`\n491\t(`closedNbhd`, `IsEmptyPair` from `FirstMoment.lean`), with `n = Fintype.card V`,\n492\t`t = (n − k + 1) / 2`, `N − t = (n − k) / 2` in `ℕ` arithmetic.\n493\t\n494\t| name | hypotheses | conclusion | rests on |\n495\t|---|---|---|---|\n496\t| `exists_balanced_separator` (L1) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R S : Finset V`, pairwise disjoint, `A ∪ R ∪ S = univ`, `A.card = t`, `R.card = N − t`, `S.card = k`, `∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `vertexSeparation_eq_pathwidth`, `exists_empty_pair_of_layout` at `i = t − 1`, `Finset.exists_subset_card_eq`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n497\t| `IsBalancedBip G S k` (def) | — | `∃ A R, Disjoint A R ∧ A ∪ R = univ \\ S ∧ A.card = t ∧ R.card = N − t ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R` — the pair form of `E_bip`, which L1′ (c) shows equivalent to the component form | — |\n498\t| `exists_isBalancedBip` (L1′ a) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ S, S.card = k ∧ IsBalancedBip G S k` | `exists_balanced_separator` |\n499\t| `connectedComponent_subset_side` (connectivity step) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `C` a connected component of `G.induce (A ∪ R)` | `C ⊆ A ∨ C ⊆ R` | induction on `SimpleGraph.Walk` in the induced graph |\n500\t| `isBalancedBip_iff_components` (L1′ c) | `S.card = k` | `IsBalancedBip G S k ↔` the components of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` admit a grouping into sizes `t`, `N − t` | the previous row; the converse by taking unions |\n501\t| `IsRelax G S k` (def) and `isRelax_of_isBalancedBip` (L1′ b) | `IsBalancedBip G S k` | every component of `G.induce (univ \\ S)` has `card ≤ t` | `connectedComponent_subset_side`, `Finset.card_le_card` |\n502\t| `not_isBalancedBip_threeEdges` (L1′ d) | the graph `3K₂` on `Fin 6`, `k = 0`, `S = ∅` | `IsRelax ∧ ¬ IsBalancedBip` | `decide` |\n503\t| `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` (Galois step) | — | `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A ↔ Disjoint A R ∧ ∀ u ∈ A, ∀ v ∈ R, ¬ G.Adj u v` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff`, `G.adj_symm` |\n504\t| `closedNbhd_mono` | `A ⊆ A'` | `closedNbhd G A ⊆ closedNbhd G A'` | `Finset.biUnion_subset_biUnion_of_subset_left` |\n505\t| `IsClosedPair G A R` (def) | — | `A = univ \\ closedNbhd G R ∧ R = univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | — |\n506\t| `closure_subset` (L2 i) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `A ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` and `R ⊆ univ \\ closedNbhd G A` | `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` twice |\n507\t| `closure_isClosedPair` (L2 ii–iii) | `IsEmptyPair G A R` | `IsClosedPair G (univ \\ closedNbhd G (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)) (univ \\ closedNbhd G A)` | `closure_subset`, `closedNbhd_mono`, `subset_compl_closedNbhd_iff` |\n508\t| `sdiff_isClosedPair_eq_inter_boundary` (L2 iv) | `IsClosedPair G A R` | `univ \\ (A ∪ R) = (closedNbhd G A \\ A) ∩ (closedNbhd G R \\ R)` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n509\t| `closure_card` (L2 v) | `IsEmptyPair G A R`, `t ≤ A.card`, `N − t ≤ R.card`, `(univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the same three bounds for the closed pair | `closure_subset`, `Finset.card_le_card`, `Finset.card_sdiff` |\n510\t| `exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le` (L1 + L2) | `pathwidth G ≤ k`, `k < n` | `∃ A R, IsClosedPair G A R ∧ IsEmptyPair G A R ∧ t ≤ A.card ∧ N − t ≤ R.card ∧ (univ \\ (A ∪ R)).card ≤ k` | the two rows above and `exists_balanced_separator` |\n511\t| `mem_boundary_coverGraph_iff` (L3 c) | `S : Fin m → Finset C` | `c ∈ closedNbhd (coverGraph S) A \\ A ↔ c ∉ A ∧ ∃ j, c ∈ S j ∧ ∃ a ∈ A, a ≠ c ∧ a ∈ S j` | `coverGraph`'s adjacency, `mem_closedNbhd_iff` |\n512\t| `exists_balanced_separator_cover` (L3 b) | an instance with `mospValue ≤ k + 1`, `k < n` | `exists_balanced_separator` for `mospGraph`, with the empty pair stated as \"no pattern meets both\" | `MOSPGraph.lean`'s equality, `isEmptyPair_coverGraph_iff` |\n513\t\n514\tObstacle noted for item 10, not for this item: `isBalancedBip_iff_components`\n515\tneeds connected components of an induced subgraph as `Finset`s with their\n516\tcardinalities, which the development has not used so far (Mathlib's\n517\t`SimpleGraph.ConnectedComponent` is a quotient type; `Fintype` instances\n518\texist, the union-of-supports bookkeeping does not). The pair form\n519\t`IsBalancedBip` is what the probabilistic statement of item 08 needs, and\n520\tit is cheap; the component form is for the LDP comparison and can be\n521\tleft as a named `Prop` with this obstacle if it does not fit.\n522\t\n523\t### 2.7 Regenerate, size range, kill verdict, what this does not claim\n524\t\n525\t* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/02_lemmas/check.py --max-n 9 --workers 8`"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"e6087e18-62e6-4d50-8c90-6a869dbd9d5d","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:59:22.390Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3/tool-results/b4726b81c.txt","content":"Math.*, DOI 10.1137/120891265; **arXiv 1311.6555**, not 1109.0689 as the\nitem says — that number is a paper on online payment systems), Gao 2012\n(*Discrete Appl. Math.*, DOI 10.1016/j.dam.2011.10.013; §§1–3), the\ntwo-page previews of Gao 2006 (COCOON, DOI 10.1007/11809678_25) and of\nKloks 1994 (LNCS 842, DOI 10.1007/BFb0045375 — **the held preview is\nchapter 12, on cocomparability graphs of bounded dimension, and contains\nnothing about random graphs**; his first moment is cited here only as\nrestated by Do–Erde–Kang p. 1, Gao 2006 p. 226, Gao 2012 §3 and\nPerarnau–Serra p. 4 and 9), and `reports/scratch/first_moment/lit/turner1986.txt`\n(Turner 1986, *SIAM J. Comput.* 15, read by the checker of\n`reports/random_graph_first_moment.md` §4; its Lemmas 2.1–2.2 re-read here).\n\nFetched into `literature/` this item (open copies; nothing executed from\na download; text extracted with `pdftotext`):\n\n| file | what it is | DOI of the published version (Crossref) |\n|---|---|---|\n| `2017-Dembo-Montanari-Sen-Extremal-Cuts-Sparse-Random-Graphs-AOP-arXiv.pdf` | arXiv 1503.03923v2 | *Ann. Probab.* 45(2), 10.1214/15-AOP1084 |\n| `2021-Andreis-Konig-Patterson-LDP-All-Cluster-Sizes-Sparse-Erdos-Renyi-Graph-RSA-preprint.pdf` | the authors' revision of 19 August 2020, from W. König's WIAS page | *Random Structures Algorithms* 59(4) 522–553, 10.1002/rsa.21007 |\n| `2005-Puhalskii-Stochastic-Processes-Random-Graphs-AOP-arXiv.pdf` | arXiv math/0402183v1 (59 pp.) | *Ann. Probab.* 33(1) 337–412, 10.1214/009117904000000784 — **the item's venue \"Stochastic Processes and their Applications\" is wrong** |\n| `2003-Diaz-Serna-Wormald-Computation-Bisection-Width-Random-d-Regular-Graphs-ALCOMFT-TR-03-44.pdf` | the ALCOM-FT technical report (Aarhus mirror, PostScript converted), the preprint of the 2007 paper; its analysis is explicitly \"not rigorous\" and sketches how to make it so | *Theoret. Comput. Sci.* 382(2) 120–130 (2007), 10.1016/j.tcs.2007.03.003 — the journal version is **not held** (Elsevier, blocked) |\n| `2012-Lampis-Local-Improvement-Gives-Better-Expanders-arXiv.pdf` | arXiv 1211.0524v1; not in the brief, fetched because Kolesnik–Wormald cite it as the locally-optimal count that improves Bollobás, which is what the brief attributes to Díaz–Serna–Wormald (§1.6) | no journal version found in Crossref |\n\nNot obtained, recorded in `literature/MISSING.md`: **O'Connell 1998**\n(*Probab. Theory Related Fields* 110, 277–285, DOI 10.1007/s004400050149;\nSpringer paywall — Semantic Scholar lists a \"bronze\" PDF at the publisher\nthat the publisher does not serve without a login; cited here only through\nPuhalskii's and Andreis–König–Patterson's restatements), **Luczak &\nMcDiarmid 2001** (*Random Structures Algorithms* 18(1) 31–38, DOI\n10.1002/1098-2418(200101)18:1<31::AID-RSA3>3.0.CO;2-1; Wiley paywall; the\nLSE repository record 17554 says \"full text not available\"; cited through\nLichev–Mitsche p. 2, Coja-Oghlan et al. p. 4, Dembo–Montanari–Sen p. 1 and\nDo–Erde–Kang §1.3), **Bollobás 1988** (*European J. Combin.* 9(3) 241–244,\nDOI 10.1016/S0195-6698(88)80014-3; Elsevier, blocked; its content is\nrestated in detail by Kolesnik–Wormald §§1, 2, 7 and Lampis §§1, 3, which\nre-derive its bound), **Díaz–Serna–Wormald 2007** (journal version; the\n2003 report is held), and **the random-graph chapter of Kloks 1994**.\n\n### 1.2 Table A — who counts what\n\n\"Inner probability\" is the probability, for one fixed object, that it is\nwhat is counted; \"count\" is what the union bound runs over. Page numbers\nare of the