.04 | 12.72 | 0.55 | 0.055 | 0.126 | 0.127 | 0.298 |\n58\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 15 | 9, 5, 4 | 8% | 20 | 154.2 | 1538 | 21.84 | 63.45 ± 11.47 | 62.97 | 0.61 | 0.125 | 0.173 | 0.173 | 0.322 |\n59\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 17 | 11, 6, 5 | 4% | 28.5 | 206.9 | 3498 | 28.42 | 110.01 ± 22.61 | 124.86 | 0.65 | 0.120 | 0.168 | 0.172 | 0.304 |\n60\t\n61\tWhat the table says:\n62\t\n63\t- **The formula and the enumerator agree where the tail is sampled.** In\n64\t  20 of the 26 uncensored cells the sample mean of `X′` is within two\n65\t  standard errors of `E[X²]/E[X] − 1`; the six exceptions are *all below*\n66\t  the prediction (`z = −2.3` to `−7.7`), the largest at `G(32, 10/32)`,\n67\t  `β*` (47 ± 8 against 112), and the censored cell is below it by its\n68\t  censoring. A sample mean under a heavy tail is biased down when the\n69\t  instances that carry the mean are rarer than one in 200, and these\n70\t  cells are where the top 10% carry 68–94% of the sum. The identity is\n71\t  checked exactly at small `n` in the tests; here the agreement in 20\n72\t  cells and the sign of every disagreement are what the enumerator and\n73\t  the formula could show.\n74\t- **The mean is carried by the tail.** The top 10% of instances carry\n75\t  48–94% of `ΣX′` at `β*` (42–79% at `β* + 0.1`); at `G(32, 6/32)`, `β*`,\n76\t  the median is 64 against a mean of 1,479 and a maximum of 120,314. The\n77\t  planted mean is not what a planted instance looks like.\n78\t- **At `β*` the median planted instance has a small cluster.** `G(n, 6/n)`:\n79\t  median `X′` = 2, 3, 19, 39, 64 at `n = 16 … 32`; `G(n, 10/n)`: 0, 0, 1, 1,\n80\t  5.5 (the \"1\" is the reversed pair where `N` is even); covers `(6, 1)`:\n81\t  **`X′ = 1` at every size, the reversed pair and nothing else** (`N` is\n82\t  even in all four cells), with `X′ ≥ 2` on 16, 21, 36 and 47% of the\n83\t  instances at `n = 16, 20, 24, 28` — growing with `n`, but not yet the\n84\t  median — and `X′ = 1` on the rest. At `β* + 0.1` the counts are large everywhere (median\n85\t  11,067 at `G(32, 6/32)`), but there `E[X] = e^{g n}` with `g > 0` and the\n86\t  other pairs are the instance's own population, not a cluster of `P₀`\n87\t  (§4.4).\n88\t- **Finite sizes.** The exact planted mean's exponent at `n = 32` is\n89\t  `0.23` (`c = 6`), `0.15` (`c = 10`), `0.11` (covers, `n = 28`) against\n90\t  asymptotic `0.35, 0.31, 0.22`: at these `n` nothing is near its limit,\n91\t  and the asymptotic `0.23` of §8.5 (a) is at `n = ∞`; the finite exact\n92\t  value is what the medians are compared with.\n93\t\n94\t### 4.3 Growth in `n`, and the fork\n95\t\n96\tOLS slopes in `n` (nats per vertex, fitted over five sizes for the cell\n97\tstatistics and over 645–987 instances for the per-instance one; `slopes.csv`):\n98\t\n99\t| model | `β` | sizes | slope `log(median X′)` | slope `log E[X′]` | slope `log(mean X′)` | per-instance slope `log X′` (`X′ > 0`) | at the largest `n`: `log med / n` | `log E / n` | ratio |\n100\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n101\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 16–28 | 0.000 ± 0.000 | 0.163 ± 0.003 | 0.192 ± 0.009 | 0.094 ± 0.010 | 0.000 | 0.109 | **0.00** |\n102\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16–28 | 0.299 ± 0.051 | 0.265 ± 0.032 | 0.263 ± 0.035 | 0.216 ± 0.012 | 0.120 | 0.172 | **0.69** |\n103\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 16–32 | 0.237 ± 0.033 | 0.313 ± 0.034 | 0.307 ± 0.015 | 0.172 ± 0.012 | 0.130 | 0.229 | **0.57** |\n104\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16–32 | 0.450 ± 0.050 | 0.542 ± 0.031 | 0.475 ± 0.039 | 0.450 ± 0.012 | 0.291 | 0.386 | **0.75** |\n105\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 16–32 | 0.213 ± 0.123 | 0.296 ± 0.027 | 0.276 ± 0.045 | 0.116 ± 0.013 | 0.053 | 0.147 | **0.36** |\n106\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16–32 | 0.321 ± 0.029 | 0.396 ± 0.026 | 0.375 ± 0.010 | 0.249 ± 0.012 | 0.170 | 0.245 | **0.69** |\n107\t\n108\t**The fork, by the item's letter** (`log(median X′)/n` against `log\n109\tE[X′ | planted]/n` at the largest `n`): ratio **0.57** at `G(32, 6/32)`,\n110\t`β*` (within a factor 2 — \"typical\"); **0.36** at `G(32, 10/32)`, `β*`\n111\t(between a quarter and a half — \"both\"); **0 on covers at `β*`** (the\n112\tmedian has no pair beyond the reverse at any size — \"artefact\"); 0.69–0.75\n113\tat `β* + 0.1` (\"typical\", but see §4.4: there the pairs are not a cluster\n114\tof `P₀`). The growth rates say the same with less noise: at `β*` the\n115\tmedian grows at `0.24 ± 0.03` (`c = 6`) against the mean's `0.31 ± 0.03`,\n116\tand the per-instance geometric mean at `0.17 ± 0.01` (`c = 6`) and `0.12 ±\n117\t0.01` (`c = 10`) against `0.31` and `0.30`; on covers the median does not\n118\tgrow at all (`0.00`) against `0.16`. So at `β*`: **the cluster exists on\n119\tthe typical planted instance and grows exponentially on `G(n, c/n)` at\n120\troughly half the mean's rate, and does not grow at all on the `(6, 1)`\n121\tcovers, where §8.5's `e^{0.23 n}` lives.** The second moment at `(6, 1)`\n122\tis a rare-instance artefact; on `G(n, c/n)` it is half typical and half\n123\ttail, and the \"in between\" branch of the fork applies.\n124\t\n125\t### 4.4 Where the other pairs are: two lobes, and an empty middle\n126\t\n127\t![Hamming distance of the other exact-size empty pairs from the planted pair, per cell at the largest n](scratch/separator_moment/04_planted/distance_hist.png)\n128\t\n129\t*Distance from the planted pair in units of `n`, pooled over the 200\n130\tplanted instances of each cell at the largest `n` (`distance_hist.csv`);\n131\tthe solid line is the far/near cut at `0.2 n`, the dashed line the\n132\tposition of the reversed pair, `N/n`.*\n133\t\n134\tAt `β*` the histogram is **bimodal with an empty middle**: one lobe at\n135\t`d ≈ 0.1–0.4 n` (perturbations of `P₀`), one at `d ≈ N/n ± 0.15` (perturbations\n136\tof the reversed pair), and almost nothing at `d ≈ 0.5–0.6 n` on `G(32, 6/32)`\n137\t(5,000 of 295,701 pairs at `d = 17–19`, 1.7%) and nothing at all on covers\n138\t(none at `d = 10–11`, four of 4,584 at `d = 9–12`). At `β* + 0.1` on `G(n, c/n)` the middle\n139\tfills and the distribution is broad and unimodal around `0.55–0.6 n`:\n140\tthe other pairs are then the instance's whole pair population. The shares\n141\t(pooled over pairs; `cells.csv`):\n142\t\n143\t| model | `β` | `n` | share far (`d > 0.2 n`) | within `0.05 n` of `P₀` / of `P₀` or its reverse | `0.1 n` | `0.2 n` | `0.3 n` | per-instance median share within `0.2 n` of `P₀` or reverse | far pairs with `∣A′ ∩ R∣ ≥ ∣A′ ∩ A∣` | median move-class of `P₀` (max) | `X/X₄` |\n144\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n145\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.77 | 0.000 / 0.000 | 0.000 / 0.033 | 0.234 / 0.434 | 0.390 / 0.675 | 0.79 | 0.53 | 1 (356) | 1.81 |\n146\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 24 | 0.87 | 0.000 / 0.008 | 0.039 / 0.086 | 0.134 / 0.276 | 0.295 / 0.598 | 0.86 | 0.59 | 2 (1423) | 3.50 |\n147\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.90 | 0.000 / 0.001 | 0.012 / 0.020 | 0.102 / 0.183 | 0.296 / 0.548 | 0.63 | 0.54 | 9 (10680) | 4.79 |\n148\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 0.90 | 0.000 / 0.001 | 0.011 / 0.019 | 0.098 / 0.176 | 0.289 / 0.507 | 0.88 | 0.50 | 8 (71548) | 5.17 |\n149\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.93 | 0.000 / 0.015 | 0.000 / 0.015 | 0.073 / 0.162 | 0.137 / 0.290 | 0.55 | 0.60 | 1 (3810) | 2.76 |\n150\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 0.97 | 0.000 / 0.001 | 0.006 / 0.013 | 0.034 / 0.068 | 0.160 / 0.322 | 0.24 | 0.54 | 37.5 (25712) | 7.38 |\n151\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.99 | 0.000 / 0.000 | 0.001 / 0.002 | 0.012 / 0.024 | 0.082 / 0.163 | 0.07 | 0.54 | 1151 (243332) | 16.44 |\n152\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 0.99 | 0.000 / 0.000 | 0.001 / 0.001 | 0.012 / 0.023 | 0.062 / 0.122 | 0.08 | 0.51 | 4196 (267543) | 20.38 |\n153\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.43 | 0.000 / 0.000 | 0.000 / 0.066 | 0.572 / 0.958 | 0.602 / 1.000 | 1.00 | 0.93 | 1 (7) | 1.15 |\n154\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 24 | 0.68 | 0.000 / 0.086 | 0.160 / 0.406 | 0.320 / 0.726 | 0.421 / 0.929 | 1.00 | 0.80 | 1 (125) | 1.66 |\n155\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.84 | 0.000 / 0.004 | 0.046 / 0.074 | 0.156 / 0.289 | 0.357 / 0.711 | 1.00 | 0.60 | 1 (2759) | 1.87 |\n156\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 0.64 | 0.000 / 0.004 | 0.102 / 0.175 | 0.361 / 0.672 | 0.493 / 0.903 | 1.00 | 0.72 | 1 (654) | 2.19 |\n157\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.73 | 0.000 / 0.000 | 0.000 / 0.028 | 0.267 / 0.481 | 0.445 / 0.766 | 1.00 | 0.55 | 1 (96) | 1.36 |\n158\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 0.89 | 0.000 / 0.013 | 0.039 / 0.091 | 0.112 / 0.237 | 0.262 / 0.537 | 0.78 | 0.57 | 1 (1407) | 2.37 |\n159\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.90 | 0.000 / 0.003 | 0.016 / 0.034 | 0.095 / 0.193 | 0.278 / 0.558 | 0.46 | 0.57 | 9 (5652) | 3.54 |\n160\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 0.94 | 0.000 / 0.001 | 0.006 / 0.013 | 0.059 / 0.119 | 0.159 / 0.319 | 0.34 | 0.55 | 19 (67758) | 4.05 |\n161\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.74 | 0.000 / 0.418 | 0.000 / 0.418 | 0.264 / 0.946 | 0.291 / 1.000 | 1.00 | 0.96 | 1 (15) | 1.28 |\n162\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.63 | 0.000 / 0.044 | 0.158 / 0.359 | 0.370 / 0.784 | 0.478 / 1.000 | 1.00 | 0.83 | 1 (456) | 2.92 |\n163\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.71 | 0.000 / 0.176 | 0.000 / 0.176 | 0.292 / 0.759 | 0.390 / 0.951 | 1.00 | 0.84 | 1 (20) | 1.98 |\n164\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.77 | 0.000 / 0.002 | 0.060 / 0.082 | 0.227 / 0.328 | 0.567 / 0.810 | 0.70 | 0.42 | 7.5 (2898) | 5.90 |\n165\t\n166\tThree readings. (i) **Few other pairs are small perturbations of `P₀`\n167\talone**: within `0.05 n` of `P₀` or its reverse sit 0–4% of the other\n168\tpairs at the largest `n` (the single-swap neighbours are a negligible\n169\tshare); within `0.1 n` of `P₀` itself, 1% (`c = 6`), 10% (`c = 10`) and 16%\n170\t(covers); within `0.1 n` of `P₀` *or its reverse*, 2%, 18% and 36%. Within\n171\t`0.3 n` of either, 51% (`c = 6`), 90% (`c = 10`) and 99.96% (covers). (ii) **The per-instance\n172\tmedian is tighter than the pool**: on the median planted instance at\n173\t`β*`, 88% (`c = 6`), 100% (`c = 10`, covers) of the other pairs sit within\n174\t`0.2 n` of `P₀` or its reverse, against 18%, 67%, 78% pooled — the far pairs\n175\tbelong to the rare instances with large `X′`, which is the tail of §4.2\n176\tagain. (iii) **The reversed pair is half the cluster.** At `c = 6` the far\n177\tpairs split evenly between the two orientations (50–59% have `|A′ ∩ R| ≥\n178\t|A′ ∩ A|` over the five sizes); at `c = 10` and on covers the reversed\n179\tlobe dominates (72%, 83% at the largest `n`). The move-class of `P₀`\n180\t(single swaps) holds 8–9 pairs at the median on `G(n, 6/n)`, `β*`, at\n181\t`n = 28–32` (1–2 below; max 71,548), and 1 — `P₀` alone — at `c = 10` and\n182\ton covers; `X/X₄` is 1.2–5.2 at `β*` and up to 20 at `β* + 0.1`.\n183\t\n184\tThe far and near pairs have different mean overlap tables\n185\t(`overlap_far_near.csv`, `n = 32`, `c = 6`, `β*`, in units of `n`): near\n186\tpairs keep `A ∩ A′ = 0.39`, `R ∩ R′ = 0.36`, `S ∩ S′ = 0.095` with\n187\t`A ∩ R′ = R ∩ A′ = 0.02` (`P₀` with a few swaps); far pairs average `A ∩ A′\n188\t= A ∩ R′ = R ∩ A′ = 0.19` — the average of the two lobes, not a table any\n189\tpair has. At `c = 10` the far table is the reversed pair's (`A ∩ R′ = R ∩\n190\tA′ = 0.21`, `A ∩ A′ = 0.08`), which is §8.5 (a)'s extremal table with\n191\t`S ∩ S′ = 0.25` of `|S| = 0.34 n`.\n192\t\n193\t### 4.5 The mechanism\n194\t\n195\tFor every far pair, the vertices of `S` that joined a side (`S ∩ (A′ ∪\n196\tR′)`), their degree into the planted sides `A ∪ R`, and how many of those\n197\tneighbours were *evicted* into `S′` (a side neighbour of a vertex now in\n198\t`A′` must be in `A′` or in `S′`); against the degree law of all `S`\n199\tvertices (`mechanism.csv`, `mechanism_summary.csv`; the mechanism is\n200\tsampled at 200,000 far pairs per instance where there are more):\n201\t\n202\t| model | `β` | `n` | moved `S` vertices (far pairs) | their planted-side degree: mean | share `≤ 1` | all `S` vertices: mean | share `≤ 1` | evicted neighbours: mean | share 0 | share `≤ 1` |\n203\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n204\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 3,141 | 2.87 | 0.23 | 4.95 | 0.017 | 0.80 | 0.42 | 0.80 |\n205\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 864,223 | 2.67 | 0.24 | 5.11 | 0.028 | 0.64 | 0.51 | 0.87 |\n206\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 24,378 | 2.21 | 0.35 | 4.53 | 0.028 | 0.83 | 0.38 | 0.82 |\n207\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 38,802,029 | 3.07 | 0.14 | 4.29 | 0.056 | 1.11 | 0.30 | 0.69 |\n208\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 65 | 3.79 | 0.00 | 6.83 | 0.000 | 0.69 | 0.42 | 0.89 |\n209\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 17,567 | 4.45 | 0.02 | 6.59 | 0.004 | 1.35 | 0.18 | 0.60 |\n210\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 1,136 | 3.18 | 0.09 | 5.58 | 0.003 | 1.29 | 0.17 | 0.64 |\n211\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 1,697,525 | 3.80 | 0.03 | 5.68 | 0.009 | 1.91 | 0.10 | 0.38 |\n212\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 28 | 5,636 | 5.10 | 0.00 | 9.09 | 0.000 | 1.27 | 0.21 | 0.64 |\n213\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 58,721 | 4.25 | 0.04 | 6.72 | 0.004 | 2.08 | 0.07 | 0.33 |\n214\t\n215\t**The hypothesis holds in direction and not in its strong form.** The\n216\tseparator vertices that recombine into a side have about half the\n217\tplanted-side degree of a typical `S` vertex (2.7 against 5.1 at `c = 6`,\n218\t4.4 against 6.6 at `c = 10`, 5.1 against 9.1 on covers at `β*`), but they\n219\tare not degree-zero vertices: at the largest sizes 0–1% have no side\n220\tneighbour (14% at `G(16, 6/16)`) and 0.1–24% at most one. What is small is the number they *evict*: at `β*` the\n221\tmoved vertex evicts no neighbour in 18–51% of cases and at most one in\n222\t60–87%, with a mean of 0.6–1.4 — the rest of its side neighbours are on\n223\tits own new side. So the recombination is \"a low-degree separator vertex\n224\twhose side neighbours mostly lie on one side joins that side, pushing\n225\tone or two of its other-side neighbours into the separator\", and on the\n226\treversed lobe the same with the sides exchanged.\n227\t\n228\t### 4.6 The union bound's slack, measured directly\n229\t\n230\tBy identity 2, `P(X > 0)/E[X] = E[1/X | P₀ empty]`, and the planted sample\n231\tgives it with a standard error of `0.01–0.03` (`cells.csv`,\n232\t`inv_X_mean`). The conversion to `β` uses the pair exponent's slope at\n233\t`β*`: `3.38` per unit of `β` at `c = 6`, `3.30` at `c = 10`, `2.82` on\n234\t`(6, 1)` covers (computed here by central differences of\n235\t`gnp_pair_rate` / `rate`; the header's `3.3–3.4` for `c = 6–10`).\n236\t\n237\t| model | `β` | `n` | `E_planted[1/X]` ± se | `−log E_planted[1/X]` (nats) | per vertex | in `β` | `log E[X′ ∣ planted] / n` |\n238\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n239\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.453 ± 0.027 | 0.79 | 0.050 | 0.015 | 0.175 |\n240\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 20 | 0.337 ± 0.025 | 1.09 | 0.054 | 0.016 | 0.170 |\n241\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 24 | 0.155 ± 0.013 | 1.86 | 0.078 | 0.023 | 0.213 |\n242\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.116 ± 0.016 | 2.15 | 0.077 | 0.023 | 0.244 |\n243\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 0.124 ± 0.018 | 2.08 | 0.065 | 0.019 | 0.229 |\n244\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.183 ± 0.014 | 1.70 | 0.106 | 0.031 | 0.245 |\n245\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 20 | 0.105 ± 0.014 | 2.25 | 0.113 | 0.033 | 0.284 |\n246\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 0.043 ± 0.007 | 3.14 | 0.131 | 0.039 | 0.312 |\n247\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.004 ± 0.001 | 5.41 | 0.193 | 0.057 | 0.375 |\n248\t| `G(n, 6/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 0.003 ± 0.001 | 5.95 | 0.186 | 0.055 | 0.386 |\n249\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.835 ± 0.021 | 0.18 | 0.011 | 0.003 | -0.022 |\n250\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 20 | 0.667 ± 0.027 | 0.41 | 0.020 | 0.006 | 0.072 |\n251\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 24 | 0.344 ± 0.014 | 1.07 | 0.044 | 0.013 | 0.102 |\n252\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.499 ± 0.029 | 0.70 | 0.025 | 0.008 | 0.111 |\n253\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β*` | 32 | 0.291 ± 0.024 | 1.23 | 0.039 | 0.012 | 0.147 |\n254\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.613 ± 0.027 | 0.49 | 0.031 | 0.009 | 0.079 |\n255\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 20 | 0.260 ± 0.023 | 1.35 | 0.067 | 0.020 | 0.162 |\n256\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 0.176 ± 0.014 | 1.74 | 0.072 | 0.022 | 0.171 |\n257\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.081 ± 0.010 | 2.51 | 0.090 | 0.027 | 0.212 |\n258\t| `G(n, 10/n)` | `β* + 0.1` | 32 | 0.039 ± 0.007 | 3.25 | 0.102 | 0.031 | 0.245 |\n259\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 16 | 0.439 ± 0.010 | 0.82 | 0.051 | 0.018 | 0.069 |\n260\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 20 | 0.416 ± 0.012 | 0.88 | 0.044 | 0.016 | 0.086 |\n261\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 24 | 0.348 ± 0.015 | 1.06 | 0.044 | 0.016 | 0.099 |\n262\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β*` | 28 | 0.298 ± 0.016 | 1.21 | 0.043 | 0.015 | 0.109 |\n263\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 16 | 0.323 ± 0.014 | 1.13 | 0.071 | 0.025 | 0.115 |\n264\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 20 | 0.274 ± 0.015 | 1.29 | 0.065 | 0.023 | 0.127 |\n265\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 24 | 0.172 ± 0.020 | 1.76 | 0.073 | 0.026 | 0.173 |\n266\t| cover `(6, 1)` | `β* + 0.1` | 28 | 0.117 ± 0.015 | 2.15 | 0.077 | 0.027 | 0.172 |\n267\t\n268\tAt `β*` and the largest sizes the union bound is loose by **0.065 nats\n269\tper vertex at `c = 6`, 0.039 at `c = 10`, 0.043 on `(6, 1)` covers** —\n270\t**0.019, 0.012 and 0.015 in `β`** — against planted-mean exponents of\n271\t0.23, 0.15 and 0.11 that the second-moment ratio would suggest. The\n272\ttotal slack in nats (`−log E[1/X]`) is 0.8, 1.1, 1.9, 2.2, 2.1 at `n = 16\n273\t… 32` on `G(n, 6/n)` and 0.8, 0.9, 1.1, 1.2 on covers at `n = 16 … 28`:\n274\tgrowing slowly and sub-linearly at these sizes, so the per-vertex figures\n275\tabove are upper estimates of the rate and the limit is not determined by\n276\tthis instrument. The rows at `β* + 0.1` are not a gain in the constant:\n277\tthere `E[X] = e^{g n}` with `g > 0` and `E[X]/P(X > 0) ≥ E[X]`.\n278\t\n279\tThis is the number items 09, 11 and 12 need, and it is small: the *entire*\n280\tunion-bound slack at these cells, if the per-vertex figure held\n281\tasymptotically, is `0.012–0.019` in `β`, below the brief's kill of `0.02`\n282\tfor any single representative count and consistent with §3's prediction\n283\t(`≤ 0.003` for the separator and closure) plus the reversed lobe (a factor\n284\t2 where `N` is even) plus the component flips. The ideal weighted count\n285\t`Y = Σ_P 1[P empty]/X` is exactly `1[X > 0]`, so no choice of `Z ≤ X` in\n286\titem 09 can gain more than this at the same `(n, k)`.\n287\t\n288\t### 4.7 Kill verdict (the fork), and what it sends on\n289\t\n290\t**Stated as a fork, decided per model.** On `G(n, c/n)` at `β*` the\n291\tratio is 0.57 (`c = 6`) and 0.36 (`c = 10`): the letter says \"typical\" at\n292\t`c = 6` and \"in between\" at `c = 10`; the growth rates (median at about\n293\thalf the mean's rate; per-instance geometric mean at 0.4–0.55 of it) put\n294\tboth in the item's middle branch, and **both are written up with the\n295\tnumbers**. On `(6, 1)` covers at `β*` the median `X′` is 1 — the reversed\n296\tpair — at every size and the ratio is 0: **the `e^{0.23 n}` of §8.5 (a)\n297\tis a rare-instance artefact of `E[X²]` on the cell it was computed for**,\n298\tand the closed-pair count is near-tight for `P(X > 0)` there in the sense\n299\tof identity 2: the union bound's measured slack is `0.04` nats per vertex\n300\t(`0.015` in `β`).\n301\t\n302\tFor **item 09**: the weighted count's gain is bounded by §4.6 at these\n303\tsizes (`0.012–0.019` in `β` at `β*`), and the planted lower tail is\n304\tdominated by instances with `X ≤ 2` (the pair and its reverse) on covers\n305\t(53–84% of planted instances at `n = 28 … 16`) and by `X ≤ 65` on\n306\t`G(32, 6/32)`: item 09 is\n307\treduced to the paragraph the fork prescribes, with the Laplace-transform\n308\tcomputation optional. For **item 12**: say that `P(X > 0)` is within\n309\t`e^{0.04–0.07 n}` of `E[X]` at `n ≤ 32` (measured, by identity 2), that the\n310\tremaining gap to the fitted slopes (`0.08–0.10` in `β` on `G(n, c/n)`,\n311\t`0.06` on `(6, 1)`) is therefore mostly the **necessary condition** — empty\n312\tpairs exist on instances whose pathwidth is larger — and not the union\n313\tbound; and that the cluster around a planted pair is two lobes (the pair\n314\tand its reverse, each with its single-swap perturbations) with an empty\n315\tmiddle at `β*`.\n316\t\n317\t### 4.8 Regenerate, size range, what this does not claim\n318\t\n319\t* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/04_planted/run.py --workers 8`\n320\t  (stages `run`, `stats`, `draw`; 305 + 1 + 1 s; `--probe` times the\n321\t  largest cells on three instances each); `python3 -m pytest\n322\t  tests/test_separator_moment.py` (50 tests, 14 new: planting deletes\n323\t  exactly the forbidden edges and is symmetric and idempotent; the planted\n324\t  cover meets at most one side per pattern; the overlap table sums to `n`,\n325\t  agrees with `overlap_tables` and with the Hamming distance; `lq_sides`\n326\t  against `lq` and against counting; `second_moment_exact_cover` against\n327\t  `second_moment_exact` at `r = t − 1`; identity 1 against simulation and\n328\t  against the finite forms; identity 2 on every labelled graph at `n = 6`;\n329\t  `beta_star_gnp` against the record).\n330\t* **Size range.** `n = 16–32` planted `G(n, c/n)` at `c = 6, 10`; `n =\n331\t  16–28` planted `(6, 1)` covers; `β ∈ {β*, β* + 0.1}` rounded to integer\n332\t  `k`; 200 instances per cell.\n333\t* **Does not claim.** A value of `P(X > 0)` or `P(pw ≤ k)`: the planted\n334\t  law weights instances by `X`. Anything asymptotic: the fork is decided\n335\t  at `n ≤ 32` with the growth in `n` beside it, the per-vertex slack of\n336\t  §4.6 is sub-linear in `n` over the range and is an upper estimate of its\n337\t  rate, and the exact planted mean at `n = 32` is itself a third below its\n338\t  limit. That the mechanism of §4.5 is the only one: it is the dominant\n339\t  one on far pairs at these sizes. That `β_sep` or `β_closed` fail item\n340\t  06's kill — §4.6 makes that likelier than §3 did, and item 06 decides\n341\t  it. That the `0.23` of §8.5 (a) is wrong: it is the planted mean's\n342\t  asymptotic exponent and this item measured its finite form exactly\n343\t  (identity 1) and found it correct; what is withdrawn is the reading that\n344\t  it is the typical cluster.\n345\t\n346\t---\n347\t\n348\t## 5. The inner probability, exactly: `P(G(N, p)` has a balanced empty bipartition`)` (item 05, 2026-11-01)\n349\t\n350\t**Question (C of the brief).** For a fixed `k`-set `S`, `G − S` is\n351\t`G(N, p)` with `N = n − k`. What is `P_N(E_bip)` exactly at small `N`, what\n352\tis its exponential rate in `N`, and how much smaller is it than the\n353\tproduct bound `C(N, t)(1 − p)^{t(N − t)}` that the pair count uses for the\n354\tsame event?\n355\t\n356\t**Events counted.** `E_bip` = the components of `G(N, p)` can be grouped\n357\tinto two sides of sizes `t = ⌈N/2⌉` and `N − t`; `E_relax` = no component\n358\thas more than `t` vertices (`E_bip ⊆ E_relax`, §2.2). The product bound is\n359\tthe union bound over the `C(N, t)` bipartitions, each empty with\n360\tprobability `(1 − p)^{t(N − t)}`; when `N` is even it counts every\n361\tbipartition twice. The finite bounds at a pilot cell are\n362\t`B_pair = C(n, t) C(n − t, t − 1)(1 − p)^{t(t − 1)}` (the Lean convention of\n363\t`FirstMoment.lean`, `finite_bound.py`, with `|R| = t − 1`),\n364\t`B_xpair = C(n, k) · C(N, t)(1 − p)^{t(N − t)}` (the exact-size pair count,\n365\tsides `t` and `N − t`), `B_sep = C(n, k) · P_N(E_bip)` and\n366\t`B_rel = C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax)`; each is an upper bound on `P(pw ≤ k)`\n367\t(L1′, §2.2; the separator form is item 08's theorem on paper).\n368\t\n369\t**Size range.** `P_N(E_bip)` exact for `N ≤ 120` (the item asked for 60,\n370\t80 if cheap); `P_N(E_relax)` exact to `N = 400`; the finite table at\n371\t`n = 60, 120`, `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10`, every `k`; the rate at\n372\t`c′ ∈ {2, 3, 4, 5, 6.6, 8, 10}`. Checks: Gilbert's recursion against the\n373\tenumeration of every labelled graph at `j ≤ 6` and Monte Carlo at `j = 20`;\n374\tthe partition law sums to one at `N ≤ 20`; `P_N(E_bip)` against the\n375\tenumeration of every labelled graph at `N ≤ 6` (every `t`) and at `N = 7`\n376\t(2,097,152 graphs) and Monte Carlo at `N = 20`, `p = 0.1`;\n377\t`P(E_bip) ≤ P(E_relax) ≤ 1` and `P(E_bip) ≤` product bound on a grid\n378\t(`run.log`).\n379\t\n380\t**Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/05_inner/run.py --max-N 120`\n381\t(stages `check`, `inner`, `rates`, `draw`; 9 + 193 + 24 + 2 s on 8 workers\n382\tat nice 19, about 0.5 core-hours against 1 budgeted; `--max-N 60` is the\n383\titem's command and takes 40 s less). Outputs beside it: `inner.csv` (every\n384\t`(n, c, k)`: the four finite bounds and the three inner quantities),\n385\t`floors.csv`, `rates.csv` (every `(N, c′)`), `rate_fits.csv`, `rates.png`,\n386\t`run.log`. Tests: `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`\n387\t(60 tests, 10 new).\n388\t\n389\t### 5.1 Method\n390\t\n391\tFour functions in `learning/separator_moment.py`:\n392\t\n393\t* `connected_probs(N, p)` / `log_connected`: Gilbert's recursion\n394\t  `C_1 = 1`, `C_j = 1 − Σ_{i<j} C(j−1, i−1) C_i (1−p)^{i(j−i)}` in `mpmath`;\n395\t  the subtraction cancels to `C_j ≈ e^{−j(1−p)^j}` at small `p`, so the\n396\t  working precision is `60 + 0.6 · N(1−p)^{N−1}` digits (94 at `N = 400`,\n397\t  `c′ = 2`). The item's 60 digits would have failed at `N = 400`, `c′ = 2`\n398\t  (`C_400 ≈ e^{−54}`, 24 digits of cancellation, and the terms carry the\n399\t  rounding of 400 further subtractions).\n400\t* `log_prob_partition(λ, N, p)`: `N!/(Π λ_i! Π_s m_s!) · Π C_{λ_i} · (1−p)^{(N² − Σ λ_i²)/2}`.\n401\t  With `a(s) = log C_s − log s! − (s²/2) log(1−p)` this is\n402\t  `N! (1−p)^{N²/2} Π_s e^{m_s a(s)}/m_s!`, i.e. the exponential formula:\n403\t  the sum over all partitions is `[x^N] exp(Σ_s e^{a(s)} x^s)`.\n404\t* `log_prob_rel(N, p, M)`: that coefficient with the sum cut at `s ≤ M`,\n405\t  the `O(N · M)` recursion the item asked for (over the multiplicity of the\n406\t  largest part rather than the component of the first vertex; the two are\n407\t  the same table). `N = 400` in under a second once `C_j` is known.\n408\t* `log_prob_bip(N, p, t)`: the sum over the partitions with a sub-multiset\n409\t  summing to `t`, by a **memoised recursion over the part sizes** (largest\n410\t  first) carrying the set of achievable sub-sums as a bitset, with two\n411\t  closures: a branch whose bitset already contains `t` is closed by the\n412\t  coefficient table (every completion qualifies; this is what makes it\n413\t  fast), and a part larger than `t` closes a branch at zero. The bitset is\n414\t  reduced to the bits in `[t − r, t]` before memoisation (`r` the vertices\n415\t  left to place), which is what keeps the state count polynomial-looking in\n416\t  practice: 20,863 states at `N = 60` (0.1 s), 115,065 at 80 (0.5 s),\n417\t  514,206 at 100 (2.5 s), 1,980,380 at 120 (18 s). The item's plain\n418\t  enumeration of the 966,467 partitions of 60 was never needed; `N = 160`\n419\t  would be about 8 M states and was not run.\n420\t\n421\tAll sums are of positive terms and are done in floating-point logs; only\n422\tthe connectedness recursion needs multiple precision.\n423\t\n424\t### 5.2 The finite table at the pilot cells\n425\t\n426\tLargest `k` with each bound below `1` and below `0.01`; `β*(c)·n` the\n427\tasymptotic threshold of the pair count; the pilot's observed minimum\n428\tpathwidth where the cell is proved (`n = 60`) and the solver's smallest\n429\tlower bound where it is not (`n = 120`, 13 graphs per cell, none proved\n430\tat `c ≥ 4`, 3 of 13 at `c = 3`; `reports/scratch/gnp_pilot/results.csv`).\n431\t\n432\t| `n` | `c` | pair `< 1` | pair `< .01` | xpair `< 1` | xpair `< .01` | **sep `< 1`** | **sep `< .01`** | relax `< 1` | relax `< .01` | `β*n` | graphs | exact | min pw | min LB |\n433\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n434\t| 60 | 3 | 1 | — | 1 | 0 | **3** | **1** | 3 | 1 | 0.5 | 136 | 136 | 5 | — |\n435\t| 60 | 4 | 5 | 3 | 5 | 3 | **6** | **4** | 6 | 4 | 3.7 | 136 | 136 | 8 | — |\n436\t| 60 | 6 | 11 | 11 | 12 | 11 | **12** | **11** | 12 | 11 | 10.2 | 134 | 134 | 16 | — |\n437\t| 60 | 8 | 17 | 17 | 18 | 17 | **18** | **17** | 18 | 17 | 15.8 | 132 | 132 | 20 | — |\n438\t| 60 | 10 | 23 | 21 | 23 | 21 | **23** | **22** | 23 | 22 | 20.2 | 133 | 133 | 25 | — |\n439\t| 120 | 3 | 1 | 1 | 2 | 1 | **5** | **4** | 5 | 4 | 1.0 | 13 | 3 | 11 | 12 |\n440\t| 120 | 4 | 9 | 7 | 9 | 7 | **11** | **10** | 11 | 10 | 7.4 | 13 | 0 | — | 17 |\n441\t| 120 | 6 | 21 | 21 | 22 | 21 | **24** | **22** | 24 | 22 | 20.5 | 13 | 0 | — | 25 |\n442\t| 120 | 8 | 33 | 33 | 34 | 33 | **34** | **33** | 34 | 33 | 31.5 | 13 | 0 | — | 34 |\n443\t| 120 | 10 | 43 | 41 | 43 | 42 | **44** | **42** | 44 | 42 | 40.5 | 13 | 0 | — | 41 |\n444\t\n445\t(`inner.csv`, `floors.csv`.) Read it by the 0.01 column, where the pair\n446\tcount's parity rounding (`B_pair` depends on `k` only through `t`) does\n447\tnot confuse the count:\n448\t\n449\t* **At `c ≥ 6` the separator floor is the pair floor or one stack above\n450\t  it** in all six cells (`+0, +0, +1` at `n = 60`; `+1, +0, +1` at 120),\n451\t  and never above the exact-size pair floor by more than one. That is the\n452\t  kill criterion, met (§5.5).\n453\t* **At `c = 3–4` it is two to three stacks above**: `−1 → 1` and `3 → 4`\n454\t  at `n = 60`; `1 → 4` and `7 → 10` at `n = 120`. Against the data these\n455\t  are still far below the truth (observed minimum 5 at `(60, 3)`, 8 at\n456\t  `(60, 4)`; lower bounds 12 and 17 at `n = 120`): the separator count\n457\t  moves the floor from \"excludes nothing\" to \"excludes a few stacks\" on\n458\t  the sparse flank and no further.\n459\t* **The relaxed and the bipartition floors coincide in every cell** at\n460\t  both thresholds. The number-partitioning condition costs nothing at\n461\t  these sizes (§5.4).\n462\t* The gain per vertex `(log B_pair − log B_sep)/n` at the pair's 0.01\n463\t  floor: `0.058, 0.023, 0.011, 0.006` at `c = 4, 6, 8, 10` (`n = 60`);\n464\t  `0.113, 0.065, 0.026, 0.013, 0.007` at `c = 3 … 10` (`n = 120`). Against\n465\t  the exact-size pair at the separator's own floor:\n466\t  `0.106, 0.062, 0.023, 0.011, 0.014` and `0.116, 0.068, 0.028, 0.013,\n467\t  0.010`. In `β` (÷ 3.3 at `c ≥ 6`): `0.003–0.008` at `c = 6–10`,\n468\t  `0.015–0.020` at `c = 4`, `0.02–0.035` at `c = 3` (÷ 5.5).\n469\t\n470\tAt `(120, 10)` around the floors, in decades: `k = 43`: pair `−0.99`,\n471\txpair `−0.99`, sep `−1.38`; `k = 44`: pair `+1.76`, xpair `+0.40`,\n472\t**sep `−0.17`**, relax `−0.16`; `k = 45`: sep `+1.33`. So at `n = 120` the\n473\tseparator count says `P(pw ≤ 44) ≤ 0.68` where the pair count says\n474\tnothing, and the solver's lower bounds there are 41–42 with the truth\n475\t`≥ 44` almost surely by either count. The inner numbers behind that row:\n476\t`log` product bound `−75.36`, `log P(E_bip)` `−76.65`, `log P(E_relax)`\n477\t`−76.64` — the separator count is 1.3 nats below the product bound at\n478\t`N = 76`, `c′ = 6.3`, of which `ln 2 = 0.69` is the double count of each\n479\tbipartition at even `N`.\n480\t\n481\t### 5.3 The rate\n482\t\n483\t`−log P_N(E_bip)/N` at `N = 20 … 120` and `−log P_N(E_relax)/N` to\n484\t`N = 400`, with linear fits in `N` (intercept free; \"fitted\"), fits with a\n485\t`log N` term (`−log P = I N + b log N + a`, the form a polynomial\n486\tprefactor gives), the plan's analytic routes — the pair count's rate\n487\t`c′/4 − ln 2` (a lower bound on the rate, from `P(E_bip) ≤` product\n488\tbound), `c′/4` (an upper bound, from one fixed bipartition) and the\n489\tthinning route `c′/2 − ln 2 + ln 2 · ln(2 ln 2/c′)` — and the LDP rate\n490\t`I_{c′}(½)` for `E_relax` quoted in §1.4 (`rate_check.py`).\n491\t\n492\t| `c′` | `−log P_bip/N` at `N = 20` | at 60 | at 120 | linear fit, bip, `N ≥ 60` | log-fit, bip, `N ≥ 40` | `−log P_rel/N` at 400 | linear fit, rel, `N ≥ 200` | log-fit, rel, `N ≥ 100` | `c′/4 − ln 2` | `c′/4` | thinning | **LDP `I(½)`** |\n493\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n494\t| 2 | 0.107 | 0.060 | 0.049 | 0.0383 | 0.0392 | 0.0414 | 0.0380 | 0.0375 | 0 | 0.500 | 0.053 | **0.0362** |\n495\t| 3 | 0.308 | 0.221 | 0.202 | 0.1825 | 0.1864 | 0.1896 | 0.1846 | 0.1853 | 0.057 | 0.750 | 0.272 | **0.1831** |\n496\t| 4 | 0.571 | 0.436 | 0.407 | 0.3767 | 0.3818 | 0.3877 | 0.3799 | 0.3812 | 0.307 | 1.000 | 0.572 | **0.3796** |\n497\t| 5 | 0.877 | 0.682 | 0.639 | 0.5955 | 0.6013 | 0.6107 | 0.5991 | 0.6005 | 0.557 | 1.250 | 0.918 | **0.5997** |\n498\t| 6.6 | 1.432 | 1.110 | 1.040 | 0.9704 | 0.9793 | 0.9938 | 0.9744 | 0.9759 | 0.957 | 1.650 | 1.525 | **0.9756** |\n499\t| 8 | 1.983 | 1.504 | 1.407 | 1.3097 | 1.3256 | 1.3421 | 1.3148 | 1.3163 | 1.307 | 2.000 | 2.092 | **1.3161** |\n500\t| 10 | 2.894 | 2.091 | 1.945 | 1.7995 | 1.8358 | 1.8496 | 1.8091 | 1.8115 | 1.807 | 2.500 | 2.937 | **1.8102** |\n501\t\n502\t(`rates.csv`, `rate_fits.csv`; standard errors of the linear slopes\n503\t`0.0001–0.0025`, meaningless as uncertainty since the residual is the\n504\t`log N` term, not noise.) Figure: `rates.png`, `−log P/N` against `N` per\n505\t`c′` with the bracket shaded, the LDP rate dashed.\n506\t\n507\t**What the table says.**\n508\t\n509\t1. **The exact computation reproduces the quoted large-deviation rate.**\n510\t   The log-corrected fit of `E_relax` over `N = 100–400` agrees with\n511\t   `I_{c′}(½)` to `0.0003–0.002` at every `c′`, and the linear fit over\n512\t   `N ≥ 200` to `0.001–0.002`. Item 01 could check the quoted formula only\n513\t   at its zero and its sign (§1.8); this is an independent check of its\n514\t   *value* at `u = ½` for seven `c′`, by an exact finite computation that\n515\t   shares nothing with the formula. The misprint risk recorded in §1.6 is\n516\t   retired for the form used in `rate_check.py`.\n517\t2. **`E_bip` has the rate of `E_relax`.** `log P(E_bip) − log P(E_relax)`\n518\t   at `N = 120` is `−0.0001` to `−0.008` nats in total (not per vertex),\n519\t   and the bip fits sit on the relax fits. The reading of the AKP\n520\t   minimiser in §1.8 (\"the rate of `E_bip` equals that of `E_relax`\") is\n521\t   confirmed to `N = 120` by exact computation and remains unproved as a\n522\t   limit; the finite inequality `P(E_bip) ≤ P(E_relax)` is exact.\n523\t3. **The bracket holds, in the form the finite computation can test.**\n524\t   The fitted rate lies in `[c′/4 − ln 2, min(c′/4, thinning)]` at every\n525\t   `c′` for the log-corrected fit, and for the linear fit at every `c′`\n526\t   but 10, where the linear slope over `N = 60–120` (`1.7995`) falls\n527\t   `0.007` below the asymptotic lower end (`1.8069`). That is the `log N`\n528\t   term, not an error: the lower route is the product bound, and the same\n529\t   linear fit of `−log` product bound over the same `N` gives `1.7927`,\n530\t   below the bip fit as it must be (`rate_fits.csv`,\n531\t   `above_finite_lower = True` at every `c′`); the log-corrected fit\n532\t   gives `1.836`, inside. The item's \"the fitted rate must lie in the\n533\t   bracket, or the computation is wrong\" is satisfied once the bracket is\n534\t   read at finite `N`; the asymptotic ends are limits and a linear fit\n535\t   on `N ≤ 120` is biased by `b/N` with `b ≈ −½` to `−3`.\n536\t4. **The thinning route is never the binding end** and at `c′ ≥ 5` lies\n537\t   above `c′/4`. Both of the planner's routes sit far above the truth at\n538\t   every `c′` (at `c′ = 5`: 0.918 and 1.25 against 0.600), as §1.6\n539\t   predicted from the quotation. The plan's bracket at `c = 6` was\n540\t   `[0.552, 1.245]`; the rate is `0.600` at `c′ = 5`.\n541\t\n542\t### 5.4 How much smaller than the product bound\n543\t\n544\t`log[C(N, t)(1−p)^{t(N−t)}] − log P_N(E_bip)`, total nats and per vertex of\n545\t`N` (`N` even throughout, so `ln 2 = 0.693` of it is the double count):\n546\t\n547\t| `c′` | `N = 20` | 40 | 60 | 80 | 100 | 120 | per vertex at 120 | growth per vertex, 60 → 120 |\n548\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n549\t| 2 | 3.74 | 7.99 | 12.41 | 16.89 | 21.41 | 25.96 | 0.216 | 0.226 |\n550\t| 3 | 2.03 | 4.11 | 6.39 | 8.75 | 11.15 | 13.60 | 0.113 | 0.120 |\n551\t| 4 | 1.23 | 2.23 | 3.40 | 4.65 | 5.96 | 7.29 | 0.061 | 0.065 |\n552\t| 5 | 0.89 | 1.35 | 1.93 | 2.58 | 3.28 | 4.00 | 0.033 | 0.035 |\n553\t| 6.6 | 0.72 | 0.84 | 1.01 | 1.22 | 1.45 | 1.70 | 0.014 | 0.012 |\n554\t| 8 | 0.70 | 0.73 | 0.78 | 0.84 | 0.92 | 1.01 | 0.008 | 0.004 |\n555\t| 10 | 0.69 | 0.70 | 0.70 | 0.71 | 0.73 | 0.74 | 0.006 | 0.0007 |\n556\t\n557\t**At `c′ ≥ 8` the whole gain is the factor 2**: the gap is `ln 2` plus\n558\t`0.01–0.3` nats at `N = 120` and grows at under `0.004` nats per vertex.\n559\tWhat the separator count merges there is the two orientations of one\n560\tbipartition and nothing else, as item 03 measured on typical instances\n561\t(`X₁/X₂ = 2.0–2.4` with `N` even, §3.2) and as the LDP says in the limit\n562\t(`I(½) − (c′/4 − ln 2) = 0.009` at `c′ = 8`, `0.003` at 10). **Below\n563\t`c′ ≈ 5` the gap is linear in `N`** at `0.035, 0.065, 0.120, 0.226` nats\n564\tper vertex of `N` for `c′ = 5, 4, 3, 2`, the limit values being\n565\t`I(½) − max(0, c′/4 − ln 2) = 0.043, 0.073, 0.126, 0.036`\n566\t(at `c′ = 2` the pair count's rate is negative, so the pair bound is\n567\tvacuous and the gap per vertex is `ln 2 − c′/4` plus the rate: the\n568\tfinite table's `0.226` has the product bound still *growing*). This is\n569\tthe component structure doing real work: the bipartitions of `G(N, c′/N)`\n570\tthat are empty cluster onto the same component partition, and the\n571\tsmaller `c′` the more of them per partition.\n572\t\n573\tIn the loop's units (nats per vertex of `n`, then `β`): at a pilot cell\n574\t`N = (1 − β) n`, so the gain per vertex of `n` is `(1 − β)` times the\n575\tcolumn above at `c′ = c(1 − β)`. Linearising the pair exponent at `β*`\n576\twith the LDP value of the gain gives `+0.023, +0.020, +0.011, +0.006,\n577\t+0.004` in `β` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10` — the same numbers as §1.6's\n578\tpreview from the quotation (`+0.028, +0.021, +0.011, +0.004` at\n579\t`c = 3, 4, 6, 10`, by the exact root rather than the linearisation), now\n580\tresting on the exact finite computation at `N ≤ 120` and the fitted\n581\trate beyond. Item 06 computes `β_sep(c)` properly; this is the preview\n582\tit inherits.\n583\t\n584\t### 5.5 Kill verdict\n585\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"5c3e4d8a-172b-49b0-a90f-4ff5de464dcc","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:59:23.292Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3/tool-results/bbmp6rwp2.txt","content":"its Hamming distance `d = n − |A ∩ A′| − |R ∩ R′| − |S ∩ S′|` from `P₀`,\nand the same distance from the *reversed* planted pair `(R, A)`, which is\nitself an exact-size empty pair whenever `N` is even (`has_reverse`) and\nsits at distance `N`; \"far\" means `d > 0.2 n`. `X₂`, `X₃`, `X₃′`, `X₄` and\nthe move-class of `P₀` as in §3.\n\n### 4.1 Instances\n\n| model | cells | per cell | instances | `k` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| planted `G(n, c/n)` | `n ∈ {16, 20, 24, 28, 32}` × `c ∈ {6, 10}` × `β ∈ {β*(c), β*(c) + 0.1}` | 200 | 4,000 | `round(β n)`; `β*(6) = 0.1706`, `β*(10) = 0.3371` (`beta_star_gnp`, the root of `H(a, a, 1 − 2a) − c a²`) |\n| planted covers `(6, 1)` | `n ∈ {16, 20, 24, 28}` × `β ∈ {β*, β* + 0.1}` | 200 | 1,600 | `β*(6, 1) = 0.5132` (`beta_star`) |\n\nSeeds `crc32(\"sep04:{source}:{n}:{c}:{β label}:{i}\")`; the planted sides are\nthe first `t` and the next `r` vertices. The realised `β = k/n` is in the\ntable below (rounding moves it by up to `0.03`); every prediction uses the\nrealised `(n, t, r)`. The whole run is **305 s on 8 workers at nice 