{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_017JvhNo5QKhTTEwKEP1VYEd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t\n2\t**Question (E of the brief).** On random clique covers — `m = μ n` patterns,\n3\teach a uniform subset of the `n` customers of size `d` (or of the class's\n4\tempirical size law), the MOSP graph their union of cliques\n5\t(`CliqueCoverMoment.lean`, `coverGraph`) — what do the closed-pair count, the\n6\tcomponent flips and the half-giant line give against `β*(d, μ)` and the\n7\tcertified slopes `b` of `excess_phase_diagram.md` §2.5, and where does the\n8\tfloor turn on in excess coordinates?\n9\t\n10\t**Events counted.** Three, each a valid upper bound on\n11\t`P(mospValue ≤ k + 1)` by L3 of §2 (L1, L1′, L2 hold for `mospGraph`) and the\n12\tunion bound:\n13\t\n14\t1. the **pair count** on record, at the exact forced sizes (`|A| = t`,\n15\t   `|R| = N − t`, the Lean statement) and at the record's `|R| = t − 1`;\n16\t2. the **closed-pair count** `E[X₃]`: ordered triples `(A, R, S)` partitioning\n17\t   the customers, `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ N − t`, `|S| ≤ k`, no pattern meeting\n18\t   both `A` and `R`, and every customer of `S` sharing a pattern with `A` and\n19\t   one with `R` (L3 c);\n20\t3. the **weighted count** `Y = Σ_P 1[P empty] / C(I_A + I_R, I_A)` over the\n21\t   exact-size pairs, with `I_A` the customers of `A` isolated in the `A`-side\n22\t   graph (in no pattern with another customer of `A`): swapping any `i`\n23\t   isolated customers of `A` with any `i` of `R` gives another exact-size\n24\t   empty pair (an isolated customer's patterns lie in itself and `S`), the\n25\t   `Σ_i C(I_A, i) C(I_R, i) = C(I_A + I_R, I_A)` swapped pairs are distinct\n26\t   empty pairs of the same sizes, so `Z(P) = C(I_A + I_R, I_A) ≤ X` and\n27\t   `Y ≥ 1[X > 0]` — the first instance of item 08's L4, on covers.\n28\t\n29\tThe **separator count** is not computed on covers: item 01 found no\n30\tlarge-deviation principle for the giant of the random intersection graph,\n31\tand §6.3 (3) showed that bounding the colourings of the components costs\n32\tmore than the separator gains; what the item does with the separator event\n33\tis the threshold proposition of §7.4, stated conditionally.\n34\t\n35\t**Size range.** Asymptotic constants (roots of exponents per customer,\n36\t`n → ∞` at fixed `(d, μ)` or the pooled 125 × 125 size law); the\n37\tcancellation-free bound checked against the exact alternating sum at\n38\t`n = 8–26`; the floors on the 200 Chu & Stuckey instances at 30–125\n39\tcustomers; the half-giant check against the measured largest-component\n40\tfractions at `n = 50–75` (the `fixed` ensemble frames in\n41\t`learning/data/excess.csv`).\n42\t\n43\t**Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/07_cover/run.py --workers 4`\n44\t(stages `check`, `slack`, `grid`, `giant`, `floors`; outputs beside it:\n45\t`check.log`, `slack.csv`, `grid.csv`, `giant.csv`, `half_giant.csv`,\n46\t`floors_cs.csv`, `run.log`). Tests: `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`\n47\t(74 tests, 4 new). Nothing was solved; `solutions/` was not read (the\n48\toptima come from `learning/data/excess.csv`, provenance\n49\t`certified:refutation` on all 200).\n50\t\n51\t### 7.1 Method\n52\t\n53\t**(a) The closed pair, exactly.** For fixed `(A, R, S)` of sizes\n54\t`(a, r, s)`, inclusion–exclusion over the set `T_A ⊆ S` of customers sharing\n55\tno pattern with `A` and `T_R ⊆ S` of those sharing none with `R`:\n56\t\n57\t    P(closed) = Σ_{T_A, T_R ⊆ S} (−1)^{|T_A| + |T_R|} P(no pattern meets A and R, and T_A, T_R are bad).\n58\t\n59\tGiven `(T_A, T_R)` the event is a product over the independent patterns, and\n60\ta pattern of size `s_j` is allowed iff it lies inside `S`, or meets `R`\n61\tinside `R ∪ (S ∖ T_R)`, or meets `A` inside `A ∪ (S ∖ T_A)` (a pattern meeting\n62\t`A` cannot contain a customer of `T_A`; one meeting `R` cannot contain one of\n63\t`T_R`; the three cases are disjoint). The count depends on `T_A, T_R` only\n64\tthrough `u = |S ∖ T_R|` and `w = |S ∖ T_A|`:\n65\t\n66\t    allowed(u, w; s_j) = C(s, s_j) + [C(r + u, s_j) − C(u, s_j)] + [C(a + w, s_j) − C(w, s_j)],\n67\t\n68\tso the `4^s` terms collapse to `(s + 1)²`:\n69\t\n70\t    P(closed) = Σ_{u, w = 0}^{s} (−1)^{u + w} C(s, u) C(s, w) Π_j allowed(u, w; s_j) / C(n, s_j),\n71\t\n72\tsummed in exact integers (`closed_cover_log_prob_exact`; at `u = w = s`,\n73\t`T_A = T_R = ∅`, the summand is the empty-pair probability\n74\t`avoidCount` of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`). This is the item's 4-cell\n75\tstructure `(A, R, T, F)` with `T` split by side; the `NestedStructure`\n76\tmachinery is not needed because the Möbius coefficients are explicit. The\n77\talternation cancels catastrophically in floating point (terms up to `4^s`\n78\tagainst a sum of order `e^{−Θ(n)}`), which is why the sum is in integers;\n79\tit is affordable to about `n = 30` for the whole count (every admissible\n80\ttriple) and at any `n` for one triple with `s ≲ 100`.\n81\t\n82\t**(a′) The closed pair, without cancellation.** Condition on each pattern's\n83\t*type* (`A`-type: meets `A` and avoids `R`; `R`-type; inside `S`) and on\n84\t`i_j = |P_j ∩ A|` (resp. `|P_j ∩ R|`). Given this vector `τ`, the `S`-parts\n85\tof the patterns are independent uniform subsets of `S` of sizes `s_j − i_j`,\n86\twhose membership indicators are negatively associated (sampling without\n87\treplacement: Joag-Dev & Proschan 1983, *Ann. Statist.* 11, Theorem 2.11 and\n88\tproperty P7 for independent unions), so the coverage indicators\n89\t`C_v^A = max_{j A-type} Y_{jv}`, `C_v^R` — nondecreasing functions of\n90\tdisjoint index sets (property P6) — are negatively associated, and\n91\t\n92\t    P(every v ∈ S covered on both sides | τ) ≤ Π_v P(C_v^A | τ) P(C_v^R | τ) = (1 − e^{−L̃_A})^s (1 − e^{−L̃_R})^s,\n93\t    L̃_A = −Σ_{j A-type} ln(1 − (s_j − i_j)/s).\n94\t\n95\t`ln(1 − e^{−L})` is concave, so for any `ℓ* > 0`, with\n96\t`θ = s e^{−ℓ*}/(1 − e^{−ℓ*})` and the cap `M = ℓ* − ln F(ℓ*)/θ` (for a\n97\tpattern covering all of `S`), `F(L̃) ≤ F(ℓ*) e^{θ (min(L̃, M) − ℓ*)}`, and the\n98\texpectation over `τ` factorises over the patterns:\n99\t\n100\t    P(closed) ≤ F(ℓ*_A) F(ℓ*_R) e^{−θ_A ℓ*_A − θ_R ℓ*_R} Π_j M_j(θ_A, θ_R),\n101\t    M_j = [C(s, s_j) + Σ_{i ≥ 1} C(a, i) C(s, s_j − i) e^{θ_A min(ℓ̃_i, M_A)} + Σ_{i ≥ 1} C(r, i) C(s, s_j − i) e^{θ_R min(ℓ̃_i, M_R)}] / C(n, s_j),\n102\t\n103\tvalid for every `(ℓ*_A, ℓ*_R)`, minimised over them, and never above the\n104\tempty-pair probability (`closed_cover_log_prob_na`). Its limit per customer\n105\tat fractions `(α, ρ, σ)` is\n106\t\n107\t    g_NA(α, ρ, σ) = H(α, ρ, σ) + inf_{ℓ_A, ℓ_R > 0} [σ ln(1 − e^{−ℓ_A}) + σ ln(1 − e^{−ℓ_R}) − σ (ϑ_A ℓ_A + ϑ_R ℓ_R) + μ E_s ln M_s(ϑ_A, ϑ_R)],\n108\t    ϑ = e^{−ℓ}/(1 − e^{−ℓ}),   M_s = σ^s + (α + σ e^{ϑ_A})^s − (σ e^{ϑ_A})^s + (ρ + σ e^{ϑ_R})^s − (σ e^{ϑ_R})^s\n109\t\n110\t(`closed_cover_exponent_na`; `M_s(0, 0)` is the avoid probability\n111\t`(1 − α)^s + (1 − ρ)^s − σ^s`, so at `ϑ = 0` the exponent is the pair\n112\texponent plus the `ln F` terms). Beside it, the **independent-sites form**\n113\t`g_ind = pair exponent + σ [ln(1 − e^{−λ_A}) + ln(1 − e^{−λ_R})]`,\n114\t`λ_A = μ E_s s [(1 − ρ)^{s−1} − σ^{s−1}] / Q_s` the mean number of `A`-type\n115\tpatterns containing a given separator customer — the `G(n, c/n)` form of\n116\t§6 (b), **an approximation and not a bound** here (coverage events are\n117\tpositively associated through shared patterns), reported for comparison.\n118\t`g_NA ≥ g_ind` always (the Chernoff optimum includes the tilting cost);\n119\t`β_closed` is the root of `sup` of `g_NA` over L2's admissible region.\n120\t\n121\t**(b) The flips.** The binomial moments of the number `I` of isolated\n122\tcustomers of a random cover of `a` customers are\n123\t`B_i = C(a, i) Π_j C(a − i, s_j)/C(a, s_j)` (patterns of size 1 isolate\n124\tnobody and are dropped), the item's generating function\n125\t`E[z^I] = Σ_i (z − 1)^i B_i` (`isolated_pgf`), and the pmf follows by Möbius\n126\tinversion (`isolated_pmf`). Jointly with the empty event, for `u` given\n127\tcustomers of `A` and `u′` of `R` to be isolated on their sides a pattern of\n128\tsize `s_j` must lie in `S`, or in `A ∪ S` with at most one customer of `A`\n129\tor none of the `u`, or likewise in `R ∪ S`:\n130\t\n131\t    B(u, u′) = C(a, u) C(r, u′) Π_j [C(a − u + s, s_j) + u C(s, s_j − 1) + C(r − u′ + s, s_j) + u′ C(s, s_j − 1) − C(s, s_j)] / C(n, s_j)\n132\t\n133\t(`flip_joint_moments`), the joint pmf `P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)` by a\n134\ttwo-dimensional inversion (`flip_joint_pmf`; in integers over the common\n135\tdenominator and separated into two one-dimensional sums,\n136\t`flip_log_bound_fast`, which does `n = 125` in under a second), and\n137\t`E[Y] = C(n, t) C(N, N − t) Σ_{i, i′} P(i, i′, empty) / C(i + i′, i)` exactly.\n138\tAsymptotically the same negative-association argument applies to the\n139\tindicators `D_v = 1[v lies in a pattern with ≥ 2 customers of A]` (given\n140\t`τ`, nondecreasing in the memberships of disjoint patterns' `A`-parts), so\n141\tfor `θ_A, θ_R ≥ 0`\n142\t\n143\t    P(I_A ≤ x n, I_R ≤ y n, empty) ≤ e^{θ_A x n + θ_R y n} e^{−θ_A a − θ_R r} E_τ[1[empty] (1 + c_A (1 − e^{−K_A}))^a (1 + c_R (1 − e^{−K_R}))^r],\n144\t    c = e^θ − 1,   K_A = −Σ_{j: |P_j ∩ A| ≥ 2} ln(1 − |P_j ∩ A| / a),\n145\t\n146\tand `a ln(1 + c (1 − e^{−K}))` is concave increasing in `K`, so a tangent\n147\tat `K*` factorises the expectation over the patterns. Summing\n148\t`e^{−ln C(i + i′, i)} P(I = (i, i′), empty)` over cells (polynomially\n149\tmany) gives the exponent\n150\t\n151\t    E[Y] ≈ exp n · { H(α, ρ, σ) + sup_{x ≤ α, y ≤ ρ} [ −(x + y) H₂(x/(x + y)) + Φ(x, y) ] },\n152\t\n153\t`Φ` the tail exponent above minimised over `(θ_A, θ_R, K*_A, K*_R)`\n154\t(`flip_cover_tail_exponent`, `flip_cover_exponent_na`); the **flip gain** is\n155\tthe pair exponent minus this, `≥ 0`, and `β_flip` the root.\n156\t\n157\t**(c) The half-giant line.** The customer → patterns → customers branching\n158\tprocess of the random intersection graph with `m = μ n` patterns and size\n159\tlaw `p`: a customer lies in `Poisson(μ p_s s)` patterns of size `s`, each\n160\tbringing `s − 1` new customers, so the survival probability `θ(d, μ)` — the\n161\tlimiting fraction of customers in the giant component of the MOSP graph\n162\t(Behrisch 2007, *Electron. J. Combin.* 14, R17, Theorem 1; Lagerås & Lindholm\n163\t2008, *Electron. J. Combin.* 15, R10) — is the largest root of\n164\t\n165\t    1 − θ = exp(−μ Σ_s p_s s (1 − (1 − θ)^{s−1})),\n166\t\n167\tzero iff the branching factor `μ E_s[s (s − 1)] ≤ 1` (`rig_giant`, checked at\n168\tthe critical cells exactly). At fixed size `d` the branching factor is\n169\t`μ d (d − 1)`, which is one exactly on loop0010's `e_g(r)` line (with\n170\t`e = μ(d − 1)`: `e (1 + e/μ) = 1`), so `e_g` is the giant's onset and the\n171\thalf-giant line `θ = ½` lies above it (`half_giant_line`, bisection in a\n172\treal `d`, the device of `threshold_d`).\n173\t\n174\t**(d), (e)** are the tables of §7.2–§7.3 and §7.5; the floors use the\n175\texact-size and old pair bounds (`cover_log_pair_bound`,\n176\t`learning.first_moment.floors`), the closed count with the candidate tangent\n177\tpoints only (valid at every `k`; optimising the tangent point per triple\n178\tlowers the bound by up to 7.8 decades at the top of the window on the\n179\tdensest 30 × 30 instance and changes a floor on 1 of 46 `(instance, k)`\n180\tcases checked at 30–50, so the closed floors below are valid and not\n181\tthe largest this count can give) and the exact flip count over the `k` window\n182\t`[xpair₀.₀₁ − 4, xpair₁ + 6]` (floors in `k + 2`); a floor is the largest `k` *in the window*\n183\twith bound below the threshold, plus 2, so every reported floor is a valid\n184\tstatement and possibly not the largest.\n185\t\n186\t### 7.2 The constants on the grid\n187\t\n188\t(`grid.csv`; `b` is the certified slope of `exc