1\t| CS 125×125 d=6 | 0.5300 | 0.599 | 0.880 | 0.5426 | +0.0126 | 0.5430 | 0.5324 | +0.0024 | 0.5441 (+0.0141) | 2.71 | 0.0310 (0.0317) | 0.0063 | 0.998 | met |\n222\t| CS 125×125 d=8 | 0.6700 | 0.722 | 0.936 | 0.6784 | +0.0084 | 0.6786 | 0.6709 | +0.0009 | 0.6788 (+0.0088) | 2.73 | 0.0212 (0.0217) | 0.0025 | 1.000 | met |\n223\t| CS 125×125 d=10 | 0.7626 | 0.804 | 0.960 | 0.7677 | +0.0051 | 0.7678 | 0.7629 | +0.0004 | 0.7678 (+0.0052) | 2.85 | 0.0136 (0.0139) | 0.0010 | 1.000 | met |\n224\t\n225\tWhat the table says:\n226\t\n227\t1. **The closed-pair count gains `+0.0111` at `(6, 1)` and `+0.0110` at\n228\t   `(4, 1)`: the brief's kill is met** at both named cells, as it was on\n229\t   `G(n, c/n)` (§6.7, `+0.0097 / +0.0082` at `c = 6 / 10`). The supremum of\n230\t   the exponent sits at the corner (`α = ρ = (1 − β)/2`, `σ = β`) in every\n231\t   cell (`closed_cover_rate` returns the corner to `10⁻⁷`), so the gain is\n232\t   the coverage term alone: on covers `σ · 2 ln(1 − e^{−λ})` with\n233\t   `λ = 3.57` at `(6, 1)` (`cover_lambda`), the probability that every\n234\t   separator customer shares a pattern with both sides. It is 17–18% of\n235\t   the gap to the certified slope at `μ = 1`, `d = 4–8`, 12% at `d = 10`,\n236\t   5–15% at `μ = 2`.\n237\t2. **The gain is a sparse-side one, largest at few, large patterns.** At\n238\t   `μ = ½` it is `+0.018, +0.021, +0.020` at `d = 6, 8, 10` — **above the\n239\t   `0.02` line at `(8, ½)`** and within `0.0004` of it at `(10, ½)`, 22–32% of\n240\t   the gap to `b` — because `λ_A` is small there (`2.4–3.2` against\n241\t   `3.6–4.7` at `μ = 1`): fewer patterns per customer make \"every separator\n242\t   customer sees both sides\" a rarer event. The brief's kill names `(6, 1)`\n243\t   and `(4, 1)`, and at those it is met; the `μ = ½` cells are recorded as\n244\t   the place where this count is worth most, not as a reversal of the\n245\t   verdict (the gain is `0.02` of `β` where `b − β* ≈ 0.07`).\n246\t3. **The negative-association bound costs nothing at the exponential\n247\t   scale**: `β_closed` from the valid NA/tangent exponent and from the\n248\t   independent-sites approximation differ by `0.0001–0.0010` in `β`\n249\t   (`0.0003–0.003` nats), and §7.3's finite check puts the NA bound's slack\n250\t   at a *constant* `0.2` nats, not a rate. So the exact closed-pair rate on\n251\t   covers is the independent-sites one to the fit's accuracy, and the\n252\t   `G(n, c/n)` form of §6 (b) carries over with `q(a) → 1 − e^{−λ}`.\n253\t4. **The flips are worth `0.001–0.006` in `β` at `μ ≥ 1` and `d ≥ 4`**, and\n254\t   the mechanism is the one item 09 was told to expect: the supremum over\n255\t   the isolated fractions `(x, y)` sits at **one side with no isolated\n256\t   customer** (`x* = 0` or `y* = 0` in 21 of 21 cells with `β* > 0`), where\n257\t   the swap entropy vanishes and the cost is the one-sided lower tail\n258\t   `α ln(1 − e^{−λ′})` (`−0.0069` nats at `(6, 1)`, tilted to `−0.0063`).\n259\t   The flip count is a *sparse*-side gain too: `+0.017` at `(4, ½)`,\n260\t   `+0.011` at the Chu & Stuckey `d = 2` sizes, `+0.009` at `(3, 1)` —\n261\t   where it beats the closed count — and `< 0.001` at `d ≥ 8`.\n262\t5. **Edwards & McDermid's line is far above the truth on covers**:\n263\t   `0.66, 0.86, 0.96` at `(4, 1), (6, 1), (10, 1)` against certified slopes\n264\t   `0.35, 0.58, 0.80` — a degree-sequence bound sees the clique-dense\n265\t   degrees (`Poisson(μ d) × (d − 1)`) and not the cliques. The published\n266\t   sandwich on covers is therefore `[β_closed, E&M]` = `[0.297, 0.660]`,\n267\t   `[0.524, 0.861]`, `[0.761, 0.957]` at `μ = 1`, `d = 4, 6, 10`, wide on\n268\t   the upper side; the project's own `β⁺` (`excess_phase_diagram.md` §7)\n269\t   is the tighter upper end and is not a published line.\n270\t6. **The additive estimate** reaches `+0.023–0.027` at `μ = ½`, `d = 4–10`\n271\t   and `+0.016` at `(4, 1)` and `(3, 1)`: if a single count realised both\n272\t   mechanisms it would pass the `0.02` line on the sparse side at `μ ≤ 1`.\n273\t   No such count is constructed here (a closed pair's isolated customers\n274\t   need not swap into another *closed* pair), and the number is an\n275\t   estimate.\n276\t\n277\t### 7.3 The checks: the exact alternating sum against the cancellation-free bound, and the half-giant line against the ensembles\n278\t\n279\t**The NA/tangent bound's slack is a constant, not a rate** (`slack.csv`;\n280\t`log E[X₃]` exactly by the `(u, w)` sum over every admissible triple\n281\tagainst `closed_cover_log_bound` with the per-triple optimisation, at\n282\t`k = round(β* n)`, `n = 8–26` in steps of 2; fitted slopes of\n283\t`NA − exact` in nats per customer): `−0.002` at `(6, 1)` (intercept 0.22),\n284\t`+0.002` at `(4, 1)` (0.17), `−0.040` at `(10, 1)` (1.07, on eight finite\n285\tpoints with `k` close to `n`, where the bound's candidate tangent points\n286\tare poor; the `n ≤ 10` points have no admissible triple). The exact closed\n287\tcount sits `0.05–0.3` nats below the NA bound at every size; the pair\n288\tcount sits above the exact closed count by a growing margin\n289\t(`+0.017, +0.026` nats per customer fitted at `(6, 1), (4, 1)`, noisy\n290\tbecause `k` is rounded). The check of §7.1 (a) is on record in `check.log`:\n291\t66 `(n, sizes, A, R, S)` cases at `n = 3–8`, `m ≤ 3`, against the\n292\tenumeration of every cover, maximum `|log diff| = 9 × 10⁻¹⁶`; the NA bound\n293\tabove the exact probability on all of them and on 60 random cases at\n294\t`n = 8–16` (gap `0.3–4.9` nats on single triples, which is where the\n295\tconstant comes from); 36 isolated-customer and 30 joint `(I_A, I_R, empty)`\n296\tpmf cases in exact agreement with enumeration; `pgf(1) = 1`; `θ = 0` at\n297\tbranching factor exactly one.\n298\t\n299\t**The half-giant line** (`half_giant.csv`; `θ = ½` at a real size `d`, in\n300\texcess `e = μ(d − 1)`; `e_g` is loop0010's giant-onset line, `e = 1` the\n301\tpair count's threshold):\n302\t\n303\t| `μ` | `d` at `θ = ½` | `e` at `θ = ½` | `e_g(μ)` | `e = 1` at `d` |\n304\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n305\t| ⅛ | 5.758 | 0.595 | 0.297 | 9 |\n306\t| ¼ | 3.413 | 0.603 | 0.390 | 5 |\n307\t| ½ | 2.316 | 0.658 | 0.500 | 3 |\n308\t| 1 | 1.736 | 0.736 | 0.618 | 2 |\n309\t| 2 | 1.408 | 0.816 | 0.732 | 1.5 |\n310\t\n311\tThe line lies between `e_g` and `1` at every `μ`, as the plan predicted\n312\t(§0: \"between `e_g(r)` and 1, inside regime II\"), at `e = 0.60–0.82`, i.e.\n313\t`0.26–0.30` above `e_g` on the sparse side and `0.18–0.40` below the pair\n314\tthreshold. For integer sizes it says: at `μ = 1` and `μ = 2` *every*\n315\tinteger `d ≥ 2` is above it (`θ(2, 1) = 0.80`, `θ(2, 2) = 0.98`), so for\n316\t`m ≥ n` the separator count's floor would be positive on every class,\n317\tincluding the `d = 2` classes where `β* = 0` at fixed size (`0.098` at the\n318\trealised sizes); at `μ = ½` it separates `d = 2` (`θ = 0`, the branching\n319\tfactor is exactly one) from `d = 3` (`0.75`); at `μ = ¼` it passes between\n320\t`d = 3` (`0.33`) and `d = 4` (`0.60`); at `μ = ⅛` between `d = 5` and `6`.\n321\t\n322\t**Against the measured largest-component fractions** (`giant.csv`, the\n323\t`fixed` ensemble frames at `n = 50–75`, 150 instances per cell; the\n324\tChu & Stuckey classes from the files): the uniform model's `θ` is **below\n325\tthe measured fraction in every cell**, by `0.1–0.4` where `θ < 0.95` — the\n326\t`fixed` generator repairs (every customer in a pattern, every pattern with\n327\ttwo customers), which lifts the realised mean size to `4.95` at nominal\n328\t`(3, ¼)` and `9.07` at `(4, ⅛)` and with it the giant (`0.65` and `0.62`\n329\tmeasured against `θ(d) = 0.33, 0.23` and `θ(mean size) = 0.70, 0.66`); and\n330\tthe Chu & Stuckey generator rejects disconnected instances, so all 200\n331\tfiles have `lcc = 1` against `θ = 0.81–0.94` for their `d = 2` size laws.\n332\tSo the check the item asked for cannot confirm the line on the ensembles\n333\ton record: the measured fractions are those of repaired and\n334\tconnectivity-conditioned models, and the line is a statement about the\n335\ti.i.d. model of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`. What the measurements do show is\n336\tthe ordering: the one cell on record near the line, `(d, μ) = (2, ½)`\n337\t(`e = 0.86` realised, branching factor exactly 1 at the nominal size), has\n338\tmeasured fraction `0.39`, the only cell below one half, and `(3, ¼)`\n339\t(`e = 0.96`) has `0.65` — consistent with a half-point near `e ≈ 0.6–0.9`\n340\ton the realised scale and with nothing finer.\n341\t\n342\t### 7.4 The half-giant line, and the threshold proposition\n343\t\n344\t**Proposition 7.1 (conditional).** Fix a bounded size law `p` and `μ > 0`,\n345\tand let `θ = θ(p, μ)` be the survival probability of §7.1 (c). *Hypothesis\n346\t(not verified by us):* the largest component `L₁` of the MOSP graph of the\n347\trandom cover has an exponentially small lower tail — for every `u < θ`\n348\tthere is `I(u) > 0` with `P(L₁ ≤ u n) ≤ e^{−I(u) n + o(n)}`. Then, if\n349\t`θ(p, μ) > ½`, the separator count's constant is positive:\n350\t`lim inf P(mospValue ≥ β n) → 1` for some `β > 0`, i.e. the first-moment\n351\tfloor turns on strictly below the pair count's threshold `e = 1`.\n352\t\n353\t*Proof.* By L3 (b) every instance with `mospValue ≤ k + 1`, `k = βn`, has a\n354\tset `S` of `k` customers whose removal leaves no component larger than\n355\t`t = ⌈(n − k)/2⌉` (`E_relax`). For a fixed `S`, the patterns restricted to\n356\t`V ∖ S` are independent and, given their restricted sizes, uniform subsets\n357\tof the `N = n − k` remaining customers (a uniform `s`-subset of `V` meets\n358\t`V ∖ S` in a uniform subset of its size), so `G − S` is the MOSP graph of a\n359\trandom cover on `N` customers with `μ_β = μ/(1 − β)` patterns per customer\n360\tand the sizes thinned by `1 − β`; its survival probability `θ_β → θ` as\n361\t`β → 0`. So `P(mospValue ≤ βn + 1) ≤ C(n, βn) · P(L₁(G − S) ≤ N/2)\n362\t≤ exp(n [H(β) − (1 − β) I_β(½)])` by the hypothesis applied to the thinned\n363\tcover, and for `θ > ½` the bracket is negative for all small `β > 0`\n364\t(`H(β) → 0`, `I_β(½) → I(½) > 0`). ∎\n365\t\n366\tThe citation status is the same as §6.4's: for `G(n, c/n)` the hypothesis is\n367\tO'Connell's theorem; for random intersection graphs item 01 found the giant's\n368\t*law of large numbers* (Behrisch 2007; Lagerås & Lindholm 2008) and no large\n369\tdeviation principle in any held paper, so the proposition is stated with its\n370\thypothesis and the line is a prediction of where the floor would turn on,\n371\tnot a theorem that it does. What is unconditional is the deterministic part\n372\t(L3 b and the thinning), and the location of the line (§7.3).\n373\t\n374\t### 7.5 The finite floors on the 200 Chu & Stuckey instances\n375\t\n376\t(`floors_cs.csv`; per instance with its empirical pattern sizes and its\n377\t`m`; the optima from `learning/data/excess.csv`, all 200\n378\t`certified:refutation`; \"old\" is the record's single pair with\n379\t`|R| = t − 1` (`learning.first_moment.floors`), \"exact-size\" the pair at\n380\t`|R| = N − t`, \"closed\" the closed-pair count with the NA/tangent bound of\n381\t§7.1 (a′), \"flip\" the exact weighted count of §7.1 (b); a floor is the\n382\tlargest `k` with bound below the threshold, plus 2; `< 1` is a statement\n383\twith up to 99% failure probability and is not a floor the paper quotes.\n384\t0.60 core-hours on 4 workers at nice 19, against 1 budgeted.)\n385\t\n386\t| floor | violations (`< 1`) | violations (`< 0.01`) | mean floor `< 1` / `< 0.01` | mean gap to optimum `< 1` / `< 0.01` |\n387\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n388\t| old single pair | 1 | 0 | 36.76 / 35.13 | 4.39 / 6.03 |\n389\t| exact-size pair | 2 | 0 | 37.28 / 35.72 | 3.87 / 5.44 |\n390\t| closed pair (NA) | 3 | 0 | 37.99 / 36.28 | 3.17 / 4.87 |\n391\t| flip (weighted count) | 2 | 0 | 37.52 / 35.90 | 3.64 / 5.25 |\n392\t\n393\t**Zero violations at the 0.01 floor for every count, as required**; the\n394\tstop condition of `TASK.md` did not fire. (The old and exact-size rows\n395\treproduce §8.4 of `excess_phase_diagram.md` to the instance.) At `< 1`\n396\tthe closed count exceeds the optimum on three instances (`Random-30-30-10-3`, `Random-40-40-8-3`, `Random-50-50-4-5`), the\n397\ttwo of §8.4 and one more, which is what a 99%-failure statement is allowed\n398\tto do and says nothing about the 0.01 floor.\n399\t\n400\tAgainst the exact-size pair floor at `< 0.01`, the **closed floor is\n401\thigher on 97 instances, equal on 103 and lower on none** (it is never\n402\tlower by construction: the per-triple bound is capped by the pair\n403\tprobability and the pair is one of the triples); the **flip floor is\n404\thigher on 35, equal on 165, lower on none** (`Y ≤ X` always). Class means:\n405\t\n406\t| `(n, m, d)` | opt | old | exact | closed | flip | old `< 0.01` | exact `< 0.01` | closed `< 0.01` | flip `< 0.01` | log10 at the exact floor's `k`: exact / closed / flip |\n407\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n408\t| (30, 30, 2) | 8.6 | 5.4 | 6.2 | 6.2 | 6.2 | 4.6 | 4.8 | 4.8 | 4.8 | −2.72 / −2.79 / −2.88 |\n409\t| (30, 30, 4) | 16.0 | 13.4 | 14.0 | 14.6 | 14.2 | 11.8 | 12.4 | 12.6 | 12.4 | −2.71 / −2.96 / −2.73 |\n410\t| (30, 30, 6) | 21.2 | 19.0 | 19.8 | 20.2 | 19.8 | 17.8 | 18.2 | 18.6 | 18.2 | −2.77 / −2.96 / −2.77 |\n411\t| (30, 30, 8) | 25.2 | 23.8 | 24.4 | 24.4 | 24.4 | 21.8 | 22.6 | 22.8 | 22.6 | −2.97 / −3.05 / −2.97 |\n412\t| (30, 30, 10) | 27.0 | 26.6 | 27.0 | 26.4 | 27.0 | 24.6 | 25.6 | 25.6 | 25.6 | −2.83 / −2.85 / −2.83 |\n413\t| (40, 40, 2) | 11.0 | 7.4 | 7.6 | 7.8 | 8.0 | 5.4 | 6.2 | 6.6 | 6.6 | −3.01 / −3.17 / −3.27 |\n414\t| (40, 40, 4) | 20.2 | 17.0 | 17.4 | 17.8 | 17.4 | 15.0 | 15.6 | 15.8 | 15.6 | −2.66 / −3.07 / −2.71 |\n415\t| (40, 40, 6) | 28.2 | 24.6 | 25.4 | 26.0 | 25.4 | 23.4 | 23.8 | 24.2 | 23.8 | −2.56 / −2.88 / −2.57 |\n416\t| (40, 40, 8) | 32.6 | 30.6 | 31.2 | 31.6 | 31.2 | 29.0 | 29.6 | 