{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01BK9gnQGA3PaEf7KLYSaDNr","type":"tool_result","content":"526\t  (run with 4 workers on 2026-11-01 because the one-minute load was\n527\t  above 24; the log says the wall time); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`.\n528\t* **Size range.** Every graph on 1–9 vertices; 20,000 random `G(n, p)`\n529\t  graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` with `p ∈ {0.15, 0.3, 0.45, 0.6}` (seed = job\n530\t  index); 2,000 random covers with 4–10 customers, `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 2–4\n531\t  customers per pattern (`generate_structured_instance(n, m, cpp, seed = index)`).\n532\t  The lemmas are for every graph; the counts in the table are for these\n533\t  sizes only.\n534\t* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero counterexamples to L1, L1′, L2 or L3 on\n535\t  310,266 graphs. The item's premise — that a narrow layout forces a\n536\t  balanced separator, a balanced empty bipartition and a closed pair at the\n537\t  forced sizes — stands; the three structures exist on every graph checked\n538\t  at `k = pw`, and at `k = pw − 1` they exist on 39–46% of graphs at\n539\t  7–9 vertices (27% of the covers), so at these sizes the *necessary* condition is far from\n540\t  sufficient — the slack the loop is about is already visible on nine\n541\t  vertices, and it is the condition's, not only the union bound's.\n542\t* **Does not claim.** Anything probabilistic: no rate, no constant, no\n543\t  statement about `P_N(E_bip)`. That the closed pair is canonical (the\n544\t  `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ on 3–14% of the pairs\n545\t  checked). That `E_relax` is a cheap relaxation at scale (it differs from\n546\t  `E_bip` on 2.2% of graphs at 9 vertices and 8% of the random graphs at\n547\t  12; the rate question is item 05's). That the share of graphs with a structure at `k = pw − 1` says\n548\t  anything beyond nine vertices. The Lean rows of §2.6 are statements,\n549\t  not proofs; item 10 proves them or names the obstacle.\n550\t\n551\t---\n552\t\n553\t## 3. The clumping decomposed: pairs, separators, closed pairs and move-classes on instances with exact pathwidth (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n554\t\n555\t**Question (B of the brief).** On a graph of pathwidth `k`, how many\n556\texact-size empty pairs are there (`X₁`), how many distinct separators\n557\tamong them (`X₂`), how many closed pairs of the forced sizes (`X₃`; and\n558\t`X₃′`, the image of the exact-size pairs under the `A`-first closure of\n559\tL2), and how many move-classes (`X₄`)? How fast do the ratios `X₁/X₂`,\n560\t`X₁/X₃`, `X₁/X₄` grow with `n`, in nats per vertex, against the `0.066`\n561\tthat a `0.02` gain in `β` needs at `c = 10` (the pair exponent's slope at\n562\t`β*` is `3.3` per unit of `β` there; `3.4, 3.9` at `c = 6, 4`;\n563\t`rate_preview.log`)? Each ratio is what one representative count merges:\n564\t`X₂` merges every pair with the same separator (the component flips of\n565\tplan §0, and the reversal), `X₃` merges every pair with the same closure,\n566\t`X₄` merges every pair reachable by single swaps.\n567\t\n568\t**The events counted, exactly.** `X₁` is the number of ordered pairs\n569\t`(A, R)` with `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge (the count of\n570\t`FirstMoment.lean`). `X₂` is the number of distinct `S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` over\n571\tthose pairs, which by L1′ (c) is the number of `k`-sets in `E_bip`. `X₃` is\n572\tthe number of ordered closed pairs with `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ N − t`,\n573\t`|S| ≤ k` (§2.3; enumerated by the pruned search of\n574\t`closed_pairs_pruned`, which agrees with the `2ⁿ` enumeration of\n575\t`closed_pairs` on all 2,200 rows at `n ≤ 16`, `k = pw`). `X₄` is the\n576\tnumber of connected components of the graph on exact-size pairs in which\n577\ttwo pairs are adjacent when their labellings `V → {A, R, S}` differ on at\n578\tmost two vertices; at exact sizes a single vertex cannot change class, so\n579\tan edge is exactly one swap of two vertices between two classes\n580\t(`A ↔ S`, `R ↔ S` or `A ↔ R`). When `N` is even, `(R, A)` is an exact-size\n581\tpair whenever `(A, R)` is, with the same `S`: the reversal is in `X₁` and\n582\tnot in `X₂`, and the parity split below separates it from everything else.\n583\t\n584\t### 3.1 Instances\n585\t\n586\t| source | cells | per cell | instances | exact value from |\n587\t|---|---|---|---|---|\n588\t| fresh `G(n, c/n)` | `n ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24}` × `c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 10}` | 200 | 4,800 | the C program `pathwidth_solver/cli/pathwidth -l -t 60` (every graph proved, `refutation` or `bound`; the layout kept in `instances.csv`); at `n ≤ 16` the subset DP `vertex_separation` agrees on all 1,600 |\n589\t| pilot `G(20, c/20)` | `c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 10}` | 137 | 548 | on record (`gnp_pilot/results.csv`); the overlap check at one size |\n590\t| certified covers, fixed generator | `n ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25}` × `d ∈ {4, 6}`, `m = n` | 150 | 1,200 | on record (`learning/data/ensemble/results.csv`, `pw = optimum − 1`); regenerated from seed; the DP agrees at `n ≤ 16` |\n591\t\n592\tSeeds of the fresh graphs: `crc32(\"sep03:{n}:{c}:{i}\")`, `i < 200`;\n593\tgenerator `networkx.gnp_random_graph(n, c/n, seed)` as in the pilot.\n594\tNothing on record was re-solved. The counts are taken at\n595\t`k = pw − 1, pw, pw + 1, pw + 2` on every instance (caps on the\n596\tenumeration were never reached: no row is marked skipped). The whole run\n597\tis **7 s** for the solves and **66 s** for the counts on 8 workers at nice 19\n598\t(516 s of enumeration in total, about 0.15 core-hours against the 3\n599\tbudgeted; the one-minute load was below 2).\n600\t\n601\t### 3.2 The counts at `k = pw`\n602\t\n603\tGeometric means over the cell (every instance has `X₁ > 0` at `k = pw`, as\n604\tL1 says). `X₁/X₂` and the others are geometric means of the per-instance\n605\tratios. (`cells.csv`; the medians and maxima are there too.)\n606\t\n607\t**Fresh `G(n, c/n)`:**\n608\t\n609\t| `c` | `n` | mean `pw/n` | `X₁` | `X₁/X₂` | `X₁/X₃` | `X₁/X₃′` | `X₁/X₄` | max `X₁/X₂` | max `X₁/X₃` | max `X₁/X₄` |\n610\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n611\t| 4 | 14 | 0.310 | 18.8 | 1.82 | 1.55 | 1.55 | 2.15 | 8.4 | 7.4 | 200 |\n612\t| 4 | 16 | 0.291 | 27.8 | 1.94 | 1.59 | 1.59 | 2.29 | 5.6 | 9.9 | 120 |\n613\t| 4 | 18 | 0.280 | 32.1 | 1.81 | 1.54 | 1.54 | 2.02 | 6.3 | 10.2 | 44.5 |\n614\t| 4 | 20 | 0.269 | 54.1 | 2.05 | 1.64 | 1.64 | 2.53 | 6.7 | 11.9 | 97.5 |\n615\t| 4 | 22 | 0.266 | 61.2 | 2.04 | 1.57 | 1.57 | 2.27 | 11.5 | 15.5 | 41.7 |\n616\t| 4 | 24 | 0.259 | 62.4 | 1.99 | 1.48 | 1.48 | 2.03 | 6.4 | 5.4 | 37.3 |\n617\t| 6 | 14 | 0.449 | 12.5 | 1.63 | 1.39 | 1.39 | 1.57 | 7.3 | 7.6 | 18.4 |\n618\t| 6 | 24 | 0.370 | 33.1 | 1.63 | 1.41 | 1.41 | 1.62 | 4.5 | 9.5 | 19.0 |\n619\t| 8 | 14 | 0.562 | 9.4 | 1.58 | 1.37 | 1.37 | 1.48 | 4.0 | 6.1 | 15.3 |\n620\t| 8 | 24 | 0.468 | 26.0 | 1.58 | 1.40 | 1.40 | 1.52 | 4.0 | 17.0 | 34.0 |\n621\t| 10 | 14 | 0.654 | 8.0 | 1.38 | 1.39 | 1.39 | 1.44 | 3.0 | 4.7 | 6.6 |\n622\t| 10 | 16 | 0.621 | 9.5 | 1.62 | 1.29 | 1.29 | 1.36 | 3.7 | 4.5 | 10.0 |\n623\t| 10 | 18 | 0.594 | 12.4 | 1.41 | 1.35 | 1.35 | 1.42 | 2.8 | 5.0 | 9.0 |\n624\t| 10 | 20 | 0.573 | 14.7 | 1.44 | 1.32 | 1.32 | 1.37 | 2.5 | 5.2 | 6.7 |\n625\t| 10 | 22 | 0.556 | 17.4 | 1.53 | 1.33 | 1.33 | 1.39 | 2.9 | 5.6 | 9.8 |\n626\t| 10 | 24 | 0.545 | 19.6 | 1.43 | 1.31 | 1.31 | 1.39 | 2.4 | 7.0 | 10.3 |\n627\t| pilot 10 | 20 | 0.571 | 13.7 | 1.50 | 1.30 | 1.30 | 1.36 | 2.6 | 4.9 | 7.5 |\n628\t| pilot 4 | 20 | 0.272 | 44.4 | 1.90 | 1.58 | 1.58 | 2.21 | 5.5 | 7.5 | 82.6 |\n629\t\n630\t**Certified covers (`m = n`):**\n631\t\n632\t| `d` | `n` | mean `pw/n` | `X₁` | `X₁/X₂` | `X₁/X₃` | `X₁/X₄` |\n633\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n634\t| 4 | 10 | 0.612 | 5.6 | 1.64 | 1.75 | 2.07 |\n635\t| 4 | 15 | 0.540 | 12.3 | 1.44 | 1.95 | 2.22 |\n636\t| 4 | 20 | 0.496 | 16.9 | 1.49 | 1.71 | 1.88 |\n637\t| 4 | 25 | 0.468 | 28.3 | 1.47 | 2.03 | 2.26 |\n638\t| 6 | 10 | 0.846 | 4.8 | 1.43 | 3.30 | 4.71 |\n639\t| 6 | 15 | 0.764 | 6.6 | 1.45 | 2.02 | 2.32 |\n640\t| 6 | 20 | 0.729 | 11.5 | 1.40 | 1.91 | 2.03 |\n641\t| 6 | 25 | 0.695 | 11.0 | 1.44 | 1.69 | 1.80 |\n642\t\n643\tWhat the table says, at these sizes:\n644\t\n645\t- **A typical instance at its own pathwidth has few witnesses.** `X₁` is\n646\t  8–62 (geometric mean) on `G(n, c/n)` at `n = 14–24`, 5–28 on the covers,\n647\t  falling with density and rising with `n` at `0.09–0.13` nats per vertex\n648\t  (fitted, §3.3). The pilot's `n = 20` cells agree with the fresh ones\n649\t  (`pw` means within 0.1, `X₁` and the ratios within 10–20%).\n650\t- **One separator carries, at the median, one pair — and its reverse.**\n651\t  `X₁/X₂` is 1.4–2.0 on the graphs and 1.4–1.6 on the covers, and the\n652\t  parity split shows what it is (`checks.log`): when `N` is odd (no\n653\t  reversal) the ratio is **1.08, 1.14, 1.23, 1.57** at `c = 10, 8, 6, 4`\n654\t  and **1.03–1.09** on the covers; when `N` is even it is **2.04, 2.05,\n655\t  2.14, 2.43** and `2.0–2.04`. So beyond the reversal a separator merges\n656\t  a factor of `1.03–1.6` of pairs, more on sparse graphs; `X₂ = X₁` outright\n657\t  on 13–14% of the graphs and 29% of the covers. The maximum over 200\n658\t  instances is 2.4–11.5.\n659\t- **The closure merges a factor 1.3–1.6 on the graphs, 1.7–3.3 on the\n660\t  covers**, and **`X₃′ = X₃` on every fresh and pilot graph at `k = pw`**\n661\t  (99.8% of the covers): at the pathwidth, every closed pair of the forced\n662\t  sizes is the closure of some exact-size pair, so the two counts of §2.3\n663\t  remark (iv) are one count here. They separate at `pw + 2` (`X₃′ < X₃`\n664\t  on 27–40% of instances), where the closed pairs that no exact-size\n665\t  pair reaches appear.\n666\t- **The move-classes merge a factor 1.4–2.5 on the graphs**, `X₄ = X₁` on\n667\t  39% of the graphs (every pair alone in its class), and the maximum over\n668\t  a cell is 7–200: the clusters a typical instance carries at `k = pw`\n669\t  are small, and the large ones are rare instances.\n670\t\n671\t### 3.3 The rates\n672\t\n673\tOLS slope of the per-instance `log` against `n` at fixed `c` (fresh graphs,\n674\t`n = 14–24`, 1,200 instances per `c`) and against `n` on the covers\n675\t(`n = 10–25`, 600 per `d`), with its standard error; nats per vertex of\n676\t`n`. (`slopes.csv`.)\n677\t\n678\t| source | `c` or `d` | `k` | slope `log X₁` | slope `log X₁/X₂` | slope `log X₁/X₃` | slope `log X₁/X₃′` | slope `log X₁/X₄` |\n679\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n680\t| fresh | 4 | `pw` | 0.127 ± 0.011 | 0.010 ± 0.003 | −0.003 ± 0.004 | −0.003 ± 0.004 | −0.001 ± 0.007 |\n681\t| fresh | 6 | `pw` | 0.100 ± 0.010 | 0.004 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.004 | 0.002 ± 0.004 | 0.003 ± 0.005 |\n682\t| fresh | 8 | `pw` | 0.100 ± 0.009 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.003 ± 0.004 |\n683\t| **fresh** | **10** | **`pw`** | 0.092 ± 0.008 | **0.000 ± 0.003** | **−0.003 ± 0.003** | −0.003 ± 0.003 | **−0.003 ± 0.004** |\n684\t| cover | 4 | `pw` | 0.104 ± 0.007 | −0.006 ± 0.002 | 0.006 ± 0.005 | 0.006 ± 0.005 | 0.002 ± 0.006 |\n685\t| cover | 6 | `pw` | 0.060 ± 0.007 | −0.000 ± 0.003 | −0.041 ± 0.005 | −0.041 ± 0.005 | −0.061 ± 0.005 |\n686\t| fresh | 10 | `pw + 1` | 0.193 ± 0.006 | 0.001 ± 0.003 | 0.007 ± 0.002 | 0.005 ± 0.002 | −0.070 ± 0.006 |\n687\t| fresh | 10 | `pw + 2` | 0.328 ± 0.004 | 0.005 ± 0.002 | 0.060 ± 0.002 | 0.038 ± 0.002 | −0.152 ± 0.007 |\n688\t| fresh | 4 | `pw + 2` | 0.280 ± 0.007 | 0.015 ± 0.003 | 0.015 ± 0.003 | 0.014 ± 0.003 | −0.126 ± 0.010 |\n689\t\n690\tAt `k = pw` **no ratio grows**: every slope of `log X₁/X₂`, `log X₁/X₃`,\n691\t`log X₁/X₄` is within `0.01` of zero at every `c` and both `d`, and at\n692\t`c = 10` the two the kill names are `0.000 ± 0.003` and `−0.003 ± 0.003`\n693\t— more than twenty standard errors below `0.066`. In the conversion of the\n694\theader, the separator and the closure merge **at most `0.01` nats per\n695\tvertex, i.e. at most `0.003` in `β`** at these sizes, against the `0.02`\n696\tthe brief's kill asks for. The covers at `d = 6` show the closure and\n697\tmove-class ratios *falling* with `n` (the `n = 10` cell, `pw/n = 0.85`, is\n698\tnearly complete and its forced sizes are 1–2, where everything closes to\n699\teverything). At `k = pw + 2`, with 100× more pairs, the closure merges\n700\tmore as `n` grows (`0.06` per vertex at `c = 10`, `0.04` for the image\n701\t`X₃′`) while the move-classes multiply *faster* than the pairs (`−0.15`):\n702\tabundant pairs fragment into many small classes rather than one fat one.\n703\tThese `pw + 2` rows are at a fixed offset, not a fixed `β`, and are not\n704\tcomparable across `n` as rates.\n705\t\n706\tAt `k = pw − 1` the share of instances with no exact-size pair at all\n707\t(the necessary condition alone refutes `pw − 1`) is 43–57% at `c = 4`,\n708\t60–68% at `c = 6`, 62–72% at `c = 8` and 65–74% at `c = 10` on the graphs,\n709\t47–87% on the covers, with no trend in `n` at these sizes; where a pair\n710\texists the median count is 1–4 (two is a pair and its reverse).\n711\t\n712\t**Beside them, the second moment on `G(n, c/n)`** (`second_moment_rate_gnp`,\n713\tthe `3 × 3` overlap table of `second_moment_rate` with Bernoulli edges:\n714\ttwo pairs are empty together iff no edge lies in `A × R ∪ A′ × R′`, of size\n715\t`2 t r − |A ∩ A′||R ∩ R′| − |A ∩ R′||R ∩ A′|`; exact finite form checked\n716\tagainst the enumeration of every pair of pairs at `n = 8, 10`; the\n717\tasymptotic sup checked against a `61⁴` grid at five `(a, c)`, agreeing to\n718\tfour decimals; `second_moment_gnp.csv`, `checks.log`):\n719\t\n720\t| `c` | at the cells' mean `β` (`n = 14 → 24`): `g(a)` | ratio exponent `sup − 2g` | maximiser | at `β*`: ratio exponent | maximiser |\n721\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n722\t| 4 | 0.62 → 0.54 | 0.12 → 0.16 | symmetric (`AA′ = AR′ = RA′ = RR′`) | 0.390 | same pair (`AA′ = RR′ = 0.221`) |\n723\t| 6 | 0.61 → 0.50 | 0.08 → 0.13 | symmetric | 0.370 | same pair (`0.259`) |\n724\t| 8 | 0.61 → 0.49 | 0.04 → 0.09 | symmetric | 0.335 | reversed pair (`AR′ = RA′ = 0.252`) |\n725\t| 10 | 0.59 → 0.49 | 0.02 → 0.06 | symmetric | 0.300 | reversed pair (`0.233`) |\n726\t\n727\tAt `β*` the Bernoulli second moment reproduces §8.5 (a)'s picture\n728\texactly: `E[X²]/E[X]² = e^{0.30–0.39 n}`, carried by the pair itself or\n729\tits reverse perturbed through `S` (the two are mirror images of one\n730\tcluster). At the `β` where typical instances sit (`g(a) ≈ 0.5–0.6 > 0`,\n731\t`E[X] = e^{0.5 n}`) the maximiser is the *symmetric* table — the second\n732\tpair overlaps `A` and `R` equally — and the ratio exponent is `0.02–0.16`.\n733\tSo the `e^{0.23 n}` is a statement about the large-deviation regime, where\n734\t`E[X]` is carried by rare instances; this item measures the typical\n735\tregime, and the two are different questions (the kill criterion says so).\n736\t\n737\t### 3.4 The overlap distribution at `n = 20`, `c = 10`\n738\t\n739\tTwenty fresh graphs (`pw` mean `11.25`, `β = 0.5625`, `a = 0.219`), every\n740\tordered pair of *distinct* exact-size empty pairs at `k = pw` (23,412 of\n741\tthem), the `3 × 3` table in units of `n` (`overlap_summary.csv`,\n742\t`overlap_hist.csv`, `overlap_per_graph.csv`):\n743\t\n744\t| cell | measured mean | median | p90 | extremal table at this `a` | product table |\n745\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n746\t| `A ∩ A′` | 0.061 | 0.05 | 0.15 | 0.056 | 0.048 |\n747\t| `A ∩ R′` | 0.059 | 0.05 | 0.15 | 0.056 | 0.048 |\n748\t| `A ∩ S′` | 0.084 | 0.10 | 0.15 | 0.106 | 0.123 |\n749\t| `S ∩ S′` | **0.453** | 0.45 | 0.55 | 0.350 | 0.316 |\n750\t\n751\t![Overlap of pairs of exact-size empty pairs at n = 20, c = 10, k = pw](scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/overlap_hist.png)\n752\t\n753\t*The overlap of two empty pairs on the same instance, `n = 20`, `c = 10`,\n754\t`k = pw`, over 23,412 ordered pairs of distinct pairs on 20 graphs: the\n755\tshares of `n` in `A ∩ A′`, `A ∩ R′`, `A ∩ S′` and `S ∩ S′`, with the\n756\tmaximising table of the Bernoulli second moment at this `a` (solid) and\n757\tthe product of the margins (dashed).*\n758\t\n759\t**Is §8.5 (a)'s extremal table what instances show?** Not at `k = pw`. On\n760\tthe instances, two pairs share **`0.45 n` of their separators** (`|S| =\n761\t0.56 n`, so 80% of `S`; median one vertex of `A` in `A′` and one in `R′`),\n762\tagainst `0.35 n` for the extremal table at this `a` and `0.32 n` for\n763\tthe product: pairs on an instance are *closer* to each other through `S`\n764\tthan the extremal table says, and `A′` meets `A` and `R` equally (the\n765\tsymmetric picture of §3.3, not the reversed pair of the large-deviation\n766\tregime). `A ∩ S′` sits *below* both reference tables: fewer vertices of\n767\t`A` move into `S′` than independence would give, i.e. the second pair is a\n768\tsmall perturbation of the first that keeps most of the separator and\n769\tswaps one or two vertices — the move-class picture, with the small\n770\tclasses §3.2 counts.\n771\t\n772\t### 3.5 Kill verdict\n773\t\n774\t**Pre-registered criterion 1: met.** At `c = 10`, `k = pw`, the slope of\n775\t`log(X₁/X₂)` is `0.000 ± 0.003` and of `log(X₁/X₃)` is `−0.003 ± 0.003`\n776\tnats per vertex, below `0.066` by more than twice the standard error (by\n777\ttwenty times). **Prediction recorded: the separator count and the\n778\tclosed-pair count fail item 06's kill** (`β_sep − β* < 0.02` and\n779\t`β_closed − β* < 0.02` at `c = 6` and `10`); by this instrument each\n780\tmerges at most `0.01` nats per vertex, `0.003` in `β`. The prediction is at\n781\t`k = pw` on typical instances at `n ≤ 24`, not in the large-deviation\n782\tregime at `β*`, where the maximising overlap is a different table (§3.3);\n783\t**items 05–06 are kept and only item 06 decides.** Item 09, the weighted\n784\tcount, is the route this item points to.\n785\t\n786\t**Pre-registered criterion 2: not met.** `X₁/X₄` does not grow at\n787\t`≥ 0.15` nats per vertex (`−0.003 ± 0.004` at `c = 10`; within `0.01` of\n788\tzero everywhere at `k = pw`). The clusters on typical instances are not\n789\tfat: the move-classes merge a factor 1.4–2.5, flat in `n`. §8.5's\n790\t`e^{0.23 n}` is therefore **not the typical cluster** at these sizes — it\n791\tis the second moment's statement about the rare instances that carry\n792\t`E[X]` at `β*`, and item 04 (the planted model, which *is* that regime)\n793\tis where its cluster is measured; this item's finding is that the planted\n794\tmeasurement cannot be replaced by typical instances.\n795\t\n796\t### 3.6 Regenerate, size range, what this does not claim\n797\t\n798\t* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/run.py --workers 8`\n799\t  (stages `solve`, `count`, `rates`, `overlap`; 7 + 66 + 1 + 1 s on 8\n800\t  workers); `python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/checks.py`\n801\t  (the grid check of the maximiser, the parity split, the `X₃` cross-check,\n802\t  the pilot against the fresh cells); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`\n803\t  (37 tests, 16 new: the enumeration against `exact_pairs`,\n804\t  `count_exact_pairs`, `empty_pair_count_brute` and `count_separators`;\n805\t  the pruned closed pairs against the direct enumeration and\n806\t  `X₃′ ⊆ X₃`; the down-closed family against brute force; move-classes on\n807\t  the path `P₆` and on two triangles with a pendant, against a BFS over the\n808\t  relation; the Bernoulli second moment against the enumeration of pairs\n809\t  of pairs at `n = 8, 10` and the rate against the finite form at\n810\t  `n = 30–120`; the overlap tables' margins).\n811\t* **Size range.** `n = 14–24` for the fresh graphs (`c = 4, 6, 8, 10`,\n812\t  200 per cell), `n = 20` for the pilot (137 per `c`), `n = 10–25` for the\n813\t  covers (`d = 4, 6`, `m = n`, 150 per cell); `k = pw − 1 … pw + 2`.\n814\t* **Does not claim.** Anything asymptotic: the slopes are fitted over\n815\t  six sizes at the small-size end (`n ≤ 24`, where §14 of\n816\t  `reports/ml_nature.md` found `n ≤ 40` constants failing at 125) and are\n817\t  reported with their standard errors and no extrapolation; \"flat in `n`\"\n818\t  means within `0.01` nats per vertex over `14–24`. Anything about the\n819\t  large-deviation regime at `β*`: the counts here are at `k = pw` on\n820\t  typical instances, and the second-moment table says the two regimes\n821\t  have different maximisers. That `β_sep` or `β_closed` will fail item\n822\t  06's kill — that is a *prediction* from this instrument, recorded as\n823\t  the kill asks, and item 06 decides it. That the overlap histogram at one\n824\t  cell (`n = 20`, `c = 10`, 20 graphs) is the picture at other `c` or\n825\t  sizes. The `pw + 2` rows are at a fixed offset from the pathwidth, not\n826\t  at a fixed `β`, and their slopes are not rates of anything.\n827\t\n828\t\n829\t---\n830\t\n831\t## 4. The planted cluster: typical, or a rare-instance artefact of `E[X²]`? (item 04, 2026-11-01)\n832\t\n833\t**Question.** Given one exact-size empty pair, how many *other* exact-size\n834\tempty pairs does the instance typically have, how far away are they, and\n835\tby what mechanism? §8.5 (a) of `reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` found\n836\t`E[X²]/E[X]² = e^{0.23 n}` at `(6, 1)`, `β*`, and §3 of this report found\n837\tthat typical instances at their own pathwidth carry no such cluster. The\n838\tplanted model is the large-deviation regime itself: `E[X²]/E[X]²` is the\n839\tmean of `X` under the law `P(· | P₀ empty)`, over the unconditional mean.\n840\tIf the *median* of `X′ = X − 1` under that law grows at the mean's rate,\n841\tthe cluster is typical and a representative that merges it gains the\n842\twhole exponent; if the median grows far slower, the second moment is\n843\tcarried by rare planted instances.\n844\t\n845\t**What the planted measure is, and is not.** `P(· | P₀ empty)` weights an\n846\tinstance by the number of its empty pairs: an instance with `X` pairs is\n847\tdrawn with probability proportional to `X`. Nothing here is a value of\n848\t`P(X > 0)` or of `P(pw ≤ k)`; the planted measure is the *right* measure\n849\tfor the cluster question precisely because it is tilted by `X`. No\n850\tpathwidth is computed: the planted pair makes `X ≥ 1` by construction and\n851\tthe pathwidth of a planted instance is irrelevant to the question.\n852\t\n853\t**Two identities, used throughout** (both from exchangeability of the\n854\tvertex labels, which `G(n, p)` and the uniform-pattern cover model have;\n855\tboth checked exactly against enumeration in `tests/test_separator_moment.py`):\n856\t\n857\t1. `E[X² ] = Σ_P P(P empty) · E[X | P empty] = E[X] · E[X | P₀ empty]`, so\n858\t   **`E[X′ | P₀ empty] = E[X²]/E[X] − 1`** — the planted mean is the\n859\t   second-moment ratio times the unconditional mean, computed exactly at\n860\t   the cell's `(n, t, r)` by the `3 × 3` overlap sum (`planted_mean_gnp`,\n861\t   `planted_mean_cover`; `second_moment_exact_cover` is the general-`r`\n862\t   form of `second_moment_exact`, pinned to it at `r = t − 1`). Checked\n863\t   against a simulation of the planted law at `n = 9`\n864\t   (`test_planted_mean_is_the_mean_of_the_other_pairs_on_gnp`).\n865\t2. `Σ_P 1[P empty] / X = 1[X > 0]`, so **`P(X > 0) / E[X] = E[1/X | P₀\n866\t   empty]`** — the whole slack of the union bound at a cell is the mean of\n867\t   `1/X` under the planted law, a bounded statistic that 200 planted\n868\t   instances estimate with a standard error of `0.01–0.03`, where the mean\n869\t   of `X` itself is dominated by the tail. Checked on every labelled graph\n870\t   at `n = 6` (`test_planted_inverse_mean_is_the_union_bound_slack`).\n871\t\n872\t**The events counted.** On `G(n, c/n)`: delete every `A–R` edge of a\n873\tfresh graph for a fixed `A` (`|A| = t`) and `R` (`|R| = r = N − t`), which\n874\tis exactly the conditional law. On covers at `(6, 1)`: `m = n` patterns,\n875\teach a uniform 6-set conditioned to meet at most one side (rejection\n876\tsampling; acceptance 0.28–0.43), which is exactly the conditional law\n877\tof the i.i.d.-pattern model of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean` (not the\n878\tensemble's `fixed` generator, which repairs empty rows). `X` is the number\n879\tof ordered exact-size empty pairs (item 03's enumerator); `X′ = X − 1`;\n880\tfor every other pair its `3 × 3` overlap table with `P₀` (`overlap_table`),\n881\tits Hamming distance `d = n − |A ∩ A′| − |R ∩ R′| − |S ∩ S′|` from `P₀`,\n882\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"aff6f89f-a521-42ba-b1bd-b72fdd32d4ff","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:59:34.370Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3/tool-results/b4726b81c.txt","content":"  (run with 4 workers on 2026-11-01 because the one-minute load was\n  above 24; the log says the wall time); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`.\n* **Size range.** Every graph on 1–9 vertices; 20,000 random `G(n, p)`\n  graphs at `n = 10, 11, 12` with `p ∈ {0.15, 0.3, 0.45, 0.6}` (seed = job\n  index); 2,000 random covers with 4–10 customers, `m ∈ {n, 2n}`, 2–4\n  customers per pattern (`generate_structured_instance(n, m, cpp, seed = index)`).\n  The lemmas are for every graph; the counts in the table are for these\n  sizes only.\n* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero counterexamples to L1, L1′, L2 or L3 on\n  310,266 graphs. The item's premise — that a narrow layout forces a\n  balanced separator, a balanced empty bipartition and a closed pair at the\n  forced sizes — stands; the three structures exist on every graph checked\n  at `k = pw`, and at `k = pw − 1` they exist on 39–46% of graphs at\n  7–9 vertices (27% of the covers), so at these sizes the *necessary* condition is far from\n  sufficient — the slack the loop is about is already visible on nine\n  vertices, and it is the condition's, not only the union bound's.\n* **Does not claim.** Anything probabilistic: no rate, no constant, no\n  statement about `P_N(E_bip)`. That the closed pair is canonical (the\n  `A`-first and `R`-first fixpoints differ on 3–14% of the pairs\n  checked). That `E_relax` is a cheap relaxation at scale (it differs from\n  `E_bip` on 2.2% of graphs at 9 vertices and 8% of the random graphs at\n  12; the rate question is item 05's). That the share of graphs with a structure at `k = pw − 1` says\n  anything beyond nine vertices. The Lean rows of §2.6 are statements,\n  not proofs; item 10 proves them or names the obstacle.\n\n---\n\n## 3. The clumping decomposed: pairs, separators, closed pairs and move-classes on instances with exact pathwidth (item 03, 2026-11-01)\n\n**Question (B of the brief).** On a graph of pathwidth `k`, how many\nexact-size empty pairs are there (`X₁`), how many distinct separators\namong them (`X₂`), how many closed pairs of the forced sizes (`X₃`; and\n`X₃′`, the image of the exact-size pairs under the `A`-first closure of\nL2), and how many move-classes (`X₄`)? How fast do the ratios `X₁/X₂`,\n`X₁/X₃`, `X₁/X₄` grow with `n`, in nats per vertex, against the `0.066`\nthat a `0.02` gain in `β` needs at `c = 10` (the pair exponent's slope at\n`β*` is `3.3` per unit of `β` there; `3.4, 3.9` at `c = 6, 4`;\n`rate_preview.log`)? Each ratio is what one representative count merges:\n`X₂` merges every pair with the same separator (the component flips of\nplan §0, and the reversal), `X₃` merges every pair with the same closure,\n`X₄` merges every pair reachable by single swaps.\n\n**The events counted, exactly.** `X₁` is the number of ordered pairs\n`(A, R)` with `|A| = t`, `|R| = N − t`, no `A–R` edge (the count of\n`FirstMoment.lean`). `X₂` is the number of distinct `S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)` over\nthose pairs, which by L1′ (c) is the number of `k`-sets in `E_bip`. `X₃` is\nthe number of ordered closed pairs with `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ N − t`,\n`|S| ≤ k` (§2.3; enumerated by the pruned search of\n`closed_pairs_pruned`, which agrees with the `2ⁿ` enumeration of\n`closed_pairs` on all 2,200 rows at `n ≤ 16`, `k = pw`). `X₄` is the\nnumber of connected components of the graph on exact-size pairs in which\ntwo pairs are adjacent when their labellings `V → {A, R, S}` differ on at\nmost two vertices; at exact sizes a single vertex cannot change class, so\nan edge is exactly one swap of two vertices between two classes\n(`A ↔ S`, `R ↔ S` or `A ↔ R`). When `N` is even, `(R, A)` is an exact-size\npair whenever `(A, R)` is, with the same `S`: the reversal is in `X₁` and\nnot in `X₂`, and the parity split below separates it from everything else.\n\n### 3.1 Instances\n\n| source | cells | per cell | instances | exact value from |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fresh `G(n, c/n)` | `n ∈ {14, 16, 18, 20, 22, 24}` × `c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 10}` | 200 | 4,800 | the C program `pathwidth_solver/cli/pathwidth -l -t 60` (every graph proved, `refutation` or `bound`; the layout kept in `instances.csv`); at `n ≤ 16` the subset DP `vertex_separation` agrees on all 1,600 |\n| pilot `G(20, c/20)` | `c ∈ {4, 6, 8, 10}` | 137 | 548 | on record (`gnp_pilot/results.csv`); the overlap check at one size |\n| certified covers, fixed generator | `n ∈ {10, 15, 20, 25}` × `d ∈ {4, 6}`, `m = n` | 150 | 1,200 | on record (`learning/data/ensemble/results.csv`, `pw = optimum − 1`); regenerated from seed; the DP agrees at `n ≤ 16` |\n\nSeeds of the fresh graphs: `crc32(\"sep03:{n}:{c}:{i}\")`, `i < 200`;\ngenerator `networkx.gnp_random_graph(n, c/n, seed)` as in the pilot.\nNothing on record was re-solved. The counts are taken at\n`k = pw − 1, pw, pw + 1, pw + 2` on every instance (caps on the\nenumeration were never reached: no row is marked skipped). The whole run\nis **7 s** for the solves and **66 s** for the counts on 8 workers at nice 19\n(516 s of enumeration in total, about 0.15 core-hours against the 3\nbudgeted; the one-minute load was below 2).\n\n### 3.2 The counts at `k = pw`\n\nGeometric means over the cell (every instance has `X₁ > 0` at `k = pw`, as\nL1 says). `X₁/X₂` and the others are geometric means of the per-instance\nratios. (`cells.csv`; the medians and maxima are there too.)\n\n**Fresh `G(n, c/n)`:**\n\n| `c` | `n` | mean `pw/n` | `X₁` | `X₁/X₂` | `X₁/X₃` | `X₁/X₃′` | `X₁/X₄` | max `X₁/X₂` | max `X₁/X₃` | max `X₁/X₄` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 14 | 0.310 | 18.8 | 1.82 | 1.55 | 1.55 | 2.15 | 8.4 | 7.4 | 200 |\n| 4 | 16 | 0.291 | 27.8 | 1.94 | 1.59 | 1.59 | 2.29 | 5.6 | 9.9 | 120 |\n| 4 | 18 | 0.280 | 32.1 | 1.81 | 1.54 | 1.54 | 2.02 | 6.3 | 10.2 | 44.5 |\n| 4 | 20 | 0.269 | 54.1 | 2.05 | 1.64 | 1.64 | 2.53 | 6.7 | 11.9 | 97.5 |\n| 4 | 22 | 0.266 | 61.2 | 2.04 | 1.57 | 1.57 | 2.27 | 11.5 | 15.5 | 41.7 |\n| 4 | 24 | 0.259 | 62.4 | 1.99 | 1.48 | 1.48 | 2.03 | 6.4 | 5.4 | 37.3 |\n| 6 | 14 | 0.449 | 12.5 | 1.63 | 1.39 | 1.39 | 1.57 | 7.3 | 7.6 | 18.4 |\n| 6 | 24 | 0.370 | 33.1 | 1.63 | 1.41 | 1.41 | 1.62 | 4.5 | 9.5 | 19.0 |\n| 8 | 14 | 0.562 | 9.4 | 1.58 | 1.37 | 1.37 | 1.48 | 4.0 | 6.1 | 15.3 |\n| 8 | 24 | 0.468 | 26.0 | 1.58 | 1.40 | 1.40 | 1.52 | 4.0 | 17.0 | 34.0 |\n| 10 | 14 | 0.654 | 8.0 | 1.38 | 1.39 | 1.39 | 1.44 | 3.0 | 4.7 | 6.6 |\n| 10 | 16 | 0.621 | 9.5 | 1.62 | 1.29 | 1.29 | 1.36 | 3.7 | 4.5 | 10.0 |\n| 10 | 18 | 0.594 | 12.4 | 1.41 | 1.35 | 1.35 | 1.42 | 2.8 | 5.0 | 9.0 |\n| 10 | 20 | 0.573 | 14.7 | 1.44 | 1.32 | 1.32 | 1.37 | 2.5 | 5.2 | 6.7 |\n| 10 | 22 | 0.556 | 17.4 | 1.53 | 1.33 | 1.33 | 1.39 | 2.9 | 5.6 | 9.8 |\n| 10 | 24 | 0.545 | 19.6 | 1.43 | 1.31 | 1.31 | 1.39 | 2.4 | 7.0 | 10.3 |\n| pilot 10 | 20 | 0.571 | 13.7 | 1.50 | 1.30 | 1.30 | 1.36 | 2.6 | 4.9 | 7.5 |\n| pilot 4 | 20 | 0.272 | 44.4 | 1.90 | 1.58 | 1.58 | 2.21 | 5.5 | 7.5 | 82.6 |\n\n**Certified covers (`m = n`):**\n\n| `d` | `n` | mean `pw/n` | `X₁` | `X₁/X₂` | `X₁/X₃` | `X₁/X₄` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 10 | 0.612 | 5.6 | 1.64 | 1.75 | 2.07 |\n| 4 | 15 | 0.540 | 12.3 | 1.44 | 1.95 | 2.22 |\n| 4 | 20 | 0.496 | 16.9 | 1.49 | 1.71 | 1.88 |\n| 4 | 25 | 0.468 | 28.3 | 1.47 | 2.03 | 2.26 |\n| 6 | 10 | 0.846 | 4.8 | 1.43 | 3.30 | 4.71 |\n| 6 | 15 | 0.764 | 6.6 | 1.45 | 2.02 | 2.32 |\n| 6 | 20 | 0.729 | 11.5 | 1.40 | 1.91 | 2.03 |\n| 6 | 25 | 0.695 | 11.0 | 1.44 | 1.69 | 1.80 |\n\nWhat the table says, at these sizes:\n\n- **A typical instance at its own pathwidth has few witnesses.** `X₁` is\n  8–62 (geometric mean) on `G(n, c/n)` at `n = 14–24`, 5–28 on the covers,\n  falling with density and rising with `n` at `0.09–0.13` nats per vertex\n  (fitted, §3.3). The pilot's `n = 20` cells agree with the fresh ones\n  (`pw` means within 0.1, `X₁` and the ratios within 10–20%).\n- **One separator carries, at the median, one pair — and its reverse.**\n  `X₁/X₂` is 1.4–2.0 on the graphs and 1.4–1.6 on the covers, and the\n  parity split shows what it is (`checks.log`): when `N` is odd (no\n  reversal) the ratio is **1.08, 1.14, 1.23, 1.57** at `c = 10, 8, 6, 4`\n  and **1.03–1.09** on the covers; when `N` is even it is **2.04, 2.05,\n  2.14, 2.43** and `2.0–2.04`. So beyond the reversal a separator merges\n  a factor of `1.03–1.6` of pairs, more on sparse graphs; `X₂ = X₁` outright\n  on 13–14% of the graphs and 29% of the covers. The maximum over 200\n  instances is 2.4–11.5.\n- **The closure merges a factor 1.3–1.6 on the graphs, 1.7–3.3 on the\n  covers**, and **`X₃′ = X₃` on every fresh and pilot graph at `k = pw`**\n  (99.8% of the covers): at the pathwidth, every closed pair of the forced\n  sizes is the closure of some exact-size pair, so the two counts of §2.3\n  remark (iv) are one count here. They separate at `pw + 2` (`X₃′ < X₃`\n  on 27–40% of instances), where the closed pairs that no exact-size\n  pair reaches appear.\n- **The move-classes merge a factor 1.4–2.5 on the graphs**, `X₄ = X₁` on\n  39% of the graphs (every pair alone in its class), and the maximum over\n  a cell is 7–200: the clusters a typical instance carries at `k = pw`\n  are small, and the large ones are rare instances.\n\n### 3.3 The rates\n\nOLS slope of the per-instance `log` against `n` at fixed `c` (fresh graphs,\n`n = 14–24`, 1,200 instances per `c`) and against `n` on the covers\n(`n = 10–25`, 600 per `d`), with its standard error; nats per vertex of\n`n`. (`slopes.csv`.)\n\n| source | `c` or `d` | `k` | slope `log X₁` | slope `log X₁/X₂` | slope `log X₁/X₃` | slope `log X₁/X₃′` | slope `log X₁/X₄` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| fresh | 4 | `pw` | 0.127 ± 0.011 | 0.010 ± 0.003 | −0.003 ± 0.004 | −0.003 ± 0.004 | −0.001 ± 0.007 |\n| fresh | 6 | `pw` | 0.100 ± 0.010 | 0.004 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.004 | 0.002 ± 0.004 | 0.003 ± 0.005 |\n| fresh | 8 | `pw` | 0.100 ± 0.009 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.002 ± 0.003 | 0.003 ± 0.004 |\n| **fresh** | **10** | **`pw`** | 0.092 ± 0.008 | **0.000 ± 0.003** | **−0.003 ± 0.003** | −0.003 ± 0.003 | **−0.003 ± 0.004** |\n| cover | 4 | `pw` | 0.104 ± 0.007 | −0.006 ± 0.002 | 0.006 ± 0.005 | 0.006 ± 0.005 | 0.002 ± 0.006 |\n| cover | 6 | `pw` | 0.060 ± 0.007 | −0.000 ± 0.003 | −0.041 ± 0.005 | −0.041 ± 0.005 | −0.061 ± 0.005 |\n| fresh | 10 | `pw + 1` | 0.193 ± 0.006 | 0.001 ± 0.003 | 0.007 ± 0.002 | 0.005 ± 0.002 | −0.070 ± 0.006 |\n| fresh | 10 | `pw + 2` | 0.328 ± 0.004 | 0.005 ± 0.002 | 0.060 ± 0.002 | 0.038 ± 0.002 | −0.152 ± 0.007 |\n| fresh | 4 | `pw + 2` | 0.280 ± 0.007 | 0.015 ± 0.003 | 0.015 ± 0.003 | 0.014 ± 0.003 | −0.126 ± 0.010 |\n\nAt `k = pw` **no ratio grows**: every slope of `log X₁/X₂`, `log X₁/X₃`,\n`log X₁/X₄` is within `0.01` of zero at every `c` and both `d`, and at\n`c = 10` the two the kill names are `0.000 ± 0.003` and `−0.003 ± 0.003`\n— more than twenty standard errors below `0.066`. In the conversion of the\nheader, the separator and the closure merge **at most `0.01` nats per\nvertex, i.e. at most `0.003` in `β`** at these sizes, against the `0.02`\nthe brief's kill asks for. The covers at `d = 6` show the closure and\nmove-class ratios *falling* with `n` (the `n = 10` cell, `pw/n = 0.85`, is\nnearly complete and its forced sizes are 1–2, where everything closes to\neverything). At `k = pw + 2`, with 100× more pairs, the closure merges\nmore as `n` grows (`0.06` per vertex at `c = 10`, `0.04` for the image\n`X₃′`) while the move-classes multiply *faster* than the pairs (`−0.15`):\nabundant pairs fragment into many small classes rather than one fat one.\nThese `pw + 2` rows are at a fixed offset, not a fixed `β`, and are not\ncomparable across `n` as rates.\n\nAt `k = pw − 1` the share of instances with no exact-size pair at all\n(the necessary condition alone refutes `pw − 1`) is 43–57% at `c = 4`,\n60–68% at `c = 6`, 62–72% at `c = 8` and 65–74% at `c = 10` on the graphs,\n47–87% on the covers, with no trend in `n` at these sizes; where a pair\nexists the median count is 1–4 (two is a pair and its reverse).\n\n**Beside them, the second moment on `G(n, c/n)`** (`second_moment_rate_gnp`,\nthe `3 × 3` overlap table of `second_moment_rate` with Bernoulli edges:\ntwo pairs are empty together iff no edge lies in `A × R ∪ A′ × R′`, of size\n`2 t r − |A ∩ A′||R ∩ R′| − |A ∩ R′||R ∩ A′|`; exact finite form checked\nagainst the enumeration of every pair of pairs at `n = 8, 10`; the\nasymptotic sup checked against a `61⁴` grid at five `(a, c)`, agreeing to\nfour decimals; `second_moment_gnp.csv`, `checks.log`):\n\n| `c` | at the cells' mean `β` (`n = 14 → 24`): `g(a)` | ratio exponent `sup − 2g` | maximiser | at `β*`: ratio exponent | maximiser |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4 | 0.62 → 0.54 | 0.12 → 0.16 | symmetric (`AA′ = AR′ = RA′ = RR′`) | 0.390 | same pair (`AA′ = RR′ = 0.221`) |\n| 6 | 0.61 → 0.50 | 0.08 → 0.13 | symmetric | 0.370 | same pair (`0.259`) |\n| 8 | 0.61 → 0.49 | 0.04 → 0.09 | symmetric | 0.335 | reversed pair (`AR′ = RA′ = 0.252`) |\n| 10 | 0.59 → 0.49 | 0.02 → 0.06 | symmetric | 0.300 | reversed pair (`0.233`) |\n\nAt `β*` the Bernoulli second moment reproduces §8.5 (a)'s picture\nexactly: `E[X²]/E[X]² = e^{0.30–0.39 n}`, carried by the pair itself or\nits reverse perturbed through `S` (the two are mirror images of one\ncluster). At the `β` where typical instances sit (`g(a) ≈ 0.5–0.6 > 0`,\n`E[X] = e^{0.5 n}`) the maximiser is the *symmetric* table — the second\npair overlaps `A` and `R` equally — and the ratio exponent is `0.02–0.16`.\nSo the `e^{0.23 n}` is a statement about the large-deviation regime, where\n`E[X]` is carried by rare instances; this item measures the typical\nregime, and the two are different questions (the kill criterion says so).\n\n### 3.4 The overlap distribution at `n = 20`, `c = 10`\n\nTwenty fresh graphs (`pw` mean `11.25`, `β = 0.5625`, `a = 0.219`), every\nordered pair of *distinct* exact-size empty pairs at `k = pw` (23,412 of\nthem), the `3 × 3` table in units of `n` (`overlap_summary.csv`,\n`overlap_hist.csv`, `overlap_per_graph.csv`):\n\n| cell | measured mean | median | p90 | extremal table at this `a` | product table |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `A ∩ A′` | 0.061 | 0.05 | 0.15 | 0.056 | 0.048 |\n| `A ∩ R′` | 0.059 | 0.05 | 0.15 | 0.056 | 0.048 |\n| `A ∩ S′` | 0.084 | 0.10 | 0.15 | 0.106 | 0.123 |\n| `S ∩ S′` | **0.453** | 0.45 | 0.55 | 0.350 | 0.316 |\n\n![Overlap of pairs of exact-size empty pairs at n = 20, c = 10, k = pw](scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/overlap_hist.png)\n\n*The overlap of two empty pairs on the same instance, `n = 20`, `c = 10`,\n`k = pw`, over 23,412 ordered pairs of distinct pairs on 20 graphs: the\nshares of `n` in `A ∩ A′`, `A ∩ R′`, `A ∩ S′` and `S ∩ S′`, with the\nmaximising table of the Bernoulli second moment at this `a` (solid) and\nthe product of the margins (dashed).*\n\n**Is §8.5 (a)'s extremal table what instances show?** Not at `k = pw`. On\nthe instances, two pairs share **`0.45 n` of their separators** (`|S| =\n0.56 n`, so 80% of `S`; median one vertex of `A` in `A′` and one in `R′`),\nagainst `0.35 n` for the extremal table at this `a` and `0.32 n` for\nthe product: pairs on an instance are *closer* to each other through `S`\nthan the extremal table says, and `A′` meets `A` and `R` equally (the\nsymmetric picture of §3.3, not the reversed pair of the large-deviation\nregime). `A ∩ S′` sits *below* both reference tables: fewer vertices of\n`A` move into `S′` than independence would give, i.e. the second pair is a\nsmall perturbation of the first that keeps most of the separator and\nswaps one or two vertices — the move-class picture, with the small\nclasses §3.2 counts.\n\n### 3.5 Kill verdict\n\n**Pre-registered criterion 1: met.** At `c = 10`, `k = pw`, the slope of\n`log(X₁/X₂)` is `0.000 ± 0.003` and of `log(X₁/X₃)` is `−0.003 ± 0.003`\nnats per vertex, below `0.066` by more than twice the standard error (by\ntwenty times). **Prediction recorded: the separator count and the\nclosed-pair count fail item 06's kill** (`β_sep − β* < 0.02` and\n`β_closed − β* < 0.02` at `c = 6` and `10`); by this instrument each\nmerges at most `0.01` nats per vertex, `0.003` in `β`. The prediction is at\n`k = pw` on typical instances at `n ≤ 24`, not in the large-deviation\nregime at `β*`, where the maximising overlap is a different table (§3.3);\n**items 05–06 are kept and only item 06 decides.** Item 09, the weighted\ncount, is the route this item points to.\n\n**Pre-registered criterion 2: not met.** `X₁/X₄` does not grow at\n`≥ 0.15` nats per vertex (`−0.003 ± 0.004` at `c = 10`; within `0.01` of\nzero everywhere at `k = pw`). The clusters on typical instances are not\nfat: the move-classes merge a factor 1.4–2.5, flat in `n`. §8.5's\n`e^{0.23 n}` is therefore **not the typical cluster** at these sizes — it\nis the second moment's statement about the rare instances that carry\n`E[X]` at `β*`, and item 04 (the planted model, which *is* that regime)\nis where its cluster is measured; this item's finding is that the planted\nmeasurement cannot be replaced by typical instances.\n\n### 3.6 Regenerate, size range, what this does not claim\n\n* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/run.py --workers 8`\n  (stages `solve`, `count`, `rates`, `overlap`; 7 + 66 + 1 + 1 s on 8\n  workers); `python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/03_clumping/checks.py`\n  (the grid check of the maximiser, the parity split, the `X₃` cross-check,\n  the pilot against the fresh cells); `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py`\n  (37 tests, 16 new: the enumeration against `exact_pairs`,\n  `count_exact_pairs`, `empty_pair_count_brute` and `count_separators`;\n  the pruned closed pairs against the direct enumeration and\n  `X₃′ ⊆ X₃`; the down-closed family against brute force; move-classes on\n  the path `P₆` and on two triangles with a pendant, against a BFS over the\n  relation; the Bernoulli second moment against the enumeration of pairs\n  of pairs at `n = 8, 10` and the rate against the finite form at\n  `n = 30–120`; the overlap tables' margins).\n* **Size range.** `n = 14–24` for the fresh graphs (`c = 4, 6, 8, 10`,\n  200 per cell), `n = 20` for the pilot (137 per `c`), `n = 10–25` for the\n  covers (`d = 4, 6`, `m = n`, 150 per cell); `k = pw − 1 … pw + 2`.\n* **Does not claim.** Anything asymptotic: the slopes are fitted over\n  six sizes at the small-size end (`n ≤ 24`, where §14 of\n  `reports/ml_nature.md` found `n ≤ 40` constants failing at 125) and are\n  reported with their standard errors and no extrapolation; \"flat in `n`\"\n  means within `0.01` nats per vertex over `14–24`. Anything about the\n  large-deviation regime at `β*`: the counts here are at `k = pw` on\n  typical instances, and the second-moment table says the two regimes\n  have different maximisers. That `β_sep` or `β_closed` will fail item\n  06's kill — that is a *prediction* from this instrument, recorded as\n  the kill asks, and item 06 decides it. That the overlap histogram at one\n  cell (`n = 20`, `c = 10`, 20 graphs) is the picture at other `c` or\n  sizes. The `pw + 2` rows are at a fixed offset from the pathwidth, not\n  at a fixed `β`, and their slopes are not rates of anything.\n\n\n---\n\n## 4. The planted cluster: typical, or a rare-instance artefact of `E[X²]`? (item 04, 2026-11-01)\n\n**Question.** Given one exact-size empty pair, how many *other* exact-size\nempty pairs does the instance typically have, how far away are they, and\nby what mechanism? §8.5 (a) of `reports/excess_phase_diagram.md` found\n`E[X²]/E[X]² = e^{0.23 n}` at `(6, 1)`, `β*`, and §3 of this report found\nthat typical instances at their own pathwidth carry no such cluster. The\nplanted model is the large-deviation regime itself: `E[X²]/E[X]²` is the\nmean of `X` under the law `P(· | P₀ empty)`, over the unconditional mean.\nIf the *median* of `X′ = X − 1` under that law grows at the mean's rate,\nthe cluster is typical and a representative that merges it gains the\nwhole exponent; if the median grows far slower, the second moment is\ncarried by rare planted instances.\n\n**What the planted measure is, and is not.** `P(· | P₀ empty)` weights an\ninstance by the number of its empty pairs: an instance with `X` pairs is\ndrawn with probability proportional to `X`. Nothing here is a value of\n`P(X > 0)` or of `P(pw ≤ k)`; the planted measure is the *right* measure\nfor the cluster question precisely because it is tilted by `X`. No\npathwidth is computed: the planted pair makes `X ≥ 1` by construction and\nthe pathwidth of a planted instance is irrelevant to the question.\n\n**Two identities, used throughout** (both from exchangeability of the\nvertex labels, which `G(n, p)` and the uniform-pattern cover model have;\nboth checked exactly against enumeration in `tests/test_separator_moment.py`):\n\n1. `E[X² ] = Σ_P P(P empty) · E[X | P empty] = E[X] · E[X | P₀ empty]`, so\n   **`E[X′ | P₀ empty] = E[X²]/E[X] − 1`** — the planted mean is the\n   second-moment ratio times the unconditional mean, computed exactly at\n   the cell's `(n, t, r)` by the `3 × 3` overlap sum (`planted_mean_gnp`,\n   `planted_mean_cover`; `second_moment_exact_cover` is the general-`r`\n   form of `second_moment_exact`, pinned to it at `r = t − 1`). Checked\n   against a simulation of the planted law at `n = 9`\n   (`test_planted_mean_is_the_mean_of_the_other_pairs_on_gnp`).\n2. `Σ_P 1[P empty] / X = 1[X > 0]`, so **`P(X > 0) / E[X] = E[1/X | P₀\n   empty]`** — the whole slack of the union bound at a cell is the mean of\n   `1/X` under the planted law, a bounded statistic that 200 planted\n   instances estimate with a standard error of `0.01–0.03`, where the mean\n   of `X` itself is dominated by the tail. Checked on every labelled graph\n   at `n = 6` (`test_planted_inverse_mean_is_the_union_bound_slack`).\n\n**The events counted.** On `G(n, c/n)`: delete every `A–R` edge of a\nfresh graph for a fixed `A` (`|A| = t`) and `R` (`|R| = r = N − t`), which\nis exactly the conditional law. On covers at `(6, 1)`: `m = n` patterns,\neach a uniform 6-set conditioned to meet at most one side (rejection\nsampling; acceptance 0.28–0.43), which is exactly the conditional law\nof the i.i.d.-pattern model of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean` (not the\nensemble's `fixed` generator, which repairs empty rows). `X` is the number\nof ordered exact-size empty pairs (item 03's enumerator); `X′ = X − 1`;\nfor every other pair its `3 × 3` overlap table with `P₀` (`overlap_table`),\nits Hamming distance `d = n − |A ∩ A′| − |R ∩ R′| − |S ∩ S′|` from `P₀`,\n","numLines":357,"startLine":526,"totalLines":882}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01BK9gnQGA3PaEf7KLYSaDNr","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
