{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01EzQ4NUEi1t4nmSdV2x5wew","type":"tool_result","content":"586\t**Met for the headline cells.** At every pilot cell with `c ≥ 6` the\n587\tseparator floor at `< 0.01` exceeds the pair floor by at most one stack\n588\t(`+0, +0, +1, +1, +0, +1`), so **the separator count is recorded as a\n589\trounding at these sizes**, as the exact-size pair was in\n590\t`excess_phase_diagram.md` §8.6 (4), and item 06 is told so. The rate\n591\tcomparison ran as the item requires, and is where the item's value is:\n592\t§5.3 (1)–(2) settle the two open readings of item 01, and §5.4 locates\n593\tthe gain on the sparse flank (`c′ ≤ 5`, i.e. `c ≤ 6` at `β*`), two to\n594\tthree stacks at `n = 120`, `c = 3–4`.\n595\t\n596\t**Prediction for item 06**: `β_sep(c) = β_relax(c)` of §1.6 to the\n597\taccuracy of the fit (the two events have the same rate to `N = 120` and\n598\tto three decimals in the fit); gains of `+0.011` at `c = 6` and `+0.004`\n599\tat 10 against the kill of `0.02`, so **item 06's kill is predicted met\n600\tat `c = 6` and `10`**, and not met at `c ≤ 4`. The prediction of §3.5\n601\t(the separator count merges at most `0.003` in `β` at `c = 10`, measured\n602\tat `k = pw` on typical instances) is consistent with the `0.004` at `β*`.\n603\t\n604\t### 5.6 What this does not claim\n605\t\n606\t* **A limit.** The rate of `E_bip` is computed exactly to `N = 120` and\n607\t  fitted beyond; the rate of `E_relax` is exact to `N = 400` and fitted\n608\t  beyond. That either equals `I_{c′}(½)` is a fit agreeing to three\n609\t  decimals, not a theorem; the LDP itself is cited (O'Connell 1998 via\n610\t  Puhalskii 2005), not proved here, and the upper-bound half of it is\n611\t  what a separator first moment would use. That `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n612\t  have the same rate is exact to `N = 120` and conjectural beyond.\n613\t* **The finite inequalities are the only things proved**, and the\n614\t  separator one on paper only after item 08 (the deterministic lemma is\n615\t  §2's L1′; the union bound over `S` with the inner probability is the\n616\t  statement item 08 writes and item 10 formalises). `B_pair` is the\n617\t  Lean theorem; `B_xpair`, `B_sep`, `B_rel` are valid by L1′ and the\n618\t  union bound and are not yet in Lean.\n619\t* The floors at `n = 120` are against the solver's *lower bounds* on\n620\t  unproved graphs (3 of 13 proved at `c = 3`, none at `c ≥ 4`), not\n621\t  against pathwidths; the comparison with the truth is at `n = 60` only.\n622\t* The `β` conversions in §5.4 are linearisations at `β*` for a preview;\n623\t  item 06 owns the constants.\n624\t* The thinning route's formula is the plan's, evaluated as written; the\n625\t  plan's value `1.19` at `c′ = 4.98` does not reproduce from it (`0.91`),\n626\t  and nothing rests on it since it is never the binding end.\n627\t* Nothing about covers (item 07): there is no LDP to quote there and the\n628\t  inner probability of a random clique cover minus a customer set is not\n629\t  `G(N, p)`.\n630\t\n631\t---\n632\t\n633\t## 6. `β_sep(c)`, `β_closed(c)` and `β_comb(c)`: the constants, against `β*(c)` and the slopes (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n634\t\n635\t**Question (D of the brief).** What constant does the separator first\n636\tmoment give on `G(n, c/n)` — the root `β_sep(c)` of\n637\t`H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) = 0`, `I(c′)` the rate in `N` of the inner event —\n638\twhat does the closed-pair count give (`β_closed`), what does a combination\n639\tgive (`β_comb`), how do the three compare with `β*(c)` and with the fitted\n640\tslopes at `c = 2–20`, and does the method's threshold move from `4 ln 2`?\n641\t\n642\t**Events counted.** Three first moments, each a valid upper bound on\n643\t`P(pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ βn)` by L1′ and L2 of §2 and the union bound:\n644\t\n645\t1. the **separator count** `C(n, k) · P_N(E_bip)`, `N = n − k`, and its\n646\t   relaxation with `P_N(E_relax)`; item 05 found the two inner\n647\t   probabilities equal in rate to `N = 120`, and the rate used here is the\n648\t   relaxed one (exact to `N = 400`, fitted beyond; the cited LDP rate is for\n649\t   `E_relax`);\n650\t2. the **closed-pair count** `E[X₃]`: ordered triples `(A, R, S)` partitioning\n651\t   `V`, `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ N − t`, `|S| ≤ k`, no `A–R` edge, every vertex of `S`\n652\t   adjacent to `A` and to `R` (`S = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, §2.3);\n653\t3. the **combined count**: closed pairs grouped by their separator `S*`,\n654\t   `|S*| = s′ ≤ k`. Given `G − S`, the empty bipartitions `(A, R)` with\n655\t   `|A| ∈ [t, t + k − s′]` (so that `|R| ≥ N − t`) are 2-colourings of the\n656\t   components of `G − S`, at most `2^κ` of them (`κ` the number of\n657\t   components), and each is closed with probability\n658\t   `Π_{v∈S} q_{|A|} q_{|R|} ≤ Qmax(s′)^{s′}` (`q_x = 1 − (1 − p)^x`; the edges\n659\t   from `S` to `G − S` are independent of `G − S`, and for distinct `v ∈ S`\n660\t   they are disjoint edge sets). Hence\n661\t\n662\t       P(pw ≤ k) ≤ Σ_{s′ ≤ k} C(n, s′) · Qmax(s′)^{s′} · E_{N′ = n − s′}[ 1[E_win] · 2^κ ],\n663\t\n664\t   with `E_win` the window bipartition event (one side between `t` and\n665\t   `t + k − s′` vertices; `E_bip` at `s′ = k`) or its relaxation (no\n666\t   component larger than `t + k − s′`). The inner factor is a *tilted*\n667\t   partition sum, computed exactly by the recursions of §5.1 with every\n668\t   part weighted by 2 (`tilt = ln 2` in `log_prob_bip` / `log_prob_rel`).\n669\t\n670\t**Size range.** Asymptotic constants (roots of exponents per vertex);\n671\tfitted rates from exact inner sums at `N = 100–400`; the threshold check at\n672\t`N ≤ 400`; the finite form at `n = 60, 120`, `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10`, every `k`\n673\taround the floors. Checks on record (`check.log`): the window and tilted\n674\trecursions against the enumeration of every labelled graph at `N ≤ 6`\n675\t(every window, both tilts; max difference `3 × 10⁻¹³`); the fast relaxed\n676\trecursion against the table one at `N ≤ 6` and `N = 60, 120, 200`\n677\t(`6 × 10⁻¹⁴`); the finite closed count against the enumeration of closed\n678\tpairs over every labelled graph at `n ≤ 6` (`3 × 10⁻¹³`) and Monte Carlo\n679\tat `n = 7, 8` (20,000 graphs each: `2.1, 0.8, 0.2` standard errors);\n680\t`β_sep` with the bracket's lower end equals `β*` at six `c` (to `2 × 10⁻⁶`);\n681\tEdwards & McDermid's Corollary 5.2 reproduces `0.183, 0.458, 0.656`.\n682\t\n683\t**Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/06_constants/run.py`\n684\t(stages `check`, `grid`, `constants`, `threshold`, `finite`, `draw`; 18 + 8 +\n685\t30 + 4 + 5 + 2 s, the grid and the finite stage on 8 workers at nice 19;\n686\tabout 0.05 core-hours against 0.2 budgeted). Outputs beside it:\n687\t`rate_grid.csv` (every `(c′, N, u)`), `rate_fits.csv` (the fitted rates per\n688\t`c′`), `constants.csv` (the table), `threshold.csv`, `finite.csv` and\n689\t`finite_floors.csv`, `check.log`, `run.log`, `beta_vs_c.png`. Tests:\n690\t`python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py` (70 tests, 10 new).\n691\t\n692\t### 6.1 Method\n693\t\n694\t**(a) `β_sep(c)` from three rates.** `sep_exponent(c, β, I) = H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β))`\n695\tand `beta_sep(c, I)` its root, for three rate functions `I(c′)`:\n696\t\n697\t* **fitted**: the log-corrected fit `−log P_N(E_relax) = I N + b log N + a`\n698\t  over `N = 100, 150, …, 400` (item 05's `logfit`, the form a polynomial\n699\t  prefactor gives), on a grid of 77 values `c′ = 1.00, 1.25, …, 20.00`,\n700\t  linearly interpolated between them (`rate_fits.csv`, column\n701\t  `fit_rel_half`; `load_fitted` in `run.py`);\n702\t* **bracket**: the plan's two analytic ends, `c′/4 − ln 2` (the product\n703\t  bound, i.e. the pair count's own rate) and `min(c′/4, thinning)`, each\n704\t  giving a root; the lower end gives `β*(c)` *exactly*, because\n705\t  `H(a, a, 1 − 2a) − c a² = H(β) − (1 − β)(c′/4 − ln 2)` at `a = (1 − β)/2`\n706\t  (`pair_exponent_beta`; tested);\n707\t* **cited**: `I_{c′}(½)`, the large-deviation rate of the largest component\n708\t  of `G(N, c′/N)` at `u = ½` in O'Connell's form as restated by Puhalskii\n709\t  (2005, *Ann. Probab.* 33, Cor. 2.4(3) and (2.13)–(2.14); §1.3 Table B),\n710\t  ported from item 01's `rate_check.py` into `ldp_rate`, whose value item 05\n711\t  checked against the exact computation to `0.0003–0.002` at seven `c′`.\n712\t\n713\t**(b) `β_closed(c)`.** The closed-pair exponent per vertex at side\n714\tfractions `(a, r, s)`, `a + r + s = 1`, is\n715\t\n716\t    g_closed(a, r, s) = H(a, r, s) − c a r + s ln q(a) + s ln q(r),   q(x) = 1 − e^{−cx}:\n717\t\n718\tthe multinomial counts the ordered triples; `(1 − p)^{|A||R|} → e^{−c a r n}`\n719\tis the empty-pair probability; and given `(A, R)` the events \"`v` sees `A`\n720\tand `v` sees `R`\" for the `s n` vertices of `S` are independent (disjoint\n721\tedge sets) with probability `(1 − (1 − p)^{an})(1 − (1 − p)^{rn}) → q(a) q(r)`.\n722\t`closed_rate(β, c)` is its supremum over the admissible region of L2\n723\t(`a, r ≥ (1 − β)/2`, `s = 1 − a − r ∈ [0, β]`; a grid over the triangle then\n724\tNelder–Mead), and `β_closed(c)` the root of the supremum. Checks: without\n725\tthe `q` terms the exponent at the corner `a = r = (1 − β)/2`, `s = β` is\n726\tthe pair exponent (tested); the finite count\n727\t`Σ n!/(a! r! s!) (1 − p)^{ar} q_a^s q_r^s` over the admissible integer sizes\n728\t(`log_closed_count`) equals the enumeration of `closed_pairs` over every\n729\tlabelled graph at `n ≤ 6` for every `k` (§6's check). **At every `c` of the\n730\ttable the supremum sits at the corner** (`a = r = (1 − β)/2`, `s = β`;\n731\t`constants` stage), so the closed count's gain over the pair count is the\n732\t`q` terms alone: `2β ln q((1 − β)/2)` nats per vertex at `β*`\n733\t(`−0.027` at `c = 10`, `−0.033` at `c = 6`, `−0.005` at `c = 3`), the\n734\tprobability that every separator vertex of the planted pair sees both\n735\tsides.\n736\t\n737\t**(c) `β_comb(c)`.** The combined count's `s′`-term exponent per vertex is\n738\t\n739\t    g_comb(s′; β) = H(s′) + 2 s′ ln q((1 − s′)/2) − (1 − s′) J(c(1 − s′), u),   u = ((1 + β)/2 − s′)/(1 − s′),\n740\t\n741\twhere `J(c′, u)` is the rate in `N′` of `E[1[no component > uN′] 2^κ]`\n742\t(`u` is the largest allowed component as a fraction of `N′`; at `s′ = β`,\n743\t`u = ½`). `J` is **fitted only**: there is no LDP to cite for the tilted\n744\tevent, and the fit is the same log-corrected fit over `N = 100–400` on the\n745\tgrid `c′ × u ∈ {0.5, 0.55, …, 0.8}` (`rate_fits.csv`, columns\n746\t`fit_tilted_u*`), bilinearly interpolated. `combined_rate(c, β)` is the\n747\tsupremum over `s′ ∈ [0, β]` on 42 points and `β_comb` its root; at every\n748\t`c` in the table the supremum is at `s′ = β` (`run.log`, the profiles at\n749\t`c = 3, 6, 10`: the exponent rises monotonically in `s′` to `0` at `s′ = β`).\n750\t\n751\t### 6.2 The rates on the grid, against item 05 and the LDP\n752\t\n753\t(`rate_fits.csv`; `fit_rel(½)` is the fitted rate used for the \"fitted\"\n754\tcolumn; `item05` is item 05's `logfit_rel_slope_N100` at its seven `c′`.)\n755\t\n756\t| `c′` | fitted `I(½)` | LDP `I(½)` | fitted − LDP | item 05 | tilted fit `u = 0.5` | `0.6` | `0.7` | `0.8` |\n757\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n758\t| 1.0 | 0.0001 | 0 | +0.0001 | — | −0.4431 | −0.4431 | −0.4431 | −0.4431 |\n759\t| 1.5 | 0.0021 | 0.0015 | +0.0006 | — | −0.3181 | −0.3181 | −0.3181 | −0.3181 |\n760\t| 2.0 | 0.0375 | 0.0362 | +0.0013 | 0.0375 | −0.1928 | −0.1930 | −0.1932 | −0.1932 |\n761\t| 2.5 | 0.1025 | 0.1006 | +0.0019 | — | −0.0894 | −0.0987 | −0.1041 | −0.1038 |\n762\t| 3.0 | 0.1852 | 0.1831 | +0.0022 | 0.1853 | 0.0238 | −0.0019 | −0.0297 | −0.0526 |\n763\t| 4.0 | 0.3812 | 0.3796 | +0.0017 | 0.3812 | 0.2829 | 0.2269 | 0.1553 | 0.0744 |\n764\t| 5.0 | 0.6005 | 0.5997 | +0.0009 | 0.6005 | 0.5499 | 0.4804 | 0.3654 | 0.2260 |\n765\t| 6.0 | 0.8328 | 0.8324 | +0.0004 | — | 0.8052 | 0.7375 | 0.5873 | 0.3879 |\n766\t| 8.0 | 1.3164 | 1.3161 | +0.0003 | 1.3163 | 1.3072 | 1.2343 | 1.0382 | 0.7232 |\n767\t| 10.0 | 1.8115 | 1.8102 | +0.0013 | 1.8115 | 1.8081 | 1.7229 | 1.4743 | 1.0627 |\n768\t| 12.0 | 2.3116 | 2.3081 | +0.0035 | — | 2.3101 | 2.2081 | 1.9039 | 1.3996 |\n769\t| 16.0 | 3.3178 | 3.3070 | +0.0108 | — | 3.3175 | 3.1769 | 2.7558 | 2.0573 |\n770\t| 20.0 | 4.3298 | 4.3069 | +0.0230 | — | 4.3298 | 4.1489 | 3.6077 | 2.7104 |\n771\t\n772\tThe fitted rate reproduces item 05's at all seven of its `c′` (to\n773\t`10⁻⁴`) and the LDP to `0.0004–0.002` for `c′ ≤ 10`; above `c′ = 12` the\n774\tfit drifts above the LDP (`+0.004` to `+0.023`), the `log N` term growing\n775\twith `c′` — the fit is a fit, and the LDP is what the \"cited\" column uses.\n776\tThe constants only ever need `c′ = c(1 − β) ≤ 8.9` (at `c = 20`), where the\n777\ttwo agree to `0.0005`. **The tilted rate is negative for `c′ ≤ 2.5`** at\n778\tevery `u` (the `2^κ` factor grows faster than the probability decays: the\n779\tnumber of components of `G(N, c′/N)` is a positive fraction of `N` there)\n780\tand costs, at `u = ½`, `0.161, 0.098, 0.051, 0.028, 0.009, 0.003` nats per\n781\tvertex of `N` relative to the untilted rate at `c′ = 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10`,\n782\t**more than the separator count gained over the product bound at the same\n783\t`c′`** (§5.4: `0.126, 0.073, 0.043, 0.026, 0.009, 0.003`; the\n784\t`E[2^κ 1[E]]` is dominated by partitions with more components than those\n785\tdominating `P(E)`, Jensen's inequality in the exponent).\n786\t\n787\t### 6.3 The table\n788\t\n789\t(`constants.csv`. `β_sep` three ways: fitted rate / bracket `[β*, root of\n790\tthe optimistic end]` / cited LDP rate; `β_comb` fitted only; the slope is\n791\tthe pilot's fitted `β_∞` where the cell has one, survey §7(d); E–M is\n792\tEdwards & McDermid 2015 Cor. 5.2 on Poisson(`c`) degrees, the published\n793\tupper line (owner's note of 2026-11-01); `dg/dβ` is the pair exponent's slope\n794\tat `β*` in nats per unit of `β`, the conversion of the gains.)\n795\t\n796\t| `c` | `β*` | `β_closed` | `β_sep` fitted | bracket | `β_sep` cited | `β_comb` fitted | slope | E–M | `dg/dβ` | gain sep (fitted) | gain closed | gain comb |\n797\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n798\t| 2 | 0 | 0 | 0.0059 | [0, 0.0087] | 0.0056 | 0 | 0.040 | 0.085 | 7.21 | +0.0059 (0.043 nats) | 0 | 0 |\n799\t| 2.5 | 0 | 0 | 0.0190 | [0, 0.0283] | 0.0185 | 0 | — | 0.132 | 7.46 | +0.0190 (0.142) | 0 | 0 |\n800\t| 3 | 0.0087 | 0.0096 | 0.0374 | [0.0087, 0.0558] | 0.0368 | 0.0035 | 0.099 | 0.183 | 5.53 | **+0.0287** (0.159) | +0.0009 (0.005) | −0.0052 |\n801\t| 4 | 0.0613 | 0.0671 | 0.0833 | [0.0613, 0.1210] | 0.0827 | 0.0594 | 0.158 | 0.285 | 3.91 | **+0.0220** (0.086) | +0.0058 (0.023) | −0.0019 |\n802\t| 6 | 0.1706 | 0.1803 | 0.1820 | [0.1706, 0.2480] | 0.1817 | 0.1782 | 0.268 | 0.458 | 3.38 | +0.0113 (0.038) | +0.0097 (0.033) | +0.0076 |\n803\t| 8 | 0.2628 | 0.2722 | 0.2691 | [0.2628, 0.3490] | 0.2690 | 0.2716 | 0.360 | 0.576 | 3.29 | +0.0063 (0.021) | +0.0094 (0.031) | +0.0088 |\n804\t| 10 | 0.3371 | 0.3453 | 0.3410 | [0.3371, 0.4266] | 0.3409 | 0.3451 | 0.415 | 0.656 | 3.30 | +0.0038 (0.013) | +0.0082 (0.027) | +0.0080 |\n805\t| 12 | 0.3973 | 0.4043 | 0.3999 | [0.3973, 0.4871] | 0.3998 | 0.4043 | — | 0.711 | 3.34 | +0.0025 (0.009) | +0.0069 (0.023) | +0.0069 |\n806\t| 16 | 0.4882 | 0.4933 | 0.4895 | [0.4882, 0.5750] | 0.4895 | 0.4934 | — | 0.782 | 3.45 | +0.0013 (0.005) | +0.0050 (0.017) | +0.0051 |\n807\t| 20 | 0.5535 | 0.5573 | 0.5543 | [0.5535, 0.6356] | 0.5542 | 0.5574 | — | 0.826 | 3.56 | +0.0008 (0.003) | +0.0038 (0.014) | +0.0038 |\n808\t\n809\tWhat the table says:\n810\t\n811\t1. **The separator count's gain is a near-threshold gain.** `+0.029` at\n812\t   `c = 3` (32% of the gap of 0.090 to the fitted slope), `+0.022` at `c = 4`\n813\t   (23%), `+0.011` at `c = 6` (12%), `+0.004` at `c = 10` (5%), `+0.0008` at\n814\t   `c = 20`; and a positive constant at `c = 2` and `2.5`, where `β* = 0`\n815\t   (`+0.006` and `+0.019`; 15% of the gap at `c = 2`). The fitted and the\n816\t   cited columns agree to `0.0006` everywhere; the bracket's optimistic end\n817\t   is far above both (`0.248` against `0.182` at `c = 6`) and is not the\n818\t   truth, as §5.3 said. §1.6's preview from the quotation alone is\n819\t   reproduced to `0.0005` at every `c`.\n820\t2. **The closed-pair count's gain is a dense-side gain**: `+0.001` at\n821\t   `c = 3`, `+0.006` at 4, `+0.010` at 6, `+0.009` at 8, `+0.008` at 10,\n822\t   `+0.004` at 20 — 1%, 6%, 10%, 10%, 11% of the gap where a slope exists.\n823\t   The planner's `0.1803 / 0.3453` at `c = 6 / 10` (plan §0) are reproduced\n824\t   exactly. The separator count wins at `c ≤ 6` (by a hair at 6: `0.1820`\n825\t   against `0.1803`), the closed count at `c ≥ 8`.\n826\t3. **The combined count, as constructed, is not the best on the ladder.**\n827\t   Its constant lies below the closed count's at every `c` and *below `β*`*\n828\t   at `c ≤ 4` (`0.0035` against `0.0087` at `c = 3`): the `2^κ` bound on the\n829\t   number of balanced 2-colourings of the components costs more than the\n830\t   separator count gained (§6.2), so grouping the closed pairs by `S` with\n831\t   that bound gives the separator's gain back and keeps only the `q` terms,\n832\t   less a little. At `c ≥ 8` it is within `0.0006` of the closed count. The\n833\t   best *valid* first moment at each `c` is the larger of `β_sep` and\n834\t   `β_closed`, both valid separately; a combination that beats both needs\n835\t   the closedness condition inside the component law (the probability that\n836\t   *some* balanced colouring of the components of `G − S` has every vertex\n837\t   of `S` adjacent to both colours), which is not a tilt of the partition\n838\t   sum and is not computed here.\n839\t4. **Against the data**: the best of the three constants sits at `0.037,\n840\t   0.083, 0.182, 0.272, 0.345` against fitted slopes `0.099, 0.158, 0.268,\n841\t   0.360, 0.415` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10`, closing 32%, 23%, 12%, 10%, 11%\n842\t   of the gap. The provable sandwich with the published upper line is\n843\t   `[0.037, 0.183]`, `[0.182, 0.458]`, `[0.345, 0.656]` at `c = 3, 6, 10`\n844\t   (was `[0.009, 0.183]`, `[0.171, 0.458]`, `[0.337, 0.656]` with `β*`);\n845\t   the ratio upper/lower at `c = 3` falls from 21 to 4.9.\n846\t5. **In nats**: the separator gain at `β*` is `0.159, 0.086, 0.038, 0.021,\n847\t   0.013` nats per vertex of `n` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10` (the slopes `dg/dβ`\n848\t   are `5.53, 3.91, 3.38, 3.29, 3.30`, so `g/3.3` is the conversion at\n849\t   `c ≥ 6` and `g/5.5` at `c = 3`); the closed gain `0.005, 0.023, 0.033,\n850\t   0.031, 0.027`. Item 04's measured union-bound slack on `G(n, c/n)` at `β*`\n851\t   and `n ≤ 32` was `0.065` and `0.039` nats at `c = 6` and `10` (§4.6):\n852\t   the separator count recovers 59% and 33% of it, the closed count 51%\n853\t   and 69%, and neither both.\n854\t\n855\t### 6.4 The threshold\n856\t\n857\t**Proposition (conditional).** *Suppose that for every `c′ > 2 ln 2` there\n858\tis `I(c′) > 0` with `P(no component of G(N, c′/N) has more than N/2\n859\tvertices) ≤ e^{−(I(c′) − o(1)) N}`, with `I` continuous in `c′`. Then\n860\t`β_sep(c) > 0` for every `c > 2 ln 2`: there is `β₀ = β₀(c) > 0` with\n861\t`P(pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ β₀ n) → 0`.* Proof: by L1′ (§2.2) and the union bound,\n862\t`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ C(n, βn) P_{N}(E_relax) ≤ e^{n[H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) + o(1)]}`;\n863\tas `β → 0`, `H(β) → 0` and `(1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) → I(c) > 0`, so the exponent\n864\tis negative for all small `β > 0`. ∎ (The pair count's exponent at `β → 0`\n865\tis `ln 2 − c/4`, negative only for `c > 4 ln 2`; the true threshold of linear\n866\tpathwidth is `c = 1`, Lee–Lee–Oum 2012 via `tw ≤ pw`, survey §1.1.)\n867\t\n868\t**Status of the hypothesis: cited, not verified by us as a theorem.** It is\n869\tthe upper-bound half of the large-deviation principle for the largest\n870\tcomponent of `G(N, c′/N)` at scale `N`, applied to the closed set\n871\t`[0, ½]`: O'Connell 1998 (*not held*; Theorem 3.1 as restated), Puhalskii\n872\t2005 (*Ann. Probab.* 33(2), held; Cor. 2.4(3), p. 8 of the arXiv version,\n873\twith the rate function (2.13)–(2.14) and the remark that O'Connell's\n874\tstatement has a misprint), Andreis–König–Patterson 2021 (held; Cor. 1.3\n875\tgives the macroscopic contraction). `I_{c′}(½) > 0` iff `c′ > 2 ln 2`\n876\t(§1.4, `rate_check.log`), and continuity in `c′` is read off the formula.\n877\tWhat *is* verified here is the value of the quoted formula at finite `N`\n878\t(§5.3; §6.2), not the principle.\n879\t\n880\t**Numerical check at `N ≤ 400`** (`threshold.csv`; `−log P_N(E_relax)/N` by\n881\tthe exact recursion; the log-corrected fit over `N ≥ 100`):\n882\t\n883\t| `c` | `I_c(½)` | `N = 50` | 100 | 200 | 300 | 400 | log-fit `I` (`b`) |\n884\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n885\t| 1.2 (control, below) | 0 | 0.0042 | 0.0013 | 0.0003 | 0.0001 | 0.00005 | +0.0002 (−0.12) |\n886\t| 2 ln 2 (threshold) | 0 | 0.0111 | 0.0055 | 0.0029 | 0.0020 | 0.0015 | +0.0001 (+0.02) |\n887\t| 1.5 | 0.0015 | 0.0175 | 0.0102 | 0.0066 | 0.0053 | 0.0046 | +0.0021 (+0.12) |\n888\t| 2 | 0.0362 | 0.0646 | 0.0514 | 0.0449 | 0.0427 | 0.0414 | +0.0375 (+0.16) |\n889\t| 2.5 | 0.1006 | 0.1372 | 0.1191 | 0.1107 | 0.1080 | 0.1066 | +0.1025 (−0.01) |\n890\t\n891\tAt `c = 2` and `2.5` the finite rate is bounded away from zero at every\n892\t`N` and the fit lands `0.001–0.002` above the cited value; at `c = 1.5` the\n893\tfinite values are still falling at `N = 400` (`0.0046` against a cited\n894\tlimit of `0.0015`) and the fit gives `0.0021`, of the limit's order but\n895\tnot a confirmation; the controls at and below `2 ln 2` fall like `1/N`\n896\t(`b ≈ 0`, `I ≈ 0`). So the check is consistent with the proposition at\n897\t`c = 2, 2.5`, undecided by itself at `c = 1.5`, and no finite computation\n898\tcan establish a limit. `P_N(E_bip)` at `N = 100` equals `P_N(E_relax)` to\n899\tfour decimals at every `c` in the table (column `rate_bip`).\n900\t\n901\t### 6.5 The finite form at `n = 60` and `120`, in stacks\n902\t\n903\tLargest `k` with each bound below `1` and below `0.01` (`finite_floors.csv`;\n904\tpair, exact-size pair and separator from item 05's `inner.csv`; the closed\n905\tand combined counts new; `comb` is the relaxed-window form, `comb_bip` the\n906\tbipartition-window form at `n = 60` only; the pilot's observed minimum\n907\tpathwidth at 60 and the solver's lower bound at 120, §5.2):\n908\t\n909\t| `n` | `c` | pair `<1` / `<.01` | xpair | **sep** | **closed** | comb | comb_bip | `β*n` | min pw / LB |\n910\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n911\t| 60 | 3 | 1 / −1 | 1 / 0 | **3 / 1** | 1 / 0 | 0 / −1 | 0 / −1 | 0.5 | 5 |\n912\t| 60 | 4 | 5 / 3 | 5 / 3 | **6 / 4** | 5 / 4 | 4 / 3 | 4 / 3 | 3.7 | 8 |\n913\t| 60 | 6 | 11 / 11 | 12 / 11 | 12 / 11 | **13 / 11** | 12 / 10 | 12 / 10 | 10.2 | 16 |\n914\t| 60 | 8 | 17 / 17 | 18 / 17 | 18 / 17 | **19 / 17** | 18 / 16 | 18 / 16 | 15.8 | 20 |\n915\t| 60 | 10 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 22 | 23 / 22 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 21 | 20.2 | 25 |\n916\t| 120 | 3 | 1 / 1 | 2 / 1 | **5 / 4** | 2 / 1 | 0 / −1 | — | 1.0 | 11 / 12 |\n917\t| 120 | 4 | 9 / 7 | 9 / 7 | **11 / 10** | 9 / 8 | 8 / 6 | — | 7.4 | — / 17 |\n918\t| 120 | 6 | 21 / 21 | 22 / 21 | 24 / 22 | 24 / 22 | 22 / 21 | — | 20.5 | — / 25 |\n919\t| 120 | 8 | 33 / 33 | 34 / 33 | 34 / 33 | **35 / 34** | 34 / 33 | — | 31.5 | — / 34 |\n920\t| 120 | 10 | 43 / 41 | 43 / 42 | 44 / 42 | **44 / 43** | 43 / 42 | — | 40.5 | — / 41 |\n921\t\n922\tIn stacks the picture is the asymptotic one: at `c = 3–4` the separator\n923\tcount is the best by 2–3 stacks over everything else; at `c ≥ 6` the\n924\tclosed count ties or beats the separator count by one stack (`+1` at\n925\t`(60, 6)`, `(60, 8)` at `< 1`; `+1` at `(120, 8)`, `(120, 10)` at `< 0.01`),\n926\tand both are one to two stacks above the pair count; the combined count\n927\tis one stack below the closed count or worse. At `(120, 10)` the closed\n928\tcount says `P(pw ≤ 44) ≤ 0.10` where the separator count said `0.68` and\n929\tthe pair count nothing, against solver lower bounds of 41–42; the decades\n930\taround the floor (`run.log`): `k = 43`: closed `−2.28`, sep `−1.38`, pair\n931\t`−0.99`; `k = 44`: closed `−0.98`, sep `−0.17`, pair `+1.76`; `k = 45`:\n932\tclosed `+0.28`. The bipartition-window and the relaxed-window forms of\n933\tthe combined count agree to `0.01` decades at every `(60, c, k)`: the\n934\tnumber-partitioning condition is free here too (§5.4).\n935\t\n936\t### 6.6 The figure\n937\t\n938\t`beta_vs_c.png`: left, `β` against `c` — `β*`, `β_sep` (cited rate) with\n939\tthe bracket shaded, `β_closed`, the pilot's fitted slopes, and Edwards &\n940\tMcDermid's published upper line, with the two thresholds `2 ln 2` and\n941\t`4 ln 2` marked; right, the gains over `β*` — the separator count (cited\n942\tand fitted), the closed count, the combined count, the bracket, and the\n943\tgap to the fitted slope, with the brief's kill line at `0.02`. The four\n944\tlower curves on the left are indistinguishable at the scale of the\n945\tsandwich, which is the finding; the right panel is where they separate.\n946\t(Categorical palette validated: six slots, all checks pass with the\n947\tcontrast warning on three, relieved by the legend and direct labels.)\n948\t\n949\t### 6.7 Kill verdict\n950\t\n951\t**The brief's kill: met.** `β_sep(fitted) − β* = +0.0113` at `c = 6` and\n952\t`+0.0038` at `c = 10`, both below `0.02`; **the separator route is closed\n953\tfor the headline constants**, as items 03 and 05 predicted (§3.5: at most\n954\t`0.003` at `c = 10`; §5.5: `+0.011` and `+0.004`). Beside it, as the item\n955\tasks: the closed-pair gain is `+0.0097 / +0.0082`, the combined count's\n956\t`+0.0076 / +0.0080`; none of the three, nor their maximum, reaches `0.02`\n957\tat either `c`.\n958\t\n959\t**The pre-registered separate findings, and what they are worth.**\n960\t\n961\t* *Near the threshold* the separator count is not a rounding: `+0.029` at\n962\t  `c = 3` and `+0.022` at `c = 4` in `β`, 32% and 23% of the gap to the\n963\t  fitted slopes — above the brief's kill and at the top of what §1.4's\n964\t  record of representative counts ever bought (1.7–25% of a gap) — and a\n965\t  positive constant at `c = 2, 2.5` where the pair count is vacuous. The\n966\t  plan's bracket at `c = 3` was `[0.009, 0.056]`; the answer is `0.037`,\n967\t  against a slope of `0.099`. These rest on the relaxed event's rate,\n968\t  cited (the LDP) and reproduced by exact computation to `N = 400`.\n969\t* *The threshold* moves from `4 ln 2 = 2.77` to `2 ln 2 = 1.39`,\n970\t  conditional on the cited LDP upper bound (§6.4, hypothesis explicit);\n971\t  the truth is `1`. In the logarithm this is half of the way.\n972\t* *The closed-pair count* is the better of the two at `c ≥ 8`, by the `q`\n973\t  terms alone (the supremum sits at the corner at every `c`); its gain is\n974\t  `+0.008–0.010` at `c = 6–12`, exactly the planner's estimate.\n975\t\n976\t**Where this corrects the loop's own predictions.** §3.5 predicted from\n977\ttypical instances at `k = pw` that the closed count merges at most `0.003`\n978\tin `β` at `c = 10`; the asymptotic constant gains `0.008`. The instrument\n979\tof §3 (ratios of counts on typical instances) sees the typical regime;\n980\tthe first moment is decided in the rare-instance regime, where the\n981\tmaximisers differ (§3.3's two regimes). The separator prediction\n982\t(`0.003` measured, `0.004` asymptotic) held.\n983\t\n984\t### 6.8 Where the record corrects the item\n985\t\n986\t* The item's \"combined count … stated as the best valid first moment on\n987\t  the ladder\" is not what the construction gives: with the `2^κ` bound on\n988\t  the colourings it is weaker than the closed count at every `c` and weaker\n989\t  than the pair count at `c ≤ 4` (§6.3 (3)). It is stated, valid, and\n990\t  not the best; the best valid bound at each `c` is `max(β_sep, β_closed)`.\n991\t* The item's test \"`β_closed ≥ β*` at every `c`\" holds numerically at every\n992\t  `c` tested (the supremum is at least the corner value, and at the corner\n993\t  the `q` terms lower the exponent — so the inequality is not automatic\n994\t  from the formula, and is tested, not proved).\n995\t* The bracket's lower end reproduces `β*` *exactly*, not approximately\n996\t  (an identity, §6.1 (a)), so \"the bracket on `β_sep`\" is `[β*, β_opt]`.\n997\t* The item's threshold check at `c = 1.5` is undecidable at `N ≤ 400`\n998\t  (§6.4): the limit is `0.0015`, the finite rate `0.0046` and still\n999\t  falling.\n1000\t* The fitted rate's drift above the LDP at `c′ ≥ 12` (§6.2) is outside the\n1001\t  range the constants use, and the \"cited\" column is the one quoted.\n1002\t\n1003\t### 6.9 What this does not claim\n1004\t\n1005\t* **A limit, or a theorem.** `β_sep` (fitted) rests on a fit of exact\n1006\t  values at `N ≤ 400`; `β_sep` (cited) on an LDP we cite and did not prove;\n1007\t  `β_comb` on a fit alone. The finite inequalities of §6.5 are exact and\n1008\t  valid (L1′, L2, the union bound, the recursions checked by enumeration),\n1009\t  and the separator and closed-pair ones are item 08's to write on paper\n1010\t  and item 10's to formalise; `B_pair` is the only one in Lean.\n1011\t* **The constant.** Every `β` here is a lower bound on `lim inf pw/n`\n1012\t  whatever the rate, and none is claimed to be the limit; the fitted\n1013\t  slopes are fits from `n ≤ 70–120` (survey §7(d)) and the gaps \"closed\"\n1014\t  are fractions of a fitted number.\n1015\t* **That `E_bip` and `E_relax` have the same rate** beyond `N = 120`\n1016\t  (§5.6); the \"fitted\" and \"cited\" columns use `E_relax`, which is the\n1017\t  valid relaxation either way.\n1018\t* **That the closed count's supremum is at the corner for every `c`**: it\n1019\t  is, at the ten `c` of the table, by numerical search; no proof.\n1020\t* **Priority** for the separator count, the closed-pair count or the\n1021\t  threshold statement: §1 found no prior separator first moment with an\n1022\t  exact inner probability for pathwidth, and the threshold `2 ln 2` is the\n1023\t  bisection threshold of Luczak–McDiarmid for the same reason (§1.3); \"we\n1024\t  found no prior statement\" is the strongest wording.\n1025\t* Nothing about covers (item 07), and nothing about the weighted count\n1026\t  (item 09) beyond the §4.6 ceiling the gains here are measured against.\n1027\t\n1028\t---\n1029\t\n1030\t## 7. The MOSP version: random clique covers (item 07, 2026-11-01)\n1031\t\n1032\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"85be7329-f712-426a-9f5b-9a4e2176228b","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:59:37.096Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3/tool-results/bbmp6rwp2.txt","content":"**Met for the headline cells.** At every pilot cell with `c ≥ 6` the\nseparator floor at `< 0.01` exceeds the pair floor by at most one stack\n(`+0, +0, +1, +1, +0, +1`), so **the separator count is recorded as a\nrounding at these sizes**, as the exact-size pair was in\n`excess_phase_diagram.md` §8.6 (4), and item 06 is told so. The rate\ncomparison ran as the item requires, and is where the item's value is:\n§5.3 (1)–(2) settle the two open readings of item 01, and §5.4 locates\nthe gain on the sparse flank (`c′ ≤ 5`, i.e. `c ≤ 6` at `β*`), two to\nthree stacks at `n = 120`, `c = 3–4`.\n\n**Prediction for item 06**: `β_sep(c) = β_relax(c)` of §1.6 to the\naccuracy of the fit (the two events have the same rate to `N = 120` and\nto three decimals in the fit); gains of `+0.011` at `c = 6` and `+0.004`\nat 10 against the kill of `0.02`, so **item 06's kill is predicted met\nat `c = 6` and `10`**, and not met at `c ≤ 4`. The prediction of §3.5\n(the separator count merges at most `0.003` in `β` at `c = 10`, measured\nat `k = pw` on typical instances) is consistent with the `0.004` at `β*`.\n\n### 5.6 What this does not claim\n\n* **A limit.** The rate of `E_bip` is computed exactly to `N = 120` and\n  fitted beyond; the rate of `E_relax` is exact to `N = 400` and fitted\n  beyond. That either equals `I_{c′}(½)` is a fit agreeing to three\n  decimals, not a theorem; the LDP itself is cited (O'Connell 1998 via\n  Puhalskii 2005), not proved here, and the upper-bound half of it is\n  what a separator first moment would use. That `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n  have the same rate is exact to `N = 120` and conjectural beyond.\n* **The finite inequalities are the only things proved**, and the\n  separator one on paper only after item 08 (the deterministic lemma is\n  §2's L1′; the union bound over `S` with the inner probability is the\n  statement item 08 writes and item 10 formalises). `B_pair` is the\n  Lean theorem; `B_xpair`, `B_sep`, `B_rel` are valid by L1′ and the\n  union bound and are not yet in Lean.\n* The floors at `n = 120` are against the solver's *lower bounds* on\n  unproved graphs (3 of 13 proved at `c = 3`, none at `c ≥ 4`), not\n  against pathwidths; the comparison with the truth is at `n = 60` only.\n* The `β` conversions in §5.4 are linearisations at `β*` for a preview;\n  item 06 owns the constants.\n* The thinning route's formula is the plan's, evaluated as written; the\n  plan's value `1.19` at `c′ = 4.98` does not reproduce from it (`0.91`),\n  and nothing rests on it since it is never the binding end.\n* Nothing about covers (item 07): there is no LDP to quote there and the\n  inner probability of a random clique cover minus a customer set is not\n  `G(N, p)`.\n\n---\n\n## 6. `β_sep(c)`, `β_closed(c)` and `β_comb(c)`: the constants, against `β*(c)` and the slopes (item 06, 2026-11-01)\n\n**Question (D of the brief).** What constant does the separator first\nmoment give on `G(n, c/n)` — the root `β_sep(c)` of\n`H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) = 0`, `I(c′)` the rate in `N` of the inner event —\nwhat does the closed-pair count give (`β_closed`), what does a combination\ngive (`β_comb`), how do the three compare with `β*(c)` and with the fitted\nslopes at `c = 2–20`, and does the method's threshold move from `4 ln 2`?\n\n**Events counted.** Three first moments, each a valid upper bound on\n`P(pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ βn)` by L1′ and L2 of §2 and the union bound:\n\n1. the **separator count** `C(n, k) · P_N(E_bip)`, `N = n − k`, and its\n   relaxation with `P_N(E_relax)`; item 05 found the two inner\n   probabilities equal in rate to `N = 120`, and the rate used here is the\n   relaxed one (exact to `N = 400`, fitted beyond; the cited LDP rate is for\n   `E_relax`);\n2. the **closed-pair count** `E[X₃]`: ordered triples `(A, R, S)` partitioning\n   `V`, `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ N − t`, `|S| ≤ k`, no `A–R` edge, every vertex of `S`\n   adjacent to `A` and to `R` (`S = N(A) ∩ N(R)`, §2.3);\n3. the **combined count**: closed pairs grouped by their separator `S*`,\n   `|S*| = s′ ≤ k`. Given `G − S`, the empty bipartitions `(A, R)` with\n   `|A| ∈ [t, t + k − s′]` (so that `|R| ≥ N − t`) are 2-colourings of the\n   components of `G − S`, at most `2^κ` of them (`κ` the number of\n   components), and each is closed with probability\n   `Π_{v∈S} q_{|A|} q_{|R|} ≤ Qmax(s′)^{s′}` (`q_x = 1 − (1 − p)^x`; the edges\n   from `S` to `G − S` are independent of `G − S`, and for distinct `v ∈ S`\n   they are disjoint edge sets). Hence\n\n       P(pw ≤ k) ≤ Σ_{s′ ≤ k} C(n, s′) · Qmax(s′)^{s′} · E_{N′ = n − s′}[ 1[E_win] · 2^κ ],\n\n   with `E_win` the window bipartition event (one side between `t` and\n   `t + k − s′` vertices; `E_bip` at `s′ = k`) or its relaxation (no\n   component larger than `t + k − s′`). The inner factor is a *tilted*\n   partition sum, computed exactly by the recursions of §5.1 with every\n   part weighted by 2 (`tilt = ln 2` in `log_prob_bip` / `log_prob_rel`).\n\n**Size range.** Asymptotic constants (roots of exponents per vertex);\nfitted rates from exact inner sums at `N = 100–400`; the threshold check at\n`N ≤ 400`; the finite form at `n = 60, 120`, `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10`, every `k`\naround the floors. Checks on record (`check.log`): the window and tilted\nrecursions against the enumeration of every labelled graph at `N ≤ 6`\n(every window, both tilts; max difference `3 × 10⁻¹³`); the fast relaxed\nrecursion against the table one at `N ≤ 6` and `N = 60, 120, 200`\n(`6 × 10⁻¹⁴`); the finite closed count against the enumeration of closed\npairs over every labelled graph at `n ≤ 6` (`3 × 10⁻¹³`) and Monte Carlo\nat `n = 7, 8` (20,000 graphs each: `2.1, 0.8, 0.2` standard errors);\n`β_sep` with the bracket's lower end equals `β*` at six `c` (to `2 × 10⁻⁶`);\nEdwards & McDermid's Corollary 5.2 reproduces `0.183, 0.458, 0.656`.\n\n**Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/06_constants/run.py`\n(stages `check`, `grid`, `constants`, `threshold`, `finite`, `draw`; 18 + 8 +\n30 + 4 + 5 + 2 s, the grid and the finite stage on 8 workers at nice 19;\nabout 0.05 core-hours against 0.2 budgeted). Outputs beside it:\n`rate_grid.csv` (every `(c′, N, u)`), `rate_fits.csv` (the fitted rates per\n`c′`), `constants.csv` (the table), `threshold.csv`, `finite.csv` and\n`finite_floors.csv`, `check.log`, `run.log`, `beta_vs_c.png`. Tests:\n`python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py` (70 tests, 10 new).\n\n### 6.1 Method\n\n**(a) `β_sep(c)` from three rates.** `sep_exponent(c, β, I) = H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β))`\nand `beta_sep(c, I)` its root, for three rate functions `I(c′)`:\n\n* **fitted**: the log-corrected fit `−log P_N(E_relax) = I N + b log N + a`\n  over `N = 100, 150, …, 400` (item 05's `logfit`, the form a polynomial\n  prefactor gives), on a grid of 77 values `c′ = 1.00, 1.25, …, 20.00`,\n  linearly interpolated between them (`rate_fits.csv`, column\n  `fit_rel_half`; `load_fitted` in `run.py`);\n* **bracket**: the plan's two analytic ends, `c′/4 − ln 2` (the product\n  bound, i.e. the pair count's own rate) and `min(c′/4, thinning)`, each\n  giving a root; the lower end gives `β*(c)` *exactly*, because\n  `H(a, a, 1 − 2a) − c a² = H(β) − (1 − β)(c′/4 − ln 2)` at `a = (1 − β)/2`\n  (`pair_exponent_beta`; tested);\n* **cited**: `I_{c′}(½)`, the large-deviation rate of the largest component\n  of `G(N, c′/N)` at `u = ½` in O'Connell's form as restated by Puhalskii\n  (2005, *Ann. Probab.* 33, Cor. 2.4(3) and (2.13)–(2.14); §1.3 Table B),\n  ported from item 01's `rate_check.py` into `ldp_rate`, whose value item 05\n  checked against the exact computation to `0.0003–0.002` at seven `c′`.\n\n**(b) `β_closed(c)`.** The closed-pair exponent per vertex at side\nfractions `(a, r, s)`, `a + r + s = 1`, is\n\n    g_closed(a, r, s) = H(a, r, s) − c a r + s ln q(a) + s ln q(r),   q(x) = 1 − e^{−cx}:\n\nthe multinomial counts the ordered triples; `(1 − p)^{|A||R|} → e^{−c a r n}`\nis the empty-pair probability; and given `(A, R)` the events \"`v` sees `A`\nand `v` sees `R`\" for the `s n` vertices of `S` are independent (disjoint\nedge sets) with probability `(1 − (1 − p)^{an})(1 − (1 − p)^{rn}) → q(a) q(r)`.\n`closed_rate(β, c)` is its supremum over the admissible region of L2\n(`a, r ≥ (1 − β)/2`, `s = 1 − a − r ∈ [0, β]`; a grid over the triangle then\nNelder–Mead), and `β_closed(c)` the root of the supremum. Checks: without\nthe `q` terms the exponent at the corner `a = r = (1 − β)/2`, `s = β` is\nthe pair exponent (tested); the finite count\n`Σ n!/(a! r! s!) (1 − p)^{ar} q_a^s q_r^s` over the admissible integer sizes\n(`log_closed_count`) equals the enumeration of `closed_pairs` over every\nlabelled graph at `n ≤ 6` for every `k` (§6's check). **At every `c` of the\ntable the supremum sits at the corner** (`a = r = (1 − β)/2`, `s = β`;\n`constants` stage), so the closed count's gain over the pair count is the\n`q` terms alone: `2β ln q((1 − β)/2)` nats per vertex at `β*`\n(`−0.027` at `c = 10`, `−0.033` at `c = 6`, `−0.005` at `c = 3`), the\nprobability that every separator vertex of the planted pair sees both\nsides.\n\n**(c) `β_comb(c)`.** The combined count's `s′`-term exponent per vertex is\n\n    g_comb(s′; β) = H(s′) + 2 s′ ln q((1 − s′)/2) − (1 − s′) J(c(1 − s′), u),   u = ((1 + β)/2 − s′)/(1 − s′),\n\nwhere `J(c′, u)` is the rate in `N′` of `E[1[no component > uN′] 2^κ]`\n(`u` is the largest allowed component as a fraction of `N′`; at `s′ = β`,\n`u = ½`). `J` is **fitted only**: there is no LDP to cite for the tilted\nevent, and the fit is the same log-corrected fit over `N = 100–400` on the\ngrid `c′ × u ∈ {0.5, 0.55, …, 0.8}` (`rate_fits.csv`, columns\n`fit_tilted_u*`), bilinearly interpolated. `combined_rate(c, β)` is the\nsupremum over `s′ ∈ [0, β]` on 42 points and `β_comb` its root; at every\n`c` in the table the supremum is at `s′ = β` (`run.log`, the profiles at\n`c = 3, 6, 10`: the exponent rises monotonically in `s′` to `0` at `s′ = β`).\n\n### 6.2 The rates on the grid, against item 05 and the LDP\n\n(`rate_fits.csv`; `fit_rel(½)` is the fitted rate used for the \"fitted\"\ncolumn; `item05` is item 05's `logfit_rel_slope_N100` at its seven `c′`.)\n\n| `c′` | fitted `I(½)` | LDP `I(½)` | fitted − LDP | item 05 | tilted fit `u = 0.5` | `0.6` | `0.7` | `0.8` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.0 | 0.0001 | 0 | +0.0001 | — | −0.4431 | −0.4431 | −0.4431 | −0.4431 |\n| 1.5 | 0.0021 | 0.0015 | +0.0006 | — | −0.3181 | −0.3181 | −0.3181 | −0.3181 |\n| 2.0 | 0.0375 | 0.0362 | +0.0013 | 0.0375 | −0.1928 | −0.1930 | −0.1932 | −0.1932 |\n| 2.5 | 0.1025 | 0.1006 | +0.0019 | — | −0.0894 | −0.0987 | −0.1041 | −0.1038 |\n| 3.0 | 0.1852 | 0.1831 | +0.0022 | 0.1853 | 0.0238 | −0.0019 | −0.0297 | −0.0526 |\n| 4.0 | 0.3812 | 0.3796 | +0.0017 | 0.3812 | 0.2829 | 0.2269 | 0.1553 | 0.0744 |\n| 5.0 | 0.6005 | 0.5997 | +0.0009 | 0.6005 | 0.5499 | 0.4804 | 0.3654 | 0.2260 |\n| 6.0 | 0.8328 | 0.8324 | +0.0004 | — | 0.8052 | 0.7375 | 0.5873 | 0.3879 |\n| 8.0 | 1.3164 | 1.3161 | +0.0003 | 1.3163 | 1.3072 | 1.2343 | 1.0382 | 0.7232 |\n| 10.0 | 1.8115 | 1.8102 | +0.0013 | 1.8115 | 1.8081 | 1.7229 | 1.4743 | 1.0627 |\n| 12.0 | 2.3116 | 2.3081 | +0.0035 | — | 2.3101 | 2.2081 | 1.9039 | 1.3996 |\n| 16.0 | 3.3178 | 3.3070 | +0.0108 | — | 3.3175 | 3.1769 | 2.7558 | 2.0573 |\n| 20.0 | 4.3298 | 4.3069 | +0.0230 | — | 4.3298 | 4.1489 | 3.6077 | 2.7104 |\n\nThe fitted rate reproduces item 05's at all seven of its `c′` (to\n`10⁻⁴`) and the LDP to `0.0004–0.002` for `c′ ≤ 10`; above `c′ = 12` the\nfit drifts above the LDP (`+0.004` to `+0.023`), the `log N` term growing\nwith `c′` — the fit is a fit, and the LDP is what the \"cited\" column uses.\nThe constants only ever need `c′ = c(1 − β) ≤ 8.9` (at `c = 20`), where the\ntwo agree to `0.0005`. **The tilted rate is negative for `c′ ≤ 2.5`** at\nevery `u` (the `2^κ` factor grows faster than the probability decays: the\nnumber of components of `G(N, c′/N)` is a positive fraction of `N` there)\nand costs, at `u = ½`, `0.161, 0.098, 0.051, 0.028, 0.009, 0.003` nats per\nvertex of `N` relative to the untilted rate at `c′ = 3, 4, 5, 6, 8, 10`,\n**more than the separator count gained over the product bound at the same\n`c′`** (§5.4: `0.126, 0.073, 0.043, 0.026, 0.009, 0.003`; the\n`E[2^κ 1[E]]` is dominated by partitions with more components than those\ndominating `P(E)`, Jensen's inequality in the exponent).\n\n### 6.3 The table\n\n(`constants.csv`. `β_sep` three ways: fitted rate / bracket `[β*, root of\nthe optimistic end]` / cited LDP rate; `β_comb` fitted only; the slope is\nthe pilot's fitted `β_∞` where the cell has one, survey §7(d); E–M is\nEdwards & McDermid 2015 Cor. 5.2 on Poisson(`c`) degrees, the published\nupper line (owner's note of 2026-11-01); `dg/dβ` is the pair exponent's slope\nat `β*` in nats per unit of `β`, the conversion of the gains.)\n\n| `c` | `β*` | `β_closed` | `β_sep` fitted | bracket | `β_sep` cited | `β_comb` fitted | slope | E–M | `dg/dβ` | gain sep (fitted) | gain closed | gain comb |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 2 | 0 | 0 | 0.0059 | [0, 0.0087] | 0.0056 | 0 | 0.040 | 0.085 | 7.21 | +0.0059 (0.043 nats) | 0 | 0 |\n| 2.5 | 0 | 0 | 0.0190 | [0, 0.0283] | 0.0185 | 0 | — | 0.132 | 7.46 | +0.0190 (0.142) | 0 | 0 |\n| 3 | 0.0087 | 0.0096 | 0.0374 | [0.0087, 0.0558] | 0.0368 | 0.0035 | 0.099 | 0.183 | 5.53 | **+0.0287** (0.159) | +0.0009 (0.005) | −0.0052 |\n| 4 | 0.0613 | 0.0671 | 0.0833 | [0.0613, 0.1210] | 0.0827 | 0.0594 | 0.158 | 0.285 | 3.91 | **+0.0220** (0.086) | +0.0058 (0.023) | −0.0019 |\n| 6 | 0.1706 | 0.1803 | 0.1820 | [0.1706, 0.2480] | 0.1817 | 0.1782 | 0.268 | 0.458 | 3.38 | +0.0113 (0.038) | +0.0097 (0.033) | +0.0076 |\n| 8 | 0.2628 | 0.2722 | 0.2691 | [0.2628, 0.3490] | 0.2690 | 0.2716 | 0.360 | 0.576 | 3.29 | +0.0063 (0.021) | +0.0094 (0.031) | +0.0088 |\n| 10 | 0.3371 | 0.3453 | 0.3410 | [0.3371, 0.4266] | 0.3409 | 0.3451 | 0.415 | 0.656 | 3.30 | +0.0038 (0.013) | +0.0082 (0.027) | +0.0080 |\n| 12 | 0.3973 | 0.4043 | 0.3999 | [0.3973, 0.4871] | 0.3998 | 0.4043 | — | 0.711 | 3.34 | +0.0025 (0.009) | +0.0069 (0.023) | +0.0069 |\n| 16 | 0.4882 | 0.4933 | 0.4895 | [0.4882, 0.5750] | 0.4895 | 0.4934 | — | 0.782 | 3.45 | +0.0013 (0.005) | +0.0050 (0.017) | +0.0051 |\n| 20 | 0.5535 | 0.5573 | 0.5543 | [0.5535, 0.6356] | 0.5542 | 0.5574 | — | 0.826 | 3.56 | +0.0008 (0.003) | +0.0038 (0.014) | +0.0038 |\n\nWhat the table says:\n\n1. **The separator count's gain is a near-threshold gain.** `+0.029` at\n   `c = 3` (32% of the gap of 0.090 to the fitted slope), `+0.022` at `c = 4`\n   (23%), `+0.011` at `c = 6` (12%), `+0.004` at `c = 10` (5%), `+0.0008` at\n   `c = 20`; and a positive constant at `c = 2` and `2.5`, where `β* = 0`\n   (`+0.006` and `+0.019`; 15% of the gap at `c = 2`). The fitted and the\n   cited columns agree to `0.0006` everywhere; the bracket's optimistic end\n   is far above both (`0.248` against `0.182` at `c = 6`) and is not the\n   truth, as §5.3 said. §1.6's preview from the quotation alone is\n   reproduced to `0.0005` at every `c`.\n2. **The closed-pair count's gain is a dense-side gain**: `+0.001` at\n   `c = 3`, `+0.006` at 4, `+0.010` at 6, `+0.009` at 8, `+0.008` at 10,\n   `+0.004` at 20 — 1%, 6%, 10%, 10%, 11% of the gap where a slope exists.\n   The planner's `0.1803 / 0.3453` at `c = 6 / 10` (plan §0) are reproduced\n   exactly. The separator count wins at `c ≤ 6` (by a hair at 6: `0.1820`\n   against `0.1803`), the closed count at `c ≥ 8`.\n3. **The combined count, as constructed, is not the best on the ladder.**\n   Its constant lies below the closed count's at every `c` and *below `β*`*\n   at `c ≤ 4` (`0.0035` against `0.0087` at `c = 3`): the `2^κ` bound on the\n   number of balanced 2-colourings of the components costs more than the\n   separator count gained (§6.2), so grouping the closed pairs by `S` with\n   that bound gives the separator's gain back and keeps only the `q` terms,\n   less a little. At `c ≥ 8` it is within `0.0006` of the closed count. The\n   best *valid* first moment at each `c` is the larger of `β_sep` and\n   `β_closed`, both valid separately; a combination that beats both needs\n   the closedness condition inside the component law (the probability that\n   *some* balanced colouring of the components of `G − S` has every vertex\n   of `S` adjacent to both colours), which is not a tilt of the partition\n   sum and is not computed here.\n4. **Against the data**: the best of the three constants sits at `0.037,\n   0.083, 0.182, 0.272, 0.345` against fitted slopes `0.099, 0.158, 0.268,\n   0.360, 0.415` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10`, closing 32%, 23%, 12%, 10%, 11%\n   of the gap. The provable sandwich with the published upper line is\n   `[0.037, 0.183]`, `[0.182, 0.458]`, `[0.345, 0.656]` at `c = 3, 6, 10`\n   (was `[0.009, 0.183]`, `[0.171, 0.458]`, `[0.337, 0.656]` with `β*`);\n   the ratio upper/lower at `c = 3` falls from 21 to 4.9.\n5. **In nats**: the separator gain at `β*` is `0.159, 0.086, 0.038, 0.021,\n   0.013` nats per vertex of `n` at `c = 3, 4, 6, 8, 10` (the slopes `dg/dβ`\n   are `5.53, 3.91, 3.38, 3.29, 3.30`, so `g/3.3` is the conversion at\n   `c ≥ 6` and `g/5.5` at `c = 3`); the closed gain `0.005, 0.023, 0.033,\n   0.031, 0.027`. Item 04's measured union-bound slack on `G(n, c/n)` at `β*`\n   and `n ≤ 32` was `0.065` and `0.039` nats at `c = 6` and `10` (§4.6):\n   the separator count recovers 59% and 33% of it, the closed count 51%\n   and 69%, and neither both.\n\n### 6.4 The threshold\n\n**Proposition (conditional).** *Suppose that for every `c′ > 2 ln 2` there\nis `I(c′) > 0` with `P(no component of G(N, c′/N) has more than N/2\nvertices) ≤ e^{−(I(c′) − o(1)) N}`, with `I` continuous in `c′`. Then\n`β_sep(c) > 0` for every `c > 2 ln 2`: there is `β₀ = β₀(c) > 0` with\n`P(pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ β₀ n) → 0`.* Proof: by L1′ (§2.2) and the union bound,\n`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ C(n, βn) P_{N}(E_relax) ≤ e^{n[H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) + o(1)]}`;\nas `β → 0`, `H(β) → 0` and `(1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) → I(c) > 0`, so the exponent\nis negative for all small `β > 0`. ∎ (The pair count's exponent at `β → 0`\nis `ln 2 − c/4`, negative only for `c > 4 ln 2`; the true threshold of linear\npathwidth is `c = 1`, Lee–Lee–Oum 2012 via `tw ≤ pw`, survey §1.1.)\n\n**Status of the hypothesis: cited, not verified by us as a theorem.** It is\nthe upper-bound half of the large-deviation principle for the largest\ncomponent of `G(N, c′/N)` at scale `N`, applied to the closed set\n`[0, ½]`: O'Connell 1998 (*not held*; Theorem 3.1 as restated), Puhalskii\n2005 (*Ann. Probab.* 33(2), held; Cor. 2.4(3), p. 8 of the arXiv version,\nwith the rate function (2.13)–(2.14) and the remark that O'Connell's\nstatement has a misprint), Andreis–König–Patterson 2021 (held; Cor. 1.3\ngives the macroscopic contraction). `I_{c′}(½) > 0` iff `c′ > 2 ln 2`\n(§1.4, `rate_check.log`), and continuity in `c′` is read off the formula.\nWhat *is* verified here is the value of the quoted formula at finite `N`\n(§5.3; §6.2), not the principle.\n\n**Numerical check at `N ≤ 400`** (`threshold.csv`; `−log P_N(E_relax)/N` by\nthe exact recursion; the log-corrected fit over `N ≥ 100`):\n\n| `c` | `I_c(½)` | `N = 50` | 100 | 200 | 300 | 400 | log-fit `I` (`b`) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 1.2 (control, below) | 0 | 0.0042 | 0.0013 | 0.0003 | 0.0001 | 0.00005 | +0.0002 (−0.12) |\n| 2 ln 2 (threshold) | 0 | 0.0111 | 0.0055 | 0.0029 | 0.0020 | 0.0015 | +0.0001 (+0.02) |\n| 1.5 | 0.0015 | 0.0175 | 0.0102 | 0.0066 | 0.0053 | 0.0046 | +0.0021 (+0.12) |\n| 2 | 0.0362 | 0.0646 | 0.0514 | 0.0449 | 0.0427 | 0.0414 | +0.0375 (+0.16) |\n| 2.5 | 0.1006 | 0.1372 | 0.1191 | 0.1107 | 0.1080 | 0.1066 | +0.1025 (−0.01) |\n\nAt `c = 2` and `2.5` the finite rate is bounded away from zero at every\n`N` and the fit lands `0.001–0.002` above the cited value; at `c = 1.5` the\nfinite values are still falling at `N = 400` (`0.0046` against a cited\nlimit of `0.0015`) and the fit gives `0.0021`, of the limit's order but\nnot a confirmation; the controls at and below `2 ln 2` fall like `1/N`\n(`b ≈ 0`, `I ≈ 0`). So the check is consistent with the proposition at\n`c = 2, 2.5`, undecided by itself at `c = 1.5`, and no finite computation\ncan establish a limit. `P_N(E_bip)` at `N = 100` equals `P_N(E_relax)` to\nfour decimals at every `c` in the table (column `rate_bip`).\n\n### 6.5 The finite form at `n = 60` and `120`, in stacks\n\nLargest `k` with each bound below `1` and below `0.01` (`finite_floors.csv`;\npair, exact-size pair and separator from item 05's `inner.csv`; the closed\nand combined counts new; `comb` is the relaxed-window form, `comb_bip` the\nbipartition-window form at `n = 60` only; the pilot's observed minimum\npathwidth at 60 and the solver's lower bound at 120, §5.2):\n\n| `n` | `c` | pair `<1` / `<.01` | xpair | **sep** | **closed** | comb | comb_bip | `β*n` | min pw / LB |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 60 | 3 | 1 / −1 | 1 / 0 | **3 / 1** | 1 / 0 | 0 / −1 | 0 / −1 | 0.5 | 5 |\n| 60 | 4 | 5 / 3 | 5 / 3 | **6 / 4** | 5 / 4 | 4 / 3 | 4 / 3 | 3.7 | 8 |\n| 60 | 6 | 11 / 11 | 12 / 11 | 12 / 11 | **13 / 11** | 12 / 10 | 12 / 10 | 10.2 | 16 |\n| 60 | 8 | 17 / 17 | 18 / 17 | 18 / 17 | **19 / 17** | 18 / 16 | 18 / 16 | 15.8 | 20 |\n| 60 | 10 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 22 | 23 / 22 | 23 / 21 | 23 / 21 | 20.2 | 25 |\n| 120 | 3 | 1 / 1 | 2 / 1 | **5 / 4** | 2 / 1 | 0 / −1 | — | 1.0 | 11 / 12 |\n| 120 | 4 | 9 / 7 | 9 / 7 | **11 / 10** | 9 / 8 | 8 / 6 | — | 7.4 | — / 17 |\n| 120 | 6 | 21 / 21 | 22 / 21 | 24 / 22 | 24 / 22 | 22 / 21 | — | 20.5 | — / 25 |\n| 120 | 8 | 33 / 33 | 34 / 33 | 34 / 33 | **35 / 34** | 34 / 33 | — | 31.5 | — / 34 |\n| 120 | 10 | 43 / 41 | 43 / 42 | 44 / 42 | **44 / 43** | 43 / 42 | — | 40.5 | — / 41 |\n\nIn stacks the picture is the asymptotic one: at `c = 3–4` the separator\ncount is the best by 2–3 stacks over everything else; at `c ≥ 6` the\nclosed count ties or beats the separator count by one stack (`+1` at\n`(60, 6)`, `(60, 8)` at `< 1`; `+1` at `(120, 8)`, `(120, 10)` at `< 0.01`),\nand both are one to two stacks above the pair count; the combined count\nis one stack below the closed count or worse. At `(120, 10)` the closed\ncount says `P(pw ≤ 44) ≤ 0.10` where the separator count said `0.68` and\nthe pair count nothing, against solver lower bounds of 41–42; the decades\naround the floor (`run.log`): `k = 43`: closed `−2.28`, sep `−1.38`, pair\n`−0.99`; `k = 44`: closed `−0.98`, sep `−0.17`, pair `+1.76`; `k = 45`:\nclosed `+0.28`. The bipartition-window and the relaxed-window forms of\nthe combined count agree to `0.01` decades at every `(60, c, k)`: the\nnumber-partitioning condition is free here too (§5.4).\n\n### 6.6 The figure\n\n`beta_vs_c.png`: left, `β` against `c` — `β*`, `β_sep` (cited rate) with\nthe bracket shaded, `β_closed`, the pilot's fitted slopes, and Edwards &\nMcDermid's published upper line, with the two thresholds `2 ln 2` and\n`4 ln 2` marked; right, the gains over `β*` — the separator count (cited\nand fitted), the closed count, the combined count, the bracket, and the\ngap to the fitted slope, with the brief's kill line at `0.02`. The four\nlower curves on the left are indistinguishable at the scale of the\nsandwich, which is the finding; the right panel is where they separate.\n(Categorical palette validated: six slots, all checks pass with the\ncontrast warning on three, relieved by the legend and direct labels.)\n\n### 6.7 Kill verdict\n\n**The brief's kill: met.** `β_sep(fitted) − β* = +0.0113` at `c = 6` and\n`+0.0038` at `c = 10`, both below `0.02`; **the separator route is closed\nfor the headline constants**, as items 03 and 05 predicted (§3.5: at most\n`0.003` at `c = 10`; §5.5: `+0.011` and `+0.004`). Beside it, as the item\nasks: the closed-pair gain is `+0.0097 / +0.0082`, the combined count's\n`+0.0076 / +0.0080`; none of the three, nor their maximum, reaches `0.02`\nat either `c`.\n\n**The pre-registered separate findings, and what they are worth.**\n\n* *Near the threshold* the separator count is not a rounding: `+0.029` at\n  `c = 3` and `+0.022` at `c = 4` in `β`, 32% and 23% of the gap to the\n  fitted slopes — above the brief's kill and at the top of what §1.4's\n  record of representative counts ever bought (1.7–25% of a gap) — and a\n  positive constant at `c = 2, 2.5` where the pair count is vacuous. The\n  plan's bracket at `c = 3` was `[0.009, 0.056]`; the answer is `0.037`,\n  against a slope of `0.099`. These rest on the relaxed event's rate,\n  cited (the LDP) and reproduced by exact computation to `N = 400`.\n* *The threshold* moves from `4 ln 2 = 2.77` to `2 ln 2 = 1.39`,\n  conditional on the cited LDP upper bound (§6.4, hypothesis explicit);\n  the truth is `1`. In the logarithm this is half of the way.\n* *The closed-pair count* is the better of the two at `c ≥ 8`, by the `q`\n  terms alone (the supremum sits at the corner at every `c`); its gain is\n  `+0.008–0.010` at `c = 6–12`, exactly the planner's estimate.\n\n**Where this corrects the loop's own predictions.** §3.5 predicted from\ntypical instances at `k = pw` that the closed count merges at most `0.003`\nin `β` at `c = 10`; the asymptotic constant gains `0.008`. The instrument\nof §3 (ratios of counts on typical instances) sees the typical regime;\nthe first moment is decided in the rare-instance regime, where the\nmaximisers differ (§3.3's two regimes). The separator prediction\n(`0.003` measured, `0.004` asymptotic) held.\n\n### 6.8 Where the record corrects the item\n\n* The item's \"combined count … stated as the best valid first moment on\n  the ladder\" is not what the construction gives: with the `2^κ` bound on\n  the colourings it is weaker than the closed count at every `c` and weaker\n  than the pair count at `c ≤ 4` (§6.3 (3)). It is stated, valid, and\n  not the best; the best valid bound at each `c` is `max(β_sep, β_closed)`.\n* The item's test \"`β_closed ≥ β*` at every `c`\" holds numerically at every\n  `c` tested (the supremum is at least the corner value, and at the corner\n  the `q` terms lower the exponent — so the inequality is not automatic\n  from the formula, and is tested, not proved).\n* The bracket's lower end reproduces `β*` *exactly*, not approximately\n  (an identity, §6.1 (a)), so \"the bracket on `β_sep`\" is `[β*, β_opt]`.\n* The item's threshold check at `c = 1.5` is undecidable at `N ≤ 400`\n  (§6.4): the limit is `0.0015`, the finite rate `0.0046` and still\n  falling.\n* The fitted rate's drift above the LDP at `c′ ≥ 12` (§6.2) is outside the\n  range the constants use, and the \"cited\" column is the one quoted.\n\n### 6.9 What this does not claim\n\n* **A limit, or a theorem.** `β_sep` (fitted) rests on a fit of exact\n  values at `N ≤ 400`; `β_sep` (cited) on an LDP we cite and did not prove;\n  `β_comb` on a fit alone. The finite inequalities of §6.5 are exact and\n  valid (L1′, L2, the union bound, the recursions checked by enumeration),\n  and the separator and closed-pair ones are item 08's to write on paper\n  and item 10's to formalise; `B_pair` is the only one in Lean.\n* **The constant.** Every `β` here is a lower bound on `lim inf pw/n`\n  whatever the rate, and none is claimed to be the limit; the fitted\n  slopes are fits from `n ≤ 70–120` (survey §7(d)) and the gaps \"closed\"\n  are fractions of a fitted number.\n* **That `E_bip` and `E_relax` have the same rate** beyond `N = 120`\n  (§5.6); the \"fitted\" and \"cited\" columns use `E_relax`, which is the\n  valid relaxation either way.\n* **That the closed count's supremum is at the corner for every `c`**: it\n  is, at the ten `c` of the table, by numerical search; no proof.\n* **Priority** for the separator count, the closed-pair count or the\n  threshold statement: §1 found no prior separator first moment with an\n  exact inner probability for pathwidth, and the threshold `2 ln 2` is the\n  bisection threshold of Luczak–McDiarmid for the same reason (§1.3); \"we\n  found no prior statement\" is the strongest wording.\n* Nothing about covers (item 07), and nothing about the weighted count\n  (item 09) beyond the §4.6 ceiling the gains here are measured against.\n\n---\n\n## 7. The MOSP version: random clique covers (item 07, 2026-11-01)\n\n","numLines":447,"startLine":586,"totalLines":1032}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01EzQ4NUEi1t4nmSdV2x5wew","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
