{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FBUAAwcA1kjFyU8xKm8XpR","type":"tool_result","content":"592\t`C_1, …, C_M` are known.\n593\t\n594\t**Corollary S′ (the relaxed form).** `P( pw(G(n, p)) ≤ k ) ≤ C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))`,\n595\twith `P_N(E_relax(t)) = R_N(t)` from Lemma F. It is weaker than Theorem S\n596\t(`E_bip(t) ⊆ E_relax(t)`, strictly from `N = 6` on: §2.2 (d)) and is the\n597\tform the large-deviation rate of §1.6 and §6 applies to.\n598\t\n599\t**Theorem Cl (the closed-pair count).** For `k < n` and `0 ≤ p ≤ 1`,\n600\t\n601\t    P( pw(G(n, p)) ≤ k ) ≤ E[X₃] = Σ_{a ≥ t, r′ ≥ r, a + r′ + s = n}  n! / (a! r′! s!) · (1 − p)^{a r′} · (1 − (1 − p)^a)^s · (1 − (1 − p)^{r′})^s,\n602\t\n603\tthe sum over the ordered size triples `(a, r′, s)` (so `s ≤ k`\n604\tautomatically). The right-hand side is exactly the expected number of\n605\tclosed pairs with the sizes of L2, not only a bound on it.\n606\t\n607\t**Lemma W (the weighted-count identity).** Let `𝓟` be a finite index set\n608\tof pairs, `X = Σ_{P ∈ 𝓟} 1[P empty]`, and `Z : 𝓟 → ℕ` a function (which may\n609\tdepend on the graph) with `1 ≤ Z(P) ≤ X` whenever `P` is empty. Then\n610\t\n611\t    Y := Σ_{P ∈ 𝓟} 1[P empty] / Z(P)  ≥  1[X > 0],\n612\t\n613\tpointwise on every graph, with equality when `Z = X`. Hence, for any\n614\tprobability law on graphs, `P(X > 0) ≤ E[Y] = Σ_P P(P empty) · E[1/Z(P) | P empty]`.\n615\tIn particular `P(pw ≤ k) ≤ E[Y]` whenever `𝓟` is the set of ordered pairs\n616\tof the forced sizes (L1).\n617\t\n618\t**Lemma Fl (the flip lemma, deterministic).** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair\n619\tand `I_A`, `I_R` the vertices of `A`, `R` isolated in `G[A]`, `G[R]`. For\n620\t`J ⊆ I_A`, `J′ ⊆ I_R` with `|J| = |J′|`, the pair\n621\t`((A ∖ J) ∪ J′, (R ∖ J′) ∪ J)` is an empty pair with `|A|`, `|R|` vertices;\n622\tthe map `(J, J′) ↦ ((A ∖ J) ∪ J′, (R ∖ J′) ∪ J)` is injective; and the number\n623\tof such pairs is `C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)`. Consequently\n624\t`Z(A, R) := C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)` satisfies `1 ≤ Z ≤ X` on `{(A, R) empty}`\n625\tand is admissible in Lemma W.\n626\t\n627\t**Lemma J (the joint binomial moments of the flips on random covers).** Let\n628\tthe patterns `P_1, …, P_m` be independent uniform subsets of the `n`\n629\tcustomers of sizes `s_1, …, s_m`, and `(A, R, S)` a fixed ordered partition\n630\twith sizes `(a, r′, s)`. In the MOSP graph, a customer of `A` is isolated in\n631\t`G[A]` iff no pattern contains it together with another customer of `A`.\n632\tFor `u ≤ a`, `u′ ≤ r′`,\n633\t\n634\t    B(u, u′) := Σ_{U ⊆ A, |U| = u} Σ_{U′ ⊆ R, |U′| = u′} P( (A, R) empty, U ⊆ I_A, U′ ⊆ I_R )\n635\t             = C(a, u) C(r′, u′) · Π_j allowed(u, u′; s_j) / C(n, s_j),\n636\t    allowed(u, u′; s_j) = C(a − u + s, s_j) + u C(s, s_j − 1) + C(r′ − u′ + s, s_j) + u′ C(s, s_j − 1) − C(s, s_j),\n637\t\n638\tand the joint law is recovered by two-variable inversion,\n639\t\n640\t    P( I_A = i, I_R = i′, (A, R) empty ) = Σ_{u ≥ i} Σ_{u′ ≥ i′} (−1)^{u − i + u′ − i′} C(u, i) C(u′, i′) B(u, u′).\n641\t\n642\t**Corollary W-flip (the first instance, exactly).** For a random cover as in\n643\tLemma J and `k < n`,\n644\t\n645\t    P( mospValue ≤ k + 1 ) = P( pw(mospGraph) ≤ k ) ≤ C(n, t) C(n − t, r) · Σ_{i ≤ t, i′ ≤ r} P( I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty ) / C(i + i′, i),\n646\t\n647\twith the joint law of Lemma J at `(a, r′) = (t, r)`. This is `flip_log_bound`\n648\tof §7.1 (b); the sum has `(t + 1)(r + 1)` terms.\n649\t\n650\t**Theorem Cl-cover (the closed pair on random covers, finite form).** With\n651\tthe patterns as in Lemma J, `P(mospValue ≤ k + 1)` is at most the sum over\n652\tthe ordered triples `(a, r′, s)` of `n!/(a! r′! s!)` times\n653\t\n654\t    P(closed) = Σ_{u, w = 0}^{s} (−1)^{u + w} C(s, u) C(s, w) · Π_j allowed′(u, w; s_j) / C(n, s_j),\n655\t    allowed′(u, w; s_j) = C(s, s_j) + [C(r′ + u, s_j) − C(u, s_j)] + [C(a + w, s_j) − C(w, s_j)],\n656\t\n657\tthe `(u, w)` sum of §7.1 (a). The cancellation-free upper bound of §7.1 (a′)\n658\t(negative association of sampling without replacement, Joag-Dev & Proschan\n659\t1983, *Ann. Statist.* 11, Theorem 2.11 and properties P6–P7, with a tangent\n660\tof the concave `ln(1 − e^{−L})`) is a corollary, valid at every `(n, k)`;\n661\tit is not re-proved here, and its check is item 07's (`07_cover/check.log`:\n662\tthe exact sum against the enumeration of every cover at `n = 3–8`, 66\n663\tcases, and the bound above it on all).\n664\t\n665\t**Proposition T (the threshold on `G(n, c/n)`; conditional).** *Hypothesis\n666\t(H_G), cited and not verified by us:* for every `c′ > 2 ln 2` there is\n667\t`I(c′) > 0`, continuous in `c′`, with\n668\t`P( G(N, c′/N) ∈ E_relax(⌈N/2⌉) ) ≤ exp(−(I(c′) − o(1)) N)` as `N → ∞`. Then\n669\tfor every `c > 2 ln 2` there is `β₀(c) > 0` with\n670\t`P( pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ β₀ n ) → 0`. (H_G) is the upper-bound half of the\n671\tlarge-deviation principle for the largest component of `G(N, c′/N)`\n672\tapplied to the closed set `[0, ½]`: O'Connell 1998 (not held) as restated\n673\tby Puhalskii 2005 (*Ann. Probab.* 33(2), Cor. 2.4(3), p. 8 of the arXiv\n674\tversion, with (2.13)–(2.14)) and Andreis–König–Patterson 2021 (Cor. 1.3);\n675\t`I_{c′}(½) > 0` iff `c′ > 2 ln 2` on the quoted formula (§1.4, §6.4).\n676\t\n677\t**Proposition T-cover (the threshold on random covers; conditional).** Fix a\n678\tbounded size law and `μ > 0` patterns per customer, and let `θ` be the\n679\tsurvival probability of §7.1 (c). *Hypothesis (H_C), stated in no held\n680\tpaper:* for every `u < θ` there is `I(u) > 0` with\n681\t`P( L₁(mospGraph) ≤ u n ) ≤ exp(−I(u) n + o(n))`, and the same for the\n682\tthinned covers of §7.4 with rates converging as the thinning vanishes. Then\n683\t`θ > ½` implies `P( mospValue ≤ β₀ n ) → 0` for some `β₀ > 0`. This is §7.4's\n684\tProposition 7.1 verbatim; it is repeated here so that §8 is the complete\n685\tlist.\n686\t\n687\t### 8.2 Proofs\n688\t\n689\t*Proof of Theorem S.* (Inclusion step) By L1′ (a) (§2.2), every graph with\n690\t`pw ≤ k` has a `k`-set `S` with `E_bip(S)`: `{pw ≤ k} ⊆ ⋃_{|S| = k} E_bip(S)`.\n691\t(Union step) `P_n(pw ≤ k) ≤ Σ_{|S| = k} P_n(E_bip(S))`, the weights being\n692\tnon-negative. (Factorisation step) Fix `S` and write `U = V ∖ S` (`|U| = N`)\n693\tand `E_S` for the set of the `C(n, 2) − C(N, 2)` vertex pairs that meet `S`.\n694\tThe map\n695\t\n696\t    Φ_S : {graphs on V} → {graphs on U} × 𝒫(E_S),      G ↦ ( G[U], E(G) ∩ E_S )\n697\t\n698\tis a bijection, with inverse `(H, F) ↦` the graph on `V` with edge set\n699\t`E(H) ∪ F` (the two edge sets live on the disjoint pair sets `C(U, 2)` and\n700\t`E_S`, so `|E(G)| = |E(H)| + |F|`). Hence\n701\t\n702\t    w_p(G) = p^{|E(H)| + |F|} (1 − p)^{C(N, 2) − |E(H)| + |E_S| − |F|} = w_p(H) · p^{|F|} (1 − p)^{|E_S| − |F|}.\n703\t\n704\t`E_bip(S)` depends on `G` only through `G[U] = H` (its components are the\n705\tcomponents of `H`). Summing over the fibre of `H`,\n706\t\n707\t    P_n(E_bip(S)) = Σ_{H ∈ E_bip(t)} w_p(H) · Σ_{F ⊆ E_S} p^{|F|} (1 − p)^{|E_S| − |F|} = Σ_{H ∈ E_bip(t)} w_p(H) · (p + 1 − p)^{|E_S|} = P_N(E_bip(t)),\n708\t\n709\tthe inner sum being the binomial theorem (the same step as\n710\t`sum_gnpWeight_emptyPairGraphs` in `FirstMoment.lean`). (Counting step)\n711\tThere are `C(n, k)` sets `S`. ∎\n712\t\n713\t*Proof of Lemma P.* (Component partition) For a set partition\n714\t`π = {B_1, …, B_κ}` of the `N` vertices, the event \"the components of `G`\n715\tare exactly the blocks of `π`\" is the intersection of \"`G[B_i]` is\n716\tconnected\" for each `i` and \"no edge joins `B_i` to `B_j`\" for `i ≠ j`.\n717\tThese events are determined by pairwise disjoint sets of vertex pairs (the\n718\tpairs inside `B_i`; the pairs between `B_i` and `B_j`), so under `G(N, p)`\n719\tthey are independent, and the event has probability\n720\t`Π_i C_{|B_i|} · (1 − p)^{#cross pairs}` with\n721\t`#cross pairs = C(N, 2) − Σ_i C(|B_i|, 2) = (N² − Σ_i |B_i|²)/2`. (Disjoint\n722\tand exhaustive) Every graph has exactly one component partition, so these\n723\tevents partition the space. (Grouping by type) `E_bip(t)` depends on `π`\n724\tonly through the multiset `λ` of block sizes (the subset-sum condition),\n725\tand the number of set partitions with block-size multiset `λ` is\n726\t`N!/(Π_i λ_i! · Π_s m_s!)`. Sum. ∎\n727\t\n728\t*Proof of Lemma G.* (Conditioning on the component of vertex `1`) For\n729\t`i < j` and an `i`-set `B ∋ 1` (there are `C(j − 1, i − 1)`), the event\n730\t\"the component of `1` is exactly `B`\" is \"`G[B]` connected\" ∩ \"no `B`–`B^c`\n731\tedge\", independent events on disjoint pair sets, of probability\n732\t`C_i (1 − p)^{i(j − i)}`. (Disjoint and exhaustive) These events over all\n733\t`B ∌ V` are disjoint, and their union is the complement of \"`G` is\n734\tconnected\" (`B = V`). ∎\n735\t\n736\t*Proof of Lemma F.* Same conditioning. Given that the component of `1` is\n737\t`B` with `|B| = i`, the event `E_relax(M)` holds iff `i ≤ M` and\n738\t`G[B^c] ∈ E_relax(M)`; `G[B^c]` is determined by the pairs inside `B^c`,\n739\tdisjoint from the pairs that fix \"the component of `1` is `B`\", so it is an\n740\tindependent `G(j − i, p)`. Sum over `B` with `i ≤ M`. ∎\n741\t\n742\t*Proof of Corollary S′.* L1′ (b): `E_bip(S) ⊆ E_relax(S)` for every `S`, so\n743\t`P_N(E_bip(t)) ≤ P_N(E_relax(t))`, and Theorem S gives the rest. Lemma F\n744\tevaluates the right-hand side. ∎\n745\t\n746\t*Proof of Theorem Cl.* (Inclusion step) By L1 and L2 (§2.1, §2.3), every\n747\tgraph with `pw ≤ k` has a closed pair `(A, R)` with `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ r`,\n748\t`|S| ≤ k` where `S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)`; so `{pw ≤ k} ⊆ {X₃ > 0} ⊆ {X₃ ≥ 1}` and\n749\t`P(pw ≤ k) ≤ E[X₃]`. (Characterisation step) For a fixed ordered partition\n750\t`(A, R, S)` of `V`, `(A, R)` is a closed pair iff `R = V ∖ N[A]` and\n751\t`A = V ∖ N[R]`, iff (no `A–R` edge) ∧ (every `v ∈ S` has a neighbour in\n752\t`A`) ∧ (every `v ∈ S` has a neighbour in `R`): `R = V ∖ N[A]` says `R` is\n753\tdisjoint from `N[A]` (no `A–R` edge) and everything outside `A ∪ R` is in\n754\t`N[A]`; symmetrically for `A`. (Independence step) The `1 + 2|S|` events\n755\tare determined by pairwise disjoint pair sets — the `a r′` pairs `A × R`,\n756\tand for each `v ∈ S` the `a` pairs `{v} × A` and the `r′` pairs `{v} × R` —\n757\tso they are independent under `G(n, p)`, with probabilities `(1 − p)^{a r′}`,\n758\t`1 − (1 − p)^a` and `1 − (1 − p)^{r′}`. (Counting step) There are\n759\t`n!/(a! r′! s!)` ordered partitions with the given sizes; by linearity\n760\t`E[X₃]` is the stated sum. The case `r′ = 0 < s` contributes `0`\n761\t(`1 − (1 − p)^0 = 0`: a separator vertex cannot see an empty side), and\n762\t`a ≥ t ≥ 1` always. ∎\n763\t\n764\t*Proof of Lemma W.* If `X = 0` both sides are `0`. If `X > 0`, every empty\n765\t`P` has `Z(P) ≤ X`, so `1/Z(P) ≥ 1/X` and\n766\t`Y ≥ Σ_P 1[P empty]/X = X/X = 1 = 1[X > 0]`. With `Z = X` the first\n767\tinequality is an equality. Taking expectations, `P(X > 0) = E[1[X > 0]] ≤ E[Y]`,\n768\tand `E[1[P empty]/Z(P)] = P(P empty) · E[1/Z(P) | P empty]` by the\n769\tdefinition of conditional expectation on the event `{P empty}` (for\n770\t`P(P empty) > 0`; otherwise the term is `0`). ∎\n771\t\n772\t*Proof of Lemma Fl.* Write `A′ = (A ∖ J) ∪ J′`, `R′ = (R ∖ J′) ∪ J`.\n773\t(Sizes) `J ⊆ A`, `J′ ⊆ R` are disjoint from each other and from the sets\n774\tthey are added to, and `|J| = |J′|`, so `|A′| = |A|`, `|R′| = |R|`, and\n775\t`A′ ∩ R′ = ∅`. (Emptiness) An `A′–R′` edge has one of four forms: between\n776\t`A ∖ J` and `R ∖ J′` (none: `(A, R)` is empty); between `J′ ⊆ R` and\n777\t`J ⊆ A` (none, likewise); between `A ∖ J` and `J` (none: a vertex of\n778\t`J ⊆ I_A` has no neighbour in `A`); between `J′` and `R ∖ J′` (none:\n779\t`J′ ⊆ I_R`). (Injectivity) `A′` determines `J′ = A′ ∖ A` and `J = A ∖ A′`.\n780\t(Count) `Σ_{j} C(|I_A|, j) C(|I_R|, j) = C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)` by\n781\tVandermonde's identity (`Nat.add_choose_eq` in Mathlib). The image lies in\n782\tthe exact-size empty pairs, so its cardinality is at most `X`, and it\n783\tcontains `(A, R)` itself (`J = J′ = ∅`), so `Z ≥ 1`. ∎\n784\t\n785\t*Proof of Lemma J.* (Product step) Fix `U ⊆ A`, `U′ ⊆ R`. The event\n786\t\"`(A, R)` empty, `U ⊆ I_A`, `U′ ⊆ I_R`\" is the intersection over the\n787\tpatterns `j` of the event that `P_j` (i) does not meet both `A` and `R`,\n788\t(ii) does not contain a customer of `U` together with another customer of\n789\t`A`, and (iii) likewise for `U′` and `R`; the patterns are independent and\n790\tuniform, so the probability is `Π_j allowed_j / C(n, s_j)` with `allowed_j`\n791\tthe number of `s_j`-subsets satisfying (i)–(iii). (Counting step) Such a\n792\tsubset either lies inside `S` (`C(s, s_j)` sets), or meets `A` — then by\n793\t(i) it lies in `A ∪ S`, and by (ii) either it avoids `U` entirely\n794\t(`C(a − u + s, s_j) − C(s, s_j)` sets, subtracting those inside `S`) or\n795\tits only customer of `A` is one of the `u` customers of `U`\n796\t(`u · C(s, s_j − 1)` sets) — or symmetrically meets `R`. The three cases\n797\tare disjoint, and the sum is `allowed(u, u′; s_j)`. The number of choices\n798\tof `(U, U′)` is `C(a, u) C(r′, u′)`, and every choice has the same\n799\tprobability by symmetry. (Inversion step) `B(u, u′) = E[C(I_A, u) C(I_R, u′) 1[empty]]`\n800\t(count the `(U, U′)` inside `(I_A, I_R)`), and for every integer `I`,\n801\t`Σ_{u ≥ i} (−1)^{u − i} C(u, i) C(I, u) = C(I, i) Σ_{u ≥ i} (−1)^{u − i} C(I − i, u − i) = C(I, i) (1 − 1)^{I − i} = 1[I = i]`;\n802\tapply it in `u` and in `u′`. ∎\n803\t\n804\t*Proof of Corollary W-flip.* Lemma W with `𝓟` the ordered pairs of the\n805\tforced sizes and `Z` the flip count (admissible by Lemma Fl applied to the\n806\tMOSP graph, in which `I_A` is as described in Lemma J), then\n807\t`E[Y] = Σ_P E[1[P empty]/Z(P)]`; every `P` has the same law by the symmetry\n808\tof the model under permutations of the customers, there are\n809\t`C(n, t) C(n − t, r)` of them, and for one of them\n810\t`E[1[empty]/C(I_A + I_R, I_A)] = Σ_{i, i′} P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)/C(i + i′, i)`\n811\tby Lemma J. The first equality is `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`\n812\t(`MOSPGraph.lean`). ∎\n813\t\n814\t*Proof of Theorem Cl-cover.* The inclusion and counting steps are those of\n815\tTheorem Cl on `mospGraph` (L3 b); for a fixed ordered partition the closed\n816\tevent is \"no pattern meets both `A` and `R`\" ∧ \"every `v ∈ S` shares a\n817\tpattern with `A`\" ∧ \"every `v ∈ S` shares a pattern with `R`\" (L3 c), and\n818\tinclusion–exclusion over the sets `T_A ⊆ S` of separator customers sharing\n819\tno pattern with `A` and `T_R ⊆ S` of those sharing none with `R` gives the\n820\talternating sum of §7.1 (a), whose summand depends on `(T_A, T_R)` only\n821\tthrough `u = |S ∖ T_R|`, `w = |S ∖ T_A|` and is a product over the\n822\tindependent patterns of `allowed′(u, w; s_j)/C(n, s_j)` (the three cases of\n823\t§7.1 (a), disjoint). ∎\n824\t\n825\t*Proof of Proposition T.* By Corollary S′ with `k = ⌊βn⌋`,\n826\t`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))`. (Thinning step) `G(n, c/n) − S`\n827\tis `G(N, c/n) = G(N, c′/N)` with `c′ = cN/n = c(1 − k/n) → c(1 − β)`.\n828\t(Entropy step) `C(n, k) ≤ e^{n H(k/n)}` (Lemma 1 of\n829\t`random_graph_first_moment.md` §2.1, the two-symbol case). (Hypothesis)\n830\t`P_N(E_relax(t)) ≤ e^{−(I(c′) − o(1)) N}`. So\n831\t`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ exp(n [H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) + o(1)])`, and as `β → 0`,\n832\t`H(β) → 0` and `(1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) → I(c) > 0` by continuity, so the\n833\tbracket is negative for every sufficiently small `β > 0`. ∎ (The pair\n834\tcount's bracket at `β → 0` is `ln 2 − c/4`, negative only for `c > 4 ln 2`;\n835\tthe true threshold of linear pathwidth is `c = 1`.)\n836\t\n837\t*Proof of Proposition T-cover.* §7.4. ∎\n838\t\n839\t### 8.3 The check\n840\t\n841\t`check.py` (`check.log`, `lemma_w_n10.csv`; 29 s on 8 workers at nice 19\n842\tunder a one-minute load below 2). Stage 1 enumerates every labelled graph\n843\ton `n = 1, …, 7` vertices (`2^{C(n,2)}`: 1, 2, 8, 64, 1,024, 32,768,\n844\t2,097,152), computes each pathwidth by the subset DP and, in exact\n845\trationals at `p = 1/2` and `p = 1/3`, for every `k < n`:\n846\t\n847\t* the weighted count of `{pw ≤ k}` against `C(n, k) · P_N(E_bip)` (Theorem S),\n848\t  against `C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))` (Corollary S′), against `E[X₃]`\n849\t  (Theorem Cl) and against `E[X₁]` (the count on record at exact sizes);\n850\t* the enumerated `E[X₃]` against Theorem Cl's formula, and `E[X₁]` against\n851\t  `C(n, t) C(n − t, r) (1 − p)^{t r}` — equalities;\n852\t* on `N` vertices, `P_N(connected)` against Lemma G, `P_N(E_bip)` against\n853\t  Lemma P and `P_N(E_relax(M))` for every `M` against Lemma F — equalities\n854\t  (and at `p = 1/2` Gilbert's recursion returns the labelled connected\n855\t  counts 1, 1, 4, 38, 728, 26,704, 1,866,256, OEIS A001187);\n856\t* at `n ≤ 6`, for **every** `k`-set `S`, the weighted count of `{E_bip(S)}`\n857\t  against `P_N(E_bip)` — the factorisation identity, equality on all 120\n858\t  `(n, k, S)` triples at each `p`;\n859\t* at `n ≤ 6`, the flip lemma on every exact-size empty pair at every `k`\n860\t  (1,422,005 pairs): every swap is an exact-size empty pair, the swaps are\n861\t  distinct, their number is `C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|) ≤ X`;\n862\t* the floating-point `log_prob_bip`, `log_prob_rel` (§5) and\n863\t  `log_closed_count` (§6) pinned to the exact values to `10⁻⁹`.\n864\t\n865\tThe `n = 7` rows (`n ≤ 6` in the log; `P` is the weighted count, i.e. the\n866\tprobability under `G(7, p)`):\n867\t\n868\t| `k` | `N` | `t` | `p` | `P(pw ≤ k)` | `C(n,k) P_N(E_bip)` | `C(n,k) P_N(E_relax)` | `E[X₃]` (Cl) | `E[X₁]` (pairs) |\n869\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n870\t| 0 | 7 | 4 | 1/2 | 0.0000005 | 0.00639 | 0.00639 | 0.00854 | 0.00854 |\n871\t| 1 | 6 | 3 | 1/2 | 0.0172 | 0.1156 | 0.1188 | 0.2264 | 0.2734 |\n872\t| 2 | 5 | 3 | 1/2 | 0.3389 | 2.174 | 2.174 | 1.948 | 3.281 |\n873\t| 3 | 4 | 2 | 1/2 | 0.8852 | 5.469 | 5.469 | 6.006 | 13.125 |\n874\t| 4 | 3 | 2 | 1/2 | 0.9985 | 17.50 | 17.50 | 10.18 | 26.25 |\n875\t| 5 | 2 | 1 | 1/2 | 1.0000 | 10.50 | 10.50 | 14.37 | 21.00 |\n876\t| 6 | 1 | 1 | 1/2 | 1.0000 | 7.000 | 7.000 | 15.37 | 7.000 |\n877\t| 0 | 7 | 4 | 1/3 | 0.0002 | 0.1245 | 0.1245 | 0.2698 | 0.2698 |\n878\t| 1 | 6 | 3 | 1/3 | 0.2449 | 1.137 | 1.167 | 2.343 | 3.642 |\n879\t| 2 | 5 | 3 | 1/3 | 0.8467 | 8.330 | 8.330 | 7.351 | 18.44 |\n880\t| 3 | 4 | 2 | 1/3 | 0.9959 | 14.60 | 14.60 | 13.58 | 41.48 |\n881\t| 4 | 3 | 2 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 25.93 | 25.93 | 17.87 | 46.67 |\n882\t| 5 | 2 | 1 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 14.00 | 14.00 | 22.16 | 28.00 |\n883\t| 6 | 1 | 1 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 7.000 | 7.000 | 23.16 | 7.000 |\n884\t\n885\tRead across at seven vertices only: every bound is above the probability\n886\t(as it must be; most are above `1` and say nothing); `E_bip` and `E_relax`\n887\tdiffer in measure for the first time at `N = 6` (`0.1156` against\n888\t`0.1188` at `p = 1/2`; at `N ≤ 5` the two events coincide on every graph,\n889\t`3K₂` being the first separation, §2.2 d); the separator count is the\n890\tsmaller of the two new counts at `k = 0–1`, the closed count at\n891\t`k = 2–4` — a seven-vertex echo of §6.3's \"separator on the sparse side,\n892\tclosed pair on the dense side\", and nothing more at this size; at `k = n − 1`\n893\tthe closed count exceeds the pair count (it admits sides larger than the\n894\tforced ones, `s < k`). None of these numbers is quoted anywhere as a\n895\tbound.\n896\t\n897\tStage 2, Lemma W at `n = 10` on 1,000 random instances (600 `G(10, p)`,\n898\t`p ∈ {0.2, …, 0.7}`, seed = index; 400 covers from\n899\t`generate_structured_instance(10, m ∈ {10, 20}, 2–4 customers per pattern,\n900\tseed = index)`, the MOSP graph), at `k = pw` and `k = pw − 1`, with\n901\t`Z_move(P)` = the size of `P`'s move-class (§3) and `Z_flip` = the flip\n902\tcount: `1 ≤ Z ≤ X` on every pair, `Y_move = X₄` exactly, `1 ≤ Y_flip ≤ X`\n903\twhenever `X > 0`, `Y = 0 = 1[X > 0]` whenever `X = 0`, every swap an\n904\texact-size empty pair. Medians at `k = pw`: `X = 12`, `X₄ = 5`,\n905\t`Y_flip = 10` on the random graphs; `8`, `4`, `6` on the covers; at\n906\t`k = pw − 1`, `X = 0` on 351 of 600 and 225 of 400 (so `Y = 0` there,\n907\tand the identity is exact on both sides). The flips merge a factor\n908\t`1.2–1.3` at the median at these sizes; the move-classes `2.4`.\n909\t\n910\tStage 3, the first instance: Lemma J's inverted moments equal the\n911\tenumerated joint law `P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)` on 24 random\n912\t`(n, sizes, a, r′)` cases at `n = 3–6`, `m ≤ 3`, sizes 2–3 (every cover\n913\tenumerated; item 07's test does 66 more); and `E[Y]`, `E[X]` from\n914\t`flip_log_bound` against 1,000 Monte Carlo covers at `n = 10`, `m = 10`,\n915\t`d = 3` (i.i.d. uniform patterns, the model of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`):\n916\t\n917\t| `k` | `N`, `t` | exact `E[Y]` | sample `± s.e.` | exact `E[X]` | sample `± s.e.` | sample `P(X > 0)` |\n918\t|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n919\t| 3 | 7, 4 | 1.350 | 1.207 ± 0.084 | 1.430 | 1.291 ± 0.092 | 0.418 |\n920\t| 4 | 6, 3 | 9.419 | 9.403 ± 0.332 | 10.638 | 10.602 ± 0.406 | 0.872 |\n921\t| 5 | 5, 3 | 41.23 | 41.31 ± 0.75 | 49.48 | 49.06 ± 1.04 | 1.000 |\n922\t| 6 | 4, 2 | 70.19 | 71.22 ± 0.72 | 88.40 | 89.50 ± 1.14 | 1.000 |\n923\t\n924\tAll within 1.7 standard errors; `P(X > 0) ≤ E[Y] ≤ E[X]` in every row, as\n925\tLemma W says. At `k = 3` the weighted count sits at `1.35` against a true\n926\t`P(X > 0)` of about `0.42`: the gap between `E[Y]` and the truth at this\n927\tsize is the lower tail of `Z` (instances with `I_A = I_R = 0` have\n928\t`Z = 1`), the mechanism §7.2 (ii) and item 09 name.\n929\t\n930\t### 8.4 Lean targets for item 10\n931\t\n932\tEach row: the statement, the existing lemma it rests on, and the one new\n933\tingredient. The vocabulary is §2.6's (`IsEmptyPair`, `closedNbhd`,\n934\t`gnpWeight`, `graphsOfPathwidthLe`, `sum_biUnion_le_sum_of_nonneg`,\n935\t`sum_gnpWeight_emptyPairGraphs` from `FirstMoment.lean`;\n936\t`exists_empty_pair_of_layout` from `EmptyPair.lean`; `coverGraph`,\n937\t`emptyPairCovers`, `card_filter_avoid` from `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`).\n938\t\n939\t| target | statement | rests on | the new ingredient |\n940\t|---|---|---|---|\n941\t| `exists_isBalancedBip` (L1′ a) | `pathwidth G ≤ k → k < n → ∃ S, S.card = k ∧ IsBalancedBip G S k` (pair form) | `exists_balanced_separator` (§2.6, from `exists_empty_pair_of_layout`) | none beyond §2.6 |\n942\t| `isBalancedBip_induce` | `IsBalancedBip G S k` depends on `G` only through the edges inside `univ \\ S`: for `G, G′` agreeing on pairs disjoint from `S`, `IsBalancedBip G S k ↔ IsBalancedBip G′ S k` | `IsEmptyPair` mentions only `A × R` pairs, `A, R ⊆ univ \\ S` | the \"agree off `S`\" relation on graphs |\n943\t| `sum_gnpWeight_fiber` (**the factorisation step**) | for `S : Finset V` and `𝓗` a predicate on graphs invariant under \"agree off `S`\": `Σ_{G : 𝓗 G} gnpWeight p G = Σ_{H on univ \\ S, 𝓗 H} gnpWeight′ p H`, i.e. the fibre sum over `F ⊆ E_S` is `(p + (1 − p))^{|E_S|} = 1` | `edges_fromEdgeSet`, `Finset.sum_pow_mul_eq_add_pow` (as in `sum_gnpWeight_emptyPairGraphs`) | the bijection `Φ_S` between graphs on `V` and pairs (graph on `univ \\ S`, subset of `E_S`); the cleanest form keeps `H` as a graph on `V` with no edge meeting `S` (`H.edgeSet ⊆ pairs off S`), which avoids a subtype vertex set |\n944\t| `weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le_separator` (**Theorem S**) | `Σ_{G : pw ≤ k} gnpWeight p G ≤ C(n, k) · innerBip p N` with `innerBip` the weighted count of the pair-form event on `N` vertices | `sum_biUnion_le_sum_of_nonneg`, `Finset.card_powersetCard`, the two rows above | none once the two rows exist |\n945\t| `innerBip_eq_partition_sum` (Lemma P) | the partition formula | — | components of a graph as `Finset`s with their sizes (`ConnectedComponent.supp` is a `Set`; `Set.toFinset` under `Fintype`), the multinomial count of set partitions of a given type — **the obstacle named in §2.6; not needed for Theorem S**, whose inner probability is defined as the weighted count |\n946\t| `gilbert_recursion`, `relax_recursion` (Lemmas G, F) | as stated | — | the fibre \"component of `v` is exactly `B`\" as `G[B]` connected ∧ no `B`–`Bᶜ` edge, and the independence (product of weighted sums over disjoint pair sets); shares the obstacle above; Corollary S′ needs `E_relax` in component form, so it is in the same bucket |\n947\t| `exists_closedPair_of_pathwidth_le` (L1 + L2) | §2.6 | `closure_isClosedPair`, `closure_card` | **no termination argument is needed**: L2 (iii) says the closure is a fixpoint after one round, so item 10's \"iterate until the fixpoint\" (method (c)) is replaced by two applications of `V \\ closedNbhd` — a correction to the item 10 brief, not an obstacle |\n948\t| `isClosedPair_iff_sees_both` (characterisation step of Cl) | `IsClosedPair G A R ↔ IsEmptyPair G A R ∧ ∀ v ∉ A ∪ R, (∃ a ∈ A, G.Adj v a) ∧ (∃ r ∈ R, G.Adj v r)` | `mem_closedNbhd_iff` | none |\n949\t| `sum_gnpWeight_closedPairGraphs` (independence step of Cl) | `Σ_{G : IsClosedPair G A R} gnpWeight p G = (1 − p)^{a r} ((1 − (1 − p)^a)(1 − (1 − p)^r))^s` | `sum_gnpWeight_emptyPairGraphs`'s binomial step | a product-form lemma: if the pairs off the diagonal are partitioned into blocks and the event is a conjunction of one event per block, the weighted count is the product of the per-block weighted counts; then `1 − (1 − p)^a` for \"some edge into `A`\" |\n950\t| `weightedSum_graphsOfPathwidthLe_le_closed` (**Theorem Cl**) | `Σ_{G : pw ≤ k} gnpWeight p G ≤ Σ_{(a, r′, s)} n!/(a! r′! s!) · (…)` | the three rows above, `Finset.card_powersetCard` twice for the ordered partitions | none |\n951\t| `weightedCount_ge_indicator` (**Lemma W**) | `(∀ P ∈ 𝓟, empty P → 1 ≤ Z P ∧ Z P ≤ (𝓟.filter empty).card) → Σ_{P ∈ 𝓟} (if empty P then 1 / Z P else 0) ≥ (if ∃ P ∈ 𝓟, empty P then 1 else 0)` in `ℝ` or `ℚ` | `Finset.sum_le_sum`, `one_div_le_one_div_of_le`, `Finset.sum_const` | nothing beyond finite sums (as the item says) |\n952\t| `flip_isEmptyPair`, `flip_injective`, `card_flips` (Lemma Fl) | as stated, with `isolatedIn G A := A.filter (fun v => ∀ a ∈ A, ¬ G.Adj v a)` | `Nat.add_choose_eq` (Vandermonde) | the four-case emptiness argument; `Finset.card_powersetCard` and a sigma over `j` for the count |\n953\t| `prob_le_weighted_flip` (Corollary W-flip) | `P(mospValue ≤ k + 1) ≤ C(n, t) C(n − t, r) Σ_{i, i′} P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)/C(i + i′, i)` | Lemma W, Lemma Fl, `MOSPGraph.lean`'s equality, `card_emptyPairIndices` | Lemma J's allowed-count (the analogue of `card_filter_avoid` with the three cases) and the two-variable inversion; **moderately long, and can be left on paper**: the Lean statement can stop at `P ≤ E[Y]` with `E[Y]` the weighted count over covers, which is the sound part |\n954\t| Theorem Cl-cover | the `(u, w)` sum | `card_filter_avoid`'s pattern | inclusion–exclusion over `(T_A, T_R)` in Lean: not within a session; leave as the paper statement |\n955\t| Propositions T, T-cover | — | — | not Lean targets: asymptotic and conditional on a cited LDP |\n956\t\n957\tRecommended order for item 10 (stop at the first that fills the session,\n958\tas its brief says): Lemma W (an hour at most), L1′ a and the closed-pair\n959\texistence (already listed in §2.6), the characterisation and independence\n960\tsteps of Cl, then the factorisation step and Theorem S; Lemmas P, G, F\n961\tand Corollary S′ only if components-as-`Finset`s turns out cheap.\n962\t\n963\t### 8.5 Regenerate, size range, kill verdict, what this does not claim\n964\t\n965\t* **Regenerate.** `nice -n 19 python3 reports/scratch/separator_moment/08_theorems/check.py --max-n 7 --workers 8`\n966\t  (29 s; `--stages 1,2,3` selects; `lemma_w_n10.csv` is written by stage 2);\n967\t  `python3 -m pytest tests/test_separator_moment.py -k \"gilbert or theorems_S or flip_lemma\"`\n968\t  (the same checks at `n ≤ 5` and on 40 random graphs at 8–10 vertices).\n969\t  Code: `learning/separator_moment.py` (`connected_probs_exact`,\n970\t  `prob_relax_exact`, `prob_bip_exact`, `pair_count_exact`,\n971\t  `closed_count_exact`, `weighted_graph_count`, `isolated_in`,\n972\t  `flip_swaps`, `flip_count`, `weighted_count`).\n973\t* **Size range.** The theorems and lemmas are for every `n`, `k < n`,\n974\t  `0 ≤ p ≤ 1` (Lemma J and Corollary W-flip for every `n`, `m` and size\n975\t  vector; Theorem Cl-cover likewise). The checks: every labelled graph at\n976\t  `n ≤ 7` (the factorisation per `S` and the flip lemma at `n ≤ 6`), 1,000\n977\t  random instances at `n = 10`, 24 enumerated cover cases at `n ≤ 6`, 1,000\n978\t  Monte Carlo covers at `n = 10`.\n979\t* **Kill verdict: not met.** Zero violations; no statement is withdrawn.\n980\t* **Does not claim.** Any rate or constant: Theorem S's inner probability\n981\t  is a finite weighted count, and what it does as `N → ∞` is §5–§6's,\n982\t  fitted or bracketed, not proved. That `E_bip` and `E_relax` have the\n983\t  same rate (measured in §5.3 to `N = 120`, not proved). That the\n984\t  closed-pair count or the separator count is the better bound at any\n985\t  size beyond the seven-vertex table (§6.3 and §7.2 say where each wins,\n986\t  at the constants). That the flips are the best `Z` (they are the first\n987\t  instance; item 09's recombinations are a larger admissible `Z`, and §4.6\n988\t  measured the ceiling any `Z` can reach). Propositions T and T-cover rest\n989\t  on hypotheses (H_G), cited and unverified by us, and (H_C), stated in no\n990\t  held paper; neither is a theorem of this document. Nothing in Lean yet:\n991\t  §8.4 is a list of statements, and item 10 proves them or names the\n992\t  obstacle. The one correction to a later item's brief: item 10's\n993\t  \"termination by `|V ∖ (A ∪ R)|` strictly decreasing\" is unnecessary, by\n994\t  L2 (iii).\n995\t\n996\t## 9. The branch: the weighted count's lower tail — can any computable `Z` merge the far pairs? (item 09, 2026-11-01)\n997\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"19823f07-1f07-4669-9ce2-3beb8c4a08ca","timestamp":"2026-11-01T22:59:39.352Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/al/.claude/projects/-home-al-dev-MOSP/705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3/tool-results/b3xxd685b.txt","content":"`C_1, …, C_M` are known.\n\n**Corollary S′ (the relaxed form).** `P( pw(G(n, p)) ≤ k ) ≤ C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))`,\nwith `P_N(E_relax(t)) = R_N(t)` from Lemma F. It is weaker than Theorem S\n(`E_bip(t) ⊆ E_relax(t)`, strictly from `N = 6` on: §2.2 (d)) and is the\nform the large-deviation rate of §1.6 and §6 applies to.\n\n**Theorem Cl (the closed-pair count).** For `k < n` and `0 ≤ p ≤ 1`,\n\n    P( pw(G(n, p)) ≤ k ) ≤ E[X₃] = Σ_{a ≥ t, r′ ≥ r, a + r′ + s = n}  n! / (a! r′! s!) · (1 − p)^{a r′} · (1 − (1 − p)^a)^s · (1 − (1 − p)^{r′})^s,\n\nthe sum over the ordered size triples `(a, r′, s)` (so `s ≤ k`\nautomatically). The right-hand side is exactly the expected number of\nclosed pairs with the sizes of L2, not only a bound on it.\n\n**Lemma W (the weighted-count identity).** Let `𝓟` be a finite index set\nof pairs, `X = Σ_{P ∈ 𝓟} 1[P empty]`, and `Z : 𝓟 → ℕ` a function (which may\ndepend on the graph) with `1 ≤ Z(P) ≤ X` whenever `P` is empty. Then\n\n    Y := Σ_{P ∈ 𝓟} 1[P empty] / Z(P)  ≥  1[X > 0],\n\npointwise on every graph, with equality when `Z = X`. Hence, for any\nprobability law on graphs, `P(X > 0) ≤ E[Y] = Σ_P P(P empty) · E[1/Z(P) | P empty]`.\nIn particular `P(pw ≤ k) ≤ E[Y]` whenever `𝓟` is the set of ordered pairs\nof the forced sizes (L1).\n\n**Lemma Fl (the flip lemma, deterministic).** Let `(A, R)` be an empty pair\nand `I_A`, `I_R` the vertices of `A`, `R` isolated in `G[A]`, `G[R]`. For\n`J ⊆ I_A`, `J′ ⊆ I_R` with `|J| = |J′|`, the pair\n`((A ∖ J) ∪ J′, (R ∖ J′) ∪ J)` is an empty pair with `|A|`, `|R|` vertices;\nthe map `(J, J′) ↦ ((A ∖ J) ∪ J′, (R ∖ J′) ∪ J)` is injective; and the number\nof such pairs is `C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)`. Consequently\n`Z(A, R) := C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)` satisfies `1 ≤ Z ≤ X` on `{(A, R) empty}`\nand is admissible in Lemma W.\n\n**Lemma J (the joint binomial moments of the flips on random covers).** Let\nthe patterns `P_1, …, P_m` be independent uniform subsets of the `n`\ncustomers of sizes `s_1, …, s_m`, and `(A, R, S)` a fixed ordered partition\nwith sizes `(a, r′, s)`. In the MOSP graph, a customer of `A` is isolated in\n`G[A]` iff no pattern contains it together with another customer of `A`.\nFor `u ≤ a`, `u′ ≤ r′`,\n\n    B(u, u′) := Σ_{U ⊆ A, |U| = u} Σ_{U′ ⊆ R, |U′| = u′} P( (A, R) empty, U ⊆ I_A, U′ ⊆ I_R )\n             = C(a, u) C(r′, u′) · Π_j allowed(u, u′; s_j) / C(n, s_j),\n    allowed(u, u′; s_j) = C(a − u + s, s_j) + u C(s, s_j − 1) + C(r′ − u′ + s, s_j) + u′ C(s, s_j − 1) − C(s, s_j),\n\nand the joint law is recovered by two-variable inversion,\n\n    P( I_A = i, I_R = i′, (A, R) empty ) = Σ_{u ≥ i} Σ_{u′ ≥ i′} (−1)^{u − i + u′ − i′} C(u, i) C(u′, i′) B(u, u′).\n\n**Corollary W-flip (the first instance, exactly).** For a random cover as in\nLemma J and `k < n`,\n\n    P( mospValue ≤ k + 1 ) = P( pw(mospGraph) ≤ k ) ≤ C(n, t) C(n − t, r) · Σ_{i ≤ t, i′ ≤ r} P( I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty ) / C(i + i′, i),\n\nwith the joint law of Lemma J at `(a, r′) = (t, r)`. This is `flip_log_bound`\nof §7.1 (b); the sum has `(t + 1)(r + 1)` terms.\n\n**Theorem Cl-cover (the closed pair on random covers, finite form).** With\nthe patterns as in Lemma J, `P(mospValue ≤ k + 1)` is at most the sum over\nthe ordered triples `(a, r′, s)` of `n!/(a! r′! s!)` times\n\n    P(closed) = Σ_{u, w = 0}^{s} (−1)^{u + w} C(s, u) C(s, w) · Π_j allowed′(u, w; s_j) / C(n, s_j),\n    allowed′(u, w; s_j) = C(s, s_j) + [C(r′ + u, s_j) − C(u, s_j)] + [C(a + w, s_j) − C(w, s_j)],\n\nthe `(u, w)` sum of §7.1 (a). The cancellation-free upper bound of §7.1 (a′)\n(negative association of sampling without replacement, Joag-Dev & Proschan\n1983, *Ann. Statist.* 11, Theorem 2.11 and properties P6–P7, with a tangent\nof the concave `ln(1 − e^{−L})`) is a corollary, valid at every `(n, k)`;\nit is not re-proved here, and its check is item 07's (`07_cover/check.log`:\nthe exact sum against the enumeration of every cover at `n = 3–8`, 66\ncases, and the bound above it on all).\n\n**Proposition T (the threshold on `G(n, c/n)`; conditional).** *Hypothesis\n(H_G), cited and not verified by us:* for every `c′ > 2 ln 2` there is\n`I(c′) > 0`, continuous in `c′`, with\n`P( G(N, c′/N) ∈ E_relax(⌈N/2⌉) ) ≤ exp(−(I(c′) − o(1)) N)` as `N → ∞`. Then\nfor every `c > 2 ln 2` there is `β₀(c) > 0` with\n`P( pw(G(n, c/n)) ≤ β₀ n ) → 0`. (H_G) is the upper-bound half of the\nlarge-deviation principle for the largest component of `G(N, c′/N)`\napplied to the closed set `[0, ½]`: O'Connell 1998 (not held) as restated\nby Puhalskii 2005 (*Ann. Probab.* 33(2), Cor. 2.4(3), p. 8 of the arXiv\nversion, with (2.13)–(2.14)) and Andreis–König–Patterson 2021 (Cor. 1.3);\n`I_{c′}(½) > 0` iff `c′ > 2 ln 2` on the quoted formula (§1.4, §6.4).\n\n**Proposition T-cover (the threshold on random covers; conditional).** Fix a\nbounded size law and `μ > 0` patterns per customer, and let `θ` be the\nsurvival probability of §7.1 (c). *Hypothesis (H_C), stated in no held\npaper:* for every `u < θ` there is `I(u) > 0` with\n`P( L₁(mospGraph) ≤ u n ) ≤ exp(−I(u) n + o(n))`, and the same for the\nthinned covers of §7.4 with rates converging as the thinning vanishes. Then\n`θ > ½` implies `P( mospValue ≤ β₀ n ) → 0` for some `β₀ > 0`. This is §7.4's\nProposition 7.1 verbatim; it is repeated here so that §8 is the complete\nlist.\n\n### 8.2 Proofs\n\n*Proof of Theorem S.* (Inclusion step) By L1′ (a) (§2.2), every graph with\n`pw ≤ k` has a `k`-set `S` with `E_bip(S)`: `{pw ≤ k} ⊆ ⋃_{|S| = k} E_bip(S)`.\n(Union step) `P_n(pw ≤ k) ≤ Σ_{|S| = k} P_n(E_bip(S))`, the weights being\nnon-negative. (Factorisation step) Fix `S` and write `U = V ∖ S` (`|U| = N`)\nand `E_S` for the set of the `C(n, 2) − C(N, 2)` vertex pairs that meet `S`.\nThe map\n\n    Φ_S : {graphs on V} → {graphs on U} × 𝒫(E_S),      G ↦ ( G[U], E(G) ∩ E_S )\n\nis a bijection, with inverse `(H, F) ↦` the graph on `V` with edge set\n`E(H) ∪ F` (the two edge sets live on the disjoint pair sets `C(U, 2)` and\n`E_S`, so `|E(G)| = |E(H)| + |F|`). Hence\n\n    w_p(G) = p^{|E(H)| + |F|} (1 − p)^{C(N, 2) − |E(H)| + |E_S| − |F|} = w_p(H) · p^{|F|} (1 − p)^{|E_S| − |F|}.\n\n`E_bip(S)` depends on `G` only through `G[U] = H` (its components are the\ncomponents of `H`). Summing over the fibre of `H`,\n\n    P_n(E_bip(S)) = Σ_{H ∈ E_bip(t)} w_p(H) · Σ_{F ⊆ E_S} p^{|F|} (1 − p)^{|E_S| − |F|} = Σ_{H ∈ E_bip(t)} w_p(H) · (p + 1 − p)^{|E_S|} = P_N(E_bip(t)),\n\nthe inner sum being the binomial theorem (the same step as\n`sum_gnpWeight_emptyPairGraphs` in `FirstMoment.lean`). (Counting step)\nThere are `C(n, k)` sets `S`. ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma P.* (Component partition) For a set partition\n`π = {B_1, …, B_κ}` of the `N` vertices, the event \"the components of `G`\nare exactly the blocks of `π`\" is the intersection of \"`G[B_i]` is\nconnected\" for each `i` and \"no edge joins `B_i` to `B_j`\" for `i ≠ j`.\nThese events are determined by pairwise disjoint sets of vertex pairs (the\npairs inside `B_i`; the pairs between `B_i` and `B_j`), so under `G(N, p)`\nthey are independent, and the event has probability\n`Π_i C_{|B_i|} · (1 − p)^{#cross pairs}` with\n`#cross pairs = C(N, 2) − Σ_i C(|B_i|, 2) = (N² − Σ_i |B_i|²)/2`. (Disjoint\nand exhaustive) Every graph has exactly one component partition, so these\nevents partition the space. (Grouping by type) `E_bip(t)` depends on `π`\nonly through the multiset `λ` of block sizes (the subset-sum condition),\nand the number of set partitions with block-size multiset `λ` is\n`N!/(Π_i λ_i! · Π_s m_s!)`. Sum. ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma G.* (Conditioning on the component of vertex `1`) For\n`i < j` and an `i`-set `B ∋ 1` (there are `C(j − 1, i − 1)`), the event\n\"the component of `1` is exactly `B`\" is \"`G[B]` connected\" ∩ \"no `B`–`B^c`\nedge\", independent events on disjoint pair sets, of probability\n`C_i (1 − p)^{i(j − i)}`. (Disjoint and exhaustive) These events over all\n`B ∌ V` are disjoint, and their union is the complement of \"`G` is\nconnected\" (`B = V`). ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma F.* Same conditioning. Given that the component of `1` is\n`B` with `|B| = i`, the event `E_relax(M)` holds iff `i ≤ M` and\n`G[B^c] ∈ E_relax(M)`; `G[B^c]` is determined by the pairs inside `B^c`,\ndisjoint from the pairs that fix \"the component of `1` is `B`\", so it is an\nindependent `G(j − i, p)`. Sum over `B` with `i ≤ M`. ∎\n\n*Proof of Corollary S′.* L1′ (b): `E_bip(S) ⊆ E_relax(S)` for every `S`, so\n`P_N(E_bip(t)) ≤ P_N(E_relax(t))`, and Theorem S gives the rest. Lemma F\nevaluates the right-hand side. ∎\n\n*Proof of Theorem Cl.* (Inclusion step) By L1 and L2 (§2.1, §2.3), every\ngraph with `pw ≤ k` has a closed pair `(A, R)` with `|A| ≥ t`, `|R| ≥ r`,\n`|S| ≤ k` where `S = V ∖ (A ∪ R)`; so `{pw ≤ k} ⊆ {X₃ > 0} ⊆ {X₃ ≥ 1}` and\n`P(pw ≤ k) ≤ E[X₃]`. (Characterisation step) For a fixed ordered partition\n`(A, R, S)` of `V`, `(A, R)` is a closed pair iff `R = V ∖ N[A]` and\n`A = V ∖ N[R]`, iff (no `A–R` edge) ∧ (every `v ∈ S` has a neighbour in\n`A`) ∧ (every `v ∈ S` has a neighbour in `R`): `R = V ∖ N[A]` says `R` is\ndisjoint from `N[A]` (no `A–R` edge) and everything outside `A ∪ R` is in\n`N[A]`; symmetrically for `A`. (Independence step) The `1 + 2|S|` events\nare determined by pairwise disjoint pair sets — the `a r′` pairs `A × R`,\nand for each `v ∈ S` the `a` pairs `{v} × A` and the `r′` pairs `{v} × R` —\nso they are independent under `G(n, p)`, with probabilities `(1 − p)^{a r′}`,\n`1 − (1 − p)^a` and `1 − (1 − p)^{r′}`. (Counting step) There are\n`n!/(a! r′! s!)` ordered partitions with the given sizes; by linearity\n`E[X₃]` is the stated sum. The case `r′ = 0 < s` contributes `0`\n(`1 − (1 − p)^0 = 0`: a separator vertex cannot see an empty side), and\n`a ≥ t ≥ 1` always. ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma W.* If `X = 0` both sides are `0`. If `X > 0`, every empty\n`P` has `Z(P) ≤ X`, so `1/Z(P) ≥ 1/X` and\n`Y ≥ Σ_P 1[P empty]/X = X/X = 1 = 1[X > 0]`. With `Z = X` the first\ninequality is an equality. Taking expectations, `P(X > 0) = E[1[X > 0]] ≤ E[Y]`,\nand `E[1[P empty]/Z(P)] = P(P empty) · E[1/Z(P) | P empty]` by the\ndefinition of conditional expectation on the event `{P empty}` (for\n`P(P empty) > 0`; otherwise the term is `0`). ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma Fl.* Write `A′ = (A ∖ J) ∪ J′`, `R′ = (R ∖ J′) ∪ J`.\n(Sizes) `J ⊆ A`, `J′ ⊆ R` are disjoint from each other and from the sets\nthey are added to, and `|J| = |J′|`, so `|A′| = |A|`, `|R′| = |R|`, and\n`A′ ∩ R′ = ∅`. (Emptiness) An `A′–R′` edge has one of four forms: between\n`A ∖ J` and `R ∖ J′` (none: `(A, R)` is empty); between `J′ ⊆ R` and\n`J ⊆ A` (none, likewise); between `A ∖ J` and `J` (none: a vertex of\n`J ⊆ I_A` has no neighbour in `A`); between `J′` and `R ∖ J′` (none:\n`J′ ⊆ I_R`). (Injectivity) `A′` determines `J′ = A′ ∖ A` and `J = A ∖ A′`.\n(Count) `Σ_{j} C(|I_A|, j) C(|I_R|, j) = C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|)` by\nVandermonde's identity (`Nat.add_choose_eq` in Mathlib). The image lies in\nthe exact-size empty pairs, so its cardinality is at most `X`, and it\ncontains `(A, R)` itself (`J = J′ = ∅`), so `Z ≥ 1`. ∎\n\n*Proof of Lemma J.* (Product step) Fix `U ⊆ A`, `U′ ⊆ R`. The event\n\"`(A, R)` empty, `U ⊆ I_A`, `U′ ⊆ I_R`\" is the intersection over the\npatterns `j` of the event that `P_j` (i) does not meet both `A` and `R`,\n(ii) does not contain a customer of `U` together with another customer of\n`A`, and (iii) likewise for `U′` and `R`; the patterns are independent and\nuniform, so the probability is `Π_j allowed_j / C(n, s_j)` with `allowed_j`\nthe number of `s_j`-subsets satisfying (i)–(iii). (Counting step) Such a\nsubset either lies inside `S` (`C(s, s_j)` sets), or meets `A` — then by\n(i) it lies in `A ∪ S`, and by (ii) either it avoids `U` entirely\n(`C(a − u + s, s_j) − C(s, s_j)` sets, subtracting those inside `S`) or\nits only customer of `A` is one of the `u` customers of `U`\n(`u · C(s, s_j − 1)` sets) — or symmetrically meets `R`. The three cases\nare disjoint, and the sum is `allowed(u, u′; s_j)`. The number of choices\nof `(U, U′)` is `C(a, u) C(r′, u′)`, and every choice has the same\nprobability by symmetry. (Inversion step) `B(u, u′) = E[C(I_A, u) C(I_R, u′) 1[empty]]`\n(count the `(U, U′)` inside `(I_A, I_R)`), and for every integer `I`,\n`Σ_{u ≥ i} (−1)^{u − i} C(u, i) C(I, u) = C(I, i) Σ_{u ≥ i} (−1)^{u − i} C(I − i, u − i) = C(I, i) (1 − 1)^{I − i} = 1[I = i]`;\napply it in `u` and in `u′`. ∎\n\n*Proof of Corollary W-flip.* Lemma W with `𝓟` the ordered pairs of the\nforced sizes and `Z` the flip count (admissible by Lemma Fl applied to the\nMOSP graph, in which `I_A` is as described in Lemma J), then\n`E[Y] = Σ_P E[1[P empty]/Z(P)]`; every `P` has the same law by the symmetry\nof the model under permutations of the customers, there are\n`C(n, t) C(n − t, r)` of them, and for one of them\n`E[1[empty]/C(I_A + I_R, I_A)] = Σ_{i, i′} P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)/C(i + i′, i)`\nby Lemma J. The first equality is `mospValue = pw(mospGraph) + 1`\n(`MOSPGraph.lean`). ∎\n\n*Proof of Theorem Cl-cover.* The inclusion and counting steps are those of\nTheorem Cl on `mospGraph` (L3 b); for a fixed ordered partition the closed\nevent is \"no pattern meets both `A` and `R`\" ∧ \"every `v ∈ S` shares a\npattern with `A`\" ∧ \"every `v ∈ S` shares a pattern with `R`\" (L3 c), and\ninclusion–exclusion over the sets `T_A ⊆ S` of separator customers sharing\nno pattern with `A` and `T_R ⊆ S` of those sharing none with `R` gives the\nalternating sum of §7.1 (a), whose summand depends on `(T_A, T_R)` only\nthrough `u = |S ∖ T_R|`, `w = |S ∖ T_A|` and is a product over the\nindependent patterns of `allowed′(u, w; s_j)/C(n, s_j)` (the three cases of\n§7.1 (a), disjoint). ∎\n\n*Proof of Proposition T.* By Corollary S′ with `k = ⌊βn⌋`,\n`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))`. (Thinning step) `G(n, c/n) − S`\nis `G(N, c/n) = G(N, c′/N)` with `c′ = cN/n = c(1 − k/n) → c(1 − β)`.\n(Entropy step) `C(n, k) ≤ e^{n H(k/n)}` (Lemma 1 of\n`random_graph_first_moment.md` §2.1, the two-symbol case). (Hypothesis)\n`P_N(E_relax(t)) ≤ e^{−(I(c′) − o(1)) N}`. So\n`P(pw ≤ βn) ≤ exp(n [H(β) − (1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) + o(1)])`, and as `β → 0`,\n`H(β) → 0` and `(1 − β) I(c(1 − β)) → I(c) > 0` by continuity, so the\nbracket is negative for every sufficiently small `β > 0`. ∎ (The pair\ncount's bracket at `β → 0` is `ln 2 − c/4`, negative only for `c > 4 ln 2`;\nthe true threshold of linear pathwidth is `c = 1`.)\n\n*Proof of Proposition T-cover.* §7.4. ∎\n\n### 8.3 The check\n\n`check.py` (`check.log`, `lemma_w_n10.csv`; 29 s on 8 workers at nice 19\nunder a one-minute load below 2). Stage 1 enumerates every labelled graph\non `n = 1, …, 7` vertices (`2^{C(n,2)}`: 1, 2, 8, 64, 1,024, 32,768,\n2,097,152), computes each pathwidth by the subset DP and, in exact\nrationals at `p = 1/2` and `p = 1/3`, for every `k < n`:\n\n* the weighted count of `{pw ≤ k}` against `C(n, k) · P_N(E_bip)` (Theorem S),\n  against `C(n, k) · P_N(E_relax(t))` (Corollary S′), against `E[X₃]`\n  (Theorem Cl) and against `E[X₁]` (the count on record at exact sizes);\n* the enumerated `E[X₃]` against Theorem Cl's formula, and `E[X₁]` against\n  `C(n, t) C(n − t, r) (1 − p)^{t r}` — equalities;\n* on `N` vertices, `P_N(connected)` against Lemma G, `P_N(E_bip)` against\n  Lemma P and `P_N(E_relax(M))` for every `M` against Lemma F — equalities\n  (and at `p = 1/2` Gilbert's recursion returns the labelled connected\n  counts 1, 1, 4, 38, 728, 26,704, 1,866,256, OEIS A001187);\n* at `n ≤ 6`, for **every** `k`-set `S`, the weighted count of `{E_bip(S)}`\n  against `P_N(E_bip)` — the factorisation identity, equality on all 120\n  `(n, k, S)` triples at each `p`;\n* at `n ≤ 6`, the flip lemma on every exact-size empty pair at every `k`\n  (1,422,005 pairs): every swap is an exact-size empty pair, the swaps are\n  distinct, their number is `C(|I_A| + |I_R|, |I_A|) ≤ X`;\n* the floating-point `log_prob_bip`, `log_prob_rel` (§5) and\n  `log_closed_count` (§6) pinned to the exact values to `10⁻⁹`.\n\nThe `n = 7` rows (`n ≤ 6` in the log; `P` is the weighted count, i.e. the\nprobability under `G(7, p)`):\n\n| `k` | `N` | `t` | `p` | `P(pw ≤ k)` | `C(n,k) P_N(E_bip)` | `C(n,k) P_N(E_relax)` | `E[X₃]` (Cl) | `E[X₁]` (pairs) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 0 | 7 | 4 | 1/2 | 0.0000005 | 0.00639 | 0.00639 | 0.00854 | 0.00854 |\n| 1 | 6 | 3 | 1/2 | 0.0172 | 0.1156 | 0.1188 | 0.2264 | 0.2734 |\n| 2 | 5 | 3 | 1/2 | 0.3389 | 2.174 | 2.174 | 1.948 | 3.281 |\n| 3 | 4 | 2 | 1/2 | 0.8852 | 5.469 | 5.469 | 6.006 | 13.125 |\n| 4 | 3 | 2 | 1/2 | 0.9985 | 17.50 | 17.50 | 10.18 | 26.25 |\n| 5 | 2 | 1 | 1/2 | 1.0000 | 10.50 | 10.50 | 14.37 | 21.00 |\n| 6 | 1 | 1 | 1/2 | 1.0000 | 7.000 | 7.000 | 15.37 | 7.000 |\n| 0 | 7 | 4 | 1/3 | 0.0002 | 0.1245 | 0.1245 | 0.2698 | 0.2698 |\n| 1 | 6 | 3 | 1/3 | 0.2449 | 1.137 | 1.167 | 2.343 | 3.642 |\n| 2 | 5 | 3 | 1/3 | 0.8467 | 8.330 | 8.330 | 7.351 | 18.44 |\n| 3 | 4 | 2 | 1/3 | 0.9959 | 14.60 | 14.60 | 13.58 | 41.48 |\n| 4 | 3 | 2 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 25.93 | 25.93 | 17.87 | 46.67 |\n| 5 | 2 | 1 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 14.00 | 14.00 | 22.16 | 28.00 |\n| 6 | 1 | 1 | 1/3 | 1.0000 | 7.000 | 7.000 | 23.16 | 7.000 |\n\nRead across at seven vertices only: every bound is above the probability\n(as it must be; most are above `1` and say nothing); `E_bip` and `E_relax`\ndiffer in measure for the first time at `N = 6` (`0.1156` against\n`0.1188` at `p = 1/2`; at `N ≤ 5` the two events coincide on every graph,\n`3K₂` being the first separation, §2.2 d); the separator count is the\nsmaller of the two new counts at `k = 0–1`, the closed count at\n`k = 2–4` — a seven-vertex echo of §6.3's \"separator on the sparse side,\nclosed pair on the dense side\", and nothing more at this size; at `k = n − 1`\nthe closed count exceeds the pair count (it admits sides larger than the\nforced ones, `s < k`). None of these numbers is quoted anywhere as a\nbound.\n\nStage 2, Lemma W at `n = 10` on 1,000 random instances (600 `G(10, p)`,\n`p ∈ {0.2, …, 0.7}`, seed = index; 400 covers from\n`generate_structured_instance(10, m ∈ {10, 20}, 2–4 customers per pattern,\nseed = index)`, the MOSP graph), at `k = pw` and `k = pw − 1`, with\n`Z_move(P)` = the size of `P`'s move-class (§3) and `Z_flip` = the flip\ncount: `1 ≤ Z ≤ X` on every pair, `Y_move = X₄` exactly, `1 ≤ Y_flip ≤ X`\nwhenever `X > 0`, `Y = 0 = 1[X > 0]` whenever `X = 0`, every swap an\nexact-size empty pair. Medians at `k = pw`: `X = 12`, `X₄ = 5`,\n`Y_flip = 10` on the random graphs; `8`, `4`, `6` on the covers; at\n`k = pw − 1`, `X = 0` on 351 of 600 and 225 of 400 (so `Y = 0` there,\nand the identity is exact on both sides). The flips merge a factor\n`1.2–1.3` at the median at these sizes; the move-classes `2.4`.\n\nStage 3, the first instance: Lemma J's inverted moments equal the\nenumerated joint law `P(I_A = i, I_R = i′, empty)` on 24 random\n`(n, sizes, a, r′)` cases at `n = 3–6`, `m ≤ 3`, sizes 2–3 (every cover\nenumerated; item 07's test does 66 more); and `E[Y]`, `E[X]` from\n`flip_log_bound` against 1,000 Monte Carlo covers at `n = 10`, `m = 10`,\n`d = 3` (i.i.d. uniform patterns, the model of `CliqueCoverMoment.lean`):\n\n| `k` | `N`, `t` | exact `E[Y]` | sample `± s.e.` | exact `E[X]` | sample `± s.e.` | sample `P(X > 0)` |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 3 | 7, 4 | 1.350 | 1.207 ± 0.084 | 1.430 | 1.291 ± 0.092 | 0.418 |\n| 4 | 6, 3 | 9.419 | 9.403 ± 0.332 | 10.638 | 10.602 ± 0.406 | 0.872 |\n| 5 | 5, 3 | 41.23 | 41.31 ± 0.75 | 49.48 | 49.06 ± 1.04 | 1.000 |\n| 6 | 4, 2 | 70.19 | 71.22 ± 0.72 | 88.40 | 89.50 ± 1.14 | 1.000 |\n\nAll within 1.7 standard errors; `P(X > 0) ≤ E[Y] ≤ E[X]` in every row, as\nLemma W says. At `k = 3` the weighted count sits at `1.35` against a true\n`P(X > 0)` of about `0.42`