{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-fable-5-1","id":"msg_011Cgd3S1tVoyFTVaaGsKvDk","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01S9nzV4FVM2WakkvSg8a8CV","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex\")\ns = p.read_text()\n\ndef rep(old, new, count=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old) == count, (s.count(old), old[:80])\n    s = s.replace(old, new)\n\n# 1. header comment\nrep(\"\"\"% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n% The paragraph \"Why the first moment is loose\" and the closing of the section\n% (loop0011 item 12, 2026-11-01): reports/separator_moment.md (section 0 is the\n% summary; Theorem S is its section 8, the constants sections 6, 7 and 9, the\n% planted cluster section 4, the ledger in stacks section 11); Lean:\n% SeparatorMoment.lean.\"\"\")\n\n# 2. the paragraph \"Why the first moment is loose\": replace its last two sentences\nrep(\"\"\"$\\\\Theta(\\\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nempty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n$\\\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\ncondition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to\nthree stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a\ncount over one representative per cluster is the route not taken.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"$\\\\Theta(\\\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nother direction is a count that names \\\\emph{fewer} vertices, and we tried\nthree: the event itself, counted once per separator; the closed pair; and a\nweighted count. A layout of width $k<n$ forces a set $S$ of $k$ vertices whose\nremoval leaves the other $N=n-k$ vertices split into two sides of sizes\n$\\\\lceil N/2\\\\rceil$ and $\\\\lfloor N/2\\\\rfloor$ with no edge between them, and\nfor a fixed $S$ the probability of that event depends only on the component\nstructure of $G-S$, which is $G(N,p)$.\n\n\\\\begin{theorem}[separators, finite form]\n\\\\label{thm:separator}\nFor $0\\\\le p\\\\le1$ and $k<n$,\n$\\\\Pr[\\\\pw(G(n,p))\\\\le k]\\\\le\\\\binom nk\\\\,P_N(E_{\\\\mathrm{bip}})$, where\n$P_N(E_{\\\\mathrm{bip}})$ is the probability that the components of $G(N,p)$ can\nbe grouped into two classes of sizes $\\\\lceil N/2\\\\rceil$ and $\\\\lfloor N/2\\\\rfloor$.\n\\\\end{theorem}\n\nThe union bound over the $\\\\binom nk$ sets $S$ is the only inequality: the\ngraphs on $n$ vertices with a given restriction to $V\\\\setminus S$ have total\nweight equal to the restriction's $G(N,p)$ weight, and the inner probability\nis the same for every $S$. It is a Lean theorem with the inner probability as a\nfinite weighted count~\\\\leanref{theoremS}; on paper that count is a sum over the\ninteger partitions of $N$ with Gilbert's connectedness recursion, which we\nevaluate exactly to $N=120$. Step~2 bounds the same event by its number of\nwitnesses, $\\\\binom Nt(1-p)^{t(N-t)}$; the theorem merges every balanced empty\nbipartition of $G-S$ into one event. With $c'=c(1-\\\\beta)$ the mean degree of\n$G-S$, the merge is worth $\\\\ln2$ plus less than $0.3$ nats at $N=120$ for\n$c'\\\\ge8$ (the two orientations of one bipartition and nothing else) and grows\nlinearly in $N$ below $c'\\\\approx5$. Writing $I(c')$ for the rate at which the\nprobability that no component of $G(N,c'/N)$ exceeds $N/2$ decays, the\nconstant becomes the root $\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}(c)$ of\n$H(\\\\beta)=(1-\\\\beta)\\\\,I(c(1-\\\\beta))$. That rate is a corollary of the\nlarge-deviation principle for the largest component\n\\\\citep{OConnell1998,Puhalskii2005,AndreisKonigPatterson2021}, whose rate\nfunction at one half is positive exactly when $c'>2\\\\ln2$; our exact inner\nprobabilities reproduce the quoted rate to $0.002$ at seven values of $c'$\n(fitted over $N=100$--$400$), and the bipartition event has the same rate as\nits relaxation to $N=120$. With it, $\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}=0.037$ at $c=3$\nagainst $\\\\beta^{*}=0.009$, and $0.341$ at $c=10$ against $0.337$: a third of\nthe gap to the slope fitted to exact pathwidths ($0.099$) at $c=3$, a\ntwentieth at $c=10$ ($0.415$). Conditional on the cited large-deviation upper\nbound, the threshold of the method moves from $4\\\\ln2$ to $2\\\\ln2$, the density\nat which the giant component passes half the vertices and the bisection width\nbecomes linear \\\\citep{LuczakMcDiarmid2001}; the true threshold is $1$. The\nseparator as the object of a width first moment is in \\\\citet{DoErdeKang2024},\nwith a deterministic bound on the bipartitions per separator in place of their\nprobability; the exact inner count of a single vertex cut is in\n\\\\citet{KolesnikWormald2014}, for random regular graphs. We found no prior\nstatement of the inner probability of the bipartition event, nor of its use\nfor a width constant.\n\nThe closed pair, in which every separator vertex is adjacent to both sides\n($S=N(A)\\\\cap N(R)$; a layout forces one with sides at least the forced sizes),\nis the dense-side counterpart. Its expected number is exactly\n$\\\\sum n!/(a!\\\\,r!\\\\,s!)\\\\,(1-p)^{ar}\\\\bigl((1-(1-p)^{a})(1-(1-p)^{r})\\\\bigr)^{s}$ over\nthe admissible size triples (the union bound and the characterisation in\nLean~\\\\leanref{theoremCl}, the independence step on paper), and its constant\nexceeds $\\\\beta^{*}$ by $0.007$--$0.010$ at $c=6$--$12$, and by $0.011$ on random\ncovers with $d=4$ and $d=6$, $\\\\mu=1$, where the separator count has no\nlarge-deviation rate to cite. A weighted count, each empty pair divided by the\nnumber of pairs reachable from it by moving low-degree separator vertices\nacross, is a valid first moment too~\\\\leanref{weightedcount}, and on this\nrecord adds nothing to the closed pair: its lower tail is the event that the\npair is closed. None of the three moves the headline constants by the $0.02$\nwe had set as worth a page; at $c=6$ and $10$ the best of them closes $12\\\\%$\nand $11\\\\%$ of the gap to the fitted slope.\n\nWhat the cluster is we measured directly, by planting an empty pair and\nenumerating the others ($n\\\\le32$ on $G(n,c/n)$ at $c=6$ and $10$, $n\\\\le28$ on\ncovers at $d=6$, $\\\\mu=1$, 200 instances per cell). The other empty pairs form\ntwo lobes, perturbations of the planted pair and of its reverse through the\nseparator, with nothing between; on the covers the median planted instance\nhas no pair but the reverse, so the $e^{0.23n}$ is carried by rare instances,\nand on $G(n,c/n)$ the median cluster grows at about half the rate of the mean.\nThe whole slack of the union bound at a cell is the mean of $1/X$ under the\nplanted law, a bounded statistic: $0.065$, $0.039$ and $0.043$ nats per vertex\nat the largest sizes, that is $0.019$, $0.012$ and $0.015$ in $\\\\beta$, growing\nsub-linearly in $n$ over the range (measured). What remains is stated in\nstacks on certified instances: the counts of this paragraph move the floor by\none to two stacks at 60 to 75 vertices, the pathwidth sits four to five stacks\nhigher, and Harper's vertex-isoperimetric bound, a deterministic consequence of\nthe empty pair, is within a stack of the pathwidth wherever our branch and\nbound reaches the forced prefix, which puts at least three quarters of the\nleftover in the union bound as read on instances, while the planted\nmeasurement at $n\\\\le32$ reads the same slack as $0.012$--$0.019$ in $\\\\beta$. The\ntwo instruments see different sizes, and we do not reconcile them.\n\"\"\")\n\n# 3. the sandwich paragraph: the published upper line\nrep(\"\"\"width below $0.12$ at $e\\\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\\\le e\\\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"width below $0.12$ at $e\\\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\\\le e\\\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest. The best published upper bound we know,\nthe degree-sequence bound of \\\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, is\nwider still on the cover degree law, $0.66$, $0.86$ and $0.96$ at $d=4$, $6$,\n$10$ with $\\\\mu=1$, because it sees the degrees of a union of cliques and not\nthe cliques.\"\"\")\n\n# 4. what is checked\nrep(\"\"\"is proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"is proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently. Theorem~\\\\ref{thm:separator} is a Lean\ntheorem with its inner probability as a finite weighted count; of the\nclosed-pair count, the union bound and the characterisation of a closed pair\nare in Lean and the independence step is on paper; the constants\n$\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}$ rest on a cited large-deviation rate, and every rate we\nfitted is called fitted.\n\"\"\")\n\n# 5. the closing sentence\nrep(\"\"\"is of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a count over one representative per cluster of empty\npairs (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:excess}), not a sharper estimate of this one.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"is of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$. Counting the event a\nnarrow layout forces instead of its witnesses, once per separator or once per\nclosed pair (the paragraph \\\\emph{Why the first moment is loose} in\nAppendix~\\\\ref{app:excess}), closes a third of it at $c=3$ and a tenth at $c=6$\nand $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on\n$\\\\pw/n$ is $[0.037,0.183]$, $[0.182,0.458]$ and $[0.345,0.656]$ there, against\nfitted slopes of $0.099$, $0.268$ and $0.415$.\"\"\")\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"ok\", len(s.splitlines()))\nPYEOF","description":"Apply the five appendix edits with exact-match replacements"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":32,"cache_creation_input_tokens":800,"cache_read_input_tokens":333585,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":800,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":93,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"705e2dc1-3bd5-4ca7-a2e8-b0a1809ff2f3","uuid":"80197a80-1bd9-4d22-9c6b-749bdcdb5e0d","timestamp":"2026-11-01T23:03:51.696Z","request_id":"req_011Cgd3S1UwC3gm761JXjrm5","wire_tool_inputs":{"toolu_01S9nzV4FVM2WakkvSg8a8CV":{"command":"cd /home/al/dev/MOSP && python3 - <<'PYEOF'\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path(\"paper1/latex/sec9e_appendix_random.tex\")\ns = p.read_text()\n\ndef rep(old, new, count=1):\n    global s\n    assert s.count(old) == count, (s.count(old), old[:80])\n    s = s.replace(old, new)\n\n# 1. header comment\nrep(\"\"\"% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\"\"\",\n\"\"\"% reports/random_graph_first_moment.md (Theorems A, A', A'' and the literature\n% verdict); Lean: EmptyPair.lean, FirstMoment.lean, FirstMomentAsymptotics.lean.\n% The beta*(c) values: reports/scratch/first_moment/beta_star.log (two codes agree).\n% The paragraph \"Why the first moment is loose\" and the closing of the section\n% (loop0011 item 12, 2026-11-01): reports/separator_moment.md (section 0 is the\n% summary; Theorem S is its section 8, the constants sections 6, 7 and 9, the\n% planted cluster section 4, the ledger in stacks section 11); Lean:\n% SeparatorMoment.lean.\"\"\")\n\n# 2. the paragraph \"Why the first moment is loose\": replace its last two sentences\nrep(\"\"\"$\\\\Theta(\\\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nempty pairs cluster ($E[X^{2}]/E[X]^{2}=e^{0.23n}$ at $\\\\beta^{*}$ for $d=6$,\n$\\\\mu=1$), and on 650 certified instances at 30--75 customers the empty-pair\ncondition is within a stack of the pathwidth while the floor sits one to\nthree stacks lower. A second moment bounds $\\\\Pr[X>0]$ from the wrong side; a\ncount over one representative per cluster is the route not taken.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"$\\\\Theta(\\\\varepsilon)$ of constraint. The slack is in the union bound. The\nother direction is a count that names \\\\emph{fewer} vertices, and we tried\nthree: the event itself, counted once per separator; the closed pair; and a\nweighted count. A layout of width $k<n$ forces a set $S$ of $k$ vertices whose\nremoval leaves the other $N=n-k$ vertices split into two sides of sizes\n$\\\\lceil N/2\\\\rceil$ and $\\\\lfloor N/2\\\\rfloor$ with no edge between them, and\nfor a fixed $S$ the probability of that event depends only on the component\nstructure of $G-S$, which is $G(N,p)$.\n\n\\\\begin{theorem}[separators, finite form]\n\\\\label{thm:separator}\nFor $0\\\\le p\\\\le1$ and $k<n$,\n$\\\\Pr[\\\\pw(G(n,p))\\\\le k]\\\\le\\\\binom nk\\\\,P_N(E_{\\\\mathrm{bip}})$, where\n$P_N(E_{\\\\mathrm{bip}})$ is the probability that the components of $G(N,p)$ can\nbe grouped into two classes of sizes $\\\\lceil N/2\\\\rceil$ and $\\\\lfloor N/2\\\\rfloor$.\n\\\\end{theorem}\n\nThe union bound over the $\\\\binom nk$ sets $S$ is the only inequality: the\ngraphs on $n$ vertices with a given restriction to $V\\\\setminus S$ have total\nweight equal to the restriction's $G(N,p)$ weight, and the inner probability\nis the same for every $S$. It is a Lean theorem with the inner probability as a\nfinite weighted count~\\\\leanref{theoremS}; on paper that count is a sum over the\ninteger partitions of $N$ with Gilbert's connectedness recursion, which we\nevaluate exactly to $N=120$. Step~2 bounds the same event by its number of\nwitnesses, $\\\\binom Nt(1-p)^{t(N-t)}$; the theorem merges every balanced empty\nbipartition of $G-S$ into one event. With $c'=c(1-\\\\beta)$ the mean degree of\n$G-S$, the merge is worth $\\\\ln2$ plus less than $0.3$ nats at $N=120$ for\n$c'\\\\ge8$ (the two orientations of one bipartition and nothing else) and grows\nlinearly in $N$ below $c'\\\\approx5$. Writing $I(c')$ for the rate at which the\nprobability that no component of $G(N,c'/N)$ exceeds $N/2$ decays, the\nconstant becomes the root $\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}(c)$ of\n$H(\\\\beta)=(1-\\\\beta)\\\\,I(c(1-\\\\beta))$. That rate is a corollary of the\nlarge-deviation principle for the largest component\n\\\\citep{OConnell1998,Puhalskii2005,AndreisKonigPatterson2021}, whose rate\nfunction at one half is positive exactly when $c'>2\\\\ln2$; our exact inner\nprobabilities reproduce the quoted rate to $0.002$ at seven values of $c'$\n(fitted over $N=100$--$400$), and the bipartition event has the same rate as\nits relaxation to $N=120$. With it, $\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}=0.037$ at $c=3$\nagainst $\\\\beta^{*}=0.009$, and $0.341$ at $c=10$ against $0.337$: a third of\nthe gap to the slope fitted to exact pathwidths ($0.099$) at $c=3$, a\ntwentieth at $c=10$ ($0.415$). Conditional on the cited large-deviation upper\nbound, the threshold of the method moves from $4\\\\ln2$ to $2\\\\ln2$, the density\nat which the giant component passes half the vertices and the bisection width\nbecomes linear \\\\citep{LuczakMcDiarmid2001}; the true threshold is $1$. The\nseparator as the object of a width first moment is in \\\\citet{DoErdeKang2024},\nwith a deterministic bound on the bipartitions per separator in place of their\nprobability; the exact inner count of a single vertex cut is in\n\\\\citet{KolesnikWormald2014}, for random regular graphs. We found no prior\nstatement of the inner probability of the bipartition event, nor of its use\nfor a width constant.\n\nThe closed pair, in which every separator vertex is adjacent to both sides\n($S=N(A)\\\\cap N(R)$; a layout forces one with sides at least the forced sizes),\nis the dense-side counterpart. Its expected number is exactly\n$\\\\sum n!/(a!\\\\,r!\\\\,s!)\\\\,(1-p)^{ar}\\\\bigl((1-(1-p)^{a})(1-(1-p)^{r})\\\\bigr)^{s}$ over\nthe admissible size triples (the union bound and the characterisation in\nLean~\\\\leanref{theoremCl}, the independence step on paper), and its constant\nexceeds $\\\\beta^{*}$ by $0.007$--$0.010$ at $c=6$--$12$, and by $0.011$ on random\ncovers with $d=4$ and $d=6$, $\\\\mu=1$, where the separator count has no\nlarge-deviation rate to cite. A weighted count, each empty pair divided by the\nnumber of pairs reachable from it by moving low-degree separator vertices\nacross, is a valid first moment too~\\\\leanref{weightedcount}, and on this\nrecord adds nothing to the closed pair: its lower tail is the event that the\npair is closed. None of the three moves the headline constants by the $0.02$\nwe had set as worth a page; at $c=6$ and $10$ the best of them closes $12\\\\%$\nand $11\\\\%$ of the gap to the fitted slope.\n\nWhat the cluster is we measured directly, by planting an empty pair and\nenumerating the others ($n\\\\le32$ on $G(n,c/n)$ at $c=6$ and $10$, $n\\\\le28$ on\ncovers at $d=6$, $\\\\mu=1$, 200 instances per cell). The other empty pairs form\ntwo lobes, perturbations of the planted pair and of its reverse through the\nseparator, with nothing between; on the covers the median planted instance\nhas no pair but the reverse, so the $e^{0.23n}$ is carried by rare instances,\nand on $G(n,c/n)$ the median cluster grows at about half the rate of the mean.\nThe whole slack of the union bound at a cell is the mean of $1/X$ under the\nplanted law, a bounded statistic: $0.065$, $0.039$ and $0.043$ nats per vertex\nat the largest sizes, that is $0.019$, $0.012$ and $0.015$ in $\\\\beta$, growing\nsub-linearly in $n$ over the range (measured). What remains is stated in\nstacks on certified instances: the counts of this paragraph move the floor by\none to two stacks at 60 to 75 vertices, the pathwidth sits four to five stacks\nhigher, and Harper's vertex-isoperimetric bound, a deterministic consequence of\nthe empty pair, is within a stack of the pathwidth wherever our branch and\nbound reaches the forced prefix, which puts at least three quarters of the\nleftover in the union bound as read on instances, while the planted\nmeasurement at $n\\\\le32$ reads the same slack as $0.012$--$0.019$ in $\\\\beta$. The\ntwo instruments see different sizes, and we do not reconcile them.\n\"\"\")\n\n# 3. the sandwich paragraph: the published upper line\nrep(\"\"\"width below $0.12$ at $e\\\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\\\le e\\\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"width below $0.12$ at $e\\\\ge14$ and $0.42$--$0.49$ for $1\\\\le e\\\\le3.5$: it says\nleast where the search works hardest. The best published upper bound we know,\nthe degree-sequence bound of \\\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, is\nwider still on the cover degree law, $0.66$, $0.86$ and $0.96$ at $d=4$, $6$,\n$10$ with $\\\\mu=1$, because it sees the degrees of a union of cliques and not\nthe cliques.\"\"\")\n\n# 4. what is checked\nrep(\"\"\"is proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently.\n\"\"\",\n\"\"\"is proved on paper, and the values in Table~\\\\ref{tab:betastar} were computed\nby two programs written independently. Theorem~\\\\ref{thm:separator} is a Lean\ntheorem with its inner probability as a finite weighted count; of the\nclosed-pair count, the union bound and the characterisation of a closed pair\nare in Lean and the independence step is on paper; the constants\n$\\\\beta_{\\\\mathrm{sep}}$ rest on a cited large-deviation rate, and every rate we\nfitted is called fitted.\n\"\"\")\n\n# 5. the closing sentence\nrep(\"\"\"is of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$; closing it would need a count over one representative per cluster of empty\npairs (Appendix~\\\\ref{app:excess}), not a sharper estimate of this one.\"\"\",\n\"\"\"is of the order of $0.1$ and does not close with $n$. Counting the event a\nnarrow layout forces instead of its witnesses, once per separator or once per\nclosed pair (the paragraph \\\\emph{Why the first moment is loose} in\nAppendix~\\\\ref{app:excess}), closes a third of it at $c=3$ and a tenth at $c=6$\nand $10$; the best published upper bound, the degree-sequence bound of\n\\\\citet[Corollary~5.2]{EdwardsMcDermid2015}, gives $0.183$, $0.458$ and $0.656$\non Poisson degrees at $c=3$, $6$ and $10$, so the provable sandwich on\n$\\\\pw/n$ is $[0.037,0.183]$, $[0.182,0.458]$ and $[0.345,0.656]$ there, against\nfitted slopes of $0.099$, $0.268$ and $0.415$.\"\"\")\np.write_text(s)\nprint(\"ok\", len(s.splitlines()))\nPYEOF","description":"Apply the five appendix edits with exact-match replacements"}},"wire_ingest_context":{"toolu_01S9nzV4FVM2WakkvSg8a8CV":{"cwd":"/home/al/dev/MOSP"}}}
